const CONTENT_PACKS = [{"id":"2026-09-27-initial-batch","released":"2026-09-27","kind":"initial","reading_passages":{"0":[{"text":"Kai has a blue bucket. He fills it with sand at the beach. Then he builds a big castle.","questions":[{"question":"What color is the bucket?","choices":["blue","red","green","yellow"],"answer":"blue"},{"question":"Where is Kai?","choices":["at the beach","at school","at home","at the zoo"],"answer":"at the beach"},{"question":"What does Kai build?","choices":["a castle","a boat","a house","a tower"],"answer":"a castle"}]},{"text":"Ruby has a little frog. The frog is green. It hops on a log by the pond.","questions":[{"question":"What color is the frog?","choices":["green","red","brown","pink"],"answer":"green"},{"question":"Where does the frog hop?","choices":["on a log","in a box","on a bed","on a bus"],"answer":"on a log"}]},{"text":"Owen likes to eat soup. Today the soup is hot. He blows on it to cool it down.","questions":[{"question":"What does Owen eat?","choices":["soup","pizza","cake","corn"],"answer":"soup"},{"question":"How is the soup?","choices":["hot","cold","sweet","green"],"answer":"hot"},{"question":"What does Owen do to cool it?","choices":["blows on it","adds ice","waits a day","drinks it fast"],"answer":"blows on it"}]},{"text":"The bus is yellow. It stops at the school. Many kids get on the bus.","questions":[{"question":"What color is the bus?","choices":["yellow","blue","red","black"],"answer":"yellow"},{"question":"Where does the bus stop?","choices":["at the school","at the park","at the zoo","at the farm"],"answer":"at the school"}]},{"text":"Zoe plants a seed in a pot. She gives it water and sun. A small green plant grows.","questions":[{"question":"What does Zoe plant?","choices":["a seed","a rock","a toy","a shoe"],"answer":"a seed"},{"question":"What grows?","choices":["a small green plant","a big tree","a red flower","a bug"],"answer":"a small green plant"}]},{"text":"Max has a red kite. The wind is strong today. The kite flies high in the sky.","questions":[{"question":"What color is the kite?","choices":["red","blue","green","white"],"answer":"red"},{"question":"What is strong today?","choices":["the wind","the rain","the sun","the dog"],"answer":"the wind"},{"question":"Where does the kite fly?","choices":["high in the sky","under a tree","in a box","on the floor"],"answer":"high in the sky"}]},{"text":"Nina bakes a cake with her mom. The cake is pink. They put it on the table.","questions":[{"question":"Who bakes with Nina?","choices":["her mom","her dad","her friend","her dog"],"answer":"her mom"},{"question":"What color is the cake?","choices":["pink","brown","blue","white"],"answer":"pink"}]},{"text":"A duck swims in the pond. Her ducklings swim behind her. They all say quack.","questions":[{"question":"Who swims behind the duck?","choices":["her ducklings","her cats","her frogs","her fish"],"answer":"her ducklings"},{"question":"What do they say?","choices":["quack","moo","woof","meow"],"answer":"quack"}]},{"text":"Leo puts on his coat and hat. It is cold outside. He goes out to play in the snow.","questions":[{"question":"How is the weather?","choices":["cold","hot","warm","sunny"],"answer":"cold"},{"question":"Where does Leo play?","choices":["in the snow","in the sand","in a pool","in the mud"],"answer":"in the snow"}]},{"text":"Ava has a soft bed. At night she reads a book. Then she turns off the light and sleeps.","questions":[{"question":"What does Ava read?","choices":["a book","a map","a note","a sign"],"answer":"a book"},{"question":"What does she turn off?","choices":["the light","the radio","the fan","the tap"],"answer":"the light"}]},{"text":"The cow lives on a farm. She gives us milk. The farmer feeds her hay.","questions":[{"question":"Where does the cow live?","choices":["on a farm","in a pond","in a zoo","in a shop"],"answer":"on a farm"},{"question":"What does the cow give us?","choices":["milk","eggs","wool","honey"],"answer":"milk"},{"question":"What does the farmer feed her?","choices":["hay","fish","cake","rice"],"answer":"hay"}]},{"text":"Ben has four blocks. He stacks them up. The tower is tall.","questions":[{"question":"How many blocks does Ben have?","choices":["four","two","five","six"],"answer":"four"},{"question":"What does Ben do with the blocks?","choices":["stacks them up","throws them","hides them","paints them"],"answer":"stacks them up"}]},{"text":"Ivy sees a bee on a flower. The bee is yellow and black. It buzzes and flies away.","questions":[{"question":"Where is the bee?","choices":["on a flower","on a chair","on a cup","on a bike"],"answer":"on a flower"},{"question":"What sound does the bee make?","choices":["buzz","quack","roar","moo"],"answer":"buzz"}]},{"text":"Sam is sad. His ice cream fell on the ground. His mom gives him a new one.","questions":[{"question":"Why is Sam sad?","choices":["his ice cream fell","he lost a shoe","it is raining","he is tired"],"answer":"his ice cream fell"},{"question":"Who gives him a new one?","choices":["his mom","his dad","his friend","his teacher"],"answer":"his mom"}]},{"text":"Lily has a puppy. The puppy likes to dig in the garden. Lily gives it a bath.","questions":[{"question":"What does the puppy like to do?","choices":["dig","swim","sing","climb"],"answer":"dig"},{"question":"What does Lily give the puppy?","choices":["a bath","a toy","a hat","a bone"],"answer":"a bath"}]},{"text":"We go to the park on Sunday. We play on the swings. Then we eat a picnic.","questions":[{"question":"When do we go to the park?","choices":["Sunday","Monday","Friday","Tuesday"],"answer":"Sunday"},{"question":"What do we play on?","choices":["the swings","the slide","the bus","the boat"],"answer":"the swings"},{"question":"What do we eat?","choices":["a picnic","a pie","a pizza","a soup"],"answer":"a picnic"}]},{"text":"Theo has a toy train. It has three red cars. It goes around and around on the track.","questions":[{"question":"How many red cars does it have?","choices":["three","one","four","five"],"answer":"three"},{"question":"Where does the train go?","choices":["on the track","on the road","in the sea","up the tree"],"answer":"on the track"}]},{"text":"The moon is bright tonight. Stars twinkle in the dark sky. Mia looks at them from her window.","questions":[{"question":"When is the moon bright?","choices":["tonight","at noon","this morning","at lunch"],"answer":"tonight"},{"question":"What twinkles in the sky?","choices":["stars","cars","birds","fish"],"answer":"stars"},{"question":"Where does Mia look from?","choices":["her window","her car","her boat","her desk"],"answer":"her window"}]},{"text":"Jack drinks water from a cup. He is thirsty. He drinks it all up.","questions":[{"question":"What does Jack drink?","choices":["water","milk","juice","soup"],"answer":"water"},{"question":"How does Jack feel?","choices":["thirsty","sleepy","cold","sad"],"answer":"thirsty"}]},{"text":"The hen lays an egg. The egg is white. The farmer takes it to the kitchen.","questions":[{"question":"What does the hen lay?","choices":["an egg","a nut","a stone","a ball"],"answer":"an egg"},{"question":"What color is the egg?","choices":["white","brown","blue","pink"],"answer":"white"},{"question":"Who takes it to the kitchen?","choices":["the farmer","the cook","the baker","the dog"],"answer":"the farmer"}]},{"text":"Hana wears red boots in the rain. She jumps in a puddle. Splash! Water goes everywhere.","questions":[{"question":"What does Hana wear?","choices":["red boots","blue shoes","a green coat","a big hat"],"answer":"red boots"},{"question":"What does she jump in?","choices":["a puddle","a box","a bed","a bag"],"answer":"a puddle"}]},{"text":"Eli has a drum. He hits it with two sticks. Boom, boom, boom!","questions":[{"question":"What does Eli have?","choices":["a drum","a horn","a bell","a flute"],"answer":"a drum"},{"question":"What does he hit it with?","choices":["two sticks","his hands","a spoon","a shoe"],"answer":"two sticks"}]},{"text":"The fish is orange. It swims in a bowl. Chloe feeds it every morning.","questions":[{"question":"What color is the fish?","choices":["orange","blue","black","purple"],"answer":"orange"},{"question":"Where does the fish swim?","choices":["in a bowl","in a lake","in a cup","in a sink"],"answer":"in a bowl"},{"question":"When does Chloe feed it?","choices":["every morning","at night","at noon","on Sunday"],"answer":"every morning"}]},{"text":"Omar and his dad make a snowman. It has a carrot nose and two black eyes. They put a scarf on it.","questions":[{"question":"What nose does the snowman have?","choices":["a carrot nose","a red nose","a big nose","no nose"],"answer":"a carrot nose"},{"question":"What do they put on it?","choices":["a scarf","a coat","a bag","a shoe"],"answer":"a scarf"}]}],"1":[{"text":"Penguins cannot fly, but they are excellent swimmers. Their wings work like flippers, pushing them quickly through the water. Penguins spend most of their day hunting for fish in the cold sea.","questions":[{"question":"What do penguins use their wings for?","choices":["swimming","flying","digging","climbing"],"answer":"swimming"},{"question":"What do penguins hunt?","choices":["fish","birds","insects","seals"],"answer":"fish"},{"question":"Why can penguins move fast in water?","choices":["their wings work like flippers","they have long legs","they are very small","the water is warm"],"answer":"their wings work like flippers"}]},{"text":"Marcus wanted to bake bread, so he mixed flour, water, and yeast. He left the dough near the window for an hour. When he came back, the dough had grown twice as big. Then he baked it until it was golden.","questions":[{"question":"What did Marcus mix?","choices":["flour, water, and yeast","sugar, eggs, and milk","butter and salt","rice and beans"],"answer":"flour, water, and yeast"},{"question":"What happened to the dough?","choices":["it grew twice as big","it turned blue","it disappeared","it got smaller"],"answer":"it grew twice as big"},{"question":"Where did he leave the dough?","choices":["near the window","in the fridge","on the stove","in the sink"],"answer":"near the window"}]},{"text":"The library is a quiet place. People read books, use computers, and do homework there. The librarian helps visitors find the right book. Kids can borrow up to five books at a time.","questions":[{"question":"Who helps visitors find books?","choices":["the librarian","the teacher","the baker","the driver"],"answer":"the librarian"},{"question":"How many books can kids borrow?","choices":["five","two","ten","three"],"answer":"five"}]},{"text":"Amara's class went on a walk to collect leaves. They found red, orange, and yellow ones. Back at school, they pressed the leaves inside heavy books. After a week, the leaves were flat and dry.","questions":[{"question":"What colors of leaves did they find?","choices":["red, orange, and yellow","blue, pink, and white","black and gray","purple and silver"],"answer":"red, orange, and yellow"},{"question":"What did they press the leaves inside?","choices":["heavy books","wet towels","small boxes","warm ovens"],"answer":"heavy books"},{"question":"How long did it take for the leaves to dry?","choices":["a week","a day","a month","a year"],"answer":"a week"}]},{"text":"Bees live together in a hive. Worker bees fly out to find flowers and bring back nectar. Inside the hive, the bees turn the nectar into honey. One hive can make enough honey to feed many families.","questions":[{"question":"What do worker bees bring back?","choices":["nectar","seeds","leaves","water"],"answer":"nectar"},{"question":"What do bees make from nectar?","choices":["honey","wax","milk","sand"],"answer":"honey"},{"question":"Where do bees live?","choices":["in a hive","in a nest","in a burrow","in a cave"],"answer":"in a hive"}]},{"text":"Diego was nervous about his first swimming lesson. The water looked very deep. His teacher showed him how to float on his back. Soon Diego was smiling because floating felt easy.","questions":[{"question":"Why was Diego nervous?","choices":["it was his first lesson","he forgot his towel","the water was hot","he lost his goggles"],"answer":"it was his first lesson"},{"question":"What did the teacher show him?","choices":["how to float","how to dive","how to race","how to sing"],"answer":"how to float"}]},{"text":"A rainbow appears when sunlight shines through raindrops. The light bends and splits into many colors. A rainbow has red, orange, yellow, green, blue, and violet. You can often see one after a rain shower when the sun comes out.","questions":[{"question":"When does a rainbow appear?","choices":["when sunlight shines through raindrops","when it is very cold","when snow falls","at midnight"],"answer":"when sunlight shines through raindrops"},{"question":"How many colors does the passage name?","choices":["six","four","five","eight"],"answer":"six"},{"question":"When can you often see a rainbow?","choices":["after a rain shower","before a storm","in a dark room","during the night"],"answer":"after a rain shower"}]},{"text":"Rosa helps her grandmother in the garden every Saturday. They plant tomatoes, carrots, and beans. Rosa waters the plants with a small can. In summer, they pick the vegetables and cook a big dinner.","questions":[{"question":"When does Rosa help in the garden?","choices":["every Saturday","every Monday","every night","every winter"],"answer":"every Saturday"},{"question":"What does Rosa water the plants with?","choices":["a small can","a big hose","a bucket","a cup"],"answer":"a small can"},{"question":"What do they do in summer?","choices":["pick the vegetables","sell the house","go on a trip","paint the fence"],"answer":"pick the vegetables"}]},{"text":"Camels can go many days without water. They store fat in their humps, not water. This fat gives them energy when food is hard to find. Their wide feet also keep them from sinking in soft sand.","questions":[{"question":"What do camels store in their humps?","choices":["fat","water","sand","milk"],"answer":"fat"},{"question":"What do wide feet help camels do?","choices":["not sink in sand","swim fast","climb trees","stay cold"],"answer":"not sink in sand"},{"question":"What does the stored fat give camels?","choices":["energy","shade","noise","color"],"answer":"energy"}]},{"text":"Every night before bed, Jamal brushes his teeth for two minutes. He uses a soft brush and a little toothpaste. His dentist says this keeps teeth strong and healthy. Jamal has never had a cavity.","questions":[{"question":"How long does Jamal brush his teeth?","choices":["two minutes","ten minutes","one hour","five seconds"],"answer":"two minutes"},{"question":"Who says brushing keeps teeth healthy?","choices":["his dentist","his coach","his uncle","his neighbor"],"answer":"his dentist"},{"question":"What has Jamal never had?","choices":["a cavity","a haircut","a cold","a sticker"],"answer":"a cavity"}]},{"text":"The town holds a lemonade stand day each summer. Kids set up tables and sell cold drinks to neighbors. Priya made her lemonade with fresh lemons and a little honey. She sold every cup before noon.","questions":[{"question":"When does the town hold the event?","choices":["each summer","each winter","each spring","every night"],"answer":"each summer"},{"question":"What did Priya use in her lemonade?","choices":["fresh lemons and a little honey","orange juice and salt","sugar and milk","apples and mint"],"answer":"fresh lemons and a little honey"},{"question":"When did Priya sell every cup?","choices":["before noon","after dark","at midnight","the next day"],"answer":"before noon"}]},{"text":"Sea turtles hatch from eggs buried in warm sand. After hatching, the tiny turtles crawl toward the ocean. Many are eaten by birds on the way. Only a few survive to become adults.","questions":[{"question":"Where are sea turtle eggs buried?","choices":["in warm sand","in cold mud","under rocks","in tall grass"],"answer":"in warm sand"},{"question":"Where do the hatchlings crawl?","choices":["toward the ocean","into the forest","up a tree","into a cave"],"answer":"toward the ocean"},{"question":"Why do only a few survive?","choices":["many are eaten by birds","the water is too cold","they cannot swim","they forget the way"],"answer":"many are eaten by birds"}]},{"text":"On rainy days, Theo's family plays board games. Tonight they are playing checkers. Theo's sister always tries to win, but Theo likes just having fun. He laughs when she makes silly moves.","questions":[{"question":"What game are they playing tonight?","choices":["checkers","chess","cards","tag"],"answer":"checkers"},{"question":"Who always tries to win?","choices":["Theo's sister","Theo's dad","Theo's mom","the dog"],"answer":"Theo's sister"},{"question":"What does Theo like?","choices":["having fun","winning","sleeping","reading"],"answer":"having fun"}]},{"text":"The moon does not make its own light. It shines because it reflects light from the sun. As the moon travels around Earth, we see different parts of it lit up. This is why the moon seems to change shape each night.","questions":[{"question":"Where does the moon's light come from?","choices":["the sun","a lamp","the stars","the ocean"],"answer":"the sun"},{"question":"What does the moon travel around?","choices":["Earth","Mars","the sun","Venus"],"answer":"Earth"},{"question":"Why does the moon seem to change shape?","choices":["we see different parts lit up","clouds cover it","it melts","it grows and shrinks"],"answer":"we see different parts lit up"}]},{"text":"Chloe lost her lunch box on the bus. She looked in her bag and under her seat, but it was gone. The next morning, the bus driver returned it. Chloe thanked him with a big smile.","questions":[{"question":"Where did Chloe lose her lunch box?","choices":["on the bus","at the park","in the gym","at home"],"answer":"on the bus"},{"question":"Who returned it?","choices":["the bus driver","her teacher","her friend","her mom"],"answer":"the bus driver"},{"question":"How did Chloe thank him?","choices":["with a big smile","with a gift","with a song","with a card"],"answer":"with a big smile"}]},{"text":"Ice cream melts quickly on a hot day. The warm air turns the frozen treat into a sticky puddle. To stop this, Ravi eats his cone fast and holds a napkin. He finishes before a single drop falls.","questions":[{"question":"What makes ice cream melt?","choices":["warm air","cold wind","loud noise","dark clouds"],"answer":"warm air"},{"question":"What does Ravi hold?","choices":["a napkin","a spoon","a plate","a cup"],"answer":"a napkin"}]},{"text":"The school garden has a scarecrow. It wears an old hat and a checked shirt. Its job is to keep birds away from the seeds. The students named it Mr. Straw.","questions":[{"question":"What does the scarecrow wear?","choices":["an old hat and a checked shirt","a red coat and boots","a crown and a cape","a blue dress"],"answer":"an old hat and a checked shirt"},{"question":"What is the scarecrow's job?","choices":["keep birds away","water the plants","dig the soil","sing songs"],"answer":"keep birds away"},{"question":"What did the students name it?","choices":["Mr. Straw","Farmer Joe","Old Hat","Big Bird"],"answer":"Mr. Straw"}]},{"text":"Mountains are formed when huge pieces of Earth's crust push against each other. Over millions of years, the land is pushed upward. Wind and rain slowly wear the peaks down. This is why very old mountains look rounder than young ones.","questions":[{"question":"How are mountains formed?","choices":["pieces of crust push together","rivers dry up","volcanoes sleep","wind builds them fast"],"answer":"pieces of crust push together"},{"question":"What slowly wears peaks down?","choices":["wind and rain","snow only","sunlight","animals"],"answer":"wind and rain"},{"question":"Which mountains look rounder?","choices":["very old ones","young ones","tall trees","small hills"],"answer":"very old ones"}]},{"text":"Elena practices the piano after school. She plays scales first to warm up her fingers. Then she learns a new song, one small part at a time. When she plays the whole song without mistakes, she puts a star on her chart.","questions":[{"question":"What does Elena play first?","choices":["scales","a new song","her favorite song","a duet"],"answer":"scales"},{"question":"How does she learn a new song?","choices":["one small part at a time","all at once","by watching TV","with no practice"],"answer":"one small part at a time"},{"question":"What does she put on her chart?","choices":["a star","a heart","a flag","a number"],"answer":"a star"}]},{"text":"Frogs begin life as eggs in the water. The eggs hatch into tadpoles, which have tails and gills. Slowly, the tadpoles grow legs and lose their tails. Finally, they become frogs that can live on land.","questions":[{"question":"What do tadpoles have?","choices":["tails and gills","wings and fur","shells and claws","feathers and beaks"],"answer":"tails and gills"},{"question":"What do tadpoles slowly grow?","choices":["legs","wings","horns","feathers"],"answer":"legs"},{"question":"Where can adult frogs live?","choices":["on land","only in trees","only in caves","only in ice"],"answer":"on land"}]},{"text":"The bakery on Main Street opens at six every morning. Mr. Lopez bakes fresh rolls and muffins before the sun comes up. Neighbors line up to buy warm bread. By eight, the shelves are almost empty.","questions":[{"question":"When does the bakery open?","choices":["six every morning","noon","midnight","four in the afternoon"],"answer":"six every morning"},{"question":"Who bakes the rolls and muffins?","choices":["Mr. Lopez","Mr. Stone","Ms. Rivera","Mr. Kim"],"answer":"Mr. Lopez"},{"question":"What is almost empty by eight?","choices":["the shelves","the ovens","the street","the cash box"],"answer":"the shelves"}]},{"text":"A thunderstorm starts with warm air rising quickly. The air cools and forms tall dark clouds. Lightning is a giant spark of electricity in the cloud. Thunder is the loud sound the lightning makes.","questions":[{"question":"What is lightning?","choices":["a giant spark of electricity","a kind of rain","a loud sound","a type of wind"],"answer":"a giant spark of electricity"},{"question":"What is thunder?","choices":["the loud sound lightning makes","a bright flash","a type of cloud","a cold breeze"],"answer":"the loud sound lightning makes"}]},{"text":"Nora's little brother is learning to walk. He holds the couch and takes a tiny step. Then he wobbles and sits down with a thump. Everyone claps, and he tries again.","questions":[{"question":"What is Nora's brother learning?","choices":["to walk","to swim","to read","to draw"],"answer":"to walk"},{"question":"What does he hold?","choices":["the couch","the door","a toy","a hand"],"answer":"the couch"},{"question":"What does everyone do?","choices":["clap","cry","leave","sleep"],"answer":"clap"}]},{"text":"The ant carried a crumb twice its size across the yard. It walked around a rock and over a twig. Other ants joined to help pull the crumb. Together, they carried it into their nest.","questions":[{"question":"What did the ant carry?","choices":["a crumb","a leaf","a seed","a stick"],"answer":"a crumb"},{"question":"What did the other ants do?","choices":["helped pull the crumb","ran away","ate the crumb","hid under a rock"],"answer":"helped pull the crumb"},{"question":"Where did they take it?","choices":["into their nest","into the pond","up a tree","under the house"],"answer":"into their nest"}]}],"2":[{"text":"Engineers design suspension bridges to span wide rivers and deep valleys. Thick steel cables hang between tall towers, and smaller vertical cables connect the road to the main cables. The design spreads the weight of traffic across the whole structure, so no single part carries too much. Because the road can flex slightly in the wind, suspension bridges can be longer than most other kinds.","questions":[{"question":"What holds the road up on a suspension bridge?","choices":["vertical cables joined to the main cables","giant wooden beams below","floating platforms","heavy stone arches"],"answer":"vertical cables joined to the main cables"},{"question":"Why can suspension bridges be so long?","choices":["the road can flex slightly in the wind","they are made of stone","they use no steel","they are built on ice"],"answer":"the road can flex slightly in the wind"},{"question":"In the passage, the word span means?","choices":["to stretch across","to break apart","to paint over","to dig under"],"answer":"to stretch across"}]},{"text":"Plants make their own food through a process called photosynthesis. Their leaves absorb sunlight, take in carbon dioxide from the air, and draw up water through the roots. Using the energy of light, the plant turns these ingredients into sugar and releases oxygen. Animals, including people, depend on that oxygen to breathe, which makes plants essential to nearly all life on Earth.","questions":[{"question":"What do plants release during photosynthesis?","choices":["oxygen","carbon dioxide","nitrogen","smoke"],"answer":"oxygen"},{"question":"Why are plants essential to animals?","choices":["they release oxygen animals breathe","they give off heat at night","they stop the wind","they attract the rain"],"answer":"they release oxygen animals breathe"},{"question":"What does the plant make from light, water, and carbon dioxide?","choices":["sugar","salt","oil","soap"],"answer":"sugar"}]},{"text":"The ancient Egyptians built pyramids as tombs for their pharaohs. Thousands of workers cut enormous blocks of limestone and dragged them on wooden sledges to the building site. The Great Pyramid of Giza, completed around 2560 BCE, was the tallest structure on Earth for nearly four thousand years. Inside, chambers held treasures the Egyptians believed a pharaoh would need in the afterlife.","questions":[{"question":"Why did Egyptians build pyramids?","choices":["as tombs for their pharaohs","as homes for workers","as markets","as lighthouses"],"answer":"as tombs for their pharaohs"},{"question":"How did workers move the heavy blocks?","choices":["on wooden sledges","with steam engines","on boats only","using giant wheels"],"answer":"on wooden sledges"},{"question":"What can you infer about the Egyptians' beliefs?","choices":["they believed in an afterlife","they feared the sun","they had no religion","they never built anything else"],"answer":"they believed in an afterlife"}]},{"text":"Ocean tides rise and fall because of the moon's gravity. The moon pulls on Earth's water, creating a bulge on the side facing the moon. A second bulge forms on the opposite side of Earth. As the planet rotates, coastlines move through these bulges, which is why most places experience two high tides and two low tides every day. The sun also pulls on the oceans, but its effect is smaller.","questions":[{"question":"What mainly causes ocean tides?","choices":["the moon's gravity","ocean winds","underwater volcanoes","the sun's heat"],"answer":"the moon's gravity"},{"question":"How many high tides do most coastlines get each day?","choices":["two","one","three","four"],"answer":"two"},{"question":"Why is the sun's effect on tides smaller?","choices":["it is much farther away","it is smaller than Earth","it does not have gravity","its light is too weak"],"answer":"it is much farther away"}]},{"text":"Marie Curie was a physicist and chemist who studied radioactivity in the early 1900s. She discovered two new elements, polonium and radium, and became the first person to win Nobel Prizes in two different sciences. Her work laid the foundation for modern cancer treatments. During World War I, she also developed mobile X-ray units that doctors used near the battlefield to help wounded soldiers.","questions":[{"question":"What did Marie Curie study?","choices":["radioactivity","volcanoes","plants","sound waves"],"answer":"radioactivity"},{"question":"What was special about her Nobel Prizes?","choices":["she won in two different sciences","she won three in one year","she won them as a child","she gave them all away"],"answer":"she won in two different sciences"},{"question":"In the passage, the word foundation means?","choices":["a base for later work","a type of building","a small prize","a kind of experiment"],"answer":"a base for later work"}]},{"text":"Honeybees communicate with a remarkable waggle dance. When a bee finds good flowers, it returns to the hive and moves in a figure-eight pattern. The angle of the dance shows the direction of the flowers relative to the sun, and the length of the waggle shows the distance. Other bees watch closely, then fly straight to the food. This teamwork helps the whole colony gather nectar efficiently.","questions":[{"question":"What does the angle of the dance show?","choices":["the direction of the flowers","the type of flower","the time of day","the number of bees"],"answer":"the direction of the flowers"},{"question":"What does the length of the waggle show?","choices":["the distance to the flowers","the weather outside","the size of the hive","the color of the nectar"],"answer":"the distance to the flowers"},{"question":"What is the main idea of the passage?","choices":["bees share food locations by dancing","bees dislike the sun","bees build figure eights","bees only work alone"],"answer":"bees share food locations by dancing"}]},{"text":"A glacier is a huge river of ice that moves very slowly across land. Glaciers form where more snow falls each year than melts. Over centuries, the snow is squeezed into dense ice. As a glacier creeps downhill, it carves deep valleys and carries rocks along with it. Today, many glaciers are shrinking because temperatures are rising, which adds water to the oceans.","questions":[{"question":"Where do glaciers form?","choices":["where more snow falls than melts","where rivers dry out","near hot deserts","where the ground is flat and warm"],"answer":"where more snow falls than melts"},{"question":"What do moving glaciers do to the land?","choices":["carve deep valleys","build tall mountains overnight","stop all rainfall","create new deserts"],"answer":"carve deep valleys"},{"question":"Why are many glaciers shrinking?","choices":["temperatures are rising","more snow is falling","rivers are pulling them","the moon is closer"],"answer":"temperatures are rising"}]},{"text":"The kangaroo rat survives in the desert without ever drinking water. It gets moisture from the seeds it eats and produces very little waste, so its body loses almost no water. During the scorching day, it stays in a cool underground burrow and comes out only at night to search for food. These adaptations allow the tiny rodent to thrive where most animals could not.","questions":[{"question":"How does the kangaroo rat get moisture?","choices":["from the seeds it eats","from morning dew","from puddles","from other animals"],"answer":"from the seeds it eats"},{"question":"Why does it stay underground during the day?","choices":["to avoid the scorching heat","to hide from plants","to sleep in the sun","to dig for water"],"answer":"to avoid the scorching heat"},{"question":"In the passage, the word adaptations means?","choices":["changes that help survival","fast movements","loud sounds","large groups"],"answer":"changes that help survival"}]},{"text":"Alexander Graham Bell patented the telephone in 1876. His invention converted the sound of a voice into electrical signals that traveled along a wire, then turned them back into sound at the other end. At first, many people doubted that anyone would want such a device. Within a few decades, however, telephone lines connected cities across the country and changed how people did business and stayed in touch.","questions":[{"question":"What did Bell's telephone do?","choices":["turned voices into electrical signals","sent written letters","recorded music","measured distance"],"answer":"turned voices into electrical signals"},{"question":"How did people first react to the telephone?","choices":["many doubted it would be useful","everyone bought one","they thought it was dangerous","they ignored it for centuries"],"answer":"many doubted it would be useful"},{"question":"What is the main idea of the passage?","choices":["the telephone changed how people communicate","Bell disliked his invention","wires are hard to make","cities grew in the 1800s"],"answer":"the telephone changed how people communicate"}]},{"text":"Every fall, millions of monarch butterflies travel up to three thousand miles from Canada and the United States to forests in Mexico. No single butterfly makes the whole round trip. Instead, it takes several generations to complete the cycle, yet the newest generation somehow finds the same mountain forests its great-grandparents left. Scientists are still studying how the butterflies navigate such a long distance.","questions":[{"question":"Where do monarchs spend the winter?","choices":["forests in Mexico","deserts in Africa","icy fields in Canada","cities in Europe"],"answer":"forests in Mexico"},{"question":"Why is the migration surprising?","choices":["new generations find the same forests","the butterflies travel at night only","they never stop flying","they follow a single leader"],"answer":"new generations find the same forests"},{"question":"What can you infer about scientists?","choices":["they do not fully understand it yet","they have solved every mystery","they ignore butterflies","they disagree the trip is real"],"answer":"they do not fully understand it yet"}]},{"text":"Sound travels as vibrations that move through air, water, and solid objects. When a guitar string is plucked, it vibrates and pushes on the air around it, creating waves that reach your ears. Sound moves fastest through solids and slowest through gases, which is why you can hear a train coming by putting your ear against the track. In outer space, where there is almost no air, sound cannot travel at all.","questions":[{"question":"What creates sound?","choices":["vibrations","magnets","sunlight","cold air"],"answer":"vibrations"},{"question":"Through which material does sound travel fastest?","choices":["solids","gases","empty space","clouds"],"answer":"solids"},{"question":"Why can't sound travel in outer space?","choices":["there is almost no air","it is too dark","it is too far","there is too much light"],"answer":"there is almost no air"}]},{"text":"For centuries, the Silk Road connected China with the Mediterranean world. Merchants did not travel the entire route themselves; goods passed from trader to trader across deserts and mountains. Silk, spices, and paper moved west, while gold, glass, and horses moved east. Just as importantly, ideas, religions, and inventions spread along the same paths, linking cultures that might otherwise never have met.","questions":[{"question":"What moved west along the Silk Road?","choices":["silk, spices, and paper","gold and glass only","horses only","no goods at all"],"answer":"silk, spices, and paper"},{"question":"How did goods usually travel the route?","choices":["passing from trader to trader","one merchant made the whole trip","by airplane","on a single ship"],"answer":"passing from trader to trader"},{"question":"What else spread along the Silk Road?","choices":["ideas and inventions","only food","only animals","only money"],"answer":"ideas and inventions"}]},{"text":"Seasons happen because Earth's axis is tilted as it orbits the sun. When the northern half of Earth tilts toward the sun, it receives more direct sunlight and longer days, creating summer. Six months later, that half tilts away, so sunlight is weaker and days are shorter, bringing winter. The common belief that Earth is closer to the sun in summer is a myth; distance has little to do with it.","questions":[{"question":"What causes the seasons?","choices":["Earth's tilted axis","Earth's distance from the sun","the moon's orbit","changing ocean currents"],"answer":"Earth's tilted axis"},{"question":"What happens when a half of Earth tilts toward the sun?","choices":["it gets more direct sunlight","it gets colder","it loses its days","it stops rotating"],"answer":"it gets more direct sunlight"},{"question":"In the passage, the word myth means?","choices":["a false belief","a proven fact","a hard test","a kind of season"],"answer":"a false belief"}]},{"text":"Octopuses are among the most intelligent invertebrates. They can solve puzzles, open jars, and even learn by watching other octopuses. In labs, some have escaped from sealed tanks by squeezing through tiny gaps, since their bodies have no bones. Scientists believe their intelligence helps them hunt, hide from predators, and explore, even though they live only a few years.","questions":[{"question":"Why can octopuses squeeze through tiny gaps?","choices":["their bodies have no bones","they are very small","they can shrink to a dot","they have sharp claws"],"answer":"their bodies have no bones"},{"question":"Which is an example of octopus intelligence?","choices":["opening jars","swimming in circles","sleeping all day","changing the tide"],"answer":"opening jars"},{"question":"What is surprising about octopuses?","choices":["they are smart but live only a few years","they live for centuries","they cannot move","they avoid the ocean"],"answer":"they are smart but live only a few years"}]},{"text":"An electric circuit is a closed path that lets electricity flow. A simple circuit has a power source, such as a battery, wires, and a device like a light bulb. When the path is complete, electricity travels from the battery through the wires to the bulb, making it glow. If a switch opens the path, the flow stops and the light goes out, which is exactly how a light switch works in your home.","questions":[{"question":"What is needed for a simple circuit?","choices":["a power source, wires, and a device","only a battery","a magnet and a rope","water and metal"],"answer":"a power source, wires, and a device"},{"question":"What happens when a switch opens the path?","choices":["the electricity stops flowing","the bulb gets brighter","the battery grows","the wires melt"],"answer":"the electricity stops flowing"},{"question":"What does the passage compare a circuit to?","choices":["a light switch at home","a river with rocks","a garden hose","a school bus route"],"answer":"a light switch at home"}]},{"text":"The first Olympic Games took place in ancient Greece more than 2,700 years ago. They were held every four years in Olympia to honor Zeus, the king of the gods. Early events included running, wrestling, and chariot racing. During the Games, wars between city-states were paused so that athletes and spectators could travel safely. Winners received olive wreaths rather than medals.","questions":[{"question":"Who were the ancient Games held to honor?","choices":["Zeus","Apollo","Athena","Hercules"],"answer":"Zeus"},{"question":"Why were wars paused during the Games?","choices":["so people could travel safely","because of a storm","to save money","to build a stadium"],"answer":"so people could travel safely"},{"question":"What did winners receive?","choices":["olive wreaths","gold medals","silver cups","bags of coins"],"answer":"olive wreaths"}]},{"text":"Bats find their way in the dark using echolocation. A bat sends out high-pitched sounds that humans cannot hear. When the sounds hit an object, such as an insect, they bounce back as echoes. The bat's brain uses the time the echo takes to return to figure out the object's size, shape, and distance. Thanks to this ability, bats can catch tiny insects in complete darkness.","questions":[{"question":"How do bats find objects in the dark?","choices":["by listening to echoes","by smelling the air","by feeling the wind","by seeing well at night"],"answer":"by listening to echoes"},{"question":"What does the delay before an echo returns tell a bat?","choices":["how far away an object is","what color it is","how old it is","how tasty it is"],"answer":"how far away an object is"},{"question":"In the passage, the word echoes means?","choices":["sounds that bounce back","loud roars","soft songs","whispers of wind"],"answer":"sounds that bounce back"}]},{"text":"Composting turns kitchen scraps and yard waste into rich soil. Microscopic organisms, along with worms and insects, break down items like vegetable peels, coffee grounds, and dead leaves. A healthy compost pile needs a balance of moist green materials and dry brown materials, plus some air. After several months, the pile becomes dark, crumbly compost that gardeners mix into soil to help plants grow, while also keeping waste out of landfills.","questions":[{"question":"What do organisms do in a compost pile?","choices":["break down scraps into soil","turn scraps into plastic","keep the pile cold","stop the smell forever"],"answer":"break down scraps into soil"},{"question":"What does a healthy pile need?","choices":["a mix of green and brown materials","only dry sand","only plastic wrap","no air at all"],"answer":"a mix of green and brown materials"},{"question":"What are two benefits of composting?","choices":["richer soil and less landfill waste","more trash and less soil","louder gardens","faster cars"],"answer":"richer soil and less landfill waste"}]}]},"synonyms":{"easy":{"mad":"angry","sick":"ill","close":"near","pal":"friend","kid":"child","glad":"happy","story":"tale","road":"street","rock":"stone","start":"begin","end":"finish","leap":"jump","chilly":"cold","sleepy":"tired","unhappy":"sad","yell":"shout","grin":"smile","gift":"present","hurry":"rush","trash":"garbage","pick":"choose","repair":"fix","large":"big","little":"small","speak":"talk"},"hard":{"ample":"plentiful","brief":"short","detest":"hate","endure":"tolerate","abandon":"desert","adore":"cherish","anxious":"nervous","assist":"help","bizarre":"strange","commence":"start","courteous":"respectful","crucial":"essential","dwell":"reside","eager":"enthusiastic","fragrant":"aromatic","genuine":"authentic","hazardous":"dangerous","hilarious":"amusing","inquire":"ask","luxurious":"lavish","mimic":"imitate","obstinate":"headstrong","purchase":"buy","rapid":"swift","tremble":"shake","vacant":"empty","wealthy":"affluent"}},"antonyms":{"easy":{"cold":"hot","night":"day","wake":"sleep","give":"take","come":"go","high":"low","long":"short","fat":"thin","dark":"bright","slow":"fast","clean":"dirty","empty":"full","old":"new","sad":"glad","yes":"no","in":"out","on":"off","over":"under","below":"above","wrong":"right","sour":"sweet","smile":"frown","deep":"shallow","narrow":"wide","soft":"hard","lost":"found","boy":"girl"},"hard":{"arrogant":"humble","complex":"simple","conserve":"waste","decrease":"increase","defend":"attack","disperse":"gather","diminish":"expand","elevate":"lower","fertile":"barren","flourish":"wither","guilty":"blameless","hinder":"assist","intentional":"accidental","junior":"senior","liberty":"captivity","minimum":"maximum","mature":"immature","profit":"loss","reveal":"conceal","scatter":"collect","temporary":"permanent","vague":"precise","urban":"rural","zenith":"nadir","ascent":"descent"}},"rhymes":{"easy":[["pig","wig","dig","fig","jig"],["hop","mop","pop","shop","drop"],["bug","hug","rug","jug","tug"],["pan","fan","man","ran","van"],["pen","ten","den","men","then"],["fox","box","socks","rocks","locks"],["sing","ring","wing","swing","string"],["log","frog","fog","jog","hog"],["nut","cut","hut","shut","but"],["tree","free","knee","three","bee"],["boat","goat","float","note","vote"],["train","brain","chain","drain","plain"],["hill","fill","will","pill","spill"],["ship","chip","lip","trip","zip"],["kite","bite","right","white","quite"],["wall","fall","tall","small","call"],["nail","tail","mail","sail","pail"],["cow","now","how","bow","wow"],["sky","fly","cry","dry","try"],["clock","rock","sock","block","knock"]],"hard":[["nation","vacation","celebration","creation","education"],["weather","feather","leather","together","whether"],["through","blue","crew","threw","drew"],["fright","might","tight","delight","polite"],["scene","green","machine","between","routine"],["dough","glow","below","although","tomorrow"],["wear","bear","share","stare","prepare"],["grain","explain","complain","sustain","remain"],["cheer","appear","clear","steer","sincere"],["voice","choice","rejoice","invoice"],["trouble","double","bubble","rubble"],["flame","frame","shame","became","tame"],["believe","achieve","receive","sleeve","weave"],["mother","brother","other","another","smother"],["motion","ocean","lotion","potion","devotion"]]},"analogies":[["glove","hand","sock","foot"],["whale","ocean","camel","desert"],["pen","write","knife","cut"],["ear","hear","nose","smell"],["chef","kitchen","doctor","hospital"],["cub","bear","calf","cow"],["kitten","cat","foal","horse"],["cold","freezing","hot","boiling"],["small","tiny","big","huge"],["happy","smile","sad","frown"],["pig","oink","duck","quack"],["run","fast","crawl","slow"],["page","book","leaf","tree"],["driver","car","pilot","plane"],["teacher","school","chef","restaurant"],["lamp","light","fan","air"],["brush","paint","pen","ink"],["milk","cow","wool","sheep"],["egg","chicken","acorn","oak"],["thin","thick","short","tall"],["above","below","before","after"],["dry","desert","wet","swamp"],["freeze","ice","boil","steam"],["read","book","watch","movie"],["snow","winter","flowers","spring"],["horse","stable","cow","barn"],["shirt","wear","spoon","eat"],["ocean","salty","river","fresh"],["tall","giraffe","small","mouse"],["curtain","window","blanket","bed"]],"word_problem_templates":{"add":["{name} counted {a} {item} in the morning and {b} more {item} in the afternoon. How many {item} did {name} count in all?","A shelf holds {a} {item}. {name} puts {b} more {item} on it. How many {item} are on the shelf now?","{name} won {a} {item} at the fair and got {b} more {item} from a friend. How many {item} does {name} have?","On Saturday {name} found {a} {item}. On Sunday {name} found {b} {item}. How many {item} did {name} find over the weekend?","There were {a} {item} in the box. A helper added {b} more {item}. How many {item} are in the box now?","{name} saved {a} {item} last week and {b} {item} this week. How many {item} has {name} saved altogether?"],"sub":["{name} had {a} {item} and used {b} of them for a project. How many {item} does {name} have left?","A basket held {a} {item}. {name} took out {b} {item}. How many {item} are still in the basket?","{name} started the day with {a} {item} and lost {b} {item} on the way to school. How many {item} does {name} have now?","There were {a} {item} on the desk. Class started, and {b} {item} were handed out. How many {item} are still on the desk?","{name} collected {a} {item} and traded away {b} {item}. How many {item} are left with {name}?","{name} had {a} {item} and gave {b} of them to a friend. How many {item} does {name} still have?"],"mul":["{name} arranges {item} in {a} rows with {b} {item} in each row. How many {item} are there in all?","Each of {a} friends brings {b} {item} to the party. How many {item} do they bring altogether?","{name} puts {b} {item} in each of {a} bags. How many {item} does {name} pack in total?","A store sells {item} in packs of {b}. {name} buys {a} packs. How many {item} does {name} get?","There are {a} tables and each table has {b} {item}. How many {item} are on the tables?","{name} gets {b} {item} every day for {a} days. How many {item} does {name} get in all?"],"div":["{name} has {a} {item} to split equally into {b} piles. How many {item} go in each pile?","A teacher hands out {a} {item} equally to {b} students. How many {item} does each student get?","{name} packs {a} {item} into {b} equal boxes. How many {item} are in each box?","{a} {item} are shared equally by {b} teams. How many {item} does each team receive?","{name} sorts {a} {item} into {b} equal groups. How many {item} are in each group?","If {b} friends share {a} {item} equally, how many {item} does each friend get?"]}},{"id":"2026-09-30-advanced-vocabulary","released":"2026-09-30","kind":"manual","reading_passages":{"0":[{"text":"Mia visited a museum and saw a giant dinosaur skeleton. The dinosaur was taller than a house! Mia's favorite part was touching a real dinosaur tooth. She told everyone about her wonderful adventure.","questions":[{"question":"What did Mia see at the museum?","choices":["A shiny red firetruck","A huge space rocket","A talking robot","A dinosaur skeleton"],"answer":"A dinosaur skeleton"},{"question":"What was Mia's favorite part?","choices":["Eating a big birthday cake","Riding on a school bus","Watching a short movie","Touching a dinosaur tooth"],"answer":"Touching a dinosaur tooth"},{"question":"How did Mia feel about her trip?","choices":["It was wonderful","It was boring","It was scary","It was sad"],"answer":"It was wonderful"}],"photo":"images/flashcards/house.jpg"},{"text":"In the butterfly garden, hundreds of colorful butterflies fluttered everywhere. Ben watched one butterfly land gently on a flower. He stayed completely still so he would not frighten it away.","questions":[{"question":"Where was Ben?","choices":["Inside a school bus","Outside a small bakery","At a swimming pool","In a butterfly garden"],"answer":"In a butterfly garden"},{"question":"Why did Ben stay still?","choices":["Because he was playing a game of hide and seek","Because it was cold outside","Because he wanted to take a nap","So he would not frighten the butterfly"],"answer":"So he would not frighten the butterfly"}],"photo":"images/flashcards/flower.jpg"},{"text":"A helicopter landed on the school playground. Everyone was so excited! A pilot explained how the helicopter could fly straight up into the sky. Zoe decided she wanted to become a pilot someday.","questions":[{"question":"What landed on the playground?","choices":["A fast sailboat","A long train","A big bicycle","A helicopter"],"answer":"A helicopter"},{"question":"What does Zoe want to become?","choices":["A pilot","A teacher","A doctor","A chef"],"answer":"A pilot"}],"photo":"images/flashcards/up.jpg"},{"text":"At the aquarium, a stingray glided gently past the glass. Noah pressed his hand against the window. The stingray seemed curious and swam back and forth, as if it wanted to say hello.","questions":[{"question":"What glided past the glass?","choices":["A stingray","A dolphin","A duck","A turtle"],"answer":"A stingray"},{"question":"What did Noah do?","choices":["Climbed over the glass wall to see inside","Fed the fish a little snack","Took a nap on a bench","Pressed his hand against the window"],"answer":"Pressed his hand against the window"}],"photo":"images/flashcards/glass.jpg"},{"text":"In science class, the students built a model volcano together. They mixed vinegar and baking soda inside it. Orange bubbles erupted out of the top, and everyone cheered with excitement.","questions":[{"question":"What did the students build?","choices":["A tall wooden birdhouse","A paper puppet","A small sandcastle","A model volcano"],"answer":"A model volcano"},{"question":"What happened when they mixed the vinegar and baking soda?","choices":["Orange bubbles erupted","It turned into ice","Nothing happened","It became quiet"],"answer":"Orange bubbles erupted"}],"photo":"images/flashcards/volcano.jpg"},{"text":"At the planetarium, Lily leaned back in her chair and looked up. Suddenly, thousands of stars appeared above her! A narrator explained the names of different planets floating across the ceiling.","questions":[{"question":"Where was Lily?","choices":["At the planetarium","At a farm","At a pool","At a bakery"],"answer":"At the planetarium"},{"question":"What did Lily see above her?","choices":["Hundreds of balloons","A big bright rainbow","Some flying birds","Thousands of stars"],"answer":"Thousands of stars"}],"photo":"images/flashcards/up.jpg"}],"1":[{"text":"Meteorologists use powerful instruments to track hurricanes forming over warm ocean water. Satellites orbiting far above Earth capture images of swirling clouds, while specialized aircraft actually fly directly into the storm to measure wind speed and pressure. This information helps forecasters predict where a hurricane might travel next, giving coastal communities enough warning to prepare safely. Even with advanced technology, predicting the exact path of a hurricane remains remarkably difficult.","questions":[{"question":"What do meteorologists use to track hurricanes?","choices":["Old paper maps and a compass","Telescopes pointed at the moon at night","Only their eyes and a ruler","Powerful instruments and satellites"],"answer":"Powerful instruments and satellites"},{"question":"Why do specialized aircraft fly into storms?","choices":["To rescue people stuck in the storm","To deliver food to coastal towns","To take vacation photos for tourists","To measure wind speed and pressure"],"answer":"To measure wind speed and pressure"},{"question":"In the passage, the word \"remarkably\" means?","choices":["Not at all","Quite quickly","Rather sadly","Very notably"],"answer":"Very notably"}]},{"text":"Glassmaking begins with ordinary sand, which is heated inside a furnace until it reaches a temperature hot enough to turn the sand into a glowing, syrupy liquid. A skilled glassblower gathers a small amount of this molten glass onto the end of a long metal pipe and blows a steady breath through it, forming a bubble that can be shaped into bowls, vases, or ornaments. Because the glass cools and hardens within minutes, glassblowers must work with impressive speed and precision, constantly reheating the piece to keep it workable.","questions":[{"question":"What does heating sand in a furnace produce?","choices":["A cold, clear puddle of water","A thick cloud of grey smoke","A hard, heavy brick","A glowing, syrupy liquid"],"answer":"A glowing, syrupy liquid"},{"question":"Why must glassblowers work quickly?","choices":["The furnace shuts off automatically after a while","The metal pipe becomes too heavy to lift","The supply of sand runs out quickly","The glass cools and hardens within minutes"],"answer":"The glass cools and hardens within minutes"},{"question":"In the passage, \"precision\" most nearly means?","choices":["Careful accuracy","Great speed","Loud noise","Bright color"],"answer":"Careful accuracy"}],"photo":"images/flashcards/glass.jpg"},{"text":"Chocolate begins as a bitter seed called a cacao bean, harvested from large pods that grow directly on the trunks of cacao trees in tropical rainforests. After the beans are removed from the pod, they are left to ferment for several days, developing much of the rich flavor we associate with chocolate, before being dried, roasted, and ground into a thick paste. Ancient civilizations in Mesoamerica actually drank chocolate as a bitter, spiced beverage centuries before anyone added sugar to create the sweet treat most people recognize today.","questions":[{"question":"Where do cacao pods grow?","choices":["Deep underground like carrots","Floating on the leaves of tall palm trees","Inside the petals of large flowers","Directly on the trunks of cacao trees"],"answer":"Directly on the trunks of cacao trees"},{"question":"What happens to cacao beans before they are roasted?","choices":["They ferment for several days","They are frozen","They are painted","They are planted again"],"answer":"They ferment for several days"},{"question":"How did ancient Mesoamerican civilizations drink chocolate?","choices":["As a bitter, spiced beverage","As a sweet frozen treat","Mixed with milk and sugar","They did not drink chocolate"],"answer":"As a bitter, spiced beverage"}]},{"text":"Beavers are considered nature's engineers because of the elaborate dams they construct using branches, mud, and stones. By blocking a stream with a dam, a beaver creates a deep, calm pond, which protects the entrance to its lodge from predators and keeps the water from freezing solid in winter. Remarkably, beavers can detect the sound of running water and will instinctively work to patch any gap in a dam, even reacting to a recording of trickling water played near an already solid structure.","questions":[{"question":"Why are beavers called nature's engineers?","choices":["Because they build bridges for people to cross","Because they dig deep tunnels under cities","Because they can swim faster than fish","Because of the elaborate dams they construct"],"answer":"Because of the elaborate dams they construct"},{"question":"What does a beaver's dam create?","choices":["A deep, calm pond","A dry desert","A rocky hill","A sandy beach"],"answer":"A deep, calm pond"},{"question":"What will a beaver instinctively do if it hears running water?","choices":["Work to patch a gap in the dam","Swim away immediately","Stop building entirely","Search for new territory"],"answer":"Work to patch a gap in the dam"}],"photo":"images/flashcards/mud.jpg"},{"text":"Every late winter, when nights are still freezing but days begin to warm, sap starts flowing upward through sugar maple trees. Farmers drill a small hole into the trunk and insert a spout, allowing the clear, watery sap to drip slowly into a collection bucket. Because the sap contains only about two percent sugar, it takes roughly forty gallons of sap, boiled for hours to evaporate the water, to produce just a single gallon of the thick, amber syrup found on breakfast tables.","questions":[{"question":"When does sap start flowing in sugar maple trees?","choices":["Only in autumn, right after the leaves fall off","Only in the heat of the summer","In the middle of a heavy snowstorm","Late winter, when nights freeze and days warm"],"answer":"Late winter, when nights freeze and days warm"},{"question":"How much sap does it take to produce one gallon of syrup?","choices":["Roughly forty gallons","Exactly one gallon","About two gallons","A single cup"],"answer":"Roughly forty gallons"},{"question":"What is this passage mostly about?","choices":["A simple recipe for fluffy pancakes","The history of furniture made from maple wood","How a maple tree grows from a seed","How maple syrup is made from tree sap"],"answer":"How maple syrup is made from tree sap"}]},{"text":"Bats navigate pitch-black caves and hunt fast-moving insects at night using a remarkable technique called echolocation. A bat emits a rapid series of high-pitched clicks, far too high for human ears to detect, and then listens carefully as those sound waves bounce off nearby objects and return as echoes. By analyzing tiny differences in timing and pitch between clicks, a bat's brain constructs a detailed picture of its surroundings, precise enough to detect an insect smaller than a grain of rice while flying at full speed.","questions":[{"question":"What technique do bats use to navigate in the dark?","choices":["Echolocation","Bright eyesight","Following scent trails","Sensing heat"],"answer":"Echolocation"},{"question":"What does a bat listen for after making clicking sounds?","choices":["Other bats calling back to them","Thunder rumbling across the whole valley","Their own heartbeat slowing down","Echoes bouncing off nearby objects"],"answer":"Echoes bouncing off nearby objects"},{"question":"In the passage, \"emits\" most nearly means?","choices":["Soaks up","Hides away","Breaks apart","Sends out"],"answer":"Sends out"}],"photo":"images/flashcards/bat.jpg"}],"2":[{"text":"Inside every green leaf, microscopic structures called chloroplasts perform one of the most essential chemical reactions on Earth: photosynthesis. Using a pigment called chlorophyll, which gives leaves their green color, plants absorb sunlight and convert carbon dioxide and water into glucose, a simple sugar that fuels the plant's growth. Oxygen, released as a byproduct of this reaction, happens to be the very gas that humans and countless other organisms depend on to breathe. Scientists estimate that phytoplankton, microscopic photosynthesizing organisms drifting throughout the ocean, generate more than half of the oxygen in Earth's atmosphere, even though most people associate photosynthesis exclusively with forests and land plants. Without this remarkable chemical process occurring continuously across the planet, most complex life as we currently understand it could not exist.","questions":[{"question":"What pigment allows plants to absorb sunlight?","choices":["Chlorophyll","Oxygen","Glucose","Carbon dioxide"],"answer":"Chlorophyll"},{"question":"According to the passage, where does much of Earth's oxygen actually come from?","choices":["Phytoplankton in the ocean","Only forests","Only deserts","Underground caves"],"answer":"Phytoplankton in the ocean"},{"question":"In the passage, the word \"byproduct\" most likely means?","choices":["The main goal that a process aims to reach","A scientific tool used to measure light","A special type of plant","A side result of a larger process"],"answer":"A side result of a larger process"}],"photo":"images/flashcards/leaf.jpg"},{"text":"The Great Wall of China was not built as a single continuous project but rather assembled, extended, and repaired across more than two thousand years by numerous dynasties, each contributing new sections using whatever materials were locally available. In mountainous regions, builders relied on stone and brick; in desert areas, they compacted layers of earth, reeds, and gravel that have proven surprisingly durable. Despite popular legend, the wall cannot actually be seen with the naked eye from space, since its width, often only a few meters, is far too narrow relative to the vast distances involved. Historians estimate that constructing and maintaining the wall cost the lives of countless laborers, many of whom were soldiers, prisoners, or conscripted peasants working under grueling conditions.","questions":[{"question":"How was the Great Wall of China actually built, according to the passage?","choices":["Built entirely in a single decade","Made only out of solid stone throughout","Designed and built by one powerful emperor alone","Extended over centuries by many dynasties"],"answer":"Extended over centuries by many dynasties"},{"question":"What does the passage say about seeing the wall from space?","choices":["It cannot actually be seen with the naked eye","It is the only human structure visible from space","Astronauts photograph it every year","It glows at night and is easy to spot"],"answer":"It cannot actually be seen with the naked eye"},{"question":"What is the central idea of this passage?","choices":["It was built quickly in one decade using only imported materials","It is only a legend with no real historical basis","It was built mainly as decoration for the emperor's gardens","It was a massive, centuries-long effort with a heavy human cost"],"answer":"It was a massive, centuries-long effort with a heavy human cost"}],"photo":"images/flashcards/not.jpg"},{"text":"Glaciers, often described as slow-moving rivers of ice, form when accumulated snow compresses under its own weight over many years, eventually transforming into dense ice that begins flowing downhill under the pull of gravity. As a glacier advances, embedded rocks and sediment scrape against the underlying bedrock like sandpaper, gradually carving out distinctive U-shaped valleys, unlike the narrower V-shaped valleys typically formed by flowing rivers. When a glacier eventually retreats, often due to a warming climate, it leaves behind telltale features such as ridges of debris called moraines, revealing exactly how far the ice once extended. Studying these ancient formations helps scientists reconstruct Earth's climate history stretching back hundreds of thousands of years.","questions":[{"question":"How does a glacier's movement shape the land beneath it?","choices":["It has almost no effect on the land beneath it at all","It only changes the very top layer of soil","It smooths the ground into perfectly flat plains","It carves U-shaped valleys as it scrapes the rock"],"answer":"It carves U-shaped valleys as it scrapes the rock"},{"question":"What do moraines reveal to scientists?","choices":["How far the ice once extended","The exact temperature of the ice","The age of nearby rivers","The direction the wind blows"],"answer":"How far the ice once extended"},{"question":"In the passage, \"telltale\" most nearly means?","choices":["Revealing or informative","Completely hidden","Loud and noisy","Extremely rare"],"answer":"Revealing or informative"}],"photo":"images/flashcards/ice.jpg"},{"text":"Louis Braille lost his sight in both eyes after a childhood accident in his father's workshop, yet by the age of fifteen he had devised an entirely new system that would eventually transform literacy for blind people worldwide. Rather than relying on the raised, oversized letters used in earlier systems, which were slow to read and difficult to reproduce, Braille adapted a military code originally designed for silent nighttime communication, condensing it into a compact grid of six raised dots that could represent any letter, number, or punctuation mark. Each unique arrangement of dots can be read quickly by sliding a fingertip across the page, and because the system is based on patterns rather than exact letter shapes, it was later adapted for languages and even musical notation far beyond what Braille himself likely imagined.","questions":[{"question":"What inspired Louis Braille's writing system?","choices":["An ancient Egyptian picture alphabet","A system borrowed from sign language","A popular children's board game from France","A military code for silent night messages"],"answer":"A military code for silent night messages"},{"question":"How many raised dots make up the basic Braille grid?","choices":["Six","Two","Twelve","Twenty"],"answer":"Six"},{"question":"What is the central idea of this passage?","choices":["Braille invented a brand new spoken language for the deaf","Braille's system was never adopted by anyone anywhere","Braille's military code was too complicated for blind students to use","Braille's compact dot system transformed reading for blind people"],"answer":"Braille's compact dot system transformed reading for blind people"}],"photo":"images/flashcards/slow.jpg"},{"text":"Venus flytraps, native to a small coastal region of North and South Carolina, capture insects using specialized leaves lined with tiny trigger hairs. When an unsuspecting insect brushes against two of these hairs within about twenty seconds of each other, the trap snaps shut in less than a second, one of the fastest movements found anywhere in the plant kingdom. Digestive enzymes then slowly dissolve the insect over roughly a week, allowing the plant to absorb nutrients, particularly nitrogen, that are scarce in the nutrient-poor, boggy soil where these plants naturally grow. Interestingly, a trap that closes around something inedible, like a falling raindrop, typically reopens within half a day, conserving the considerable amount of energy required to reset the mechanism.","questions":[{"question":"What triggers a Venus flytrap to snap shut?","choices":["Very cold temperatures during the night","Loud noises coming from nearby animals","Bright sunlight shining on its leaves for hours","An insect brushing against trigger hairs"],"answer":"An insect brushing against trigger hairs"},{"question":"Why do Venus flytraps need to capture insects?","choices":["To protect their leaves from heavy rain","To make more seeds for the next season","To attract other plants to grow nearby","To get nutrients scarce in their soil"],"answer":"To get nutrients scarce in their soil"},{"question":"In the passage, \"boggy\" most likely describes soil that is?","choices":["Wet and marshy","Extremely dry","Made of sand","Frozen solid"],"answer":"Wet and marshy"}],"photo":"images/flashcards/insect.jpg"},{"text":"When a honeybee scout discovers a rich source of nectar, it returns to the hive and performs an intricate waggle dance to share the discovery with its fellow workers. By moving in a repeated figure-eight pattern and vigorously shaking its abdomen along the central line of the figure, the bee encodes both the direction and distance of the food source relative to the sun's position in the sky. The duration of each waggle run corresponds directly to distance, while the angle of the dance relative to vertical indicates the precise direction to fly. Remarkably, other bees in the pitch-dark hive interpret this dance purely through touch and vibration, then depart and navigate directly to a flower patch they have never personally seen.","questions":[{"question":"What does a honeybee do after discovering nectar?","choices":["Eats all of the nectar alone","Flies back and forth at random","Stays outside the hive until the next morning","Performs a dance to share the location"],"answer":"Performs a dance to share the location"},{"question":"What does the duration of the waggle run indicate?","choices":["The age of the dancing bee","The total size of the hive","The color of the nearest flowering plants","The distance to the food source"],"answer":"The distance to the food source"},{"question":"In the passage, \"encodes\" most nearly means?","choices":["Destroys everything it touches","Hides something from other bees","Grows larger over a long time","Sends information in a pattern"],"answer":"Sends information in a pattern"}],"photo":"images/flashcards/bee.jpg"}]}},{"id":"2026-10-04-weekly-reading","released":"2026-10-04","kind":"weekly","reading_passages":{"0":[{"text":"The garbage truck comes on Monday. It is big and green. It picks up the trash cans on our street.","questions":[{"question":"What color is the garbage truck?","choices":["blue","yellow","green","red"],"answer":"green"},{"question":"What day does the garbage truck come?","choices":["Sunday","Monday","Friday","Tuesday"],"answer":"Monday"}]},{"text":"Ana washes her hands with soap and water. She sings a song while she scrubs. Now her hands are clean.","questions":[{"question":"What does Ana use to wash her hands?","choices":["sand","soap and water","paint","milk"],"answer":"soap and water"},{"question":"What does Ana do while she scrubs?","choices":["She sings a song.","She reads a book.","She eats lunch.","She takes a nap."],"answer":"She sings a song."}],"photo":"images/flashcards/soap.jpg"},{"text":"Ben brushes his teeth after breakfast. His toothbrush is blue. He brushes for two minutes.","questions":[{"question":"When does Ben brush his teeth?","choices":["at school","in the bath","after breakfast","at the park"],"answer":"after breakfast"},{"question":"What color is Ben's toothbrush?","choices":["green","orange","blue","pink"],"answer":"blue"}]},{"text":"The train goes down the long track. It has five cars. The train horn goes toot, toot!","questions":[{"question":"How many cars does the train have?","choices":["ten","five","two","one"],"answer":"five"},{"question":"What sound does the train horn make?","choices":["buzz, buzz","toot, toot","quack, quack","moo, moo"],"answer":"toot, toot"}],"photo":"images/flashcards/train.jpg"},{"text":"Dad makes a sandwich for lunch. It has cheese and turkey. He cuts it into two triangles.","questions":[{"question":"What is in the sandwich?","choices":["eggs and ham","jam and peanut butter","fish and rice","cheese and turkey"],"answer":"cheese and turkey"},{"question":"What shape does Dad cut the sandwich into?","choices":["stars","circles","triangles","hearts"],"answer":"triangles"}]},{"text":"Kim plays the drum at music time. Boom, boom, boom! Her friends clap along to the beat.","questions":[{"question":"What does Kim play?","choices":["the flute","the piano","the guitar","the drum"],"answer":"the drum"},{"question":"What do Kim's friends do?","choices":["clap along","go to sleep","eat snacks","run outside"],"answer":"clap along"}],"photo":"images/flashcards/drum.jpg"},{"text":"The clock on the kitchen wall is round. It goes tick, tock all day. Its hands point to the numbers.","questions":[{"question":"What shape is the clock?","choices":["round","triangle","square","star"],"answer":"round"},{"question":"Where is the clock?","choices":["in the car","under the bed","on the porch","on the kitchen wall"],"answer":"on the kitchen wall"}],"photo":"images/flashcards/clock.jpg"},{"text":"Sam helps set the table for dinner. He puts a fork and a spoon next to each plate. Mom says thank you.","questions":[{"question":"What does Sam put next to each plate?","choices":["a hat and a sock","a cup of juice","a fork and a spoon","a toy and a ball"],"answer":"a fork and a spoon"},{"question":"What does Mom say?","choices":["good night","hurry up","thank you","hello"],"answer":"thank you"}],"photo":"images/flashcards/fork.jpg"},{"text":"The fire truck is shiny and red. It has a long ladder on top. Its siren is very loud.","questions":[{"question":"What is on top of the fire truck?","choices":["a long ladder","a slide","a tree","a big boat"],"answer":"a long ladder"},{"question":"How does the siren sound?","choices":["very loud","very quiet","like a bird","like a cat"],"answer":"very loud"}]},{"text":"At the library, Ms. Bell reads a book at story time. The book is about a silly goat. All the kids laugh.","questions":[{"question":"What is the book about?","choices":["a big ship","a red car","a sleepy bear","a silly goat"],"answer":"a silly goat"},{"question":"What do the kids do?","choices":["laugh","cry","leave","sleep"],"answer":"laugh"}],"photo":"images/flashcards/book.jpg"},{"text":"Tess has a box of blocks. A new girl named June sits next to her. Tess shares her blocks, and they build a castle together.","questions":[{"question":"What does Tess share?","choices":["her blocks","her lunch","her book","her hat"],"answer":"her blocks"},{"question":"What do Tess and June build?","choices":["a castle","a car","a boat","a bridge"],"answer":"a castle"}]},{"text":"Grandpa takes Lin to the farm stand. They buy six ears of corn and a big bag of peaches.","questions":[{"question":"How many ears of corn do they buy?","choices":["two","six","nine","one"],"answer":"six"},{"question":"Who takes Lin to the farm stand?","choices":["the bus driver","Grandpa","the teacher","her sister"],"answer":"Grandpa"}],"photo":"images/flashcards/farm.jpg"},{"text":"The leaves turn orange and red in the fall. Max rakes them into a big pile. Then he jumps right in!","questions":[{"question":"What colors are the leaves?","choices":["black and gray","blue and purple","pink and white","orange and red"],"answer":"orange and red"},{"question":"What does Max do after he rakes?","choices":["He jumps in the pile.","He rides a bike.","He goes to bed.","He eats a snack."],"answer":"He jumps in the pile."}]},{"text":"Ruby sorts her crayons by color. She puts all the red ones in one cup. She puts all the blue ones in another cup.","questions":[{"question":"How does Ruby sort her crayons?","choices":["by size","by smell","by name","by color"],"answer":"by color"},{"question":"Where does Ruby put the red crayons?","choices":["in one cup","in her shoe","on the floor","in a bag"],"answer":"in one cup"}]},{"text":"The tugboat is small, but it is very strong. It pushes a giant ship slowly into the harbor.","questions":[{"question":"What size is the tugboat?","choices":["small","wide","giant","long"],"answer":"small"},{"question":"What does the tugboat push?","choices":["a red car","a giant ship","a tall tree","a big truck"],"answer":"a giant ship"}],"photo":"images/flashcards/ship.jpg"},{"text":"Eva goes to the doctor for a checkup. The doctor listens to her heart. Eva gets a sticker for being brave.","questions":[{"question":"What does the doctor listen to?","choices":["her heart","a bird","the radio","a song"],"answer":"her heart"},{"question":"What does Eva get?","choices":["a sticker","a cake","a puppy","a bike"],"answer":"a sticker"}]},{"text":"Nico packs a picnic for the park. He packs grapes, crackers, and apple juice. He brings a blanket to sit on.","questions":[{"question":"What drink does Nico pack?","choices":["lemonade","hot cocoa","apple juice","milk"],"answer":"apple juice"},{"question":"What will Nico sit on?","choices":["a chair","a rock","a blanket","a bench"],"answer":"a blanket"}],"photo":"images/flashcards/grapes.jpg"},{"text":"Snow falls all night. In the morning, a snowplow clears the road. It pushes the snow to the side.","questions":[{"question":"When does the snow fall?","choices":["after school","all night","in the summer","at lunch"],"answer":"all night"},{"question":"What clears the road?","choices":["a snowplow","a bus","a horse","a bike"],"answer":"a snowplow"}]},{"text":"The mail carrier brings a letter to Owen's house. The letter is from Aunt Rose. She drew a happy sun on it.","questions":[{"question":"Who is the letter from?","choices":["Grandpa","the teacher","a friend at school","Aunt Rose"],"answer":"Aunt Rose"},{"question":"What did Aunt Rose draw?","choices":["a big fish","a red car","a happy sun","a green tree"],"answer":"a happy sun"}]},{"text":"The wind blows hard today. It pushes the clouds across the sky. Jay holds on to his hat.","questions":[{"question":"What does the wind push?","choices":["the clouds","the house","the school","the car"],"answer":"the clouds"},{"question":"What does Jay hold on to?","choices":["his dog","his cup","his hat","his book"],"answer":"his hat"}]}],"1":[{"text":"Lena wanted to send a birthday card to her grandma, who lives far away. She wrote a short note inside and drew a picture of their two cats. Her dad helped her write the address on the envelope and stick a stamp in the corner. Then Lena dropped the card into the blue mailbox. Four days later, Grandma called to say thank you.","questions":[{"question":"What did Lena draw on the card?","choices":["a flower garden","their two cats","a birthday cake","a big house"],"answer":"their two cats"},{"question":"Where did Lena put the stamp?","choices":["on her hand","on the back","in the corner","inside the card"],"answer":"in the corner"},{"question":"How did Lena know Grandma got the card?","choices":["Grandma came to visit.","The card came back.","A teacher told her.","Grandma called to say thank you."],"answer":"Grandma called to say thank you."}]},{"text":"Teeth help us bite and chew our food. To keep them healthy, dentists say we should brush two times a day for two minutes. Brushing removes a sticky layer of germs called plaque. We should also floss to clean between our teeth. Eating fewer sugary snacks and drinking water help, too. Visiting the dentist twice a year keeps a smile bright.","questions":[{"question":"How many times a day should we brush?","choices":["one","ten","two","five"],"answer":"two"},{"question":"What is plaque?","choices":["a sugary snack","a kind of toothbrush","a dentist's tool","a sticky layer of germs"],"answer":"a sticky layer of germs"},{"question":"What is this passage mostly about?","choices":["keeping teeth healthy","how to bake cookies","how dentists drive","why we sleep"],"answer":"keeping teeth healthy"}]},{"text":"Clouds come in many shapes. Puffy white clouds that look like cotton are called cumulus clouds. They often appear on sunny days. Thin, wispy clouds high in the sky are called cirrus clouds. Dark gray clouds that cover the whole sky can bring rain. Watching clouds can help you guess what the weather will do.","questions":[{"question":"What are puffy white clouds called?","choices":["cumulus clouds","cirrus clouds","fog","rain clouds"],"answer":"cumulus clouds"},{"question":"What kind of clouds can bring rain?","choices":["dark gray clouds","puffy white clouds","tiny pink clouds","thin wispy clouds"],"answer":"dark gray clouds"}],"photo":"images/flashcards/rain.jpg"},{"text":"On her first day at a new school, Hana felt nervous. She did not know anyone, and her stomach felt full of butterflies. At lunch, she sat alone at the end of a table. Then a boy named Theo asked if he could sit with her. He liked drawing dragons, and so did Hana. By the end of lunch, they had planned a comic book together.","questions":[{"question":"How did Hana feel on her first day?","choices":["nervous","angry","sleepy","bored"],"answer":"nervous"},{"question":"What did Hana and Theo both like?","choices":["drawing dragons","reading maps","playing soccer","singing songs"],"answer":"drawing dragons"},{"question":"What did they plan by the end of lunch?","choices":["a birthday party","a field trip","a soccer game","a comic book"],"answer":"a comic book"}]},{"text":"Every Saturday, Mateo and his grandpa walk to the farmers market. Farmers bring fruits and vegetables they grew themselves. Mateo's favorite stand sells honey in glass jars. Grandpa likes to buy fresh tomatoes and green beans. Buying food from local farmers helps them earn money, and the food does not have to travel very far.","questions":[{"question":"What does Mateo's favorite stand sell?","choices":["bread","flowers","honey","cheese"],"answer":"honey"},{"question":"When do Mateo and Grandpa go to the market?","choices":["every Monday","every night","every Saturday","once a year"],"answer":"every Saturday"},{"question":"Why is buying from local farmers helpful?","choices":["It makes food travel farther.","It helps them earn money.","It closes the market.","It makes food cost more."],"answer":"It helps them earn money."}],"photo":"images/flashcards/honey.jpg"},{"text":"Mr. Lopez is the crossing guard at Pine Street School. Every morning, he wears a bright orange vest so drivers can see him. When children reach the corner, he holds up a red stop sign. The cars stop, and the kids cross safely. Mr. Lopez knows every student's name and always tells them to have a great day.","questions":[{"question":"Why does Mr. Lopez wear a bright orange vest?","choices":["so drivers can see him","to stay warm in the rain","to look like a pumpkin","to match the school bus"],"answer":"so drivers can see him"},{"question":"What does Mr. Lopez hold up?","choices":["a big umbrella","a lunch box","a green flag","a red stop sign"],"answer":"a red stop sign"},{"question":"What does Mr. Lopez always tell the students?","choices":["to have a great day","to go home","to hurry up","to be quiet"],"answer":"to have a great day"}],"photo":"images/flashcards/vest.jpg"},{"text":"Sea turtles lay their eggs on sandy beaches. A mother turtle digs a hole with her flippers and lays about one hundred eggs. Then she covers them with sand and returns to the ocean. About two months later, the baby turtles hatch. They dig out of the sand and crawl toward the bright water. Many beaches turn off lights at night so the babies do not get confused.","questions":[{"question":"What does the mother turtle use to dig a hole?","choices":["her shell","a stick","her flippers","her mouth"],"answer":"her flippers"},{"question":"About how long until the baby turtles hatch?","choices":["one hour","one year","two months","two days"],"answer":"two months"},{"question":"Why do some beaches turn off lights at night?","choices":["so the turtles grow faster","so the babies do not get confused","so people can sleep","to keep the sand cool"],"answer":"so the babies do not get confused"}]},{"text":"Zoe joined a basketball team this fall. At first, she could not dribble without the ball bouncing away. Her coach told her to use her fingertips, not her palm, and to keep her eyes up. Zoe practiced in her driveway every evening. By the last game, she dribbled down the court and passed to a teammate, who scored!","questions":[{"question":"What did the coach tell Zoe to use?","choices":["her fingertips","her palm","her elbow","both feet"],"answer":"her fingertips"},{"question":"Where did Zoe practice?","choices":["in the library","at the beach","in her driveway","at the zoo"],"answer":"in her driveway"},{"question":"What lesson does this story teach?","choices":["Practice helps you improve.","Coaches are always right.","Basketball is too hard.","Never play sports."],"answer":"Practice helps you improve."}]},{"text":"Wind turbines are tall towers with giant blades that spin when the wind blows. As the blades turn, they spin a machine inside called a generator. The generator makes electricity that travels through wires to homes and schools. Wind is a clean source of energy because it does not make smoke, and the wind will never run out.","questions":[{"question":"What makes the blades spin?","choices":["the rain","the wind","the sun","a gas motor"],"answer":"the wind"},{"question":"What is the machine inside called?","choices":["a pump","an engine","a generator","a battery"],"answer":"a generator"},{"question":"Why is wind called a clean source of energy?","choices":["It only works at night.","It does not make smoke.","It is very loud.","It uses lots of water."],"answer":"It does not make smoke."}]},{"text":"Max and Leo both wanted the swing at recess. Max grabbed the chains, and Leo pulled them back. Soon both boys were upset. Their teacher suggested they take turns, with each boy counting to fifty. Leo went first while Max counted out loud, and then they switched. When the bell rang, both boys agreed it had been a fun recess.","questions":[{"question":"What did both boys want to use?","choices":["the sandbox","the swing","the slide","the ball"],"answer":"the swing"},{"question":"What did the teacher suggest?","choices":["play tag","go inside","share a snack","take turns"],"answer":"take turns"},{"question":"How did the boys feel when the bell rang?","choices":["They were still angry.","They agreed it was fun.","They were lost.","They were sleepy."],"answer":"They agreed it was fun."}]},{"text":"During a thunderstorm, you see lightning before you hear thunder. That is because light travels much faster than sound. You can count the seconds between the flash and the boom. If you count to five, the storm is about one mile away. When you hear thunder, it is a good idea to go indoors and stay safe.","questions":[{"question":"Which do you notice first in a storm?","choices":["lightning","thunder","wind","hail"],"answer":"lightning"},{"question":"If you count to five, how far away is the storm?","choices":["about one foot","about ten miles","about one mile","right overhead"],"answer":"about one mile"},{"question":"What should you do when you hear thunder?","choices":["climb a tree","fly a kite","go indoors","go swimming"],"answer":"go indoors"}]},{"text":"On Sunday, Abuela taught Sofia how to make tortillas. They mixed flour, water, salt, and a little oil into a soft dough. Sofia rolled the dough into small balls and flattened each one with a rolling pin. Abuela cooked them on a hot pan until they puffed up with brown spots. They ate the warm tortillas with beans and rice.","questions":[{"question":"Who taught Sofia to make tortillas?","choices":["her teacher","her brother","Abuela","a chef on TV"],"answer":"Abuela"},{"question":"What did Sofia use to flatten the dough?","choices":["her shoe","a spoon","a book","a rolling pin"],"answer":"a rolling pin"},{"question":"What did they eat with the tortillas?","choices":["cereal and milk","apples and cheese","soup and salad","beans and rice"],"answer":"beans and rice"}]},{"text":"Before the class hike, Ranger Diaz shared some trail rules. Stay on the path so plants do not get trampled. Do not pick flowers, so others can enjoy them too. Carry out all of your trash, even tiny wrappers. 'Take only pictures and leave only footprints,' she said. The students followed the rules and saw a deer drinking from a stream.","questions":[{"question":"Why should hikers stay on the path?","choices":["so plants do not get trampled","to scare the deer","to find hidden treasure","so they can walk faster"],"answer":"so plants do not get trampled"},{"question":"What should hikers do with their trash?","choices":["carry it out","hide it under a rock","bury it","leave it on the path"],"answer":"carry it out"},{"question":"What did the students see?","choices":["a fox on a log","an owl in a tree","a deer drinking from a stream","a bear in a cave"],"answer":"a deer drinking from a stream"}]},{"text":"Ms. Patel's class got a small robot shaped like a ladybug. Students could make it move by pressing arrow buttons on its back. Each button press was one step in a program. To guide the robot through a paper maze, Jamal planned his steps carefully: forward, forward, turn right, forward. When he pressed go, the robot reached the finish line on the first try.","questions":[{"question":"What was the robot shaped like?","choices":["a dog","a rocket","a ladybug","a car"],"answer":"a ladybug"},{"question":"What did each button press stand for?","choices":["one step in a program","one point in a game","one new color","one minute of time"],"answer":"one step in a program"},{"question":"How did Jamal get the robot through the maze?","choices":["He planned his steps carefully.","He carried it.","He shook the robot.","He guessed each step."],"answer":"He planned his steps carefully."}],"photo":"images/flashcards/robot.jpg"},{"text":"The moon seems to change shape during the month, but it is always round. The moon does not make its own light. We see the part that is lit by the sun. When the lit side faces us, we see a full moon. When it faces away, we see a new moon, and the moon looks dark. The whole pattern takes about one month to repeat.","questions":[{"question":"What lights up the moon?","choices":["city lights","the sun","the moon itself","the stars"],"answer":"the sun"},{"question":"What do we see when the lit side faces us?","choices":["a full moon","a new moon","a red moon","no moon"],"answer":"a full moon"},{"question":"How long does the pattern take to repeat?","choices":["about one day","about one hour","about one year","about one month"],"answer":"about one month"}],"photo":"images/flashcards/moon.jpg"},{"text":"Hummingbirds are the smallest birds in the world. Some weigh less than a penny. Their wings beat so fast that they make a humming sound, which is how they got their name. Hummingbirds can fly forward, backward, and even hover in one spot. They use their long, thin beaks to drink sweet nectar from flowers.","questions":[{"question":"How did hummingbirds get their name?","choices":["They live in hives.","They sing loud songs.","Their wings make a humming sound.","They hum at night."],"answer":"Their wings make a humming sound."},{"question":"What do hummingbirds drink from flowers?","choices":["milk","salty water","sweet nectar","juice"],"answer":"sweet nectar"},{"question":"What can hummingbirds do?","choices":["hover in one spot","dig tunnels","swim underwater","run very far"],"answer":"hover in one spot"}]},{"text":"On the way home from school, Ivy found a purple mitten on the sidewalk. It was small, with a white star sewn on it. Ivy thought someone must be missing it. She made a poster that said 'Found: Purple Mitten' and taped it to the playground fence. The next day, a kindergartner named Rosie ran up to thank her.","questions":[{"question":"What did Ivy find?","choices":["a toy car","a blue scarf","a red hat","a purple mitten"],"answer":"a purple mitten"},{"question":"What was sewn on the mitten?","choices":["a green leaf","a yellow moon","a white star","a pink heart"],"answer":"a white star"},{"question":"What does Ivy's poster show about her?","choices":["She is scared.","She is lazy.","She is thoughtful.","She is selfish."],"answer":"She is thoughtful."}]},{"text":"When the fire alarm rang, Mr. Green's class knew just what to do. They stopped their work, left their backpacks, and lined up at the door. They walked quietly down the hall and out to the field. Mr. Green counted everyone to make sure no one was missing. It was only a drill, but practicing helps everyone stay safe in a real emergency.","questions":[{"question":"What did the class leave behind?","choices":["their shoes","their friends","their backpacks","their teacher"],"answer":"their backpacks"},{"question":"Why did Mr. Green count everyone?","choices":["to make sure no one was missing","to start a game","to pick teams","to check homework"],"answer":"to make sure no one was missing"},{"question":"Why do schools practice fire drills?","choices":["to help everyone stay safe","to test the bell","to play outside","to skip math class"],"answer":"to help everyone stay safe"}]},{"text":"Kai wondered why trucks spread salt on icy roads, so he tried an experiment. He put two ice cubes on separate plates and sprinkled salt on only one. After ten minutes, the salty ice cube had melted much more. Kai learned that salt lowers the temperature at which water freezes, so ice melts even when it is cold outside.","questions":[{"question":"What did Kai sprinkle on one ice cube?","choices":["sugar","pepper","sand","salt"],"answer":"salt"},{"question":"What happened to the salty ice cube?","choices":["It stayed the same.","It turned blue.","It melted much more.","It grew bigger."],"answer":"It melted much more."},{"question":"Why do trucks spread salt on icy roads?","choices":["to make cars faster","to help the ice melt","to feed the birds","to paint new lines"],"answer":"to help the ice melt"}],"photo":"images/flashcards/ice.jpg"},{"text":"Nadia was afraid to put her face in the water at swim lessons. Her instructor showed her how to blow bubbles through her nose and mouth. Nadia practiced blowing bubbles in the shallow end every week. Little by little, she felt braver. By the end of summer, she could float on her back and swim across the pool.","questions":[{"question":"What was Nadia afraid to do?","choices":["ride the bus","wear goggles","jump off the diving board","put her face in the water"],"answer":"put her face in the water"},{"question":"What did her instructor show her?","choices":["how to run","how to blow bubbles","how to dive","how to splash"],"answer":"how to blow bubbles"},{"question":"What could Nadia do by the end of summer?","choices":["win a race","swim across the pool","ride a horse","fly a kite"],"answer":"swim across the pool"}]},{"text":"For a class project, Emeka made a family tree. He wrote his name at the bottom, then added his parents above him. Above them, he added his four grandparents. Emeka interviewed his grandpa, who grew up on a farm and walked two miles to school. Emeka learned that every family has stories worth sharing.","questions":[{"question":"Where did Emeka write his name?","choices":["on the back","at the top","at the bottom","on the cover"],"answer":"at the bottom"},{"question":"How many grandparents did Emeka add?","choices":["one","two","four","six"],"answer":"four"},{"question":"What did Emeka learn about his grandpa?","choices":["He had ten dogs.","He grew up on a farm.","He lived in a big city.","He was a pilot."],"answer":"He grew up on a farm."}]},{"text":"Did you know an old milk jug can become a park bench? When plastic is recycled, it is cleaned, chopped into tiny flakes, and melted. The melted plastic is pressed into long boards. Those boards can be used to build benches, picnic tables, and playground parts. Recycled plastic boards do not rot or need paint, so they last a long time.","questions":[{"question":"What happens first to recycled plastic?","choices":["It is cleaned.","It is buried.","It is frozen.","It is painted."],"answer":"It is cleaned."},{"question":"What can recycled plastic boards be used to build?","choices":["metal cans","paper bags","park benches","glass windows"],"answer":"park benches"},{"question":"Why do recycled plastic boards last a long time?","choices":["They are made of wood.","They melt in the sun.","They do not rot or need paint.","They are very soft."],"answer":"They do not rot or need paint."}],"photo":"images/flashcards/milk.jpg"},{"text":"In the fall, Ethan's family visited an apple orchard. Rows of trees were heavy with red and yellow apples. A farmer showed them how to twist an apple gently upward so it would come off without hurting the branch. Ethan filled a whole bag. That night, the family used some of the apples to bake a warm apple crisp.","questions":[{"question":"How should you pick an apple?","choices":["shake the tree","pull it down hard","hit it with a stick","twist it gently upward"],"answer":"twist it gently upward"},{"question":"What colors were the apples?","choices":["red and yellow","orange and pink","blue and purple","white and black"],"answer":"red and yellow"},{"question":"What did the family bake?","choices":["a pumpkin pie","an apple crisp","oatmeal cookies","banana bread"],"answer":"an apple crisp"}],"photo":"images/flashcards/apple.jpg"}],"2":[{"text":"Tardigrades, sometimes called water bears, are tiny animals usually less than half a millimeter long. Under a microscope, they look like chubby bears with eight stubby legs. Tardigrades live in moss, soil, and water all over the world. What makes them remarkable is their toughness. When their surroundings dry out, they curl into a shriveled state called a tun and slow their bodies almost to a stop. In this state, some can survive for years without water, endure freezing cold, and even survive a trip to outer space. When water returns, they unfold and go about their business. Scientists study tardigrades hoping to learn how to protect living cells from extreme conditions.","questions":[{"question":"In the passage, the word remarkable means?","choices":["hard to see","easy to find","very small","worthy of notice"],"answer":"worthy of notice"},{"question":"What is a tun?","choices":["a tiny microscope","a group of tardigrades","a kind of moss tardigrades eat","a dried, curled state that helps tardigrades survive"],"answer":"a dried, curled state that helps tardigrades survive"},{"question":"What is the main idea of the passage?","choices":["Scientists use tardigrades to fly rockets.","Moss is the best place to live.","Tardigrades are a kind of bear.","Tardigrades are tiny animals that survive extreme conditions."],"answer":"Tardigrades are tiny animals that survive extreme conditions."}]},{"text":"In 1783, two French brothers, Joseph and Etienne Montgolfier, noticed that smoke and hot air rose upward. They built a large balloon of fabric and paper and filled it with hot air from a fire. That September, their balloon carried the first living passengers into the sky: a sheep, a duck, and a rooster. All three landed safely. Two months later, two men rode a Montgolfier balloon over Paris, becoming the first people to fly in a balloon that was not tied down. Hot air balloons work because heated air is less dense, or lighter for its size, than the cooler air around it. That difference lets the balloon float upward, much like a cork rising in water.","questions":[{"question":"In the passage, the word dense means?","choices":["heavy for its size","very hot","brightly colored","easy to fold"],"answer":"heavy for its size"},{"question":"Who were the balloon's first passengers?","choices":["a sheep, a duck, and a rooster","a dog and a cat","the two Montgolfier brothers","the king and queen"],"answer":"a sheep, a duck, and a rooster"},{"question":"Why does a hot air balloon rise?","choices":["The paper is lighter than fabric.","Smoke pushes the balloon up.","Hot air is lighter than the cooler air around it.","Wind blows it from below."],"answer":"Hot air is lighter than the cooler air around it."}]},{"text":"For centuries, lighthouses have guided sailors along dangerous coasts. A lighthouse is a tall tower with a powerful light at the top that warns ships of rocks, reefs, and shallow water. Before electricity, keepers lived at the lighthouse and climbed its stairs each evening to light oil lamps and keep them burning all night. In 1822, a French scientist named Augustin Fresnel invented a special lens made of rings of glass that bent light into a strong beam visible for many miles. Each lighthouse also has its own pattern of flashes, so sailors can tell which one they are seeing. Today, most lighthouses are automated, and satellite navigation helps ships find their way, but many lighthouses still shine every night.","questions":[{"question":"In the passage, the word automated means?","choices":["painted in bright colors","built very tall","closed forever","run by machines without a person"],"answer":"run by machines without a person"},{"question":"Why does each lighthouse have its own pattern of flashes?","choices":["so the tower looks pretty","to save lamp oil","to warn of storms only","so sailors can tell which lighthouse they see"],"answer":"so sailors can tell which lighthouse they see"},{"question":"What is the passage mostly about?","choices":["how lighthouses have helped keep sailors safe","why satellites are better than ships","how glass is made","how to become a sailor"],"answer":"how lighthouses have helped keep sailors safe"}]},{"text":"In 1860, sending a letter from Missouri to California could take weeks. The Pony Express promised to do it in about ten days. Young riders each galloped about 75 to 100 miles, switching to fresh horses at relay stations spaced roughly 10 to 15 miles apart. Then they handed the mail pouch to the next rider. Riders carried little besides the mail so their horses could run as fast as possible. Although the Pony Express became legendary, it lasted only about eighteen months. In October 1861, the transcontinental telegraph was completed, and messages could cross the country in minutes. The Pony Express had suddenly become obsolete.","questions":[{"question":"In the passage, the word obsolete means?","choices":["newly built","no longer needed","extremely fast","very famous"],"answer":"no longer needed"},{"question":"Why did riders carry so little besides the mail?","choices":["so they could sleep on the way","so their horses could run as fast as possible","because the mail was very heavy","because they were young"],"answer":"so their horses could run as fast as possible"},{"question":"What caused the Pony Express to end?","choices":["The telegraph could send messages in minutes.","The horses ran away.","Riders refused to work.","California stopped getting mail."],"answer":"The telegraph could send messages in minutes."}]},{"text":"Before 1869, crossing the United States meant months by wagon or a long voyage by ship. Then two companies set out to build a railroad linking the East to the West. The Central Pacific built eastward from Sacramento, California, cutting tunnels through the steep Sierra Nevada mountains. Many of its workers were immigrants from China. The Union Pacific built westward from Omaha, Nebraska, across the plains. On May 10, 1869, the two lines met at Promontory Summit in Utah, where a golden spike was tapped into place. A trip that once took months now took about a week. The railroad transformed how people and goods moved across the country.","questions":[{"question":"In the passage, the word transformed means?","choices":["quickly forgot","slowly ended","greatly changed","carefully measured"],"answer":"greatly changed"},{"question":"Where did the two railroad lines meet?","choices":["Omaha in Nebraska","Sacramento in California","Promontory Summit in Utah","the top of the Sierra Nevada"],"answer":"Promontory Summit in Utah"},{"question":"What is the main idea of the passage?","choices":["Most railroad workers lived in Utah.","The transcontinental railroad made cross-country travel much faster.","Wagons were faster than trains.","Golden spikes were used on every railroad."],"answer":"The transcontinental railroad made cross-country travel much faster."}]},{"text":"In the early 1800s, a girl named Mary Anning searched the crumbling cliffs near Lyme Regis, England, for fossils. Her family was poor, and they sold the fossils to visitors. When Mary was about twelve, she and her brother uncovered the skeleton of an ichthyosaur, a sea reptile that looked a bit like a dolphin. Later she found the first complete plesiosaur, a long-necked sea reptile. Because Mary was a woman with little schooling, scientists of her time rarely gave her credit. Yet experts traveled to learn from her, and her discoveries helped show that many kinds of animals had gone extinct long ago. Today she is celebrated as a pioneer of paleontology, the study of fossils.","questions":[{"question":"In the passage, the word pioneer means?","choices":["a person who sells fossils","a student who skips school","a type of sea reptile","one of the first to do something"],"answer":"one of the first to do something"},{"question":"What did Mary's discoveries help show?","choices":["that many kinds of animals had gone extinct","that cliffs never change","that dolphins are reptiles","that fossils are worthless"],"answer":"that many kinds of animals had gone extinct"},{"question":"What can you infer about Mary Anning?","choices":["She studied at a large university.","She was skilled and determined despite unfair treatment.","She was given many awards in her lifetime.","She did not enjoy being outdoors."],"answer":"She was skilled and determined despite unfair treatment."}]},{"text":"On some winter nights in the far north, the sky glows with ribbons of green, pink, and violet light. This display is called the aurora borealis, or northern lights. It begins with the sun, which constantly releases a stream of charged particles called the solar wind. When these particles reach Earth, our planet's magnetic field guides many of them toward the poles. There, they collide with gases high in the atmosphere, making the gases glow. Oxygen usually gives off green light, while nitrogen can produce blue or purple. A similar display near the South Pole is called the aurora australis, or southern lights.","questions":[{"question":"In the passage, the word collide means?","choices":["crash into each other","change color","float away","slowly cool down"],"answer":"crash into each other"},{"question":"Why do auroras appear mostly near the poles?","choices":["Snow at the poles reflects light.","The sun is closer to the poles.","The poles are the coldest places.","Earth's magnetic field guides particles toward the poles."],"answer":"Earth's magnetic field guides particles toward the poles."},{"question":"Which gas usually gives off green light?","choices":["smoke","nitrogen","oxygen","water vapor"],"answer":"oxygen"}]},{"text":"If you spend a day at the ocean, you may notice the water creeping up the beach and then pulling back. These daily changes in sea level are called tides. They are caused mainly by the moon's gravity. As Earth spins, the moon pulls on the oceans, creating a bulge of water on the side facing the moon and another bulge on the opposite side. Most beaches pass through both bulges each day, so they usually have two high tides and two low tides in a little more than 24 hours. The sun's gravity affects tides too. When the sun and moon line up, high tides are extra high and low tides are extra low.","questions":[{"question":"In the passage, the word bulge means?","choices":["a deep hole","a strong wind","a sandy beach","a swelling or rounded lump"],"answer":"a swelling or rounded lump"},{"question":"What mainly causes tides?","choices":["strong winds","whales swimming","rainstorms","the moon's gravity"],"answer":"the moon's gravity"},{"question":"How many high tides do most beaches have each day?","choices":["none","two","four","one"],"answer":"two"}],"photo":"images/flashcards/moon.jpg"},{"text":"Mushrooms might look like plants, but they belong to a separate group of living things called fungi. Unlike plants, fungi cannot make their own food from sunlight. Instead, many fungi are decomposers. They break down dead leaves, fallen logs, and other once-living material, returning nutrients to the soil so new plants can grow. The mushroom we see is only a small part of the fungus. Beneath the ground spreads a huge web of thin threads called mycelium. Without decomposers, forests would be buried under piles of dead plants.","questions":[{"question":"In the passage, the word decomposers means?","choices":["plants that need lots of sunlight","living things that break down dead material","animals that eat mushrooms","tools used to cut logs"],"answer":"living things that break down dead material"},{"question":"What is mycelium?","choices":["a web of thin threads underground","the cap of a mushroom","a type of leaf","a kind of rock"],"answer":"a web of thin threads underground"},{"question":"Why are fungi important to forests?","choices":["They return nutrients to the soil.","They make trees grow overnight.","They make food from sunlight.","They scare away animals."],"answer":"They return nutrients to the soil."}]},{"text":"Earthworms may seem like simple creatures, but gardeners consider them valuable helpers. As earthworms tunnel through the ground, they loosen the soil, making spaces for air and water to reach plant roots. They also eat dead leaves and bits of soil. What passes through their bodies comes out as castings, a material rich in the nutrients plants need. Earthworms have no eyes, but special cells in their skin sense light. They also breathe through their skin, which must stay moist. That is why worms stay in damp places, since dry skin would make breathing impossible.","questions":[{"question":"In the passage, the word castings means?","choices":["piles of dead leaves","nutrient-rich waste left by worms","holes that worms dig","hooks used for fishing"],"answer":"nutrient-rich waste left by worms"},{"question":"How do earthworms breathe?","choices":["through their moist skin","through a nose","through their eyes","through gills"],"answer":"through their moist skin"},{"question":"What is the main idea of the passage?","choices":["Worms can see very well.","Gardeners should remove all worms.","Earthworms harm gardens.","Earthworms help keep soil healthy for plants."],"answer":"Earthworms help keep soil healthy for plants."}]},{"text":"When winter arrives and food grows scarce, some animals survive by hibernating. Hibernation is much deeper than ordinary sleep. A woodchuck, also called a groundhog, curls up in its burrow, and its heartbeat drops from about eighty beats a minute to just a few. Its body temperature falls close to that of the burrow. By slowing down, the woodchuck uses so little energy that the fat it stored during summer and fall can last until spring. Bears are often called hibernators, but their body temperature drops only a little, and they can wake more easily. Either way, these strategies help animals survive the hungry months.","questions":[{"question":"In the passage, the word scarce means?","choices":["easy to reach","very tasty","hard to find","frozen solid"],"answer":"hard to find"},{"question":"How does hibernating help a woodchuck survive?","choices":["It grows thicker fur.","It eats more food in winter.","It uses very little energy, so its fat lasts.","It flies south for the winter."],"answer":"It uses very little energy, so its fat lasts."},{"question":"How is a bear's winter rest different from a woodchuck's?","choices":["A bear rests only in summer.","A bear's body temperature drops only a little.","A bear does not rest at all.","A bear's heart stops completely."],"answer":"A bear's body temperature drops only a little."}]},{"text":"A tornado is a violently spinning column of air that stretches from a thunderstorm down to the ground. Many tornadoes form in powerful storms called supercells, where warm, moist air near the ground meets cooler, drier air above. The United States has more tornadoes than any other country, especially in an area of the central plains nicknamed Tornado Alley. Meteorologists use radar to spot rotation inside storms and issue warnings. When a tornado warning is announced, people should move to a basement or to an interior room without windows on the lowest floor. Planning ahead and practicing drills can save lives.","questions":[{"question":"In the passage, the word interior means?","choices":["inside","outdoor","broken","upstairs"],"answer":"inside"},{"question":"What tool do meteorologists use to spot rotation in storms?","choices":["a thermometer","a telescope","a compass","radar"],"answer":"radar"},{"question":"Where is the safest place to go during a tornado warning?","choices":["a basement or windowless inside room","a car on the highway","the top floor of a building","a room with large windows"],"answer":"a basement or windowless inside room"}]},{"text":"Have you ever wondered why bread is fluffy instead of flat? The secret is yeast, a tiny living fungus. When a baker mixes yeast with flour, water, and a little sugar, the yeast begins to feed on the sugars. As it eats, it releases a gas called carbon dioxide. Stretchy strands of gluten, a protein in wheat flour, trap the gas in thousands of tiny bubbles, causing the dough to rise. When the bread bakes, the heat sets the bubbles in place, leaving the soft, airy holes you see in a slice. Without yeast, the loaf would bake into a dense, heavy brick.","questions":[{"question":"In the passage, the word releases means?","choices":["freezes","hides","swallows","lets out"],"answer":"lets out"},{"question":"What traps the gas in tiny bubbles?","choices":["grains of salt","strands of gluten","drops of oil","pieces of sugar"],"answer":"strands of gluten"},{"question":"What would happen to this bread if it were made without yeast?","choices":["It would be dense and heavy.","It would be extra fluffy.","It would turn green.","It would float away."],"answer":"It would be dense and heavy."}],"photo":"images/flashcards/bread.jpg"},{"text":"Every popcorn kernel holds a tiny surprise: a small drop of water stored inside a ring of soft starch. The kernel's hard outer shell, called the hull, keeps that moisture sealed in. When the kernel is heated to about 350 degrees Fahrenheit, the water turns to steam. Pressure builds up inside until the hull suddenly bursts. The hot starch puffs out and cools almost instantly into the white, fluffy shape we eat. Kernels that do not pop often have cracked hulls or too little moisture, so the steam escapes before enough pressure can build.","questions":[{"question":"In the passage, the word hull means?","choices":["the hard outer shell of a kernel","a drop of water","the fluffy white part","a cooking pot"],"answer":"the hard outer shell of a kernel"},{"question":"What causes the kernel to burst?","choices":["cold air","salt on the outside","a sharp knife","pressure from steam inside"],"answer":"pressure from steam inside"},{"question":"Why might some kernels not pop?","choices":["They are the wrong color.","They are too fluffy.","They have too much starch.","Their hulls are cracked or they lack moisture."],"answer":"Their hulls are cracked or they lack moisture."}]},{"text":"A magnet has two ends called poles: a north pole and a south pole. Opposite poles attract, pulling toward each other, while like poles repel, pushing apart. Earth itself behaves like a giant magnet, and that is why a compass works. Its needle is a small magnet that can spin freely, and it lines up with Earth's magnetic field so one end points north. For about a thousand years, sailors and travelers have relied on compasses to find their way, even on cloudy nights when they could not see the stars. Early compasses, first used in China, were made with a naturally magnetic stone called lodestone.","questions":[{"question":"In the passage, the word repel means?","choices":["push away","break apart","spin around","pull close"],"answer":"push away"},{"question":"Why does a compass needle point north?","choices":["The stars pull on it.","It lines up with Earth's magnetic field.","One end is heavier.","The wind pushes it."],"answer":"It lines up with Earth's magnetic field."},{"question":"What was lodestone?","choices":["a naturally magnetic stone","a star used for travel","a tool for measuring depth","a kind of map"],"answer":"a naturally magnetic stone"}]},{"text":"Plants cannot walk, yet their seeds manage to travel surprisingly far. Spreading seeds away from the parent plant, called dispersal, gives young plants room, light, and water of their own. Plants have developed clever methods. Dandelion seeds ride the wind on tiny parachutes, and maple seeds spin like helicopter blades. Coconuts can float across the ocean for weeks. Burrs have hooks that cling to animal fur and hikers' socks. Many plants wrap seeds in tasty fruit; animals eat the fruit and later drop the seeds far away. Some plants, like touch-me-nots, even fling their seeds by bursting their pods.","questions":[{"question":"In the passage, the word dispersal means?","choices":["the eating of fruit","the falling of leaves","the growing of roots","the spreading out of seeds"],"answer":"the spreading out of seeds"},{"question":"Why is it helpful for seeds to travel away from the parent plant?","choices":["Seeds need to see new places.","Young plants get their own room, light, and water.","Animals need seeds to grow fur.","The parent plant needs more shade."],"answer":"Young plants get their own room, light, and water."},{"question":"How do burrs travel?","choices":["by spinning in the wind","by bursting from pods","by floating on the ocean","by clinging to animal fur"],"answer":"by clinging to animal fur"}],"photo":"images/flashcards/plant.jpg"},{"text":"The axolotl is a salamander found in the wild only in a few lakes and canals near Mexico City. Unlike most salamanders, which move onto land as adults, axolotls usually stay in the water their whole lives, keeping feathery gills that stick out from the sides of their heads. Their most amazing ability is regeneration. If an axolotl loses a leg, it can grow back a complete new one, including bones, muscles, and nerves. Scientists study axolotls hoping to learn more about healing. Sadly, wild axolotls are critically endangered because of pollution and the loss of their habitat, so people are working to protect the canals where they live.","questions":[{"question":"In the passage, the word regeneration means?","choices":["laying eggs","swimming fast","regrowing lost body parts","changing color"],"answer":"regrowing lost body parts"},{"question":"Why are wild axolotls endangered?","choices":["pollution and loss of habitat","very cold winters","a lack of sunlight","too many predators on land"],"answer":"pollution and loss of habitat"},{"question":"How are axolotls different from most salamanders?","choices":["They usually stay in the water as adults.","They have no legs.","They live high in trees.","They can fly short distances."],"answer":"They usually stay in the water as adults."}]},{"text":"Along the Pacific coast, sea otters play a surprisingly important role in underwater forests of giant kelp. Sea urchins, spiny creatures that graze on kelp, can multiply quickly and chew through entire forests, leaving bare areas called urchin barrens. Sea otters love to eat urchins, cracking them open while floating on their backs. By keeping the number of urchins in check, otters allow kelp forests to thrive. Those forests, in turn, shelter fish, seals, and countless other creatures. Because one species has such a large effect on its whole community, scientists call the sea otter a keystone species, named after the central stone that holds an arch together.","questions":[{"question":"In the passage, the word thrive means?","choices":["disappear","sink to the bottom","grow strong and healthy","become spiny"],"answer":"grow strong and healthy"},{"question":"What happens when there are too many sea urchins?","choices":["Fish find more places to hide.","They chew through kelp forests.","Sea otters grow bigger.","The water gets warmer."],"answer":"They chew through kelp forests."},{"question":"Why is the sea otter called a keystone species?","choices":["It is the largest animal in the ocean.","It builds stone arches.","It lives near rocky shores.","It has a large effect on its whole community."],"answer":"It has a large effect on its whole community."}]},{"text":"For decades, Major League Baseball was segregated. Black players were kept out and had to play in separate leagues called the Negro Leagues. That changed on April 15, 1947, when Jackie Robinson took the field for the Brooklyn Dodgers. Some fans and players shouted insults at him, and he received threatening letters. Robinson had promised not to fight back in anger, so he answered with his skill. He hit well, stole bases boldly, and was named Rookie of the Year. His courage opened the door for other Black athletes. Today, every April 15, all major league players wear his number, 42, to honor him.","questions":[{"question":"In the passage, the word segregated means?","choices":["separated by race","sold out","played only at night","very popular"],"answer":"separated by race"},{"question":"How did Jackie Robinson respond to insults?","choices":["He answered with his skill instead of fighting.","He shouted back.","He quit the team.","He switched to another sport."],"answer":"He answered with his skill instead of fighting."},{"question":"Why do players wear number 42 every April 15?","choices":["because the Dodgers won 42 games","to honor Jackie Robinson","to celebrate spring","because it is a lucky number"],"answer":"to honor Jackie Robinson"}]},{"text":"In 1804, President Thomas Jefferson sent Meriwether Lewis and William Clark to explore the vast lands west of the Mississippi River. Their group, called the Corps of Discovery, traveled by boat, on foot, and on horseback. They mapped rivers, recorded many plants and animals that were new to them, and met many Native American nations. A Shoshone woman named Sacagawea joined the journey as an interpreter, carrying her baby son along the way. Her presence showed others that the group came in peace, and she helped them get horses to cross the Rocky Mountains. In November 1805, the explorers reached the Pacific Ocean.","questions":[{"question":"In the passage, the word interpreter means?","choices":["a person who draws maps","a boat captain","a cook for the group","someone who translates between languages"],"answer":"someone who translates between languages"},{"question":"How did Sacagawea help the expedition?","choices":["She translated and helped them get horses.","She drew every map.","She paid for the trip.","She steered all the boats."],"answer":"She translated and helped them get horses."},{"question":"What did the explorers reach in November 1805?","choices":["the Atlantic Ocean","the Mississippi River","the Gulf of Mexico","the Pacific Ocean"],"answer":"the Pacific Ocean"}],"photo":"images/flashcards/boat.jpg"},{"text":"The Amazon rainforest covers much of northern South America and is the largest tropical rainforest on Earth. It is home to an astonishing variety of life, including jaguars, sloths, poison dart frogs, and huge numbers of insects. Scientists estimate that roughly one in ten known species on the planet lives there. The forest is layered like a building. Tall emergent trees poke above a thick canopy, where most of the animals live, while the dim forest floor below receives little sunlight. The Amazon also matters to the whole planet, since its trees absorb carbon dioxide and release moisture that helps form rain clouds.","questions":[{"question":"In the passage, the word astonishing means?","choices":["slightly scary","quite small","very surprising","very boring"],"answer":"very surprising"},{"question":"Where do most of the rainforest's animals live?","choices":["in the canopy","on the forest floor","in the river","above the emergent trees"],"answer":"in the canopy"},{"question":"What is the main idea of the passage?","choices":["Sloths are the most important rainforest animal.","The Amazon is a huge, layered rainforest full of life.","Rain clouds form only over oceans.","The forest floor gets lots of sunlight."],"answer":"The Amazon is a huge, layered rainforest full of life."}]},{"text":"Mount Everest, on the border between Nepal and China, is the highest mountain above sea level, rising about 29,032 feet. Near its summit, the air holds only about one third of the oxygen found at sea level, so most climbers carry bottled oxygen. Fierce winds, freezing temperatures, and deep cracks in the ice called crevasses make the climb dangerous. On May 29, 1953, Edmund Hillary of New Zealand and Tenzing Norgay, a Sherpa mountaineer, became the first people confirmed to reach the top. They climbed as a team, and both men later said the success belonged to them together.","questions":[{"question":"In the passage, the word summit means?","choices":["the bottom of a valley","a deep crack in ice","a kind of tent","the highest point of a mountain"],"answer":"the highest point of a mountain"},{"question":"Why do most climbers carry bottled oxygen?","choices":["The air near the top has much less oxygen.","It helps them see crevasses.","It makes their packs lighter.","The oxygen keeps them warm."],"answer":"The air near the top has much less oxygen."},{"question":"What can you infer about Hillary and Tenzing?","choices":["They climbed alone.","They disliked each other.","They valued teamwork.","They were racing each other."],"answer":"They valued teamwork."}]},{"text":"When Imani noticed that her neighbors each owned a ladder but rarely used it, she had an idea. Why should every family buy the same tools when they could share? With her mom's help, Imani turned an empty shed at the community center into a tool library. Neighbors donated rakes, drills, wheelbarrows, and even a pressure washer. Anyone could borrow a tool for a week, just like a library book. At first, only a few people stopped by. But after Mr. Okafor used a borrowed ladder to fix his roof and told everyone about it, the sign-up sheet filled quickly. By spring, the tool library had saved the neighborhood hundreds of dollars.","questions":[{"question":"In the passage, the word donated means?","choices":["broke on purpose","gave as a gift","sold for money","borrowed for a week"],"answer":"gave as a gift"},{"question":"What helped the tool library become popular?","choices":["The tools were given away forever.","Imani hung posters at school.","Mr. Okafor told everyone how it helped him.","The city paid people to visit."],"answer":"Mr. Okafor told everyone how it helped him."},{"question":"What is the main lesson of the story?","choices":["Libraries should only hold books.","Every family should own every tool.","Sharing resources can benefit a whole community.","Ladders are too dangerous to use."],"answer":"Sharing resources can benefit a whole community."}]},{"text":"Rafael had practiced for the school spelling bee for weeks, but as he waited backstage, his hands felt clammy and his mind went blank. His best friend Dev, who had been knocked out in the first round, slipped him a note. It read, 'You already know these words. Just picture our kitchen-table practice.' Rafael took a slow breath and imagined Dev quizzing him over bowls of cereal. When his turn came, he spelled 'nocturnal' correctly, then 'perimeter,' then 'gracious.' He finished in second place. Afterward, Rafael handed Dev the ribbon. 'Half of this is yours,' he said.","questions":[{"question":"In the passage, the word clammy means?","choices":["warm and dry","strong and steady","damp and cold","rough and scratchy"],"answer":"damp and cold"},{"question":"How did Dev help Rafael?","choices":["He reminded him of their practice sessions.","He asked the judges to go easy.","He spelled the words for him.","He gave him a dictionary."],"answer":"He reminded him of their practice sessions."},{"question":"Why did Rafael say 'Half of this is yours'?","choices":["He did not like the ribbon.","Dev had also won second place.","The judges told him to share it.","He believed Dev's help made his success possible."],"answer":"He believed Dev's help made his success possible."}]},{"text":"Because sending people to Mars is extremely difficult, scientists have explored the planet with robotic rovers. These wheeled vehicles, the newest about the size of a car, carry cameras, drills, and instruments that analyze rocks and soil. Radio signals take between about 3 and 22 minutes to travel from Earth to Mars, so engineers cannot steer a rover like a remote-control car. Instead, they send a list of instructions, and the rover carries them out on its own. Rovers have found evidence that Mars once had lakes and rivers, raising the question of whether tiny living things might once have existed there.","questions":[{"question":"In the passage, the word analyze means?","choices":["paint","bury","study closely","throw away"],"answer":"study closely"},{"question":"Why can't engineers steer a rover like a remote-control car?","choices":["Mars has no roads.","The rovers have no wheels.","The engineers live on Mars.","Radio signals take minutes to travel between planets."],"answer":"Radio signals take minutes to travel between planets."},{"question":"What evidence have rovers found?","choices":["that Mars once had lakes and rivers","that people once lived on Mars","that Mars has forests","that Mars is made of ice"],"answer":"that Mars once had lakes and rivers"}]}]},"synonyms":{"easy":{"toss":"throw","sniff":"smell","damp":"moist","quick":"speedy","sob":"weep","tug":"pull","chat":"talk","nap":"doze"},"hard":{"ponder":"consider","feeble":"weak","elated":"overjoyed","prudent":"sensible","lucid":"clear","jovial":"merry","dubious":"doubtful"}},"antonyms":{"easy":{"sunny":"cloudy","lose":"find","never":"always","before":"after","even":"odd","hello":"goodbye","few":"many","catch":"throw"},"hard":{"inhale":"exhale","ally":"enemy","frequent":"seldom","artificial":"natural","import":"export","drought":"flood","despair":"hope"}},"rhymes":{"easy":[["duck","truck","luck","stuck","tuck"],["peach","beach","reach","teach","each"],["pink","sink","wink","drink","think"],["corn","horn","born","worn","torn"]],"hard":[["kitten","mitten","bitten","smitten","written"],["rocket","pocket","socket","locket"],["giggle","wiggle","jiggle","squiggle"],["flutter","butter","clutter","gutter","shutter"]]},"analogies":[["glove","hand","boot","foot"],["painter","brush","writer","pen"],["puppy","dog","calf","cow"],["thermometer","temperature","ruler","length"],["bread","bakery","medicine","pharmacy"],["wool","sheep","honey","bee"],["chapter","book","verse","song"],["oar","rowboat","pedal","bicycle"]]},{"id":"2026-10-18-weekly-reading","released":"2026-10-18","kind":"weekly","reading_passages":{"0":[{"text":"A ladybug sits on a green leaf. She has red wings with black spots. She eats tiny bugs. Then she flies away.","questions":[{"question":"What color are the ladybug's wings?","choices":["red","pink","blue","white"],"answer":"red"},{"question":"Where does the ladybug sit?","choices":["on a cake","on a rock","on a leaf","on a hat"],"answer":"on a leaf"}],"image_prompt":"A warm, simple flat-style children's-book illustration for a kids' reading app. Scene: A ladybug sits on a green leaf. Focus on: a red ladybug with black spots on a green leaf. Bright friendly colors, soft rounded shapes, no text or letters anywhere in the image, square 1:1 aspect ratio, cheerful and inviting for young readers."},{"text":"Sam puts on his yellow boots. It rained all night. He jumps in a big puddle. Splash! His socks stay dry.","questions":[{"question":"What does Sam put on?","choices":["a hat","boots","mittens","a scarf"],"answer":"boots"},{"question":"Where does Sam jump?","choices":["in the sand","in a puddle","in a pool","on the bed"],"answer":"in a puddle"},{"question":"How do his socks stay?","choices":["cold","wet","muddy","dry"],"answer":"dry"}],"image_prompt":"A warm, simple flat-style children's-book illustration for a kids' reading app. Scene: Sam puts on his yellow boots. Focus on: a boy in yellow rain boots splashing in a puddle. Bright friendly colors, soft rounded shapes, no text or letters anywhere in the image, square 1:1 aspect ratio, cheerful and inviting for young readers."},{"text":"Grandpa and Lily bake muffins. They mix flour, eggs, and blueberries. The oven gets hot. Soon the kitchen smells sweet.","questions":[{"question":"What do Grandpa and Lily bake?","choices":["muffins","bread","pancakes","cookies"],"answer":"muffins"},{"question":"What fruit goes in the muffins?","choices":["bananas","cherries","apples","blueberries"],"answer":"blueberries"}],"image_prompt":"A warm, simple flat-style children's-book illustration for a kids' reading app. Scene: Grandpa and Lily bake muffins. Focus on: a grandpa and girl baking blueberry muffins. Bright friendly colors, soft rounded shapes, no text or letters anywhere in the image, square 1:1 aspect ratio, cheerful and inviting for young readers."},{"text":"The turtle walks very slowly. He carries his shell on his back. When he is scared, he hides his head inside.","questions":[{"question":"How does the turtle walk?","choices":["slowly","sideways","fast","backward"],"answer":"slowly"},{"question":"What does he carry on his back?","choices":["a big leaf","a small box","his shell","a bag"],"answer":"his shell"},{"question":"What does he hide when he is scared?","choices":["his toys","his tail","his head","his food"],"answer":"his head"}]},{"text":"Mia blows bubbles at the park. The bubbles are round and shiny. One lands on her nose. Pop!","questions":[{"question":"Where does Mia blow bubbles?","choices":["at the park","at school","at the beach","in the car"],"answer":"at the park"},{"question":"Where does one bubble land?","choices":["on a dog","on a tree","on her hand","on her nose"],"answer":"on her nose"}],"image_prompt":"A warm, simple flat-style children's-book illustration for a kids' reading app. Scene: Mia blows bubbles at the park. Focus on: a girl blowing shiny bubbles in a park. Bright friendly colors, soft rounded shapes, no text or letters anywhere in the image, square 1:1 aspect ratio, cheerful and inviting for young readers."},{"text":"Ben has six crayons. Ava has none. Ben gives Ava three crayons. Now they can both draw. Ava says thank you.","questions":[{"question":"How many crayons does Ben have at first?","choices":["three","ten","six","two"],"answer":"six"},{"question":"What does Ava say?","choices":["no thanks","hello","good night","thank you"],"answer":"thank you"}]},{"text":"Lena has a new umbrella. It is yellow with white dots. Rain taps on top of it. Lena splashes in a big puddle and laughs.","questions":[{"question":"What color is Lena's umbrella?","choices":["yellow","black","purple","orange"],"answer":"yellow"},{"question":"What does Lena do in the puddle?","choices":["She splashes.","She feeds a duck.","She takes a nap.","She reads a book."],"answer":"She splashes."}],"photo":"images/flashcards/umbrella.jpg"},{"text":"At night the moon comes out. It looks like a big silver ball. Stars twinkle all around it. Owls hoot in the trees.","questions":[{"question":"When does the moon come out?","choices":["after school","at night","in the morning","at lunch"],"answer":"at night"},{"question":"What hoots in the trees?","choices":["dogs","owls","frogs","ducks"],"answer":"owls"}],"photo":"images/flashcards/moon.jpg"},{"text":"Kai and Zoe build a snowman. They roll three big snowballs. They add a carrot nose and two button eyes. Brr, it is cold!","questions":[{"question":"How many snowballs do they roll?","choices":["four","seven","two","three"],"answer":"three"},{"question":"What is the snowman's nose?","choices":["a rock","a stick","a carrot","a button"],"answer":"a carrot"}],"image_prompt":"A warm, simple flat-style children's-book illustration for a kids' reading app. Scene: Kai and Zoe build a snowman. Focus on: two kids building a snowman with a carrot nose. Bright friendly colors, soft rounded shapes, no text or letters anywhere in the image, square 1:1 aspect ratio, cheerful and inviting for young readers."},{"text":"A bee lands on a red flower. It sips sweet nectar. Yellow dust sticks to its legs. Then the bee flies home to the hive.","questions":[{"question":"Where does the bee land?","choices":["in a cold glass","on a tall green plant","on a gray rock","on a red flower"],"answer":"on a red flower"},{"question":"What does the bee sip?","choices":["warm milk","cold lemonade","sweet nectar","orange juice"],"answer":"sweet nectar"},{"question":"Where does the bee fly at the end?","choices":["to the school","to the hive","to the park","to a pond"],"answer":"to the hive"}],"photo":"images/flashcards/bee.jpg"},{"text":"Dad plants a tiny seed in the dirt. Nora waters it every day. The sun shines on it. One day a green sprout pops up!","questions":[{"question":"What does Dad plant?","choices":["a seed","a coin","a tree","a rock"],"answer":"a seed"},{"question":"Who waters the seed?","choices":["the dog","Dad","Grandma","Nora"],"answer":"Nora"},{"question":"What pops up?","choices":["a green sprout","a yellow duck","a red apple","a small worm"],"answer":"a green sprout"}],"image_prompt":"A warm, simple flat-style children's-book illustration for a kids' reading app. Scene: Dad plants a tiny seed in the dirt. Focus on: a green sprout growing from dirt. Bright friendly colors, soft rounded shapes, no text or letters anywhere in the image, square 1:1 aspect ratio, cheerful and inviting for young readers."},{"text":"The bus stops at the corner. Rosa climbs the steps. She sits by the window and waves to her mom. The bus goes to school.","questions":[{"question":"Where does Rosa sit?","choices":["on the floor","in the back","by the driver","by the window"],"answer":"by the window"},{"question":"Where does the bus go?","choices":["to the farm","to the zoo","to the store","to school"],"answer":"to school"}],"photo":"images/flashcards/bus.jpg"},{"text":"A puppy named Biscuit loves to dig. He digs in the yard and finds an old bone. He wags his tail and runs inside.","questions":[{"question":"What does Biscuit love to do?","choices":["swim","dig","sleep","bark"],"answer":"dig"},{"question":"What does Biscuit find?","choices":["a toy car","a red ball","an old bone","a lost shoe"],"answer":"an old bone"}],"image_prompt":"A warm, simple flat-style children's-book illustration for a kids' reading app. Scene: A puppy named Biscuit loves to dig. Focus on: a puppy digging up a bone in a yard. Bright friendly colors, soft rounded shapes, no text or letters anywhere in the image, square 1:1 aspect ratio, cheerful and inviting for young readers."},{"text":"Lemons are yellow and sour. Ella squeezes lemons into water. She adds a little sugar. Now she has lemonade to share.","questions":[{"question":"How do lemons taste?","choices":["sour","spicy","salty","sweet"],"answer":"sour"},{"question":"What does Ella add to the water?","choices":["sugar","salt","ice cream","milk"],"answer":"sugar"}],"image_prompt":"A warm, simple flat-style children's-book illustration for a kids' reading app. Scene: Lemons are yellow and sour. Focus on: a girl making lemonade with lemons. Bright friendly colors, soft rounded shapes, no text or letters anywhere in the image, square 1:1 aspect ratio, cheerful and inviting for young readers."},{"text":"Leo feels sad. His tower of blocks fell down. His sister helps him build it again. Now the tower is tall, and Leo smiles.","questions":[{"question":"Why is Leo sad?","choices":["his dog ran off","he lost his hat","it is raining","his tower fell"],"answer":"his tower fell"},{"question":"Who helps Leo?","choices":["his dad","his teacher","his friend","his sister"],"answer":"his sister"}]},{"text":"A squirrel finds acorns in the fall. She hides them in the ground. When winter comes, she digs them up to eat.","questions":[{"question":"What does the squirrel find?","choices":["berries","acorns","pinecones","apples"],"answer":"acorns"},{"question":"When does she dig them up?","choices":["on her birthday","in summer","in winter","at lunch"],"answer":"in winter"}],"image_prompt":"A warm, simple flat-style children's-book illustration for a kids' reading app. Scene: A squirrel finds acorns in the fall. Focus on: a squirrel burying acorns in fall. Bright friendly colors, soft rounded shapes, no text or letters anywhere in the image, square 1:1 aspect ratio, cheerful and inviting for young readers."},{"text":"Ana rides her bike with her helmet on. She rings her bell, ding, ding! She rides slowly past the ducks at the pond.","questions":[{"question":"What does Ana wear?","choices":["a crown","a cape","a helmet","a mask"],"answer":"a helmet"},{"question":"What does Ana ride past?","choices":["the trains","the ducks","the horses","the cows"],"answer":"the ducks"}]},{"text":"The farmer milks the cow in the barn. The cow says moo. The milk goes in a big pail. The cat waits for a drink.","questions":[{"question":"Where is the cow?","choices":["in the house","in a boat","in the barn","at the park"],"answer":"in the barn"},{"question":"Who waits for a drink?","choices":["the horse","the cat","the farmer","the pig"],"answer":"the cat"}],"photo":"images/flashcards/cow.jpg"},{"text":"It is windy today. The leaves blow off the trees. Red, orange, and brown leaves fall. Jack jumps into a big leaf pile.","questions":[{"question":"What kind of day is it?","choices":["foggy","windy","snowy","sunny and hot"],"answer":"windy"},{"question":"What does Jack jump into?","choices":["a sandbox","a puddle","a leaf pile","a pool"],"answer":"a leaf pile"}],"photo":"images/flashcards/leaf.jpg"},{"text":"Ruby has a new toothbrush. She brushes her teeth in the morning and at night. She brushes for two whole minutes. Her teeth are clean.","questions":[{"question":"When does Ruby brush her teeth?","choices":["only before naps","only after lunch","on Saturdays only","morning and night"],"answer":"morning and night"},{"question":"How long does Ruby brush?","choices":["five seconds","one hour","ten minutes","two minutes"],"answer":"two minutes"}]}],"1":[{"text":"At low tide, the ocean pulls back and leaves small pools of water among the rocks. These tide pools are full of life. Starfish cling to the stones, and tiny crabs scurry between them. Sea anemones wave soft arms, waiting for a snack to drift by. When the tide comes back in, the waves cover the pools again.","questions":[{"question":"What are tide pools?","choices":["Puddles that collect after a rainstorm","Pools left among the rocks at low tide","Ponds on a farm","Large lakes found high in the mountains"],"answer":"Pools left among the rocks at low tide"},{"question":"What do sea anemones do?","choices":["dig deep holes in the sand","wave soft arms and wait for a snack","hide inside a shell","swim quickly across the whole ocean floor"],"answer":"wave soft arms and wait for a snack"},{"question":"What happens when the tide comes back in?","choices":["The waves cover the pools.","The crabs dig tunnels in sand","The rocks turn into sand","The starfish march up the beach"],"answer":"The waves cover the pools."}]},{"text":"Omar could not find his library book. He looked under his bed and in his backpack. Then he remembered reading in the car. He ran outside and found the book on the back seat. He returned it to the library right on time.","questions":[{"question":"Where did Omar find the book?","choices":["on the kitchen table","on the back seat","in his backpack","under his bed"],"answer":"on the back seat"},{"question":"What did Omar remember?","choices":["giving it to a friend","reading in the car","reading at school","leaving it at Grandma's"],"answer":"reading in the car"}],"photo":"images/flashcards/book.jpg"},{"text":"Fog is a cloud that sits close to the ground. It forms when warm, wet air cools down. On foggy mornings, it can be hard to see far away. Drivers turn on their lights and go slowly. When the sun warms the air, the fog goes away.","questions":[{"question":"What is fog?","choices":["dust blown by the wind","smoke from a big campfire","a cloud near the ground","melted snow on the road"],"answer":"a cloud near the ground"},{"question":"What do drivers do in fog?","choices":["stop for lunch","turn on their lights","open their windows","honk their horns"],"answer":"turn on their lights"},{"question":"What makes the fog go away?","choices":["the sun warming the air","a heavy rain falling down","the birds flapping wings","the cars driving past it"],"answer":"the sun warming the air"}],"image_prompt":"A warm, simple flat-style children's-book illustration for a kids' reading app. Scene: Fog is a cloud that sits close to the ground. Focus on: a foggy morning street with cars using headlights. Bright friendly colors, soft rounded shapes, no text or letters anywhere in the image, square 1:1 aspect ratio, cheerful and inviting for young readers."},{"text":"Long ago, in 1891, a teacher named James Naismith wanted a game his students could play indoors in winter. He nailed two peach baskets high on the walls. Players tried to toss a ball into the baskets. That was the very first game of basketball!","questions":[{"question":"What did James Naismith use for the first hoops?","choices":["metal buckets","plastic hoops","peach baskets","cardboard boxes"],"answer":"peach baskets"},{"question":"Why did he make up the game?","choices":["to play indoors in winter","to sell his peach baskets","to have fun at the beach","to win a big trophy prize"],"answer":"to play indoors in winter"}],"image_prompt":"A warm, simple flat-style children's-book illustration for a kids' reading app. Scene: Long ago, in 1891, a teacher named James Naismith wanted a game his students could play indoors in winter. Focus on: students tossing a ball into peach baskets in an old gym. Bright friendly colors, soft rounded shapes, no text or letters anywhere in the image, square 1:1 aspect ratio, cheerful and inviting for young readers."},{"text":"Maple syrup comes from maple trees. In early spring, farmers make a small hole in the tree trunk. Clear, watery sap drips out into a bucket. The farmers boil the sap for a long time. The water goes away, and sweet, sticky syrup is left.","questions":[{"question":"When do farmers collect the sap?","choices":["late summer","early spring","fall","winter nights"],"answer":"early spring"},{"question":"What do the farmers do to the sap?","choices":["boil it","freeze it","paint it","drink it"],"answer":"boil it"},{"question":"What does the sap look like?","choices":["red and thick","brown and sticky","clear and watery","white and fluffy"],"answer":"clear and watery"}],"image_prompt":"A warm, simple flat-style children's-book illustration for a kids' reading app. Scene: Maple syrup comes from maple trees. Focus on: maple sap dripping into a bucket on a tree. Bright friendly colors, soft rounded shapes, no text or letters anywhere in the image, square 1:1 aspect ratio, cheerful and inviting for young readers."},{"text":"A new girl named Priya joined Mr. Lee's class. She sat alone at lunch. Carlos noticed and invited her to sit with his friends. Priya told them about her old school. By the end of lunch, everyone was laughing together.","questions":[{"question":"Where did Priya sit at first?","choices":["alone at lunch","next to the teacher","with Carlos","on the playground"],"answer":"alone at lunch"},{"question":"What did Carlos do?","choices":["gave her a new pencil box","walked her to the art room after lunch","showed her the library books","invited her to sit with friends"],"answer":"invited her to sit with friends"}],"image_prompt":"A warm, simple flat-style children's-book illustration for a kids' reading app. Scene: A new girl named Priya joined Mr. Focus on: children welcoming a new girl at a lunch table. Bright friendly colors, soft rounded shapes, no text or letters anywhere in the image, square 1:1 aspect ratio, cheerful and inviting for young readers."},{"text":"Sea turtles spend most of their lives in the ocean. But a mother sea turtle crawls onto a sandy beach to lay her eggs. She digs a hole and covers the eggs with sand. Weeks later, tiny baby turtles hatch and hurry to the water.","questions":[{"question":"Where does the mother lay her eggs?","choices":["in a coral reef","on a floating log","in a tall tree","on a sandy beach"],"answer":"on a sandy beach"},{"question":"What do the babies do after they hatch?","choices":["wait for their mother","sleep in the sand","dig a deeper hole","hurry to the water"],"answer":"hurry to the water"}]},{"text":"Jada wanted to know how much rain fell. She put an empty jar outside in the yard. After a rainy night, she used a ruler to measure the water in the jar. It was two inches deep! She wrote the number in her weather notebook.","questions":[{"question":"What did Jada use to measure the water?","choices":["a ruler","a scale","a cup","a clock"],"answer":"a ruler"},{"question":"How deep was the water?","choices":["ten inches","half an inch","two inches","one foot"],"answer":"two inches"},{"question":"Where did Jada write the number?","choices":["in a letter to Grandma","on the empty jar lid","in her weather notebook","on the kitchen wall"],"answer":"in her weather notebook"}],"photo":"images/flashcards/rain.jpg"},{"text":"A shadow is made when something blocks the light. On a sunny day, your body blocks the sun's light, so a dark shape appears on the ground. In the morning and evening, when the sun is low, your shadow is long and thin. At noon, when the sun is high overhead, your shadow gets short. If it is cloudy, there may be no shadow at all.","questions":[{"question":"What makes a shadow?","choices":["A dark color painted on the ground","The moon shining at night","Something blocking the light","Wind blowing on the grass"],"answer":"Something blocking the light"},{"question":"When is your shadow longest?","choices":["Only at lunchtime","When the sun is high overhead","At noon","In the morning and evening"],"answer":"In the morning and evening"},{"question":"What happens to your shadow at noon?","choices":["It disappears forever","It gets long and thin","It gets short","It turns blue"],"answer":"It gets short"}]},{"text":"Saturn is the sixth planet from the sun. It is famous for its beautiful rings. The rings are made of billions of pieces of ice and rock. Some pieces are as small as sand, and some are as big as a house. Saturn is so far away that one year there lasts about 29 Earth years.","questions":[{"question":"What are Saturn's rings made of?","choices":["ice and rock","gas and fire","sand and water","dust and leaves"],"answer":"ice and rock"},{"question":"How long is one year on Saturn?","choices":["about 6 Earth years","about 100 Earth days","about 29 Earth years","about one Earth year"],"answer":"about 29 Earth years"}],"image_prompt":"A warm, simple flat-style children's-book illustration for a kids' reading app. Scene: Saturn is the sixth planet from the sun. Focus on: the planet Saturn and its rings. Bright friendly colors, soft rounded shapes, no text or letters anywhere in the image, square 1:1 aspect ratio, cheerful and inviting for young readers."},{"text":"Hannah's hands shook before her piano recital. Her stomach felt full of butterflies. Her teacher told her to take three deep breaths. Hannah breathed in and out. She felt calmer, sat down, and played her song from start to finish.","questions":[{"question":"What did Hannah's teacher tell her to do?","choices":["take three deep breaths","practice her song one more time","drink a glass of water","go home and rest"],"answer":"take three deep breaths"},{"question":"What instrument did Hannah play?","choices":["flute","drum","violin","piano"],"answer":"piano"}],"image_prompt":"A warm, simple flat-style children's-book illustration for a kids' reading app. Scene: Hannah's hands shook before her piano recital. Focus on: a girl taking a deep breath at a piano. Bright friendly colors, soft rounded shapes, no text or letters anywhere in the image, square 1:1 aspect ratio, cheerful and inviting for young readers."},{"text":"Sloths live high in the trees of rainforests. They move so slowly that tiny green plants called algae grow on their fur. The green color helps sloths hide from hungry animals. Sloths climb down from their tree only about once a week.","questions":[{"question":"What grows on a sloth's fur?","choices":["algae","flowers","moss","mushrooms"],"answer":"algae"},{"question":"How does the green color help sloths?","choices":["it keeps them warm","it helps them swim","it makes them fast","it helps them hide"],"answer":"it helps them hide"},{"question":"How often do sloths climb down?","choices":["about every hour","twice every single day","about once a week","only once a year"],"answer":"about once a week"}],"image_prompt":"A warm, simple flat-style children's-book illustration for a kids' reading app. Scene: Sloths live high in the trees of rainforests. Focus on: a sloth hanging in a rainforest tree. Bright friendly colors, soft rounded shapes, no text or letters anywhere in the image, square 1:1 aspect ratio, cheerful and inviting for young readers."},{"text":"On Friday, the Garcia family makes pizza together. Dad rolls the dough into a circle. Lucia spreads tomato sauce on top. Her brother sprinkles cheese and adds peppers. Mom puts the pizza in the hot oven. Twenty minutes later, dinner is ready!","questions":[{"question":"Who spreads the tomato sauce?","choices":["Lucia","her brother","Mom","Dad"],"answer":"Lucia"},{"question":"How long does the pizza bake?","choices":["five minutes","twenty minutes","about an hour","two whole hours"],"answer":"twenty minutes"}],"photo":"images/flashcards/pizza.jpg"},{"text":"A robot vacuum cleans floors all by itself. It has sensors that work like eyes. When it bumps into a chair, it turns and goes another way. When its battery runs low, it rolls back to its charger to rest.","questions":[{"question":"What helps the robot find its way?","choices":["a remote","wheels","a map","sensors"],"answer":"sensors"},{"question":"What does the robot do when its battery is low?","choices":["turns off its lights","beeps loudly for help","stops in the hallway","rolls back to its charger"],"answer":"rolls back to its charger"}],"photo":"images/flashcards/robot.jpg"},{"text":"On Saturday, Theo and his mom helped at the animal shelter. Theo filled water bowls and brushed a fluffy gray cat. His mom walked three dogs around the block. Before they left, the gray cat purred and rubbed against Theo's leg.","questions":[{"question":"Where did Theo and his mom help?","choices":["at the animal shelter","at the library","at the city zoo gate","at a pet store"],"answer":"at the animal shelter"},{"question":"What color was the cat Theo brushed?","choices":["black","gray","orange","white"],"answer":"gray"},{"question":"How many dogs did Mom walk?","choices":["two","eight","four","three"],"answer":"three"}],"photo":"images/flashcards/cat.jpg"},{"text":"When thunder roars, go indoors! Lightning can strike tall things outside, like trees and poles. During a storm, stay inside and away from windows. Wait thirty minutes after the last thunder before you go back outside to play.","questions":[{"question":"How long should you wait after the last thunder?","choices":["five minutes","two hours","one minute","thirty minutes"],"answer":"thirty minutes"},{"question":"What can lightning strike?","choices":["only puddles of water","tall things like trees","small flowers in the grass","only cars on the road"],"answer":"tall things like trees"}]},{"text":"The Grand Canyon is a giant valley in Arizona. It is more than a mile deep! The Colorado River carved it slowly over millions of years. The rock walls have stripes of red, orange, and tan. Each stripe is a different layer of rock.","questions":[{"question":"What carved the Grand Canyon?","choices":["a giant moving glacier","the Colorado River","a big earthquake","strong desert winds"],"answer":"the Colorado River"},{"question":"What are the stripes on the canyon walls?","choices":["water marks","layers of rock","painted lines","roots of trees"],"answer":"layers of rock"}],"image_prompt":"A warm, simple flat-style children's-book illustration for a kids' reading app. Scene: The Grand Canyon is a giant valley in Arizona. Focus on: the striped rock walls of the Grand Canyon. Bright friendly colors, soft rounded shapes, no text or letters anywhere in the image, square 1:1 aspect ratio, cheerful and inviting for young readers."},{"text":"The first time Mei went ice skating, she fell down five times. Her dad held her hand and told her to bend her knees. Mei kept trying. By the end of the day, she could skate all the way around the rink by herself.","questions":[{"question":"How many times did Mei fall?","choices":["ten","seven","two","five"],"answer":"five"},{"question":"What did her dad tell her to do?","choices":["close her eyes","skate faster","bend her knees","stand very still"],"answer":"bend her knees"}],"image_prompt":"A warm, simple flat-style children's-book illustration for a kids' reading app. Scene: The first time Mei went ice skating, she fell down five times. Focus on: a girl learning to ice skate holding her dad's hand. Bright friendly colors, soft rounded shapes, no text or letters anywhere in the image, square 1:1 aspect ratio, cheerful and inviting for young readers."},{"text":"A hermit crab does not grow its own shell. It lives inside an empty shell that another sea animal left behind. As the crab grows bigger, its shell gets too tight. Then it looks for a larger shell and moves in.","questions":[{"question":"Where does a hermit crab live?","choices":["in an empty shell","under a big rock","in a sandcastle","inside a sea star"],"answer":"in an empty shell"},{"question":"What does the crab do when its shell is too tight?","choices":["stays inside forever","swims to the deep ocean","grows a new shell","looks for a larger shell"],"answer":"looks for a larger shell"}],"photo":"images/flashcards/crab.jpg"},{"text":"Our class cleaned up Maple Street Park. We wore gloves and picked up trash. We filled six big bags! Then we planted yellow flowers near the slide. Now the park looks bright and cheerful, and more families come to play.","questions":[{"question":"How many bags did the class fill?","choices":["twelve","three","nine","six"],"answer":"six"},{"question":"What did the class plant?","choices":["pumpkins","yellow flowers","green beans","small trees"],"answer":"yellow flowers"}],"image_prompt":"A warm, simple flat-style children's-book illustration for a kids' reading app. Scene: Our class cleaned up Maple Street Park. Focus on: kids picking up litter and planting flowers in a park. Bright friendly colors, soft rounded shapes, no text or letters anywhere in the image, square 1:1 aspect ratio, cheerful and inviting for young readers."},{"text":"Every snowflake has six sides or six points. Snowflakes form high in cold clouds when tiny drops of water freeze onto specks of dust. As a snowflake falls, more ice crystals stick to it. No two snowflakes look exactly alike.","questions":[{"question":"How many sides does a snowflake have?","choices":["six","five","eight","four"],"answer":"six"},{"question":"Where do snowflakes form?","choices":["in cold clouds","in the ocean","on cold windows","inside freezers"],"answer":"in cold clouds"}],"image_prompt":"A warm, simple flat-style children's-book illustration for a kids' reading app. Scene: Every snowflake has six sides or six points. Focus on: a close-up six-pointed snowflake. Bright friendly colors, soft rounded shapes, no text or letters anywhere in the image, square 1:1 aspect ratio, cheerful and inviting for young readers."},{"text":"When Isaac bit into an apple, his front tooth wiggled loose and came out. He was not scared because it was a baby tooth. His dentist had told him a bigger adult tooth was growing underneath. Isaac smiled at the gap in the mirror.","questions":[{"question":"What did Isaac bite into?","choices":["a sandwich","a cookie","a carrot","an apple"],"answer":"an apple"},{"question":"What was growing under the baby tooth?","choices":["a tiny bone","a new gum","another baby tooth","an adult tooth"],"answer":"an adult tooth"}],"image_prompt":"A warm, simple flat-style children's-book illustration for a kids' reading app. Scene: When Isaac bit into an apple, his front tooth wiggled loose and came out. Focus on: a smiling boy with a missing front tooth. Bright friendly colors, soft rounded shapes, no text or letters anywhere in the image, square 1:1 aspect ratio, cheerful and inviting for young readers."},{"text":"Pumpkins grow on long, twisty vines. In spring, farmers plant pumpkin seeds. Yellow flowers bloom in summer, and small green pumpkins appear. By fall, most pumpkins have turned bright orange. People carve them, bake them into pies, and roast their seeds.","questions":[{"question":"When do farmers plant pumpkin seeds?","choices":["in winter","in fall","in spring","at night"],"answer":"in spring"},{"question":"What color are most pumpkins by fall?","choices":["purple","yellow","green","orange"],"answer":"orange"},{"question":"What do pumpkins grow on?","choices":["vines","rocks","bushes","trees"],"answer":"vines"}],"image_prompt":"A warm, simple flat-style children's-book illustration for a kids' reading app. Scene: Pumpkins grow on long, twisty vines. Focus on: orange pumpkins on twisty vines. Bright friendly colors, soft rounded shapes, no text or letters anywhere in the image, square 1:1 aspect ratio, cheerful and inviting for young readers."}],"2":[{"text":"In the early 1900s, city streets were becoming crowded with automobiles, horse-drawn wagons, bicycles, and people on foot. Early traffic signals had only two commands, stop and go, and they switched with no warning, which led to collisions at busy intersections. Garrett Morgan, an inventor in Cleveland, Ohio, witnessed a serious crash and decided to address the problem. In 1923 he received a patent for a signal that added a third position, which halted traffic in every direction for a moment before the signal changed. That brief pause gave drivers time to react. Today's yellow light serves a similar purpose: it warns drivers that the signal is about to change.","questions":[{"question":"In the passage, the word witnessed means?","choices":["heard about from a friend","read about in a paper","caused by his own car","saw with his own eyes"],"answer":"saw with his own eyes"},{"question":"Why was the third position in Morgan's signal helpful?","choices":["It gave drivers time to react before the signal changed","It let people on foot cross at any time","It allowed horses and cars to use separate lanes","It made the signal easier to see at night"],"answer":"It gave drivers time to react before the signal changed"},{"question":"Which modern signal serves a similar purpose to Morgan's third position?","choices":["the walk sign","the yellow light","the flashing green arrow","the red light"],"answer":"the yellow light"}],"image_prompt":"A warm, simple flat-style children's-book illustration for a kids' reading app. Scene: In the early 1900s, city streets were becoming crowded with automobiles, horse-drawn wagons, bicycles, and people on foot. Focus on: an early 1900s street intersection with a three-position traffic signal. Bright friendly colors, soft rounded shapes, no text or letters anywhere in the image, square 1:1 aspect ratio, cheerful and inviting for young readers."},{"text":"Periodical cicadas have one of the strangest life cycles in the insect world. After hatching, the young insects, called nymphs, burrow underground, where they feed on sap from tree roots. Depending on the type, they remain hidden for either 13 or 17 years. Then, in a single spring, millions of nymphs tunnel to the surface at once, climb nearby trees, and shed their skins to become winged adults. The males produce a loud, buzzing chorus to attract mates. Scientists think emerging in such enormous numbers helps the species survive: predators like birds can eat only a small fraction of them, so plenty remain to lay eggs for the next generation.","questions":[{"question":"In the passage, the word emerging means?","choices":["growing larger quickly","sleeping for a long time","coming out into view","flying south together"],"answer":"coming out into view"},{"question":"Why might it help cicadas to appear in huge numbers all at once?","choices":["The trees grow more sap when many arrive","Their loud songs scare away hungry birds","They can share food more easily in large groups","Predators cannot eat them all, so many survive"],"answer":"Predators cannot eat them all, so many survive"},{"question":"Where do cicada nymphs spend most of their lives?","choices":["in nests high in the trees","inside hollow tree branches","underground, feeding on root sap","under rocks near streams"],"answer":"underground, feeding on root sap"}],"image_prompt":"A warm, simple flat-style children's-book illustration for a kids' reading app. Scene: Periodical cicadas have one of the strangest life cycles in the insect world. Focus on: cicadas climbing a tree and shedding their skins. Bright friendly colors, soft rounded shapes, no text or letters anywhere in the image, square 1:1 aspect ratio, cheerful and inviting for young readers."},{"text":"Seahorses are fish, even though they look nothing like most fish. They swim upright and steer with a small fin on their back, but they are poor swimmers. To keep from drifting away in ocean currents, a seahorse wraps its curled tail around seagrass or coral. Seahorses are also unusual parents. The female places her eggs into a pouch on the male's belly, and the male carries them until the babies are born. A single male can release hundreds of tiny seahorses at once.","questions":[{"question":"In the passage, the word drifting means?","choices":["hiding under the sandy floor","diving down very deep","being carried along slowly","eating small plants"],"answer":"being carried along slowly"},{"question":"What is the main idea of the passage?","choices":["Seahorses spend most of their lives buried in sand","Seahorses are the fastest swimmers on a coral reef","Seahorses are not really fish but a kind of horse","Seahorses are unusual fish in how they swim and raise young"],"answer":"Seahorses are unusual fish in how they swim and raise young"},{"question":"Why does a seahorse wrap its tail around seagrass?","choices":["to build a nest for its eggs","to keep from drifting away","to catch small fish","to sleep upside down"],"answer":"to keep from drifting away"}],"image_prompt":"A warm, simple flat-style children's-book illustration for a kids' reading app. Scene: Seahorses are fish, even though they look nothing like most fish. Focus on: a seahorse with its tail wrapped around seagrass. Bright friendly colors, soft rounded shapes, no text or letters anywhere in the image, square 1:1 aspect ratio, cheerful and inviting for young readers."},{"text":"In 1872, Congress set aside a vast area of land in the West as Yellowstone, the first national park in the United States and widely considered the first in the world. Explorers had returned with stories of boiling springs, bubbling mud pots, and geysers that shot water high into the air. Many people back east did not believe these reports until they saw paintings and photographs. Leaders decided the land was too remarkable to be sold or built upon, so it was protected for everyone to enjoy. Today, millions of visitors come each year, and the national park system includes more than sixty parks.","questions":[{"question":"In the passage, the word remarkable means?","choices":["difficult to travel across","worth noticing because it is unusual","too dangerous for families to visit alone","owned by a single family"],"answer":"worth noticing because it is unusual"},{"question":"Why did paintings and photographs matter to Yellowstone's story?","choices":["They were sold to raise money for building roads","They were used to draw the park's first maps","They helped convince doubtful people the reports were true","They showed where gold could be found in the park"],"answer":"They helped convince doubtful people the reports were true"},{"question":"What was Yellowstone the first of in the United States?","choices":["state forest","city playground","public zoo","national park"],"answer":"national park"}],"image_prompt":"A warm, simple flat-style children's-book illustration for a kids' reading app. Scene: In 1872, Congress set aside a vast area of land in the West as Yellowstone, the first national park in the United States and widely considered the first in the world. Focus on: Yellowstone geysers and hot springs. Bright friendly colors, soft rounded shapes, no text or letters anywhere in the image, square 1:1 aspect ratio, cheerful and inviting for young readers."},{"text":"Dev's robotics team had spent three weeks building a robot to sort colored blocks. At the regional contest, the robot rolled forward, paused, and dropped every block on the floor. The team was crushed. On the bus ride home, nobody spoke. The next morning, though, Dev pulled out the notebook where they had recorded every test. He noticed that the robot had always been tested under the classroom's dim lights, while the contest hall was flooded with bright light. The color sensor had been confused. The team adjusted the sensor's settings and tested it in sunlight, in shade, and under lamps. At the next contest, the robot sorted every block perfectly.","questions":[{"question":"In the passage, the word crushed means?","choices":["very disappointed","completely lost","proud and happy","squeezed until flat"],"answer":"very disappointed"},{"question":"What caused the robot to fail at the first contest?","choices":["Its battery ran out during the contest","The bright lights confused its color sensor","The judges used blocks that were too heavy for it to lift","A wheel fell off on the way to the hall"],"answer":"The bright lights confused its color sensor"},{"question":"What lesson does the story best show?","choices":["Careful records can help you learn from a failure","It is best to quit after losing a contest","Robots should only be used in dark rooms","Winning matters more than teamwork"],"answer":"Careful records can help you learn from a failure"}],"photo":"images/flashcards/robot.jpg"},{"text":"Beavers are sometimes called nature's engineers. Using their strong, orange front teeth, which never stop growing, they cut down trees and drag branches into streams. They pack the branches with mud and stones to build dams. The dam slows the water and creates a calm pond, where the beavers build a lodge with an underwater entrance that keeps predators out. These ponds do more than shelter beavers. They provide homes for fish, frogs, ducks, and insects, and they help store water during dry seasons.","questions":[{"question":"In the passage, the word shelter means?","choices":["carry downstream","wash away quickly","feed with fresh plants","protect and give a safe place to"],"answer":"protect and give a safe place to"},{"question":"Which sentence best states the main idea?","choices":["Beaver dams change streams in ways that help many living things","Beavers' teeth stop growing after they build their very first dam","Beavers build lodges only on dry land far from water","Beavers are dangerous to fish and frogs that live in streams"],"answer":"Beaver dams change streams in ways that help many living things"},{"question":"How does the underwater entrance help beavers?","choices":["It helps them store branches","It keeps the pond from freezing","It keeps predators out of the lodge","It lets them catch fish more easily"],"answer":"It keeps predators out of the lodge"}],"image_prompt":"A warm, simple flat-style children's-book illustration for a kids' reading app. Scene: Beavers are sometimes called nature's engineers. Focus on: a beaver dam and lodge in a pond. Bright friendly colors, soft rounded shapes, no text or letters anywhere in the image, square 1:1 aspect ratio, cheerful and inviting for young readers."},{"text":"When a phone shows your location on a map, it is relying on satellites orbiting thousands of miles above Earth. Each satellite constantly sends out signals that include the exact time the signal was sent. A receiver on the ground picks up signals from at least four satellites and calculates how long each one took to arrive. Because the signals travel at a known speed, the receiver can figure out its distance from each satellite and pinpoint where it is. The system depends on extremely precise clocks; an error of just one millionth of a second could place you hundreds of feet away.","questions":[{"question":"In the passage, the word pinpoint means?","choices":["draw a picture of","find the exact position of","send a message to","make something smaller and sharper"],"answer":"find the exact position of"},{"question":"Why does the system need extremely precise clocks?","choices":["The clocks keep the satellites from falling","Satellites must know when to switch off at night","Tiny timing errors can cause big location errors","Phones use the clocks to set their alarms"],"answer":"Tiny timing errors can cause big location errors"},{"question":"How many satellites must a receiver hear from?","choices":["exactly one","at least ten","no more than two","at least four"],"answer":"at least four"}],"photo":"images/flashcards/map.jpg"},{"text":"Every December, Abuela Rosa invites the whole family to her kitchen to make tamales. This year, Sofia was finally old enough to help. Abuela showed her how to spread a thin layer of masa, a soft corn dough, across a soaked corn husk. Sofia's first tamales were lumpy and lopsided, and a few fell apart in the steamer. Abuela only laughed and said her own first tamales had looked even worse. By the end of the afternoon, Sofia's tamales were neat and tidy, and she stacked them proudly on the platter beside Abuela's.","questions":[{"question":"In the passage, the word lopsided means?","choices":["wrapped very tightly with extra string","sweet and full of flavor","cooked for too long","uneven, with one side lower or bigger"],"answer":"uneven, with one side lower or bigger"},{"question":"Why did Abuela tell Sofia about her own first tamales?","choices":["to tell her to stop helping","to encourage Sofia not to give up","to explain why the steamer broke","to show that she had always been the better cook"],"answer":"to encourage Sofia not to give up"},{"question":"According to the passage, what is masa?","choices":["a spicy red sauce","a kind of steamer","a soaked corn husk","a soft corn dough"],"answer":"a soft corn dough"}],"image_prompt":"A warm, simple flat-style children's-book illustration for a kids' reading app. Scene: Every December, Abuela Rosa invites the whole family to her kitchen to make tamales. Focus on: a grandmother and girl making tamales. Bright friendly colors, soft rounded shapes, no text or letters anywhere in the image, square 1:1 aspect ratio, cheerful and inviting for young readers."},{"text":"Have you ever touched a doorknob after shuffling across a carpet and felt a tiny zap? That shock is static electricity. Everything is made of atoms, which contain particles with electric charges. When two materials rub together, tiny particles called electrons can move from one material to the other. Your body builds up extra charge as you walk. When your finger gets close to a metal doorknob, the extra electrons suddenly jump across the gap, and you feel a spark. Static shocks happen more often in winter because dry air allows charge to build up instead of leaking away.","questions":[{"question":"In the passage, the word shuffling means?","choices":["running as fast as possible","jumping up and down on both feet","walking while dragging your feet","standing perfectly still"],"answer":"walking while dragging your feet"},{"question":"Why do static shocks happen more often in winter?","choices":["Dry air lets charge build up","People wear heavier shoes in winter","Carpets are cleaned less often","Cold doorknobs hold more electricity"],"answer":"Dry air lets charge build up"},{"question":"What jumps from your finger to the doorknob?","choices":["specks of dust","extra electrons","bits of carpet","tiny drops of water"],"answer":"extra electrons"}],"image_prompt":"A warm, simple flat-style children's-book illustration for a kids' reading app. Scene: Have you ever touched a doorknob after shuffling across a carpet and felt a tiny zap? That shock is static electricity. Focus on: a child touching a doorknob with a tiny static spark. Bright friendly colors, soft rounded shapes, no text or letters anywhere in the image, square 1:1 aspect ratio, cheerful and inviting for young readers."},{"text":"In the late 1800s, farmers across the American South grew cotton year after year in the same fields. Cotton drained nutrients from the soil, and harvests grew smaller and smaller. George Washington Carver, a scientist and teacher at the Tuskegee Institute in Alabama, urged farmers to rotate their crops, planting peanuts in some seasons instead of cotton. Peanuts, like beans, add nitrogen back into the soil, which helped restore the fields. To make sure farmers could earn money from their new crop, Carver developed hundreds of uses for peanuts, from foods to dyes. Although many people believe he invented peanut butter, he did not; his greatest contribution was helping farmers protect their land.","questions":[{"question":"In the passage, the word restore means?","choices":["cover with a thin layer","bring back to a good condition","sell at a higher price","dig up and carry away to another field"],"answer":"bring back to a good condition"},{"question":"What was Carver's most important contribution, according to the passage?","choices":["growing more cotton than any other farmer","helping farmers protect and improve their soil","inventing peanut butter and selling it to American families","building the Tuskegee Institute in Alabama"],"answer":"helping farmers protect and improve their soil"},{"question":"Why did cotton harvests grow smaller over time?","choices":["Cotton drained nutrients from the soil","Insects ate the peanuts nearby","Farmers planted too few seeds","There was too much rain each year"],"answer":"Cotton drained nutrients from the soil"}],"image_prompt":"A warm, simple flat-style children's-book illustration for a kids' reading app. Scene: In the late 1800s, farmers across the American South grew cotton year after year in the same fields. Focus on: peanut plants growing in a farm field. Bright friendly colors, soft rounded shapes, no text or letters anywhere in the image, square 1:1 aspect ratio, cheerful and inviting for young readers."},{"text":"When the Parks' beagle, Pepper, slipped out of the backyard gate, ten-year-old June felt panic rising in her chest. Instead of running around the block blindly, she took a breath and made a plan. She printed a flyer with Pepper's photo and her family's phone number, and her neighbors helped tape copies to lampposts. Mr. Okafor, who owned the corner bakery, posted one in his window. That evening the phone rang: a jogger had spotted Pepper napping on the bakery's back step, probably drawn by the smell of fresh bread.","questions":[{"question":"In the passage, the word blindly means?","choices":["without a plan or careful thought","with her eyes tightly closed","very quickly and loudly","while carrying a bright flashlight in the dark"],"answer":"without a plan or careful thought"},{"question":"Why was Pepper probably at the bakery?","choices":["June had left her there earlier","Mr. Okafor had taken her there","The smell of fresh bread attracted her","She was following the jogger"],"answer":"The smell of fresh bread attracted her"},{"question":"What did June do first after Pepper ran away?","choices":["She called the police","She ran around the block","She waited beside the open gate all night","She took a breath and made a plan"],"answer":"She took a breath and made a plan"}],"image_prompt":"A warm, simple flat-style children's-book illustration for a kids' reading app. Scene: When the Parks' beagle, Pepper, slipped out of the backyard gate, ten-year-old June felt panic rising in her chest. Focus on: a beagle napping on a bakery's back step. Bright friendly colors, soft rounded shapes, no text or letters anywhere in the image, square 1:1 aspect ratio, cheerful and inviting for young readers."},{"text":"For seventy-six years after its discovery in 1930, Pluto was known as the ninth planet. Then astronomers began finding other icy objects in the same distant region, some nearly as large as Pluto. In 2006, scientists at the International Astronomical Union agreed on a definition: a planet must orbit the sun, be massive enough to become round, and clear other objects from its orbital path. Pluto meets the first two rules but not the third, so it was reclassified as a dwarf planet. In 2015, the New Horizons spacecraft flew past Pluto and revealed mountains of ice and a giant heart-shaped plain.","questions":[{"question":"In the passage, the word reclassified means?","choices":["removed from the sky","made larger and brighter","placed into a different group","discovered a second time"],"answer":"placed into a different group"},{"question":"Why is Pluto no longer called a planet?","choices":["It is too far away to study","It has not cleared other objects from its path","It does not orbit the sun","It is not round like the other planets"],"answer":"It has not cleared other objects from its path"},{"question":"What did New Horizons find on Pluto?","choices":["forests of tall trees","a heart-shaped plain","oceans of liquid water","a ring like Saturn's"],"answer":"a heart-shaped plain"}],"image_prompt":"A warm, simple flat-style children's-book illustration for a kids' reading app. Scene: For seventy-six years after its discovery in 1930, Pluto was known as the ninth planet. Focus on: Pluto with its heart-shaped plain. Bright friendly colors, soft rounded shapes, no text or letters anywhere in the image, square 1:1 aspect ratio, cheerful and inviting for young readers."},{"text":"Sand dunes are hills of sand shaped by the wind. When wind blows across a desert or beach, it lifts loose grains and bounces them along the ground. Where something blocks the wind, such as a rock or a bush, grains pile up. Over time, the pile grows into a dune with a gentle slope on the side facing the wind and a steep slope on the sheltered side. Because wind keeps moving the grains, many dunes slowly creep forward, sometimes several feet each year. Some dunes even make a low humming or booming sound when sand slides down their slopes.","questions":[{"question":"In the passage, the word creep means?","choices":["shine brightly","disappear suddenly","jump high","move slowly"],"answer":"move slowly"},{"question":"Which sentence best summarizes the passage?","choices":["Sand dunes form only on the beaches of oceans","Rocks and bushes are made from piles of sand","Dunes stay in the same place for thousands of years","Wind builds sand dunes and keeps them slowly moving"],"answer":"Wind builds sand dunes and keeps them slowly moving"},{"question":"What can make sand begin to pile up?","choices":["a sudden earthquake","a heavy rainstorm","waves from the ocean","something that blocks the wind"],"answer":"something that blocks the wind"}],"image_prompt":"A warm, simple flat-style children's-book illustration for a kids' reading app. Scene: Sand dunes are hills of sand shaped by the wind. Focus on: wind blowing sand over desert dunes. Bright friendly colors, soft rounded shapes, no text or letters anywhere in the image, square 1:1 aspect ratio, cheerful and inviting for young readers."},{"text":"During recess, Elijah accidentally kicked his soccer ball into the window of the art room, leaving a long crack across the glass. Nobody else had seen it happen. For the rest of the afternoon, he felt a heavy knot in his stomach and could barely focus on his spelling words. Finally, he walked to the principal's office and explained what had happened. Ms. Rivera thanked him for his honesty and asked him to help the custodian measure the window for a new pane. Walking home, Elijah noticed that the knot in his stomach was gone.","questions":[{"question":"In the passage, the word honesty means?","choices":["playing a sport well","feeling hungry","being very fast","telling the truth"],"answer":"telling the truth"},{"question":"Why did the knot in Elijah's stomach go away?","choices":["The custodian told him the window was never really cracked","He won his soccer game at recess","He ate a big snack after school","He stopped worrying after telling the truth"],"answer":"He stopped worrying after telling the truth"},{"question":"What did Ms. Rivera ask Elijah to do?","choices":["stay inside at recess","clean the art room","help measure the window","write a letter"],"answer":"help measure the window"}],"image_prompt":"A warm, simple flat-style children's-book illustration for a kids' reading app. Scene: During recess, Elijah accidentally kicked his soccer ball into the window of the art room, leaving a long crack across the glass. Focus on: a boy talking honestly with a school principal. Bright friendly colors, soft rounded shapes, no text or letters anywhere in the image, square 1:1 aspect ratio, cheerful and inviting for young readers."},{"text":"Mangroves are trees that grow along tropical coastlines, where few other trees can survive. Their roots arch above the muddy ground like stilts, holding the trees steady as tides rise and fall twice a day. Mangroves can tolerate salt water; some filter salt out through their roots, while others push extra salt out through their leaves. The tangled roots also create a nursery for young fish, crabs, and shrimp, which hide among them from larger predators. During storms, mangrove forests act as a natural barrier, slowing waves and protecting the coast.","questions":[{"question":"In the passage, the word tolerate means?","choices":["turn into","run away from","survive in spite of","grow much taller than"],"answer":"survive in spite of"},{"question":"What is the main idea of the passage?","choices":["Mangroves grow best in cold, dry mountain regions","Mangrove roots grow deep underground where no one can see them","Mangroves are specially suited to coasts and help protect them","Mangroves are harmful to fish and other sea animals"],"answer":"Mangroves are specially suited to coasts and help protect them"},{"question":"How do mangrove forests help during storms?","choices":["They slow waves and protect the coast","They warn animals to swim away","They soak up all the rainwater","They make the wind stop blowing"],"answer":"They slow waves and protect the coast"}],"image_prompt":"A warm, simple flat-style children's-book illustration for a kids' reading app. Scene: Mangroves are trees that grow along tropical coastlines, where few other trees can survive. Focus on: mangrove trees with stilt roots along a coast. Bright friendly colors, soft rounded shapes, no text or letters anywhere in the image, square 1:1 aspect ratio, cheerful and inviting for young readers."},{"text":"Hailstones are balls of ice that fall from powerful thunderstorms, usually in spring and summer. Inside a storm cloud, strong upward winds called updrafts carry raindrops high into air that is far below freezing. The drops freeze into small pellets of ice. As they fall, they collect a coating of water, then get swept upward again, where that water freezes into a new layer. A hailstone may travel up and down many times, growing like an onion with rings. When it becomes too heavy for the updraft to hold, it finally falls to the ground.","questions":[{"question":"In the passage, the word coating means?","choices":["a hole in the middle","a warm and heavy winter jacket","a thin layer covering something","a loud clap of thunder in the cloud"],"answer":"a thin layer covering something"},{"question":"Why does a hailstone finally fall to the ground?","choices":["The cloud runs out of water","The sun melts the updraft","The storm turns into snow","It gets too heavy for the updraft to hold"],"answer":"It gets too heavy for the updraft to hold"},{"question":"Why does the passage compare a hailstone to an onion?","choices":["Both have a strong smell","Both grow underground","Both are made of many layers","Both can be found in a kitchen freezer"],"answer":"Both are made of many layers"}],"image_prompt":"A warm, simple flat-style children's-book illustration for a kids' reading app. Scene: Hailstones are balls of ice that fall from powerful thunderstorms, usually in spring and summer. Focus on: layered hailstones falling from a storm cloud. Bright friendly colors, soft rounded shapes, no text or letters anywhere in the image, square 1:1 aspect ratio, cheerful and inviting for young readers."},{"text":"Before 1825, moving goods from the Great Lakes to the Atlantic coast meant long, bumpy trips by wagon over rough roads. The Erie Canal changed that. Stretching about 363 miles across New York, from the Hudson River to Lake Erie, the human-made waterway allowed flat boats to carry heavy loads cheaply. Mules walked along towpaths beside the canal, pulling the boats with ropes. Because the land rose and fell, workers built locks, chambers that raised or lowered boats like water elevators. The cost of shipping dropped sharply, and New York City grew into the nation's busiest port.","questions":[{"question":"In the passage, the word chambers means?","choices":["teams of hardworking mules","large cargo boats","enclosed spaces or rooms","tall wooden bridges"],"answer":"enclosed spaces or rooms"},{"question":"What was one important effect of the Erie Canal?","choices":["Wagons became more popular than boats","Mules were no longer needed","Lake Erie dried up completely","Shipping goods became much cheaper"],"answer":"Shipping goods became much cheaper"},{"question":"What job did the mules do?","choices":["built the locks","carried mail","dug the canal","pulled the boats with ropes"],"answer":"pulled the boats with ropes"}],"image_prompt":"A warm, simple flat-style children's-book illustration for a kids' reading app. Scene: Before 1825, moving goods from the Great Lakes to the Atlantic coast meant long, bumpy trips by wagon over rough roads. Focus on: a mule pulling a canal boat along a towpath. Bright friendly colors, soft rounded shapes, no text or letters anywhere in the image, square 1:1 aspect ratio, cheerful and inviting for young readers."},{"text":"For the science fair, Nadia wondered whether plants grow better with music. She planted six bean seeds in identical cups with the same soil. Three cups sat near a speaker that played soft music each afternoon, and three sat in a quiet room. She gave every cup the same amount of water and sunlight. After three weeks, the plants in both groups were nearly the same height. Nadia was surprised, but her teacher pointed out that a result that disproves a guess is still valuable. Nadia's poster explained what she learned and suggested testing louder music next time.","questions":[{"question":"In the passage, the word identical means?","choices":["full of holes","very large","brightly colored","exactly alike"],"answer":"exactly alike"},{"question":"Why did Nadia give every cup the same water and sunlight?","choices":["so only the music would be different","so the speaker on the table would never get wet","so she could use less water","so the plants would grow very tall"],"answer":"so only the music would be different"},{"question":"What did the teacher say about Nadia's result?","choices":["It was too small","It must be wrong","It was a failure","It was still valuable"],"answer":"It was still valuable"}],"image_prompt":"A warm, simple flat-style children's-book illustration for a kids' reading app. Scene: For the science fair, Nadia wondered whether plants grow better with music. Focus on: bean plants in identical cups near a music speaker. Bright friendly colors, soft rounded shapes, no text or letters anywhere in the image, square 1:1 aspect ratio, cheerful and inviting for young readers."},{"text":"Look closely at almost any package in a grocery store and you will find a barcode, a pattern of black bars and white spaces of different widths. Each pattern stands for a number that identifies the product. When a cashier passes the package over a scanner, a beam of light sweeps across the code. Black bars absorb the light while white spaces reflect it, and a sensor reads the pattern of reflections. A computer instantly looks up the number to find the item's name and price. The first product scanned this way, in 1974, was a pack of chewing gum.","questions":[{"question":"In the passage, the word absorb means?","choices":["soak up","break apart","bounce back","make louder"],"answer":"soak up"},{"question":"What does the computer do after the scanner reads the code?","choices":["prints a new barcode","cleans the scanner glass","looks up the item's name and price","turns off the beam of light"],"answer":"looks up the item's name and price"},{"question":"What is the passage mostly about?","choices":["why some packages are black and white","how barcodes and scanners work","how grocery stores choose prices","why chewing gum was invented"],"answer":"how barcodes and scanners work"}],"image_prompt":"A warm, simple flat-style children's-book illustration for a kids' reading app. Scene: Look closely at almost any package in a grocery store and you will find a barcode, a pattern of black bars and white spaces of different widths. Focus on: a barcode being scanned at a grocery checkout. Bright friendly colors, soft rounded shapes, no text or letters anywhere in the image, square 1:1 aspect ratio, cheerful and inviting for young readers."},{"text":"Lena and Maya had planned their bake sale for weeks, but on the morning of the sale they argued about everything: the prices, the sign, even which cookies to bake. Maya stomped off, and Lena sat alone behind the table. After an hour without a single customer, Lena realized she missed her friend more than she cared about the cookies. She walked to Maya's house and apologized. Maya admitted she had been stubborn too. Together they painted a bright new sign, and by evening they had sold every last cookie.","questions":[{"question":"In the passage, the word stubborn means?","choices":["unwilling to change your mind","very tired and sleepy","very good at baking cookies and bread","quiet and shy"],"answer":"unwilling to change your mind"},{"question":"What is the main lesson of the story?","choices":["Friendship matters more than small disagreements","It is always better to work alone than with others","Bake sales only work with a bright sign","Customers care most about the price of cookies"],"answer":"Friendship matters more than small disagreements"},{"question":"Why did Lena go to Maya's house?","choices":["She needed more cookies to sell","She forgot the sign there","She wanted to ask about the prices","She missed her friend and wanted to make up"],"answer":"She missed her friend and wanted to make up"}],"image_prompt":"A warm, simple flat-style children's-book illustration for a kids' reading app. Scene: Lena and Maya had planned their bake sale for weeks, but on the morning of the sale they argued about everything: the prices, the sign, even which cookies to bake. Focus on: two friends at a bake sale table with cookies. Bright friendly colors, soft rounded shapes, no text or letters anywhere in the image, square 1:1 aspect ratio, cheerful and inviting for young readers."},{"text":"Every time you breathe in, air travels down your windpipe and into your lungs, where it flows through smaller and smaller tubes. At the ends of these tubes are millions of tiny air sacs called alveoli. Each sac is wrapped in tiny blood vessels. Oxygen passes from the air sacs into the blood, which carries it to every part of your body. At the same time, carbon dioxide, a waste gas, moves from the blood into the sacs so you can breathe it out. Exercise strengthens the muscles that help you breathe, which is one reason athletes can run long distances without tiring as quickly.","questions":[{"question":"In the passage, the word waste means?","choices":["a kind of food","something the body does not need","a large tube that carries fresh blood","something very valuable"],"answer":"something the body does not need"},{"question":"What moves from the blood into the air sacs?","choices":["drops of water","fresh oxygen gas","bits of food","carbon dioxide"],"answer":"carbon dioxide"},{"question":"Why might athletes run long distances without tiring as quickly?","choices":["They have more air sacs than other people","Their windpipes are much shorter","Exercise has strengthened their breathing muscles","They breathe only carbon dioxide"],"answer":"Exercise has strengthened their breathing muscles"}]},{"text":"In the 1700s, books in the American colonies were expensive, and most had to be shipped from England. In 1731, Benjamin Franklin and a group of friends in Philadelphia came up with a clever solution. Each member paid a fee to join, and the money was pooled to buy books that everyone could borrow. This group, called the Library Company of Philadelphia, became one of the first lending libraries in America. The idea spread to other towns. Today, public libraries across the country lend books, movies, and computers for free, carrying on the idea that knowledge should be shared.","questions":[{"question":"In the passage, the word pooled means?","choices":["spent on swimming","combined into one shared amount","poured into water","saved by one person in a secret box"],"answer":"combined into one shared amount"},{"question":"What is the main idea of the passage?","choices":["Libraries today only lend books to paying members","Sharing resources helped make books available to more people","Books were cheaper in the colonies than in England","Benjamin Franklin wrote most of the books in early libraries"],"answer":"Sharing resources helped make books available to more people"},{"question":"Why was it hard to get books in the colonies?","choices":["People were not allowed to read","Books were only written in French","There were no printers in the world","They were expensive and shipped from England"],"answer":"They were expensive and shipped from England"}],"image_prompt":"A warm, simple flat-style children's-book illustration for a kids' reading app. Scene: In the 1700s, books in the American colonies were expensive, and most had to be shipped from England. Focus on: colonial readers borrowing books from an early library. Bright friendly colors, soft rounded shapes, no text or letters anywhere in the image, square 1:1 aspect ratio, cheerful and inviting for young readers."},{"text":"Snow leopards live in the rugged mountains of Central Asia, at heights where the air is thin and temperatures drop far below freezing. Their thick, smoky-gray fur, spotted with dark rosettes, keeps them warm and helps them blend into rocky slopes. Wide, furry paws act like natural snowshoes, spreading their weight so they do not sink into deep snow. A snow leopard's long tail, nearly as long as its body, helps it balance on narrow ledges and can be wrapped around its face like a scarf while it sleeps. Because they are so well hidden, people sometimes call them the ghosts of the mountains.","questions":[{"question":"In the passage, the word rugged means?","choices":["soft and flat","rough and uneven","crowded and busy","warm and sunny"],"answer":"rough and uneven"},{"question":"Why are snow leopards called the ghosts of the mountains?","choices":["They only come out at night","They are very hard to see","They are white like ghosts","They make spooky sounds"],"answer":"They are very hard to see"},{"question":"How do wide paws help snow leopards?","choices":["They keep them from sinking into snow","They help them swim across icy mountain rivers","They help them climb tall trees","They help them dig deep burrows"],"answer":"They keep them from sinking into snow"}],"image_prompt":"A warm, simple flat-style children's-book illustration for a kids' reading app. Scene: Snow leopards live in the rugged mountains of Central Asia, at heights where the air is thin and temperatures drop far below freezing. Focus on: a snow leopard on a rocky mountain ledge. Bright friendly colors, soft rounded shapes, no text or letters anywhere in the image, square 1:1 aspect ratio, cheerful and inviting for young readers."},{"text":"Tariq volunteered to write an article for the school newspaper about the cafeteria staff. He assumed the job was simple: serve lunch and clean up. But when he interviewed Ms. Delgado, the head cook, he learned that her team arrived at six every morning, prepared meals for four hundred students, and adjusted recipes for children with food allergies. Tariq's article, titled The Lunch Heroes, surprised many readers. The next week, several classes made thank-you cards for the cafeteria team, and Ms. Delgado hung them all on the kitchen wall.","questions":[{"question":"In the passage, the word assumed means?","choices":["asked a question","believed without knowing for sure","cooked a meal","wrote down carefully"],"answer":"believed without knowing for sure"},{"question":"Why did Tariq's article most likely surprise many readers?","choices":["They did not know Tariq could write","They thought lunch was served at six","It showed how hard the staff really worked","They believed the school did not have a cafeteria"],"answer":"It showed how hard the staff really worked"},{"question":"How did classes respond to the article?","choices":["They wrote new recipes","They cooked lunch","They visited a restaurant","They made thank-you cards"],"answer":"They made thank-you cards"}],"image_prompt":"A warm, simple flat-style children's-book illustration for a kids' reading app. Scene: Tariq volunteered to write an article for the school newspaper about the cafeteria staff. Focus on: a student interviewing a school cafeteria cook. Bright friendly colors, soft rounded shapes, no text or letters anywhere in the image, square 1:1 aspect ratio, cheerful and inviting for young readers."},{"text":"Limestone caves form over thousands of years. Rainwater picks up a gas called carbon dioxide from the air and soil, which turns the water slightly acidic. As this water seeps into cracks in limestone rock, it slowly dissolves the rock, widening the cracks into tunnels and rooms. Inside the cave, water dripping from the ceiling leaves behind tiny bits of minerals. Over centuries, these deposits build icicle-shaped stalactites that hang down and stalagmites that rise up from the floor. When the two meet, they form a column.","questions":[{"question":"In the passage, the word seeps means?","choices":["jumps quickly into the air","freezes into solid sheets of ice overnight","stays still in one place","flows slowly through small openings"],"answer":"flows slowly through small openings"},{"question":"What forms when a stalactite and a stalagmite meet?","choices":["a tunnel","a crack","a column","a waterfall"],"answer":"a column"},{"question":"What causes the limestone to dissolve?","choices":["animals digging","slightly acidic rainwater","heat from the sun","strong winds"],"answer":"slightly acidic rainwater"}],"image_prompt":"A warm, simple flat-style children's-book illustration for a kids' reading app. Scene: Limestone caves form over thousands of years. Focus on: stalactites and stalagmites inside a limestone cave. Bright friendly colors, soft rounded shapes, no text or letters anywhere in the image, square 1:1 aspect ratio, cheerful and inviting for young readers."}]},"synonyms":{"easy":{"chuckle":"laugh","rush":"dash","soggy":"soaked","grab":"snatch","plump":"chubby","sturdy":"solid","cozy":"snug","stroll":"wander"},"hard":{"tenacious":"persistent","lavish":"extravagant","ominous":"threatening","placid":"peaceful","feasible":"possible","vibrant":"lively","trivial":"unimportant"}},"antonyms":{"easy":{"crooked":"straight","rough":"smooth","cheap":"expensive","clumsy":"graceful","busy":"idle","together":"apart","dim":"bright","forward":"backward"},"hard":{"reckless":"cautious","obscure":"famous","triumph":"defeat","affluent":"impoverished","rigid":"flexible","sparse":"dense","mandatory":"optional"}},"rhymes":{"easy":[["map","cap","tap","nap","lap"],["team","beam","cream","dream","steam"],["leg","peg","beg","egg"],["gum","drum","plum","hum","come"]],"hard":[["tumble","rumble","grumble","mumble","humble"],["candle","handle","sandal","scandal","vandal"],["winner","dinner","thinner","spinner","beginner"],["winter","splinter","printer","sprinter"]]},"analogies":[["feather","bird","scale","fish"],["glove","hand","hat","head"],["chef","kitchen","pilot","cockpit"],["cow","milk","hen","egg"],["pages","book","keys","piano"],["calf","cow","lamb","sheep"],["paw","dog","hoof","horse"],["thirsty","drink","tired","sleep"]]},{"id":"2026-10-25-weekly-reading","released":"2026-10-25","kind":"weekly","reading_passages":{"0":[{"text":"Farmer Jo drives a green tractor. It has big black wheels. The tractor pulls a cart full of hay to the barn.","questions":[{"question":"What color is the tractor?","choices":["red","yellow","blue","green"],"answer":"green"},{"question":"What does the tractor pull?","choices":["a big school bus","a cart of hay","a little red boat","a long blue train"],"answer":"a cart of hay"}],"image_prompt":"A warm, simple flat-style children's-book illustration for a kids' reading app. Scene: Farmer Jo drives a green tractor. Focus on: a green farm tractor pulling a cart of hay. Bright friendly colors, soft rounded shapes, no text or letters anywhere in the image, square 1:1 aspect ratio, cheerful and inviting for young readers."},{"text":"Nia plays a xylophone. She taps the bars with two sticks. The long bars sound low. The short bars sound high. Ding, ding!","questions":[{"question":"What does Nia tap the bars with?","choices":["a big spoon","a feather","two sticks","her shoes"],"answer":"two sticks"},{"question":"How do the short bars sound?","choices":["low","high","quiet","sad"],"answer":"high"}],"photo":"images/flashcards/xylophone.jpg"},{"text":"A llama lives on a farm. It has soft, fluffy wool and long ears. The llama hums when it is happy.","questions":[{"question":"What does the llama do when it is happy?","choices":["It barks.","It roars.","It hums.","It quacks."],"answer":"It hums."},{"question":"What is the llama's wool like?","choices":["smooth and shiny","hard and sharp","soft and fluffy","wet and cold"],"answer":"soft and fluffy"}],"image_prompt":"A warm, simple flat-style children's-book illustration for a kids' reading app. Scene: A llama lives on a farm. Focus on: a fluffy llama on a farm. Bright friendly colors, soft rounded shapes, no text or letters anywhere in the image, square 1:1 aspect ratio, cheerful and inviting for young readers."},{"text":"The tiger is a big cat. It has orange fur with black stripes. The tiger likes to swim in cool water on hot days.","questions":[{"question":"What color are the tiger's stripes?","choices":["green","black","white","purple"],"answer":"black"},{"question":"What does the tiger like to do on hot days?","choices":["climb a tree","swim in cool water","run up a hill","eat ice cream"],"answer":"swim in cool water"}],"photo":"images/flashcards/tiger.jpg"},{"text":"The ostrich is a very big bird. It cannot fly. But it can run very fast on its two long legs!","questions":[{"question":"What can the ostrich NOT do?","choices":["walk","fly","eat","run"],"answer":"fly"},{"question":"How many legs does the ostrich have?","choices":["six","four","eight","two"],"answer":"two"}],"image_prompt":"A warm, simple flat-style children's-book illustration for a kids' reading app. Scene: The ostrich is a very big bird. Focus on: an ostrich running on its long legs. Bright friendly colors, soft rounded shapes, no text or letters anywhere in the image, square 1:1 aspect ratio, cheerful and inviting for young readers."},{"text":"A skunk has black and white fur. It has a big, bushy tail. If a skunk is scared, it makes a bad smell. Stay away!","questions":[{"question":"What colors is the skunk's fur?","choices":["gray and orange","red and blue","black and white","brown and pink"],"answer":"black and white"},{"question":"What does a skunk do when it is scared?","choices":["hides in a box","sings a sweet song","jumps up high","makes a bad smell"],"answer":"makes a bad smell"}],"image_prompt":"A warm, simple flat-style children's-book illustration for a kids' reading app. Scene: A skunk has black and white fur. Focus on: a black and white skunk with a bushy tail. Bright friendly colors, soft rounded shapes, no text or letters anywhere in the image, square 1:1 aspect ratio, cheerful and inviting for young readers."},{"text":"Kai eats noodles for lunch. The noodles are long and slippery. Kai slurps them up with a fork. Yum!","questions":[{"question":"What does Kai eat for lunch?","choices":["pancakes","noodles","pizza","carrots"],"answer":"noodles"},{"question":"What does Kai use to eat?","choices":["a fork","his hands","a cup","a spoon"],"answer":"a fork"}],"image_prompt":"A warm, simple flat-style children's-book illustration for a kids' reading app. Scene: Kai eats noodles for lunch. Focus on: a child slurping long noodles with a fork. Bright friendly colors, soft rounded shapes, no text or letters anywhere in the image, square 1:1 aspect ratio, cheerful and inviting for young readers."},{"text":"Dad puts yogurt in a bowl. Ella adds red berries on top. The yogurt is cool and creamy.","questions":[{"question":"What does Ella add on top?","choices":["nuts","popcorn","red berries","green peas"],"answer":"red berries"},{"question":"How is the yogurt?","choices":["hot and spicy","hard and crunchy","dry and salty","cool and creamy"],"answer":"cool and creamy"}],"image_prompt":"A warm, simple flat-style children's-book illustration for a kids' reading app. Scene: Dad puts yogurt in a bowl. Focus on: a bowl of yogurt topped with red berries. Bright friendly colors, soft rounded shapes, no text or letters anywhere in the image, square 1:1 aspect ratio, cheerful and inviting for young readers."},{"text":"Grandpa pushes a wheelbarrow. It is full of dirt for the garden. The wheelbarrow has one wheel in the front.","questions":[{"question":"What is in the wheelbarrow?","choices":["water","toys","snow","dirt"],"answer":"dirt"},{"question":"How many wheels does the wheelbarrow have?","choices":["two","four","one","three"],"answer":"one"}],"image_prompt":"A warm, simple flat-style children's-book illustration for a kids' reading app. Scene: Grandpa pushes a wheelbarrow. Focus on: a grandpa pushing a one-wheeled wheelbarrow of dirt. Bright friendly colors, soft rounded shapes, no text or letters anywhere in the image, square 1:1 aspect ratio, cheerful and inviting for young readers."},{"text":"Lucy sweeps the floor with a broom. Swish, swish! She sweeps the crumbs into a pile. Now the floor is clean.","questions":[{"question":"What does Lucy sweep with?","choices":["a pillow","a broom","a mop","a book"],"answer":"a broom"},{"question":"What does she sweep into a pile?","choices":["the crumbs","the leaves","her socks","the toys"],"answer":"the crumbs"}],"photo":"images/flashcards/broom.jpg"},{"text":"Owen finds a pinecone under a tall tree. It is brown and bumpy. He puts it in his basket to show his class.","questions":[{"question":"Where does Owen find the pinecone?","choices":["on the slide","under a tall tree","in his bed","inside the car"],"answer":"under a tall tree"},{"question":"Where does he put the pinecone?","choices":["in a box","in his soup","in his basket","in the sink"],"answer":"in his basket"}],"image_prompt":"A warm, simple flat-style children's-book illustration for a kids' reading app. Scene: Owen finds a pinecone under a tall tree. Focus on: a child holding a brown pinecone under a tall tree. Bright friendly colors, soft rounded shapes, no text or letters anywhere in the image, square 1:1 aspect ratio, cheerful and inviting for young readers."},{"text":"Ava folds a paper boat. She puts it in a puddle. The boat floats! Then the wind pushes it along.","questions":[{"question":"What does Ava fold?","choices":["a big kite","a blanket","a paper hat","a paper boat"],"answer":"a paper boat"},{"question":"What pushes the boat?","choices":["a fish","the wind","a duck","a dog"],"answer":"the wind"}],"image_prompt":"A warm, simple flat-style children's-book illustration for a kids' reading app. Scene: Ava folds a paper boat. Focus on: a folded paper boat floating in a puddle. Bright friendly colors, soft rounded shapes, no text or letters anywhere in the image, square 1:1 aspect ratio, cheerful and inviting for young readers."},{"text":"We eat watermelon at the picnic. It is green on the outside and red on the inside. It has little black seeds.","questions":[{"question":"What color is the watermelon on the inside?","choices":["red","blue","green","yellow"],"answer":"red"},{"question":"What color are the seeds?","choices":["orange","black","white","pink"],"answer":"black"}],"image_prompt":"A warm, simple flat-style children's-book illustration for a kids' reading app. Scene: We eat watermelon at the picnic. Focus on: slices of red watermelon with black seeds at a picnic. Bright friendly colors, soft rounded shapes, no text or letters anywhere in the image, square 1:1 aspect ratio, cheerful and inviting for young readers."},{"text":"Max rolls a big ball down the lane. Crash! The ball knocks down all ten pins. Max cheers and jumps up and down.","questions":[{"question":"How many pins fall down?","choices":["ten","five","six","two"],"answer":"ten"},{"question":"What does Max do after the pins fall?","choices":["cheers and jumps","cries and sits","goes to sleep","eats a big snack"],"answer":"cheers and jumps"}],"image_prompt":"A warm, simple flat-style children's-book illustration for a kids' reading app. Scene: Max rolls a big ball down the lane. Focus on: a bowling ball knocking down ten pins. Bright friendly colors, soft rounded shapes, no text or letters anywhere in the image, square 1:1 aspect ratio, cheerful and inviting for young readers."},{"text":"A windmill stands on the hill. When the wind blows, its arms go round and round. It helps pump water for the farm.","questions":[{"question":"What makes the windmill's arms go round?","choices":["a dog","the wind","the moon","the rain"],"answer":"the wind"},{"question":"What does the windmill help pump?","choices":["paint","water","milk","juice"],"answer":"water"}],"image_prompt":"A warm, simple flat-style children's-book illustration for a kids' reading app. Scene: A windmill stands on the hill. Focus on: a windmill on a hill. Bright friendly colors, soft rounded shapes, no text or letters anywhere in the image, square 1:1 aspect ratio, cheerful and inviting for young readers."},{"text":"Rosa makes a bowl out of clay. She rolls it, squeezes it, and shapes it with her fingers. Then she paints it blue.","questions":[{"question":"What does Rosa make?","choices":["a car","a hat","a spoon","a bowl"],"answer":"a bowl"},{"question":"What color does she paint it?","choices":["red","blue","pink","green"],"answer":"blue"}],"image_prompt":"A warm, simple flat-style children's-book illustration for a kids' reading app. Scene: Rosa makes a bowl out of clay. Focus on: a child shaping a clay bowl with her fingers. Bright friendly colors, soft rounded shapes, no text or letters anywhere in the image, square 1:1 aspect ratio, cheerful and inviting for young readers."},{"text":"It is taco night! Mom fills the shells with beans, cheese, and lettuce. Leo adds a little salsa. Crunch!","questions":[{"question":"What night is it?","choices":["pizza night","soup night","taco night","movie night"],"answer":"taco night"},{"question":"What does Leo add?","choices":["a little salsa","honey","some ice cream","a pickle"],"answer":"a little salsa"}],"image_prompt":"A warm, simple flat-style children's-book illustration for a kids' reading app. Scene: It is taco night! Mom fills the shells with beans, cheese, and lettuce. Focus on: tacos filled with beans, cheese and lettuce. Bright friendly colors, soft rounded shapes, no text or letters anywhere in the image, square 1:1 aspect ratio, cheerful and inviting for young readers."},{"text":"Ben spills juice on Kim's drawing. Oops! Ben says, \"I am sorry.\" He gets a towel. Then he helps Kim paint a new picture. Kim smiles.","questions":[{"question":"What does Ben say?","choices":["Me first.","Go away.","Look at me.","I am sorry."],"answer":"I am sorry."},{"question":"What does Kim do at the end?","choices":["She hides.","She yells.","She cries.","She smiles."],"answer":"She smiles."}],"image_prompt":"A warm, simple flat-style children's-book illustration for a kids' reading app. Scene: Ben spills juice on Kim's drawing. Focus on: a boy helping a girl wipe spilled juice off her drawing. Bright friendly colors, soft rounded shapes, no text or letters anywhere in the image, square 1:1 aspect ratio, cheerful and inviting for young readers."},{"text":"A big hot air balloon floats in the sky. It is red and yellow. People ride in a basket under the balloon and wave.","questions":[{"question":"What colors is the balloon?","choices":["blue and green","red and yellow","black and white","pink and purple"],"answer":"red and yellow"},{"question":"Where do people ride?","choices":["on a horse","in a car","in a basket","on the roof"],"answer":"in a basket"}],"image_prompt":"A warm, simple flat-style children's-book illustration for a kids' reading app. Scene: A big hot air balloon floats in the sky. Focus on: a red and yellow hot air balloon with a basket. Bright friendly colors, soft rounded shapes, no text or letters anywhere in the image, square 1:1 aspect ratio, cheerful and inviting for young readers."},{"text":"A moose stands in the lake. It eats plants from the water. The moose has big antlers on its head.","questions":[{"question":"Where does the moose stand?","choices":["on a rock","in a barn","under a bridge","in the lake"],"answer":"in the lake"},{"question":"What does the moose have on its head?","choices":["a bird","a hat","a crown","big antlers"],"answer":"big antlers"}],"image_prompt":"A warm, simple flat-style children's-book illustration for a kids' reading app. Scene: A moose stands in the lake. Focus on: a moose with big antlers standing in a lake. Bright friendly colors, soft rounded shapes, no text or letters anywhere in the image, square 1:1 aspect ratio, cheerful and inviting for young readers."}],"1":[{"text":"The ostrich is the biggest bird in the world. It can grow taller than a grown-up person. An ostrich cannot fly, but it is a super runner. It can run faster than most cars drive on a city street! Ostriches lay the biggest eggs of any bird. One ostrich egg is as heavy as about two dozen chicken eggs. Ostriches live in Africa, where they eat plants, seeds, and bugs.","questions":[{"question":"What can an ostrich NOT do?","choices":["run","fly","lay eggs","eat seeds"],"answer":"fly"},{"question":"Where do ostriches live?","choices":["Australia","Asia","Africa","the Arctic"],"answer":"Africa"},{"question":"An ostrich egg is as heavy as about how many chicken eggs?","choices":["about one hundred","exactly one","about two dozen","about three"],"answer":"about two dozen"}],"image_prompt":"A warm, simple flat-style children's-book illustration for a kids' reading app. Scene: The ostrich is the biggest bird in the world. Focus on: an ostrich, the biggest bird, running across the African plain. Bright friendly colors, soft rounded shapes, no text or letters anywhere in the image, square 1:1 aspect ratio, cheerful and inviting for young readers."},{"text":"Skunks are famous for their smell, but they do not spray right away. First, a skunk gives a warning. It stamps its front feet, raises its tail, and hisses. If the danger does not leave, the skunk turns around and sprays a stinky mist. Skunks would rather not spray, because it takes them many days to make more. Most animals learn to stay far away from the black and white stripes.","questions":[{"question":"What does a skunk do first when it feels danger?","choices":["sprays right away","digs a hole","gives a warning","climbs a tree"],"answer":"gives a warning"},{"question":"Why would a skunk rather not spray?","choices":["It makes it sleepy.","It takes days to make more.","It hurts its tail.","It is too tired."],"answer":"It takes days to make more."},{"question":"What does a skunk raise as a warning?","choices":["its nose","its tail","its ears","one paw"],"answer":"its tail"}],"image_prompt":"A warm, simple flat-style children's-book illustration for a kids' reading app. Scene: Skunks are famous for their smell, but they do not spray right away. Focus on: a skunk stamping its feet and raising its tail as a warning. Bright friendly colors, soft rounded shapes, no text or letters anywhere in the image, square 1:1 aspect ratio, cheerful and inviting for young readers."},{"text":"Tigers are the biggest cats in the world. Each tiger has its own pattern of stripes, just like each person has their own fingerprints. The stripes help a tiger hide in tall grass while it hunts. Unlike many cats, tigers love water. They swim across rivers and cool off in ponds on hot days. Tigers live alone and mark their land so other tigers know to stay away.","questions":[{"question":"What do the stripes help a tiger do?","choices":["swim across rivers","hide in tall grass","find its friends","stay warm at night"],"answer":"hide in tall grass"},{"question":"How are tigers different from many cats?","choices":["They have fur.","They love water.","They have tails.","They eat meat."],"answer":"They love water."},{"question":"Do tigers live in groups or alone?","choices":["in pairs","with lions","in big groups","alone"],"answer":"alone"}],"photo":"images/flashcards/tiger.jpg"},{"text":"Llamas come from the high mountains of South America. People there have used llamas for thousands of years to carry heavy packs up steep trails. Llamas have thick wool that keeps them warm in the cold mountain air. Today, some farmers keep llamas to guard their sheep. A llama will chase away a coyote or a fox! Llamas often hum softly to each other.","questions":[{"question":"Where do llamas come from?","choices":["Hawaii","the forests of Canada","Africa","South America"],"answer":"South America"},{"question":"What keeps llamas warm?","choices":["a fire","a blanket","warm water","thick wool"],"answer":"thick wool"},{"question":"What job do some farmers give llamas?","choices":["laying eggs","herding cows","pulling cars","guarding their sheep"],"answer":"guarding their sheep"}],"image_prompt":"A warm, simple flat-style children's-book illustration for a kids' reading app. Scene: Llamas come from the high mountains of South America. Focus on: a llama carrying a pack on a mountain trail. Bright friendly colors, soft rounded shapes, no text or letters anywhere in the image, square 1:1 aspect ratio, cheerful and inviting for young readers."},{"text":"A magnet can pull some things toward it without even touching them! Magnets pull on things made of iron, like nails and paper clips. They do not pull on wood, plastic, or paper. Every magnet has two ends called poles. If you push two of the same poles together, they push each other away. If you turn one magnet around, they snap together. Magnets can hold notes on a refrigerator door.","questions":[{"question":"What do magnets pull on?","choices":["things made of wood","things made of iron","paper plates","plastic cups"],"answer":"things made of iron"},{"question":"What are the two ends of a magnet called?","choices":["hooks","sides","tails","poles"],"answer":"poles"},{"question":"What happens when you push two of the same poles together?","choices":["They glow.","They get hot.","They turn into iron.","They push each other away."],"answer":"They push each other away."}],"image_prompt":"A warm, simple flat-style children's-book illustration for a kids' reading app. Scene: A magnet can pull some things toward it without even touching them! Magnets pull on things made of iron, like nails and paper clips. Focus on: a magnet picking up paper clips and nails. Bright friendly colors, soft rounded shapes, no text or letters anywhere in the image, square 1:1 aspect ratio, cheerful and inviting for young readers."},{"text":"A battery is like a tiny box of stored energy. Inside, special materials hold energy until you need it. When you put batteries in a flashlight and turn it on, the energy flows out and makes the bulb glow. Toys, remote controls, and clocks can use batteries too. When a battery runs out of energy, some kinds can be charged again. Old batteries should be recycled, not thrown in the trash.","questions":[{"question":"What does a battery store?","choices":["air","water","sunshine","energy"],"answer":"energy"},{"question":"What happens when you turn on a flashlight?","choices":["The bulb glows.","The battery melts.","It gets much heavier.","It plays music."],"answer":"The bulb glows."},{"question":"What should you do with old batteries?","choices":["bury them","throw them in the trash","recycle them","put them in the freezer"],"answer":"recycle them"}],"image_prompt":"A warm, simple flat-style children's-book illustration for a kids' reading app. Scene: A battery is like a tiny box of stored energy. Focus on: batteries next to a glowing flashlight. Bright friendly colors, soft rounded shapes, no text or letters anywhere in the image, square 1:1 aspect ratio, cheerful and inviting for young readers."},{"text":"A compass helps people find their way. Inside is a small needle that can spin. The needle is a magnet, and it always points north. Once you know which way north is, you can find south, east, and west too. Long ago, sailors used a compass to cross the ocean when they could not see land. Hikers still carry one today in case they get lost in the woods.","questions":[{"question":"Which way does the compass needle point?","choices":["north","south","east","up"],"answer":"north"},{"question":"Who used a compass long ago to cross the ocean?","choices":["teachers","farmers","sailors","bakers"],"answer":"sailors"},{"question":"Why do hikers carry a compass?","choices":["to scare away bears","in case they get lost","to tell the time","to stay warm"],"answer":"in case they get lost"}],"image_prompt":"A warm, simple flat-style children's-book illustration for a kids' reading app. Scene: A compass helps people find their way. Focus on: a compass with its needle pointing north. Bright friendly colors, soft rounded shapes, no text or letters anywhere in the image, square 1:1 aspect ratio, cheerful and inviting for young readers."},{"text":"Rice is a food eaten by billions of people around the world. Rice plants grow best in wet fields called paddies. Farmers flood the paddies with shallow water. The rice plants grow tall and green, and then they turn golden. When the grains are ready, farmers cut the plants and dry them. Each tiny grain of rice is really a seed that could grow a new plant!","questions":[{"question":"What are wet rice fields called?","choices":["pastures","ponds","paddies","puddles"],"answer":"paddies"},{"question":"What color do rice plants turn when they are ready?","choices":["golden","blue","purple","white"],"answer":"golden"},{"question":"What is a grain of rice really?","choices":["a leaf","a bug","a seed","a flower"],"answer":"a seed"}],"image_prompt":"A warm, simple flat-style children's-book illustration for a kids' reading app. Scene: Rice is a food eaten by billions of people around the world. Focus on: farmers in a flooded green rice paddy. Bright friendly colors, soft rounded shapes, no text or letters anywhere in the image, square 1:1 aspect ratio, cheerful and inviting for young readers."},{"text":"Making noodles by hand is fun! First, you mix flour, eggs, and a little water into a dough. Then you knead the dough by pushing and folding it. Next, you roll it flat with a rolling pin and cut it into long, thin strips. Finally, you drop the strips into a pot of boiling water. In a few minutes, the noodles are soft and ready to eat.","questions":[{"question":"What do you mix to make the dough?","choices":["apples and cheese","sugar, milk, and salt","flour, eggs, and water","rice, beans, and oil"],"answer":"flour, eggs, and water"},{"question":"What do you use to roll the dough flat?","choices":["a fork","a cup","a spoon","a rolling pin"],"answer":"a rolling pin"},{"question":"What is the last step?","choices":["mix the flour","roll it flat","drop the strips into boiling water","knead the dough"],"answer":"drop the strips into boiling water"}],"image_prompt":"A warm, simple flat-style children's-book illustration for a kids' reading app. Scene: Making noodles by hand is fun! First, you mix flour, eggs, and a little water into a dough. Focus on: hands rolling noodle dough and cutting long strips. Bright friendly colors, soft rounded shapes, no text or letters anywhere in the image, square 1:1 aspect ratio, cheerful and inviting for young readers."},{"text":"Yogurt starts out as milk. To make yogurt, people warm the milk and add tiny living things called good bacteria. The bacteria are so small you cannot see them. They eat the sugar in the milk and make it thick and a little bit sour. After many hours in a warm place, the milk has turned into yogurt. Good bacteria help keep your tummy healthy, too.","questions":[{"question":"What does yogurt start out as?","choices":["water","flour","milk","juice"],"answer":"milk"},{"question":"What do the good bacteria eat?","choices":["the salt in the bowl","the sugar in the milk","the cup","the fruit on top"],"answer":"the sugar in the milk"},{"question":"How does the milk change?","choices":["It turns bright green.","It gets sweeter and thinner.","It turns into ice.","It gets thick and a little sour."],"answer":"It gets thick and a little sour."}],"image_prompt":"A warm, simple flat-style children's-book illustration for a kids' reading app. Scene: Yogurt starts out as milk. Focus on: a jar of homemade yogurt and a glass of milk. Bright friendly colors, soft rounded shapes, no text or letters anywhere in the image, square 1:1 aspect ratio, cheerful and inviting for young readers."},{"text":"On field day, Priya's class ran a relay race. Each team had four runners. The first runner held a stick called a baton. She ran fast and passed it to the next runner, who ran to the third, and so on. Priya was the last runner on her team. She grabbed the baton and sprinted to the finish line. Her team did not win, but everyone cheered for each other.","questions":[{"question":"What is the stick in a relay race called?","choices":["a ruler","a baton","a bat","a paddle"],"answer":"a baton"},{"question":"How many runners were on each team?","choices":["two","ten","six","four"],"answer":"four"},{"question":"What did Priya's team do even though they did not win?","choices":["cheered for each other","argued with the judges","ran the race again","went home sad"],"answer":"cheered for each other"}],"image_prompt":"A warm, simple flat-style children's-book illustration for a kids' reading app. Scene: On field day, Priya's class ran a relay race. Focus on: kids passing a baton in a relay race. Bright friendly colors, soft rounded shapes, no text or letters anywhere in the image, square 1:1 aspect ratio, cheerful and inviting for young readers."},{"text":"For his birthday, Marcus went bowling with his family. The bowling ball had three holes for his fingers. He used bumpers in the gutters so the ball would not roll off the lane. On his first try, he knocked down six pins. On his last turn, he knocked down all ten pins. That is called a strike! Marcus did a happy dance.","questions":[{"question":"Why did Marcus go bowling?","choices":["for a contest","for his grandma's party","for a school trip","for his birthday"],"answer":"for his birthday"},{"question":"How many pins did he knock down on his first try?","choices":["six","one","three","ten"],"answer":"six"},{"question":"What is it called when you knock down all ten pins?","choices":["a home run","a goal","a spare","a strike"],"answer":"a strike"}],"image_prompt":"A warm, simple flat-style children's-book illustration for a kids' reading app. Scene: For his birthday, Marcus went bowling with his family. Focus on: a boy bowling a strike at a family birthday. Bright friendly colors, soft rounded shapes, no text or letters anywhere in the image, square 1:1 aspect ratio, cheerful and inviting for young readers."},{"text":"At recess, Hana and Rosie both wanted the swing. Hana got there first and said, \"It is mine!\" Rosie felt sad and walked away. Later, Hana thought about how Rosie felt. She found Rosie by the slide and said, \"I am sorry. Let's take turns.\" Rosie smiled. They counted to fifty for each turn, and both of them had fun.","questions":[{"question":"What did both girls want?","choices":["the swing","the slide","the jump rope","the ball"],"answer":"the swing"},{"question":"How did Rosie feel when Hana said \"It is mine!\"?","choices":["proud","excited","sleepy","sad"],"answer":"sad"},{"question":"How did they solve the problem?","choices":["They found a new swing.","They took turns.","They told the teacher.","They went inside."],"answer":"They took turns."}],"image_prompt":"A warm, simple flat-style children's-book illustration for a kids' reading app. Scene: At recess, Hana and Rosie both wanted the swing. Focus on: two girls taking turns on a playground swing. Bright friendly colors, soft rounded shapes, no text or letters anywhere in the image, square 1:1 aspect ratio, cheerful and inviting for young readers."},{"text":"Jamal took a pottery class at the art center. The teacher showed him how to put a lump of wet clay on a spinning wheel. Jamal pressed his thumbs into the middle, and slowly the clay grew into a cup. Next, the cup went into a very hot oven called a kiln. The heat made the clay hard. Last, Jamal painted it with a shiny blue glaze.","questions":[{"question":"What did Jamal make?","choices":["a plate","a bowl","a vase","a cup"],"answer":"a cup"},{"question":"What is the hot oven for clay called?","choices":["a stove","a grill","a kiln","a toaster"],"answer":"a kiln"},{"question":"What did the heat do to the clay?","choices":["made it blue","made it melt","made it soft","made it hard"],"answer":"made it hard"}],"image_prompt":"A warm, simple flat-style children's-book illustration for a kids' reading app. Scene: Jamal took a pottery class at the art center. Focus on: a boy shaping a clay cup on a spinning pottery wheel. Bright friendly colors, soft rounded shapes, no text or letters anywhere in the image, square 1:1 aspect ratio, cheerful and inviting for young readers."},{"text":"The moose is the biggest member of the deer family. A grown moose can be taller than a horse. Only the males have antlers, and they lose them every winter and grow new ones in the spring. Moose are great swimmers. In summer, they wade into lakes to eat water plants and to get away from biting bugs. They live in cold northern forests.","questions":[{"question":"Which moose have antlers?","choices":["all of them","only the babies","only the females","only the males"],"answer":"only the males"},{"question":"Why do moose wade into lakes in summer?","choices":["to find snow","to drink water","to eat plants and escape bugs","to sleep"],"answer":"to eat plants and escape bugs"},{"question":"Moose are the biggest member of which family?","choices":["the horse family","the cat family","the deer family","the bear family"],"answer":"the deer family"}],"image_prompt":"A warm, simple flat-style children's-book illustration for a kids' reading app. Scene: The moose is the biggest member of the deer family. Focus on: a moose wading in a lake. Bright friendly colors, soft rounded shapes, no text or letters anywhere in the image, square 1:1 aspect ratio, cheerful and inviting for young readers."},{"text":"A spider makes a web using silk that comes from its own body. First, it lets out a thin thread and the wind carries it to a twig. Then the spider adds more threads to build a frame and spokes like a bicycle wheel. At last it spins a sticky spiral. When a fly bumps into the web, it gets stuck, and the spider feels the web shake.","questions":[{"question":"Where does a spider's silk come from?","choices":["the dirt on the ground","the leaves of a plant","its own body","a ball of cotton in the grass"],"answer":"its own body"},{"question":"What does the spider do after it builds the frame and spokes?","choices":["takes a long nap in the sun","spins a sticky spiral","eats the web and starts over","paints the threads bright colors"],"answer":"spins a sticky spiral"},{"question":"How does the spider know a fly is caught?","choices":["It sees a bright light turn on.","The fly tells the spider.","It feels the web shake.","It hears the fly buzzing from far away."],"answer":"It feels the web shake."}]},{"text":"Long ago, during the Ice Age, giant animals called woolly mammoths roamed the cold land. They were related to elephants, but they had long, shaggy brown hair to keep warm. Their curved tusks could be as long as a car. Mammoths used their tusks to dig through snow to find grass. Scientists have found mammoth bones and even frozen mammoths in icy ground.","questions":[{"question":"Which animals were mammoths related to?","choices":["bears","elephants","horses","tigers"],"answer":"elephants"},{"question":"What kept mammoths warm?","choices":["feathers","long, shaggy hair","a warm cave","a fire"],"answer":"long, shaggy hair"},{"question":"How did mammoths use their tusks?","choices":["to swim","to dig through snow for grass","to climb trees","to fly"],"answer":"to dig through snow for grass"}],"image_prompt":"A warm, simple flat-style children's-book illustration for a kids' reading app. Scene: Long ago, during the Ice Age, giant animals called woolly mammoths roamed the cold land. Focus on: woolly mammoths in an Ice Age snowy land. Bright friendly colors, soft rounded shapes, no text or letters anywhere in the image, square 1:1 aspect ratio, cheerful and inviting for young readers."},{"text":"Every Saturday, Leah and her dad volunteer at the food bank. People give cans of soup, boxes of pasta, and jars of peanut butter. Leah and her dad sort the food into groups and put it on shelves. Then families who need help can come and pick out what they need. Leah likes knowing that her work helps neighbors have enough to eat.","questions":[{"question":"When do Leah and her dad volunteer?","choices":["every Monday","every Saturday","every night","on her birthday"],"answer":"every Saturday"},{"question":"What do Leah and her dad do with the food?","choices":["give it to pets","sort it and put it on shelves","eat it for lunch","sell it at a store"],"answer":"sort it and put it on shelves"},{"question":"Why does Leah like the work?","choices":["She gets free toys.","It helps neighbors have enough to eat.","She can skip school.","She likes to stay up late."],"answer":"It helps neighbors have enough to eat."}],"image_prompt":"A warm, simple flat-style children's-book illustration for a kids' reading app. Scene: Every Saturday, Leah and her dad volunteer at the food bank. Focus on: a girl and her dad sorting cans onto food bank shelves. Bright friendly colors, soft rounded shapes, no text or letters anywhere in the image, square 1:1 aspect ratio, cheerful and inviting for young readers."},{"text":"One morning, Theo found a small brown dog sitting on his porch. The dog had a collar but no tag. Theo and his mom made posters that said, \"Found: Brown Dog.\" They hung them on poles around the block. That evening, a girl named Grace knocked on the door. \"That's my dog, Peanut!\" she cried. Peanut wagged his tail and licked Grace's face.","questions":[{"question":"Where did Theo find the dog?","choices":["in the garage","at school","at the park","on his porch"],"answer":"on his porch"},{"question":"What did Theo and his mom make?","choices":["cookies","a doghouse","a leash","posters"],"answer":"posters"},{"question":"What was the dog's name?","choices":["Pepper","Buddy","Peanut","Biscuit"],"answer":"Peanut"}],"photo":"images/flashcards/dog.jpg"},{"text":"The cuttlefish is a sea animal with eight arms and two long tentacles. It is not really a fish at all! A cuttlefish is a master of disguise. Its skin is covered with tiny color cells. In less than a second, it can change color to match rocks, sand, or seaweed. It can even make its skin look bumpy. This helps it hide from sharks and sneak up on crabs.","questions":[{"question":"How many arms does a cuttlefish have?","choices":["twelve","eight","three","four"],"answer":"eight"},{"question":"How fast can a cuttlefish change color?","choices":["in less than a second","overnight","in about an hour","in one week"],"answer":"in less than a second"},{"question":"Why does the cuttlefish change color?","choices":["to hide from sharks","to fall asleep","to scare away crabs","to make the water warmer"],"answer":"to hide from sharks"}],"image_prompt":"A warm, simple flat-style children's-book illustration for a kids' reading app. Scene: The cuttlefish is a sea animal with eight arms and two long tentacles. Focus on: a cuttlefish changing color to match the seaweed. Bright friendly colors, soft rounded shapes, no text or letters anywhere in the image, square 1:1 aspect ratio, cheerful and inviting for young readers."},{"text":"In the snowy mountains of Japan live monkeys with thick gray fur and bright red faces. They are often called snow monkeys. When winter gets very cold, some snow monkeys climb into natural hot springs. The warm water bubbles up from deep under the ground. The monkeys sit in the water to stay warm while snow falls around them. Young monkeys sometimes even play with snowballs!","questions":[{"question":"Where do snow monkeys live?","choices":["Japan","Brazil","Mexico","Egypt"],"answer":"Japan"},{"question":"What color are their faces?","choices":["red","gray","blue","white"],"answer":"red"},{"question":"Why do the monkeys sit in the hot springs?","choices":["to stay warm","to take a drink","to catch fish","to wash their fur"],"answer":"to stay warm"}],"image_prompt":"A warm, simple flat-style children's-book illustration for a kids' reading app. Scene: In the snowy mountains of Japan live monkeys with thick gray fur and bright red faces. Focus on: snow monkeys sitting in a steaming hot spring. Bright friendly colors, soft rounded shapes, no text or letters anywhere in the image, square 1:1 aspect ratio, cheerful and inviting for young readers."},{"text":"Most years have 365 days. But it actually takes Earth about 365 and one-quarter days to travel all the way around the sun. To keep our calendar on track, we add one extra day every four years. That day is February 29, and the year is called a leap year. People born on February 29 only get to see their real birthday once every four years!","questions":[{"question":"How often do we add an extra day?","choices":["every four years","every week","every year","every month"],"answer":"every four years"},{"question":"What is the extra day?","choices":["March 31","January 1","December 25","February 29"],"answer":"February 29"},{"question":"What is a year with the extra day called?","choices":["a birthday year","a short year","a leap year","a jump year"],"answer":"a leap year"}],"image_prompt":"A warm, simple flat-style children's-book illustration for a kids' reading app. Scene: Most years have 365 days. Focus on: a calendar page showing February 29. Bright friendly colors, soft rounded shapes, no text or letters anywhere in the image, square 1:1 aspect ratio, cheerful and inviting for young readers."},{"text":"Eli's new classmate, Sofia, is deaf. She talks with her hands using American Sign Language. Eli wanted to be her friend, so he asked the teacher to help him learn some signs. He learned how to sign \"hello,\" \"friend,\" and \"thank you.\" The next day, Eli signed \"hello, friend\" to Sofia. Her face lit up, and she signed \"thank you\" back.","questions":[{"question":"How does Sofia talk?","choices":["by writing","with a whistle","with her hands","by singing"],"answer":"with her hands"},{"question":"Why did Eli learn some signs?","choices":["to surprise his mom","to win a prize","to do his homework","to be Sofia's friend"],"answer":"to be Sofia's friend"},{"question":"What did Sofia sign back?","choices":["thank you","goodbye","hello","see you later"],"answer":"thank you"}],"image_prompt":"A warm, simple flat-style children's-book illustration for a kids' reading app. Scene: Eli's new classmate, Sofia, is deaf. Focus on: two children using sign language to say hello. Bright friendly colors, soft rounded shapes, no text or letters anywhere in the image, square 1:1 aspect ratio, cheerful and inviting for young readers."}],"2":[{"text":"Before telephones existed, sending news across the country could take days or even weeks. A letter had to travel by horse, boat, or train. In the 1830s and 1840s, an American inventor named Samuel Morse helped change that. He worked on the electric telegraph, a machine that sent signals through long wires. Morse and his partner, Alfred Vail, created a code made of short and long signals, called dots and dashes. Each letter of the alphabet had its own pattern. In 1844, Morse sent a famous message from Washington, D.C., to Baltimore, and it arrived almost instantly. Soon, telegraph wires stretched across the nation, and news could travel in minutes instead of days.","questions":[{"question":"In the passage, the word \"instantly\" means?","choices":["in a loud way","by mail","right away","very slowly"],"answer":"right away"},{"question":"What is the main idea of the passage?","choices":["Baltimore and Washington were the first cities to have phones.","Samuel Morse traveled by horse, boat, and train.","The telegraph made sending messages much faster.","Letters were the fastest way to send news in 1844."],"answer":"The telegraph made sending messages much faster."},{"question":"Why did Morse need a code of dots and dashes?","choices":["He wanted to keep every message secret from Vail.","People in Baltimore could not read English words.","Wires could send only simple signals, not voices.","Vail could not hear well."],"answer":"Wires could send only simple signals, not voices."}],"image_prompt":"A warm, simple flat-style children's-book illustration for a kids' reading app. Scene: Before telephones existed, sending news across the country could take days or even weeks. Focus on: an old telegraph key sending dots and dashes. Bright friendly colors, soft rounded shapes, no text or letters anywhere in the image, square 1:1 aspect ratio, cheerful and inviting for young readers."},{"text":"In 1860, there was no fast way to send mail from the eastern United States to California. A company decided to try something bold: the Pony Express. Young riders on fast horses carried mail nearly 2,000 miles, from St. Joseph, Missouri, to Sacramento, California. Every ten to fifteen miles, a rider stopped at a station and switched to a fresh horse. After about 75 to 100 miles, a new rider took over. Riding day and night, the riders delivered mail in about ten days, much faster than the weeks it took by stagecoach. But the Pony Express lasted only about eighteen months. In 1861, telegraph lines reached California, and messages could be sent in minutes.","questions":[{"question":"In the passage, the word \"bold\" means?","choices":["quiet","boring","daring","careless"],"answer":"daring"},{"question":"Why did riders switch horses so often?","choices":["because each horse belonged to a different town","because the mail bags were too heavy to lift","so the riders could sleep while the horses ran","so a rested horse could keep up the fast pace"],"answer":"so a rested horse could keep up the fast pace"},{"question":"Why did the Pony Express end?","choices":["Telegraph lines reached California.","The mail was too heavy.","The horses got too tired.","The riders quit."],"answer":"Telegraph lines reached California."}],"image_prompt":"A warm, simple flat-style children's-book illustration for a kids' reading app. Scene: In 1860, there was no fast way to send mail from the eastern United States to California. Focus on: a Pony Express rider galloping with a mail bag. Bright friendly colors, soft rounded shapes, no text or letters anywhere in the image, square 1:1 aspect ratio, cheerful and inviting for young readers."},{"text":"During the 1930s, a large part of the Great Plains in the United States became known as the Dust Bowl. For years, farmers had plowed up the deep-rooted prairie grasses that held the soil in place. When a long drought arrived, crops died and the bare, dry soil turned to dust. Strong winds lifted the dust into huge, dark clouds called black blizzards. Some storms were so thick that people had to light lamps at noon. Many families lost their farms and moved west to look for work. Afterward, the government helped farmers learn new methods, such as planting rows of trees to block the wind and rotating crops to protect the soil.","questions":[{"question":"In the passage, the word \"drought\" means?","choices":["a season with too much flooding","a long time without rain","a heavy snowstorm","a strong wind"],"answer":"a long time without rain"},{"question":"What was one cause of the Dust Bowl?","choices":["Heavy rains washed all the topsoil into rivers.","Too many trees blocked the wind from the fields.","Farmers moved west before planting their crops.","Plowing removed the grass that held the soil."],"answer":"Plowing removed the grass that held the soil."},{"question":"After learning the new methods, what would a farmer most likely do?","choices":["move to a big city","stop growing crops forever","plow up more grass","plant trees to block the wind"],"answer":"plant trees to block the wind"}],"image_prompt":"A warm, simple flat-style children's-book illustration for a kids' reading app. Scene: During the 1930s, a large part of the Great Plains in the United States became known as the Dust Bowl. Focus on: a dust storm rolling over a 1930s farm. Bright friendly colors, soft rounded shapes, no text or letters anywhere in the image, square 1:1 aspect ratio, cheerful and inviting for young readers."},{"text":"In the Black Hills of South Dakota, four giant faces look out from the side of a mountain. This is Mount Rushmore. The faces show four U.S. presidents: George Washington, Thomas Jefferson, Theodore Roosevelt, and Abraham Lincoln. Each face is about 60 feet tall, as tall as a six-story building. A sculptor named Gutzon Borglum led the project, which began in 1927. Around 400 workers used dynamite, drills, and hand tools to remove rock. They worked for fourteen years, finishing in 1941. Workers often dangled over the cliff in leather harnesses. Amazingly, no one died during the carving. Today, millions of visitors come to see the monument each year.","questions":[{"question":"In the passage, the word \"monument\" means?","choices":["a tool for cutting stone","a kind of rock","a tall building where people live","a structure built to honor someone"],"answer":"a structure built to honor someone"},{"question":"How long did the carving take?","choices":["four years","one year","fourteen years","about sixty years"],"answer":"fourteen years"},{"question":"Why were the leather harnesses important?","choices":["They kept workers safe as they hung over the cliff.","They made the workers move faster than drills.","They helped workers carry heavy rocks up the hill.","They held the faces in place while the paint dried."],"answer":"They kept workers safe as they hung over the cliff."}],"image_prompt":"A warm, simple flat-style children's-book illustration for a kids' reading app. Scene: In the Black Hills of South Dakota, four giant faces look out from the side of a mountain. Focus on: the four carved faces of Mount Rushmore. Bright friendly colors, soft rounded shapes, no text or letters anywhere in the image, square 1:1 aspect ratio, cheerful and inviting for young readers."},{"text":"The Colorado River once flooded farms and towns in the spring and nearly dried up in late summer. To control it, workers built the Hoover Dam between Nevada and Arizona from 1931 to 1936. The dam is a huge curved wall of concrete more than 700 feet tall. Behind it, the river filled a canyon and formed Lake Mead, one of the largest reservoirs in the country. The stored water is released slowly so farms and cities have water all year. As the water rushes through the dam, it spins machines called turbines that make electricity for more than a million people.","questions":[{"question":"In the passage, the word \"reservoir\" means?","choices":["a wall made of concrete","a machine that makes electricity","a small stream in the mountains","a large lake used to store water"],"answer":"a large lake used to store water"},{"question":"What problem was the dam built to solve?","choices":["Farmers wanted the river to flow much faster all year.","Nevada and Arizona wanted a lake for boat races.","Cities needed a bridge to cross the wide canyon.","The river flooded in spring and ran low in summer."],"answer":"The river flooded in spring and ran low in summer."},{"question":"Besides storing water, what does the dam do?","choices":["It makes electricity.","It catches fish for towns.","It heats homes with steam.","It cleans the ocean."],"answer":"It makes electricity."}],"image_prompt":"A warm, simple flat-style children's-book illustration for a kids' reading app. Scene: The Colorado River once flooded farms and towns in the spring and nearly dried up in late summer. Focus on: Hoover Dam holding back Lake Mead. Bright friendly colors, soft rounded shapes, no text or letters anywhere in the image, square 1:1 aspect ratio, cheerful and inviting for young readers."},{"text":"Harriet Tubman was born into slavery in Maryland around 1822. In 1849, she escaped and traveled about 90 miles north to Pennsylvania, where she could live free. But Tubman did not stop there. She returned to Maryland about thirteen times to lead family members and others to freedom. She guided around seventy people along the Underground Railroad, a secret network of routes and safe houses. They traveled mostly at night and followed the North Star. Tubman was known for her courage and careful planning. She later said that on her journeys, she never lost a single passenger. During the Civil War, she also served as a scout and nurse for the Union Army.","questions":[{"question":"In the passage, the word \"network\" means?","choices":["a fishing net used in rivers","a map drawn on a tall wall","a real railroad with tracks underground","a group of connected paths and people"],"answer":"a group of connected paths and people"},{"question":"Why did travelers on the Underground Railroad move mostly at night?","choices":["The roads were too hot to walk on during the day.","They wanted to sleep in the sunshine.","The trains only ran after the sun went down.","It was harder for anyone to see them."],"answer":"It was harder for anyone to see them."},{"question":"Which sentence best tells the main idea?","choices":["Harriet Tubman bravely led many people to freedom.","Pennsylvania is about 90 miles from Maryland.","Harriet Tubman was a nurse.","The North Star is the brightest star in the sky."],"answer":"Harriet Tubman bravely led many people to freedom."}],"image_prompt":"A warm, simple flat-style children's-book illustration for a kids' reading app. Scene: Harriet Tubman was born into slavery in Maryland around 1822. Focus on: travelers following the North Star at night on the Underground Railroad. Bright friendly colors, soft rounded shapes, no text or letters anywhere in the image, square 1:1 aspect ratio, cheerful and inviting for young readers."},{"text":"Curling is a winter sport played on a long sheet of ice. Two teams take turns sliding heavy granite stones toward a target called the house, which looks like a giant bull's-eye. Each stone weighs about 40 pounds. As a stone glides down the ice, two teammates move ahead of it with brooms and sweep hard. Sweeping warms the ice slightly, which helps the stone travel farther and straighter. The team with the stone closest to the center scores points. Curling began in Scotland hundreds of years ago and became an official Winter Olympic sport in 1998.","questions":[{"question":"In the passage, the word \"glides\" means?","choices":["moves smoothly","stops suddenly","bounces","breaks apart"],"answer":"moves smoothly"},{"question":"Why do teammates sweep the ice?","choices":["to brush away snow","to make the ice colder and rougher","to help the stone travel farther and straighter","to keep the other team from scoring"],"answer":"to help the stone travel farther and straighter"},{"question":"Where did curling begin?","choices":["Switzerland","Norway","Canada","Scotland"],"answer":"Scotland"}],"image_prompt":"A warm, simple flat-style children's-book illustration for a kids' reading app. Scene: Curling is a winter sport played on a long sheet of ice. Focus on: curling players sweeping in front of a sliding stone. Bright friendly colors, soft rounded shapes, no text or letters anywhere in the image, square 1:1 aspect ratio, cheerful and inviting for young readers."},{"text":"Japanese macaques, often called snow monkeys, live farther north than any other monkey on Earth. In parts of Japan, winter temperatures fall well below freezing, and snow can pile up several feet deep. The macaques survive with thick coats that grow even thicker in winter. In one mountain valley, a group of macaques began soaking in natural hot springs to stay warm. Scientists noticed that the habit spread as monkeys watched and copied one another, until much of the group was bathing. Researchers also found that monkeys who bathed showed lower levels of stress. Macaques have even been seen washing food in water and rolling snowballs just for fun.","questions":[{"question":"In the passage, the word \"habit\" means?","choices":["a deep pool","a loud sound","something done again and again","a warm spot"],"answer":"something done again and again"},{"question":"What does the hot spring story show about macaques?","choices":["They dislike snow more than any other animal.","They need hot water to find their food.","They can learn new things by watching others.","They are not able to feel the cold at all."],"answer":"They can learn new things by watching others."},{"question":"How do the macaques' coats change in winter?","choices":["They turn bright white.","They grow thicker.","They turn red.","They fall out."],"answer":"They grow thicker."}],"image_prompt":"A warm, simple flat-style children's-book illustration for a kids' reading app. Scene: Japanese macaques, often called snow monkeys, live farther north than any other monkey on Earth. Focus on: snow monkeys soaking in a hot spring while snow falls. Bright friendly colors, soft rounded shapes, no text or letters anywhere in the image, square 1:1 aspect ratio, cheerful and inviting for young readers."},{"text":"Despite its name, the cuttlefish is not a fish. It is a mollusk, related to squids and octopuses. Inside its body is a light, hollow shell called a cuttlebone. The cuttlefish can fill the tiny chambers of the cuttlebone with gas or liquid to float up or sink down, a bit like a submarine. Cuttlefish are also famous for changing color. Their skin contains thousands of special cells that stretch or shrink to show different colors, which they use to blend in and to send signals to other cuttlefish. After a cuttlefish dies, its cuttlebone often washes up on beaches, and people give it to pet birds as a source of calcium.","questions":[{"question":"In the passage, \"blend in\" means?","choices":["swim away from danger quickly","glow in dark water","grow bigger and stronger","match their surroundings"],"answer":"match their surroundings"},{"question":"How does the cuttlebone help a cuttlefish?","choices":["It lets the cuttlefish float up or sink down.","It stores extra food.","It helps the cuttlefish see in dark water.","It protects the cuttlefish from sharp teeth."],"answer":"It lets the cuttlefish float up or sink down."},{"question":"Why is \"cuttlefish\" a surprising name?","choices":["It is not really a fish.","It cannot cut anything.","It has no bones of any kind.","It lives only in rivers."],"answer":"It is not really a fish."}],"image_prompt":"A warm, simple flat-style children's-book illustration for a kids' reading app. Scene: Despite its name, the cuttlefish is not a fish. Focus on: a cuttlefish blending into a coral reef. Bright friendly colors, soft rounded shapes, no text or letters anywhere in the image, square 1:1 aspect ratio, cheerful and inviting for young readers."},{"text":"Woolly mammoths lived during the last Ice Age, when sheets of ice covered much of the northern world. These relatives of elephants were about as tall as an African elephant but were built for cold. They had thick, shaggy fur, small ears that lost less heat, and a layer of fat under their skin. Most mammoths died out about 10,000 years ago as the climate warmed and their grassy habitat shrank. Human hunting may have played a part, too. However, a small group survived on Wrangel Island, off the coast of Siberia, until about 4,000 years ago. That means some mammoths were still alive when the Great Pyramid of Egypt was being built!","questions":[{"question":"In the passage, the word \"habitat\" means?","choices":["a place where scientists study bones","the natural home of an animal","the tusks of an elephant","a thick coat of winter fur"],"answer":"the natural home of an animal"},{"question":"Why did small ears help mammoths?","choices":["They kept snow out of their eyes.","They made the mammoths run faster.","They lost less body heat.","They could hear wolves from far away."],"answer":"They lost less body heat."},{"question":"What surprising idea does the last sentence point out?","choices":["The pyramids were built during the Ice Age.","Mammoths lived more recently than many people think.","Egyptians hunted mammoths.","Mammoths helped the Egyptians move giant stones."],"answer":"Mammoths lived more recently than many people think."}],"image_prompt":"A warm, simple flat-style children's-book illustration for a kids' reading app. Scene: Woolly mammoths lived during the last Ice Age, when sheets of ice covered much of the northern world. Focus on: a woolly mammoth with curved tusks in the snow. Bright friendly colors, soft rounded shapes, no text or letters anywhere in the image, square 1:1 aspect ratio, cheerful and inviting for young readers."},{"text":"Jupiter is the largest planet in our solar system, so big that more than 1,300 Earths could fit inside it. Unlike Earth, Jupiter has no solid surface. It is a gas giant, made mostly of hydrogen and helium. Its colorful bands are clouds pushed by powerful winds. Its most famous feature is the Great Red Spot, a giant storm wider than Earth that has been raging for at least 150 years. Some scientists think Jupiter also acts like a shield for the inner planets, because its strong gravity pulls in or flings away many comets that might otherwise head toward Earth. More than 90 moons orbit Jupiter, including Ganymede, the largest moon in the solar system.","questions":[{"question":"In the passage, the word \"raging\" means?","choices":["moving gently","slowly fading away","acting with great force","shining brightly"],"answer":"acting with great force"},{"question":"Why can't a spacecraft land on Jupiter's surface?","choices":["Its moons block every path to the planet.","Its surface is covered with deep oceans.","Jupiter has no solid ground to land on.","Jupiter is too far for any spacecraft to see."],"answer":"Jupiter has no solid ground to land on."},{"question":"How might Jupiter help Earth?","choices":["Its light keeps Earth warm during the long winter.","Its winds push clouds toward Earth.","Its gravity pulls away some comets.","Its moons give Earth extra water."],"answer":"Its gravity pulls away some comets."}],"image_prompt":"A warm, simple flat-style children's-book illustration for a kids' reading app. Scene: Jupiter is the largest planet in our solar system, so big that more than 1,300 Earths could fit inside it. Focus on: the planet Jupiter and its Great Red Spot. Bright friendly colors, soft rounded shapes, no text or letters anywhere in the image, square 1:1 aspect ratio, cheerful and inviting for young readers."},{"text":"Almost everyone gets the hiccups now and then. A hiccup starts with the diaphragm, a dome-shaped muscle below your lungs that helps you breathe. Usually the diaphragm moves smoothly up and down. Sometimes, though, it has a spasm and suddenly tightens, pulling air into your lungs very quickly. A split second later, your vocal cords snap shut and stop the rush of air. That quick stop makes the \"hic\" sound. Hiccups are often set off by eating too fast, gulping fizzy drinks, or swallowing air when you are excited. Most cases go away on their own within a few minutes. Scientists are still not sure why people hiccup, but babies even hiccup before they are born.","questions":[{"question":"In the passage, the word \"spasm\" means?","choices":["a sudden tightening of a muscle","a slow, deep breath","a kind of fizzy drink","a bone in the chest"],"answer":"a sudden tightening of a muscle"},{"question":"What makes the \"hic\" sound?","choices":["The heart skips a beat.","Air rushes out of the stomach.","The lungs pop like a balloon.","The vocal cords snap shut."],"answer":"The vocal cords snap shut."},{"question":"Which would most likely give someone the hiccups?","choices":["sleeping late on a Saturday","taking a slow walk in the park","sitting quietly and reading a book","gulping a fizzy drink quickly"],"answer":"gulping a fizzy drink quickly"}],"image_prompt":"A warm, simple flat-style children's-book illustration for a kids' reading app. Scene: Almost everyone gets the hiccups now and then. Focus on: a child with the hiccups holding a fizzy drink. Bright friendly colors, soft rounded shapes, no text or letters anywhere in the image, square 1:1 aspect ratio, cheerful and inviting for young readers."},{"text":"Batteries power everything from flashlights to electric cars, but how do they work? A battery has three main parts: two ends made of different materials, called electrodes, and a substance between them called an electrolyte. When you connect a battery to a device, a chemical reaction begins. Tiny particles called electrons flow out of one end, through the device, and back into the other end. This flow of electrons is electricity. As the chemicals are used up, the battery weakens. In rechargeable batteries, plugging in a charger pushes the electrons back, resetting the chemicals so the battery can be used again. Because batteries contain metals and chemicals, they should be recycled instead of thrown in the trash.","questions":[{"question":"In the passage, the word \"substance\" means?","choices":["a type of battery","a part of a flashlight","a loud noise","a kind of material"],"answer":"a kind of material"},{"question":"According to the passage, what is electricity?","choices":["a kind of battery acid","a flow of electrons","the metal at each end","light from a bulb"],"answer":"a flow of electrons"},{"question":"What happens when a rechargeable battery is plugged in?","choices":["The battery melts and turns into new metal.","The battery becomes heavier and bigger.","The electrons escape into the air.","The chemicals are reset for reuse."],"answer":"The chemicals are reset for reuse."}],"image_prompt":"A warm, simple flat-style children's-book illustration for a kids' reading app. Scene: Batteries power everything from flashlights to electric cars, but how do they work? A battery has three main parts: two ends made of different materials, called electrodes, and a substance between them called an electrolyte. Focus on: a battery connected to a small glowing light bulb. Bright friendly colors, soft rounded shapes, no text or letters anywhere in the image, square 1:1 aspect ratio, cheerful and inviting for young readers."},{"text":"Long before refrigerators, people needed ways to keep food from spoiling. One clever method was fermentation. During fermentation, helpful microbes such as bacteria and yeast feed on sugars in food and change them into other substances. In yogurt, bacteria turn milk sugar into an acid that thickens the milk and gives it a tangy taste. The acid also makes it hard for harmful germs to grow, so the food lasts longer. Fermentation turns cabbage into sauerkraut and cucumbers into pickles. Yeast produces gas bubbles that make bread dough rise. Today, many people enjoy fermented foods for their flavors, and some scientists think the helpful microbes are good for digestion.","questions":[{"question":"In the passage, the word \"spoiling\" means?","choices":["becoming crunchy","going bad","getting colder","tasting sweeter"],"answer":"going bad"},{"question":"Why does fermented food last longer?","choices":["Yeast seals the food inside a hard shell.","The food is kept frozen the whole time.","The microbes add extra sugar that keeps food fresh.","The acid makes it hard for harmful germs to grow."],"answer":"The acid makes it hard for harmful germs to grow."},{"question":"What makes bread dough rise?","choices":["acid made by bacteria in milk","salt mixed with pickle juice","cold air from a refrigerator","gas bubbles made by yeast"],"answer":"gas bubbles made by yeast"}],"image_prompt":"A warm, simple flat-style children's-book illustration for a kids' reading app. Scene: Long before refrigerators, people needed ways to keep food from spoiling. Focus on: jars of yogurt, pickles and rising bread dough. Bright friendly colors, soft rounded shapes, no text or letters anywhere in the image, square 1:1 aspect ratio, cheerful and inviting for young readers."},{"text":"On steep mountainsides in the Philippines, there are giant green steps carved into the land. These are the Banaue Rice Terraces, built by the Ifugao people hundreds of years ago. Rice needs flat, flooded fields to grow, but mountains are anything but flat. So the builders dug level platforms into the slopes and supported them with walls of stone and mud. Water from the forests at the top flows down through a system of channels, filling one terrace and then spilling into the next. Some people call the terraces the \"Eighth Wonder of the World.\" Families still farm many of them today, passing down skills from parents to children.","questions":[{"question":"In the passage, the word \"level\" means?","choices":["wet and muddy","round and bumpy","flat and even","high and steep"],"answer":"flat and even"},{"question":"What problem did the builders solve?","choices":["The forests at the top had grown too thick.","Families had forgotten how to plant rice.","Rice needs flat fields, but the land was steep.","The streams froze."],"answer":"Rice needs flat fields, but the land was steep."},{"question":"How does water reach the lower terraces?","choices":["It falls as snow each spring.","It spills from one terrace into the next.","Pipes pump it from the ocean.","Farmers carry it up in buckets from the river."],"answer":"It spills from one terrace into the next."}],"image_prompt":"A warm, simple flat-style children's-book illustration for a kids' reading app. Scene: On steep mountainsides in the Philippines, there are giant green steps carved into the land. Focus on: green rice terraces stepping down a mountainside. Bright friendly colors, soft rounded shapes, no text or letters anywhere in the image, square 1:1 aspect ratio, cheerful and inviting for young readers."},{"text":"People have been making pottery for more than 20,000 years. Clay is a type of soil made of tiny mineral particles. When clay is wet, it is soft and easy to shape. When it dries, it becomes stiff but fragile, and if it gets wet again, it can turn back into mud. To make pottery strong and permanent, potters bake it in a kiln, an oven that can reach over 2,000 degrees Fahrenheit. The intense heat changes the clay so it can never become soft again. Many potters also coat their work with glaze, a thin layer of glassy material that melts in the kiln and makes the surface smooth and waterproof.","questions":[{"question":"In the passage, the word \"intense\" means?","choices":["barely warm","very gentle","very strong","quick and quiet"],"answer":"very strong"},{"question":"What can happen to dried clay that has not been baked if it gets wet?","choices":["It can turn back into mud.","It becomes hard as glass.","It grows larger and stronger.","It changes into a glaze."],"answer":"It can turn back into mud."},{"question":"Why do potters use glaze?","choices":["to make the clay soft so it can be reshaped","to keep the kiln from getting too hot","to turn the clay back into soil","to make the surface smooth and waterproof"],"answer":"to make the surface smooth and waterproof"}],"image_prompt":"A warm, simple flat-style children's-book illustration for a kids' reading app. Scene: People have been making pottery for more than 20,000 years. Focus on: a potter placing clay pots into a kiln. Bright friendly colors, soft rounded shapes, no text or letters anywhere in the image, square 1:1 aspect ratio, cheerful and inviting for young readers."},{"text":"About one-quarter of the Netherlands lies below sea level. For centuries, the Dutch have worked to keep the sea and rivers from flooding their land. Hundreds of years ago, they began building walls of earth called dikes. Then they used windmills to pump water out of the low, soggy areas behind the dikes and into canals. The dried land, called a polder, could be used for farms and towns. At one time, thousands of windmills dotted the Dutch countryside. Today, electric pumps do most of the work, but many old windmills still stand. A group of nineteen windmills at Kinderdijk is now a World Heritage Site that visitors come to see.","questions":[{"question":"In the passage, the word \"dotted\" means?","choices":["were hidden underground","were spread out here and there","were falling down slowly","were covered in small paint spots"],"answer":"were spread out here and there"},{"question":"What is a polder?","choices":["land drained of water behind dikes","a large windmill with four sails","a canal that carries ships to sea","a wall of earth that holds back the sea"],"answer":"land drained of water behind dikes"},{"question":"Why are windmills less important for pumping today?","choices":["The wind no longer blows in the Netherlands.","The sea has dried up completely.","Electric pumps do most of the work.","People moved all the towns to higher land."],"answer":"Electric pumps do most of the work."}],"image_prompt":"A warm, simple flat-style children's-book illustration for a kids' reading app. Scene: About one-quarter of the Netherlands lies below sea level. Focus on: Dutch windmills beside a canal. Bright friendly colors, soft rounded shapes, no text or letters anywhere in the image, square 1:1 aspect ratio, cheerful and inviting for young readers."},{"text":"The ostrich is the largest living bird, standing up to nine feet tall and weighing as much as two grown men. Although it cannot fly, the ostrich is built for speed. Its long, powerful legs can carry it at about 45 miles per hour, and a single stride can cover more than ten feet. Each foot has only two toes, which helps it run on the open plains of Africa. You may have heard that ostriches bury their heads in the sand when they are scared. This is a myth. Ostriches lay their eggs in holes in the ground and often lower their heads to turn the eggs, which may look like hiding from a distance.","questions":[{"question":"In the passage, the word \"myth\" means?","choices":["an idea that is not true","a long race","a type of nest","a fact proven by scientists"],"answer":"an idea that is not true"},{"question":"Why might people think ostriches bury their heads?","choices":["They hide from lions by digging tunnels.","They dig for water deep under the sand.","They sleep with their heads under their wings.","They lower their heads to turn eggs in holes."],"answer":"They lower their heads to turn eggs in holes."},{"question":"Which feature helps the ostrich run fast?","choices":["a long, curved neck","long, powerful legs","wide, strong wings","soft, fluffy feathers"],"answer":"long, powerful legs"}],"image_prompt":"A warm, simple flat-style children's-book illustration for a kids' reading app. Scene: The ostrich is the largest living bird, standing up to nine feet tall and weighing as much as two grown men. Focus on: an ostrich turning its eggs in a nest in the ground. Bright friendly colors, soft rounded shapes, no text or letters anywhere in the image, square 1:1 aspect ratio, cheerful and inviting for young readers."},{"text":"Sloths spend almost their entire lives hanging upside down in the rainforest trees of Central and South America. They move so slowly that green algae grows in their fur, which helps them blend in with the leaves. Their slow lifestyle is actually a clever survival plan. Sloths eat mostly leaves, which provide very little energy and take a long time to digest. By moving slowly and resting up to fifteen hours a day, sloths save energy. Their long, curved claws let them hang from branches without much effort. Surprisingly, sloths are strong swimmers and can hold their breath for up to forty minutes.","questions":[{"question":"In the passage, the word \"digest\" means?","choices":["hang upside down","find food in tall trees","break down food in the body","move slowly from branch to branch"],"answer":"break down food in the body"},{"question":"Why do sloths move so slowly?","choices":["Their claws are too long to let them walk.","Their leafy food gives them very little energy.","Algae makes them heavy.","They are afraid of falling into the water."],"answer":"Their leafy food gives them very little energy."},{"question":"Which detail from the passage is most surprising?","choices":["Sloths eat mostly leaves.","Sloths live in trees.","Sloths are strong swimmers.","Sloths have long claws on their feet."],"answer":"Sloths are strong swimmers."}],"image_prompt":"A warm, simple flat-style children's-book illustration for a kids' reading app. Scene: Sloths spend almost their entire lives hanging upside down in the rainforest trees of Central and South America. Focus on: a sloth hanging upside down from a rainforest branch. Bright friendly colors, soft rounded shapes, no text or letters anywhere in the image, square 1:1 aspect ratio, cheerful and inviting for young readers."},{"text":"Tigers are the largest wild cats on Earth. The biggest, the Siberian or Amur tiger, can weigh over 600 pounds. A tiger's stripes are not just on its fur; they are on its skin, too, and no two tigers have the same pattern. Unlike lions, which live in groups called prides, tigers are solitary. Each adult claims a territory and marks it with scent and scratches on trees. About a hundred years ago, there may have been 100,000 wild tigers. By 2010, only about 3,200 remained. Since then, countries have worked together to protect forests and stop illegal hunting, and tiger numbers have started to grow again in several places.","questions":[{"question":"In the passage, the word \"solitary\" means?","choices":["living in big groups","very fast","living alone","very loud"],"answer":"living alone"},{"question":"How do tigers mark their territory?","choices":["by leaving piles of stones","by building nests of grass","by roaring loudly from the top of hills","with scent and scratches on trees"],"answer":"with scent and scratches on trees"},{"question":"What can you conclude about tiger numbers?","choices":["Efforts to protect tigers are working.","Tigers no longer need any help.","Lions are taking over tiger forests.","There are too many tigers."],"answer":"Efforts to protect tigers are working."}],"photo":"images/flashcards/tiger.jpg"},{"text":"When the school announced a food drive, Marisol's class set a goal of collecting 300 cans in two weeks. By the end of the first week, they had only 85. Marisol noticed that many families walked past the collection box without stopping. \"Maybe they don't know what to bring,\" she said. She and her friends made a colorful poster that listed the foods the food bank needed most: peanut butter, canned beans, cereal, and soup. They also wrote a short note for the morning announcements. The next Monday, the box began to overflow. On the final day, the class counted 342 items. The food bank director thanked them and said their list had made a big difference.","questions":[{"question":"In the passage, the word \"overflow\" means?","choices":["be locked up for the weekend","be moved","become so full that things spill out","be carried away by a truck"],"answer":"become so full that things spill out"},{"question":"Why did Marisol think families walked past the box?","choices":["They were not sure what to bring.","They did not like the color of the box.","They had already given cans the week before.","They thought the drive was over."],"answer":"They were not sure what to bring."},{"question":"Did the class reach its goal?","choices":["No, they collected only 85 cans.","Yes, they collected exactly 300 cans.","No, they stopped after the first week.","Yes, they collected 342 items."],"answer":"Yes, they collected 342 items."}],"image_prompt":"A warm, simple flat-style children's-book illustration for a kids' reading app. Scene: When the school announced a food drive, Marisol's class set a goal of collecting 300 cans in two weeks. Focus on: students filling a food drive box with cans. Bright friendly colors, soft rounded shapes, no text or letters anywhere in the image, square 1:1 aspect ratio, cheerful and inviting for young readers."},{"text":"Dev had been building a model of the solar system for three weeks. On Thursday, his friend Sam knocked it off the table by accident, and the foam Saturn rolled under a desk with a crack. Dev was so upset that he refused to talk to Sam for the rest of the day. That night, Dev's grandmother told him about a time she had broken her sister's favorite vase. \"She forgave me, and we laughed about it for fifty years,\" she said. The next morning, Sam was waiting at Dev's locker with a tube of glue and a new foam ball. \"I painted it with rings last night,\" Sam said quietly. Dev took a deep breath and smiled. \"Want to help me fix the rest?\"","questions":[{"question":"In the passage, the word \"refused\" means?","choices":["forgot how to","was too tired to","was excited to","would not agree to"],"answer":"would not agree to"},{"question":"Why did Grandma tell the story about the vase?","choices":["to teach Dev how to glue broken things","to explain why she does not like vases anymore","to make Dev forget about his model","to show that forgiving can save a friendship"],"answer":"to show that forgiving can save a friendship"},{"question":"What does Dev's question at the end show?","choices":["He wants to give up on his model.","He is still angry at Sam.","He has forgiven Sam.","He wants Sam to do all the work."],"answer":"He has forgiven Sam."}],"image_prompt":"A warm, simple flat-style children's-book illustration for a kids' reading app. Scene: Dev had been building a model of the solar system for three weeks. Focus on: two boys fixing a solar system model together. Bright friendly colors, soft rounded shapes, no text or letters anywhere in the image, square 1:1 aspect ratio, cheerful and inviting for young readers."},{"text":"Lena had practiced her violin solo every day for a month, but on the night of the spring concert, her hands felt cold and shaky. Backstage, she peeked at the crowd and spotted her parents in the third row. \"What if I forget the notes?\" she whispered to Mr. Ortiz, the orchestra teacher. He smiled and said, \"Everyone gets butterflies. Take three slow breaths, and remember that you know this music.\" When her turn came, Lena closed her eyes for a moment, breathed slowly, and lifted her bow. Her first note wobbled, but then the melody flowed. When she finished, the audience clapped, and her dad stood up and cheered.","questions":[{"question":"In this passage, the word \"butterflies\" means?","choices":["pretty hair bows","nervous feelings","a kind of music","colorful insects with wings"],"answer":"nervous feelings"},{"question":"How did Lena feel before the concert?","choices":["nervous","angry","bored","sleepy"],"answer":"nervous"},{"question":"What helped Lena begin playing?","choices":["reading the notes from a book","waving to her parents in the third row","taking three slow breaths","asking Mr. Ortiz to play for her"],"answer":"taking three slow breaths"}],"photo":"images/flashcards/violin.jpg"},{"text":"Skunks are famous for their smelly spray, but they use it only as a last resort. A skunk carries just enough spray for about five or six blasts, and refilling its supply can take more than a week. Without spray, a skunk has few ways to defend itself, so it gives plenty of warnings first. It stamps its front feet, hisses, and raises its fluffy tail. A spotted skunk may even do a handstand! If the threat continues, the skunk twists its body and sprays an oily, yellowish liquid that can reach about ten feet. Skunks are actually helpful neighbors, since they eat insects, grubs, and mice that damage gardens.","questions":[{"question":"In the passage, the words \"last resort\" mean?","choices":["a favorite vacation spot","a place where skunks hide during the day","the first thing tried","the final choice when nothing else works"],"answer":"the final choice when nothing else works"},{"question":"Why does a skunk give warnings before spraying?","choices":["Its spray takes a long time to refill.","Its tail gets tired if it sprays often.","It wants other animals to play with it.","It cannot see who is nearby."],"answer":"Its spray takes a long time to refill."},{"question":"Why might a gardener be glad to see a skunk?","choices":["It scares birds.","It eats insects and grubs that harm plants.","It digs holes for planting seeds.","Its smell keeps the flowers fresh."],"answer":"It eats insects and grubs that harm plants."}],"image_prompt":"A warm, simple flat-style children's-book illustration for a kids' reading app. Scene: Skunks are famous for their smelly spray, but they use it only as a last resort. Focus on: a skunk doing a handstand as a warning. Bright friendly colors, soft rounded shapes, no text or letters anywhere in the image, square 1:1 aspect ratio, cheerful and inviting for young readers."},{"text":"In the Andes Mountains of South America, people have raised llamas for thousands of years. The ancient Inca relied on them to carry goods along mountain roads. A llama can carry about a quarter of its body weight over long distances, and if its pack is too heavy, it may simply lie down and refuse to move. Llamas are well suited to high altitudes. Their blood is especially good at carrying oxygen, which helps them breathe where the air is thin. Today, some farmers in North America use llamas to guard sheep and goats. A llama will sound an alarm call and chase away coyotes. Llamas communicate with humming sounds, and they mostly spit at other llamas when they are annoyed.","questions":[{"question":"In the passage, the word \"altitudes\" means?","choices":["deep mountain valleys","large herds of sheep and goats","heights above sea level","very cold winters"],"answer":"heights above sea level"},{"question":"What does a llama do if its pack is too heavy?","choices":["It hums loudly until help arrives.","It runs back to the farm.","It lies down and refuses to move.","It spits at the person who packed it."],"answer":"It lies down and refuses to move."},{"question":"Why do llamas make good guards for sheep?","choices":["They spit at sheep.","They sound alarms and chase away coyotes.","They are smaller than sheep and goats.","They can carry sheep on their backs."],"answer":"They sound alarms and chase away coyotes."}],"image_prompt":"A warm, simple flat-style children's-book illustration for a kids' reading app. Scene: In the Andes Mountains of South America, people have raised llamas for thousands of years. Focus on: a llama guarding a flock of sheep. Bright friendly colors, soft rounded shapes, no text or letters anywhere in the image, square 1:1 aspect ratio, cheerful and inviting for young readers."}]},"synonyms":{"easy":{"tidy":"neat","rip":"tear","slim":"thin","nibble":"bite","peek":"glance","snooze":"nap","mend":"fix","speedy":"quick"},"hard":{"brittle":"breakable","frail":"weak","lofty":"high","weary":"tired","gleeful":"joyful","vast":"huge","cherish":"treasure"}},"antonyms":{"easy":{"awake":"asleep","hollow":"solid","tame":"wild","thin":"thick","tiny":"huge","frown":"smile","always":"never","sink":"float"},"hard":{"vivid":"faded","sincere":"insincere","descend":"ascend","opaque":"transparent","exterior":"interior","wealthy":"poor","minority":"majority"}},"rhymes":{"easy":[["lamp","camp","damp","ramp","stamp"],["heel","peel","feel","wheel","kneel"],["jump","bump","lump","pump","dump"],["nest","best","rest","test","vest"]],"hard":[["tender","blender","sender","lender","spender"],["twinkle","sprinkle","wrinkle","crinkle"],["nibble","dribble","scribble","quibble"],["battle","cattle","rattle","tattle"]]},"analogies":[["hive","bee","den","bear"],["calf","cow","foal","horse"],["ink","pen","lead","pencil"],["lens","camera","string","guitar"],["igloo","ice","cabin","wood"],["pod","pea","shell","nut"],["roar","lion","hiss","snake"],["nest","robin","web","spider"]]},{"id":"2026-11-03-monthly-app","released":"2026-11-03","kind":"monthly","reading_passages":{"0":[{"text":"The rhino has thick gray skin. It has a big horn on its nose. A little bird rides on its back and eats the bugs.","questions":[{"question":"What is on the rhino's nose?","choices":["a big horn","a red spot","a long trunk","a shiny ring"],"answer":"a big horn"},{"question":"What does the little bird eat?","choices":["the grass","some seeds","the bugs","the leaves"],"answer":"the bugs"}],"image_prompt":"A warm, simple flat-style children's-book illustration for a kids' reading app. Scene: The rhino has thick gray skin. Focus on: a gray rhino with a small bird riding on its back. Bright friendly colors, soft rounded shapes, no text or letters anywhere in the image, square 1:1 aspect ratio, cheerful and inviting for young readers."},{"text":"The pig rolls in the mud on a hot day. The mud keeps its skin cool. Then the pig naps in the shade.","questions":[{"question":"Why does the pig roll in the mud?","choices":["to find a lost toy","to make the farmer laugh","to look for its friends","to keep its skin cool"],"answer":"to keep its skin cool"},{"question":"What does the pig do after that?","choices":["naps in the shade","eats a red apple","swims away","runs to the gate"],"answer":"naps in the shade"}],"photo":"images/flashcards/pig.jpg"},{"text":"Wild turkeys walk across the field. They scratch the dirt to find seeds and bugs. At night, they fly up into a tree to sleep.","questions":[{"question":"What do the turkeys look for in the dirt?","choices":["carrots and corn","shiny rocks","seeds and bugs","lost buttons and string"],"answer":"seeds and bugs"},{"question":"Where do the turkeys sleep?","choices":["inside the barn","in a hole","under the porch steps","up in a tree"],"answer":"up in a tree"}],"image_prompt":"A warm, simple flat-style children's-book illustration for a kids' reading app. Scene: Wild turkeys walk across the field. Focus on: wild turkeys scratching in a field. Bright friendly colors, soft rounded shapes, no text or letters anywhere in the image, square 1:1 aspect ratio, cheerful and inviting for young readers."},{"text":"A monkey swings from branch to branch. It holds on with its long tail. Then it peels a banana and eats it.","questions":[{"question":"What does the monkey hold on with?","choices":["its long tail","a rope","its teeth","its big ears"],"answer":"its long tail"},{"question":"What does the monkey eat?","choices":["an orange","a banana","a cookie","a carrot"],"answer":"a banana"}],"image_prompt":"A warm, simple flat-style children's-book illustration for a kids' reading app. Scene: A monkey swings from branch to branch. Focus on: a monkey hanging by its tail eating a banana. Bright friendly colors, soft rounded shapes, no text or letters anywhere in the image, square 1:1 aspect ratio, cheerful and inviting for young readers."},{"text":"The donkey carries two baskets on its back. One basket holds carrots. The other holds apples. The donkey says hee-haw!","questions":[{"question":"What does one basket hold?","choices":["flowers","eggs","blankets","carrots"],"answer":"carrots"},{"question":"What sound does the donkey make?","choices":["quack quack","moo","hee-haw","cock-a-doodle"],"answer":"hee-haw"}],"image_prompt":"A warm, simple flat-style children's-book illustration for a kids' reading app. Scene: The donkey carries two baskets on its back. Focus on: a donkey carrying two baskets of carrots and apples. Bright friendly colors, soft rounded shapes, no text or letters anywhere in the image, square 1:1 aspect ratio, cheerful and inviting for young readers."},{"text":"After the rain, worms wiggle out of the wet ground. Pia sees five worms on the sidewalk. She gently moves them back to the grass.","questions":[{"question":"When do the worms come out?","choices":["on a snowy morning","at lunch time","when the sun sets","after the rain"],"answer":"after the rain"},{"question":"Where does Pia move the worms?","choices":["into a jar","back to the grass","into her pocket","onto the porch steps"],"answer":"back to the grass"}],"image_prompt":"A warm, simple flat-style children's-book illustration for a kids' reading app. Scene: After the rain, worms wiggle out of the wet ground. Focus on: a girl gently moving worms from a wet sidewalk to grass. Bright friendly colors, soft rounded shapes, no text or letters anywhere in the image, square 1:1 aspect ratio, cheerful and inviting for young readers."},{"text":"A red bird sits on a snowy branch. It is called a cardinal. It cracks open sunflower seeds with its strong beak.","questions":[{"question":"What is the red bird called?","choices":["a seagull","a blue jay","a cardinal","a robin"],"answer":"a cardinal"},{"question":"What does the bird crack open?","choices":["pine cones","walnut shells","acorns","sunflower seeds"],"answer":"sunflower seeds"}],"image_prompt":"A warm, simple flat-style children's-book illustration for a kids' reading app. Scene: A red bird sits on a snowy branch. Focus on: a red cardinal on a snowy branch. Bright friendly colors, soft rounded shapes, no text or letters anywhere in the image, square 1:1 aspect ratio, cheerful and inviting for young readers."},{"text":"A green grasshopper sits in the tall grass. It has long back legs. It can jump very far, like a spring!","questions":[{"question":"What color is the grasshopper?","choices":["purple","yellow","green","brown"],"answer":"green"},{"question":"What helps the grasshopper jump far?","choices":["round feet","long back legs","big wings","a curly tail"],"answer":"long back legs"}],"image_prompt":"A warm, simple flat-style children's-book illustration for a kids' reading app. Scene: A green grasshopper sits in the tall grass. Focus on: a green grasshopper in tall grass. Bright friendly colors, soft rounded shapes, no text or letters anywhere in the image, square 1:1 aspect ratio, cheerful and inviting for young readers."},{"text":"The hippo spends the hot day in the river. Only its eyes and nose stick out of the water. At night, it walks on land to eat grass.","questions":[{"question":"What sticks out of the water?","choices":["its long tail","its four big feet","its eyes and nose","its ears and back"],"answer":"its eyes and nose"},{"question":"When does the hippo eat grass?","choices":["after a bath","at night","at lunch","in the morning"],"answer":"at night"}],"image_prompt":"A warm, simple flat-style children's-book illustration for a kids' reading app. Scene: The hippo spends the hot day in the river. Focus on: a hippo with only its eyes and nose above the river. Bright friendly colors, soft rounded shapes, no text or letters anywhere in the image, square 1:1 aspect ratio, cheerful and inviting for young readers."},{"text":"A tiny seahorse swims in the sea grass. It wraps its curly tail around the grass to hold on. Its head looks like a little horse!","questions":[{"question":"What does the seahorse hold on with?","choices":["its curly tail","its tiny teeth","its fins","its long nose"],"answer":"its curly tail"},{"question":"What does its head look like?","choices":["a turtle","a little horse","a round ball","a big fish"],"answer":"a little horse"}],"image_prompt":"A warm, simple flat-style children's-book illustration for a kids' reading app. Scene: A tiny seahorse swims in the sea grass. Focus on: a tiny seahorse holding sea grass with its curly tail. Bright friendly colors, soft rounded shapes, no text or letters anywhere in the image, square 1:1 aspect ratio, cheerful and inviting for young readers."},{"text":"An alligator rests in the warm sun. Its skin is bumpy and tough. A mother alligator keeps her eggs safe in a nest of mud and leaves.","questions":[{"question":"How does the alligator's skin feel?","choices":["smooth and wet","bumpy and tough","sticky","soft and fluffy"],"answer":"bumpy and tough"},{"question":"What is the nest made of?","choices":["snow and ice","mud and leaves","sand","sticks"],"answer":"mud and leaves"}],"image_prompt":"A warm, simple flat-style children's-book illustration for a kids' reading app. Scene: An alligator rests in the warm sun. Focus on: an alligator resting in the sun beside its nest. Bright friendly colors, soft rounded shapes, no text or letters anywhere in the image, square 1:1 aspect ratio, cheerful and inviting for young readers."},{"text":"Jon gets a haircut. He sits in a tall chair. The barber snips with scissors. Little bits of hair fall on the floor.","questions":[{"question":"Who cuts Jon's hair?","choices":["the doctor","his teacher","his big sister","the barber"],"answer":"the barber"},{"question":"What does the barber use?","choices":["a pencil","a paintbrush","a spoon","scissors"],"answer":"scissors"}],"image_prompt":"A warm, simple flat-style children's-book illustration for a kids' reading app. Scene: Jon gets a haircut. Focus on: a boy getting a haircut in a barber chair. Bright friendly colors, soft rounded shapes, no text or letters anywhere in the image, square 1:1 aspect ratio, cheerful and inviting for young readers."},{"text":"Dad and Gia wash the car. Gia scrubs the tires with a sponge. Dad sprays the soap off with the hose. The car is shiny now.","questions":[{"question":"What does Gia use to scrub?","choices":["a towel and a cup","a sponge","a paper bag","a broom"],"answer":"a sponge"},{"question":"How does the car look at the end?","choices":["muddy","wet and sticky","dusty and gray","shiny"],"answer":"shiny"}],"photo":"images/flashcards/car.jpg"},{"text":"Ari helps fold the laundry. He finds a red sock. He looks and looks for its match. It is hiding inside a shirt!","questions":[{"question":"What color is the sock?","choices":["blue","green","red","white"],"answer":"red"},{"question":"Where is the other sock?","choices":["on the roof","inside a shirt","in the dog's bowl","under the bed"],"answer":"inside a shirt"}],"photo":"images/flashcards/sock.jpg"},{"text":"Mom and Lee make a pumpkin pie. Lee stirs the orange filling. They bake it in the oven. The whole house smells like spice.","questions":[{"question":"What kind of pie do they make?","choices":["pumpkin","blueberry","cherry","apple"],"answer":"pumpkin"},{"question":"What does the house smell like?","choices":["spice","flowers","smoke","wet paint"],"answer":"spice"}],"image_prompt":"A warm, simple flat-style children's-book illustration for a kids' reading app. Scene: Mom and Lee make a pumpkin pie. Focus on: a mom and child making a pumpkin pie. Bright friendly colors, soft rounded shapes, no text or letters anywhere in the image, square 1:1 aspect ratio, cheerful and inviting for young readers."},{"text":"The family sits down for a big dinner. Each person says one thing they are thankful for. Ty says he is thankful for his grandma.","questions":[{"question":"Who is Ty thankful for?","choices":["his goldfish","his grandma","his teacher","his new bike"],"answer":"his grandma"},{"question":"What does each person say?","choices":["their favorite song","a story about their pet","one thing they are thankful for","a funny joke about turkey"],"answer":"one thing they are thankful for"}],"image_prompt":"A warm, simple flat-style children's-book illustration for a kids' reading app. Scene: The family sits down for a big dinner. Focus on: a family at a dinner table sharing what they are thankful for. Bright friendly colors, soft rounded shapes, no text or letters anywhere in the image, square 1:1 aspect ratio, cheerful and inviting for young readers."},{"text":"Nell has the hiccups. Hic! Hic! She drinks a glass of water very slowly. Soon the hiccups stop.","questions":[{"question":"What does Nell have?","choices":["the hiccups","a new puppy","a cold","a sore toe"],"answer":"the hiccups"},{"question":"What does Nell drink?","choices":["milk","water","hot cocoa","juice"],"answer":"water"}],"image_prompt":"A warm, simple flat-style children's-book illustration for a kids' reading app. Scene: Nell has the hiccups. Focus on: a girl slowly sipping a glass of water. Bright friendly colors, soft rounded shapes, no text or letters anywhere in the image, square 1:1 aspect ratio, cheerful and inviting for young readers."},{"text":"Raj rolls blue play dough into a long snake. He gives it two tiny eyes. Then he makes a little hat for it.","questions":[{"question":"What does Raj make?","choices":["a round ball","a flat pancake","a long snake","a tall tower"],"answer":"a long snake"},{"question":"What color is the play dough?","choices":["red","blue","pink","yellow"],"answer":"blue"}],"image_prompt":"A warm, simple flat-style children's-book illustration for a kids' reading app. Scene: Raj rolls blue play dough into a long snake. Focus on: a boy shaping a blue play-dough snake with a little hat. Bright friendly colors, soft rounded shapes, no text or letters anywhere in the image, square 1:1 aspect ratio, cheerful and inviting for young readers."},{"text":"Bea holds a flashlight. Dad puts his hands in the light. A shadow shaped like a bird flaps on the wall!","questions":[{"question":"What does Bea hold?","choices":["a crayon","a candle","a mirror","a flashlight"],"answer":"a flashlight"},{"question":"What shape is the shadow?","choices":["a dog","a tall tree","a bird","a star"],"answer":"a bird"}],"image_prompt":"A warm, simple flat-style children's-book illustration for a kids' reading app. Scene: Bea holds a flashlight. Focus on: a hand shadow shaped like a bird on a wall. Bright friendly colors, soft rounded shapes, no text or letters anywhere in the image, square 1:1 aspect ratio, cheerful and inviting for young readers."},{"text":"Ming puts an ice cube on a plate in the sun. Drip, drip. The ice cube melts. Soon there is just a puddle of water.","questions":[{"question":"Where is the plate?","choices":["in the sun","in a cup of juice","in the freezer","under her pillow"],"answer":"in the sun"},{"question":"What is left at the end?","choices":["a snowball","a puddle of water","a tiny snowman","a pile of sand"],"answer":"a puddle of water"}],"photo":"images/flashcards/ice.jpg"},{"text":"Kofi has a magnet. He holds it near some paper clips. Click! The clips jump up and stick to it.","questions":[{"question":"What sticks to the magnet?","choices":["cotton balls","leaves","crayons","paper clips"],"answer":"paper clips"},{"question":"What sound do you hear?","choices":["splash","boom","quack","click"],"answer":"click"}],"image_prompt":"A warm, simple flat-style children's-book illustration for a kids' reading app. Scene: Kofi has a magnet. Focus on: a magnet picking up paper clips. Bright friendly colors, soft rounded shapes, no text or letters anywhere in the image, square 1:1 aspect ratio, cheerful and inviting for young readers."},{"text":"Two friends turn a long jump rope. June jumps in the middle. She counts each jump. One, two, three, all the way to ten!","questions":[{"question":"Who jumps in the middle?","choices":["June","Grandpa","the teacher","the dog"],"answer":"June"},{"question":"How high does June count?","choices":["to ten","to five","to two","to one hundred"],"answer":"to ten"}],"image_prompt":"A warm, simple flat-style children's-book illustration for a kids' reading app. Scene: Two friends turn a long jump rope. Focus on: two kids turning a jump rope while a girl jumps. Bright friendly colors, soft rounded shapes, no text or letters anywhere in the image, square 1:1 aspect ratio, cheerful and inviting for young readers."},{"text":"Lulu draws squares on the sidewalk with chalk. She writes a number in each one. Then she tosses a stone and hops to it.","questions":[{"question":"What does Lulu draw with?","choices":["a marker","chalk","paint","a stick"],"answer":"chalk"},{"question":"What does Lulu toss?","choices":["a pinecone","a leaf","a ball","a stone"],"answer":"a stone"}],"image_prompt":"A warm, simple flat-style children's-book illustration for a kids' reading app. Scene: Lulu draws squares on the sidewalk with chalk. Focus on: a girl playing hopscotch with chalk squares. Bright friendly colors, soft rounded shapes, no text or letters anywhere in the image, square 1:1 aspect ratio, cheerful and inviting for young readers."},{"text":"On a cold morning, Mel sees frost on the window. It looks like white feathers. When the sun comes up, the frost melts away.","questions":[{"question":"What does the frost look like?","choices":["green leaves","white feathers","brown sticks","blue bubbles"],"answer":"white feathers"},{"question":"What makes the frost melt?","choices":["a big dog","the sun","the wind","the rain"],"answer":"the sun"}],"image_prompt":"A warm, simple flat-style children's-book illustration for a kids' reading app. Scene: On a cold morning, Mel sees frost on the window. Focus on: frost patterns like white feathers on a window. Bright friendly colors, soft rounded shapes, no text or letters anywhere in the image, square 1:1 aspect ratio, cheerful and inviting for young readers."},{"text":"Ada and her grandma ride on an airplane. Ada looks out the small window. The clouds look like soft white pillows below her.","questions":[{"question":"Who rides with Ada?","choices":["her teacher","her cat","her grandma","her best friend"],"answer":"her grandma"},{"question":"What do the clouds look like?","choices":["pink candy","soft white pillows","tall green trees","big gray rocks"],"answer":"soft white pillows"}],"image_prompt":"A warm, simple flat-style children's-book illustration for a kids' reading app. Scene: Ada and her grandma ride on an airplane. Focus on: a girl and her grandma looking out an airplane window at clouds. Bright friendly colors, soft rounded shapes, no text or letters anywhere in the image, square 1:1 aspect ratio, cheerful and inviting for young readers."},{"text":"An ambulance drives fast down the street. Its lights flash and its siren goes wee-oo! Cars move over to let it pass.","questions":[{"question":"What do the cars do?","choices":["honk loudly","drive faster","move over","turn around"],"answer":"move over"},{"question":"What sound does the siren make?","choices":["beep beep","wee-oo","moo","ding dong"],"answer":"wee-oo"}],"image_prompt":"A warm, simple flat-style children's-book illustration for a kids' reading app. Scene: An ambulance drives fast down the street. Focus on: an ambulance with flashing lights as cars pull over. Bright friendly colors, soft rounded shapes, no text or letters anywhere in the image, square 1:1 aspect ratio, cheerful and inviting for young readers."},{"text":"A tall crane stands next to a new building. Its long arm lifts heavy beams up, up, up. A worker sits in a small cab and moves the levers.","questions":[{"question":"What does the crane lift?","choices":["cars","big boxes of toys","a small boat","heavy beams"],"answer":"heavy beams"},{"question":"Where does the worker sit?","choices":["on the roof","in a big truck","under the arm","in a small cab"],"answer":"in a small cab"}],"image_prompt":"A warm, simple flat-style children's-book illustration for a kids' reading app. Scene: A tall crane stands next to a new building. Focus on: a tall construction crane lifting beams. Bright friendly colors, soft rounded shapes, no text or letters anywhere in the image, square 1:1 aspect ratio, cheerful and inviting for young readers."},{"text":"A cement mixer comes to the new house. Its big drum spins round and round. Workers pour the wet cement to make a sidewalk.","questions":[{"question":"What spins round and round?","choices":["its front door","its yellow ladder","its seat","its big drum"],"answer":"its big drum"},{"question":"What do the workers make?","choices":["a sidewalk","a garden","a tall fence","a swimming pool"],"answer":"a sidewalk"}],"image_prompt":"A warm, simple flat-style children's-book illustration for a kids' reading app. Scene: A cement mixer comes to the new house. Focus on: a cement mixer truck at a new house. Bright friendly colors, soft rounded shapes, no text or letters anywhere in the image, square 1:1 aspect ratio, cheerful and inviting for young readers."},{"text":"Tia rinses the empty cans. She puts them in the blue recycling bin. The bin also gets paper and plastic bottles.","questions":[{"question":"What color is the bin?","choices":["blue","orange","red","black"],"answer":"blue"},{"question":"What else goes in the bin?","choices":["toys and socks","paper and plastic bottles","banana peels and bones","old shoes and socks"],"answer":"paper and plastic bottles"}],"image_prompt":"A warm, simple flat-style children's-book illustration for a kids' reading app. Scene: Tia rinses the empty cans. Focus on: a child putting cans in a blue recycling bin. Bright friendly colors, soft rounded shapes, no text or letters anywhere in the image, square 1:1 aspect ratio, cheerful and inviting for young readers."},{"text":"It is time to clean up. Owen puts the blocks in a box. He puts the books on the shelf. His room looks neat.","questions":[{"question":"Where does Owen put the books?","choices":["in the box","in the bathtub","under the rug","on the shelf"],"answer":"on the shelf"},{"question":"How does his room look at the end?","choices":["wet","dark","neat","messy"],"answer":"neat"}],"image_prompt":"A warm, simple flat-style children's-book illustration for a kids' reading app. Scene: It is time to clean up. Focus on: a boy tidying blocks and books in his room. Bright friendly colors, soft rounded shapes, no text or letters anywhere in the image, square 1:1 aspect ratio, cheerful and inviting for young readers."}],"1":[{"text":"On November 11, our country celebrates Veterans Day. A veteran is a person who served in the military, such as the Army or the Navy. Ms. Ford's class wanted to say thank you. Each student made a card with a flag and a kind message. Lily wrote, \"Thank you for keeping us safe.\" The class mailed the cards to a veterans' home. A week later, a letter came back. The veterans said the cards made them smile.","questions":[{"question":"What is a veteran?","choices":["a person who delivers mail to homes","a person who builds houses and roads","a doctor who takes care of pets","a person who served in the military"],"answer":"a person who served in the military"},{"question":"How did the veterans feel about the cards?","choices":["They were bored.","They smiled.","They were confused.","They felt angry."],"answer":"They smiled."}],"image_prompt":"A warm, simple flat-style children's-book illustration for a kids' reading app. Scene: On November 11, our country celebrates Veterans Day. Focus on: students making thank-you cards with flags for veterans. Bright friendly colors, soft rounded shapes, no text or letters anywhere in the image, square 1:1 aspect ratio, cheerful and inviting for young readers."},{"text":"In early November, many families set their clocks back one hour. This is the end of daylight saving time. Kayla was excited, because it meant one extra hour of sleep! But that evening, the sky grew dark before dinner. \"Why is it so dark already?\" she asked. Her dad explained that the sun had not changed. Only the clocks had. Now mornings are brighter, but evenings get dark earlier.","questions":[{"question":"How many hours do the clocks go back?","choices":["two","twelve","one","five"],"answer":"one"},{"question":"What did Kayla notice that evening?","choices":["The moon was bright green.","The lights went out.","Her clock had stopped ticking.","The sky got dark before dinner."],"answer":"The sky got dark before dinner."},{"question":"What changed, according to Dad?","choices":["the sun","the size of the Earth","the moon and stars","only the clocks"],"answer":"only the clocks"}],"image_prompt":"A warm, simple flat-style children's-book illustration for a kids' reading app. Scene: In early November, many families set their clocks back one hour. Focus on: a girl looking at a dark evening sky while a clock is set back. Bright friendly colors, soft rounded shapes, no text or letters anywhere in the image, square 1:1 aspect ratio, cheerful and inviting for young readers."},{"text":"On Election Day, Sam went with his mom to vote. They walked to the school gym, where voting booths stood in a long row. Mom showed a card with her name on it. Then she stepped into a booth and marked her choices on a paper ballot. Sam waited quietly. When Mom finished, a helper handed her a sticker that said \"I Voted.\" Mom gave Sam a sticker too. \"Someday you will vote,\" she said.","questions":[{"question":"Where did Sam and his mom go to vote?","choices":["the school gym","the post office","the town library","the grocery store"],"answer":"the school gym"},{"question":"What did the helper give Mom?","choices":["a balloon","a new pencil","a sticker","a cookie"],"answer":"a sticker"}],"image_prompt":"A warm, simple flat-style children's-book illustration for a kids' reading app. Scene: On Election Day, Sam went with his mom to vote. Focus on: a boy and his mom with I Voted stickers at a school gym. Bright friendly colors, soft rounded shapes, no text or letters anywhere in the image, square 1:1 aspect ratio, cheerful and inviting for young readers."},{"text":"Every fall, Canada geese fly south to find warmer weather and food. They fly in a shape like the letter V. The bird at the front works the hardest, pushing through the wind. The birds behind it have an easier time. When the leader gets tired, it moves to the back, and another goose takes the front. By sharing the hard job, the whole flock can fly a very long way.","questions":[{"question":"What shape do the geese fly in?","choices":["a square","the letter O","the letter V","a circle"],"answer":"the letter V"},{"question":"What happens when the leader gets tired?","choices":["It sings.","It lands on a roof.","It moves to the back.","It flies home alone."],"answer":"It moves to the back."},{"question":"Why do geese fly south in the fall?","choices":["to look for new friends","to find warmer weather and food","to find a quiet place to sleep","to visit the ocean waves"],"answer":"to find warmer weather and food"}],"image_prompt":"A warm, simple flat-style children's-book illustration for a kids' reading app. Scene: Every fall, Canada geese fly south to find warmer weather and food. Focus on: Canada geese flying in a V shape. Bright friendly colors, soft rounded shapes, no text or letters anywhere in the image, square 1:1 aspect ratio, cheerful and inviting for young readers."},{"text":"Cranberries grow on low vines in wet, sandy fields called bogs. In the fall, farmers flood the bogs with water. The berries have tiny pockets of air inside, so they float to the top. Soon the water looks like a red carpet! Farmers push the floating berries together and scoop them up. Many families enjoy cranberry sauce at Thanksgiving dinner.","questions":[{"question":"What are the wet cranberry fields called?","choices":["barns","hills","bogs","orchards"],"answer":"bogs"},{"question":"Why do the cranberries float?","choices":["They have air pockets inside.","They are covered in oil.","Farmers tie strings on them.","They are very heavy."],"answer":"They have air pockets inside."}],"image_prompt":"A warm, simple flat-style children's-book illustration for a kids' reading app. Scene: Cranberries grow on low vines in wet, sandy fields called bogs. Focus on: floating red cranberries in a flooded bog. Bright friendly colors, soft rounded shapes, no text or letters anywhere in the image, square 1:1 aspect ratio, cheerful and inviting for young readers."},{"text":"Sweet potatoes grow under the ground. Their leafy vines spread out above the dirt. When fall comes, farmers dig up the roots. Some sweet potatoes are orange inside, but others are white or even purple! They are full of vitamins that help your eyes and skin stay healthy. Leo's family bakes them with a little cinnamon on top.","questions":[{"question":"Where do sweet potatoes grow?","choices":["under the ground","in the ocean","inside jars","high on trees"],"answer":"under the ground"},{"question":"What colors can sweet potatoes be inside?","choices":["black or silver","pink, blue, or gray","orange, white, or purple","green and black"],"answer":"orange, white, or purple"}],"image_prompt":"A warm, simple flat-style children's-book illustration for a kids' reading app. Scene: Sweet potatoes grow under the ground. Focus on: sweet potatoes being dug from the ground. Bright friendly colors, soft rounded shapes, no text or letters anywhere in the image, square 1:1 aspect ratio, cheerful and inviting for young readers."},{"text":"Owls swallow small animals whole. Their bodies cannot break down the bones and fur, so they cough up a small gray lump called a pellet. In Mr. Tran's class, students used tweezers to pull apart owl pellets. They found tiny bones inside! By matching the bones to a chart, they learned what the owl had eaten. Most pellets held the bones of mice.","questions":[{"question":"What is an owl pellet?","choices":["a nest made of twigs","a kind of owl egg","an owl's feather","a lump of bones and fur"],"answer":"a lump of bones and fur"},{"question":"What did students use to pull apart the pellets?","choices":["tweezers","spoons","a hammer","scissors"],"answer":"tweezers"},{"question":"Whose bones were in most pellets?","choices":["mice","snakes","fish","frogs"],"answer":"mice"}],"photo":"images/flashcards/owl.jpg"},{"text":"Have you ever wondered how a pencil is made? The gray center is not lead at all. It is made of graphite mixed with clay. Workers place thin sticks of it between two strips of wood, then glue the strips together. A machine cuts them into pencils. Last, each pencil gets paint, a metal band, and a pink eraser on top.","questions":[{"question":"What is the center of a pencil made of?","choices":["lead and sand","graphite and clay","melted crayons","paint and glue"],"answer":"graphite and clay"},{"question":"What goes on top of a pencil?","choices":["a paper flag","a tiny light","a pink eraser","a sharp point"],"answer":"a pink eraser"}],"image_prompt":"A warm, simple flat-style children's-book illustration for a kids' reading app. Scene: Have you ever wondered how a pencil is made? The gray center is not lead at all. Focus on: how a pencil is made from graphite and wood. Bright friendly colors, soft rounded shapes, no text or letters anywhere in the image, square 1:1 aspect ratio, cheerful and inviting for young readers."},{"text":"Crayons are made from wax. At a crayon factory, the wax is melted until it becomes a hot liquid. Workers add colored powder to make each shade. Then they pour the colored wax into molds shaped like long, thin sticks. Cold water cools the molds so the wax turns hard. Finally, a paper label is wrapped around each crayon.","questions":[{"question":"What are crayons made from?","choices":["wood","chalk","wax","clay"],"answer":"wax"},{"question":"What helps the wax turn hard?","choices":["bright lights","loud music","cold water","hot air"],"answer":"cold water"}],"image_prompt":"A warm, simple flat-style children's-book illustration for a kids' reading app. Scene: Crayons are made from wax. Focus on: colorful crayons being made in molds. Bright friendly colors, soft rounded shapes, no text or letters anywhere in the image, square 1:1 aspect ratio, cheerful and inviting for young readers."},{"text":"Tomas has taken karate lessons for a year. Today is his belt test. His hands feel sweaty as he bows to his teacher. He shows his kicks, blocks, and punches, one by one. On the last move, he wobbles but keeps his balance. His teacher smiles and ties a new yellow belt around his waist. Tomas grins. All his practice paid off.","questions":[{"question":"How did Tomas most likely feel at the start of the test?","choices":["silly","furious","hungry","nervous"],"answer":"nervous"},{"question":"What color is Tomas's new belt?","choices":["orange","green","black","yellow"],"answer":"yellow"}],"image_prompt":"A warm, simple flat-style children's-book illustration for a kids' reading app. Scene: Tomas has taken karate lessons for a year. Focus on: a boy receiving a yellow karate belt. Bright friendly colors, soft rounded shapes, no text or letters anywhere in the image, square 1:1 aspect ratio, cheerful and inviting for young readers."},{"text":"The balance beam is only four inches wide. That is about as wide as a phone. At gymnastics class, Rosa practices walking on a low beam close to the floor. She keeps her arms out and her eyes looking ahead. At first she stepped off a lot. Now she can walk the whole beam and even turn around. Next, she will try a small hop.","questions":[{"question":"How wide is the balance beam?","choices":["four inches","two feet","a yard","one inch"],"answer":"four inches"},{"question":"What will Rosa try next?","choices":["a handstand","a small hop","a cartwheel","a big flip"],"answer":"a small hop"}],"image_prompt":"A warm, simple flat-style children's-book illustration for a kids' reading app. Scene: The balance beam is only four inches wide. Focus on: a girl walking on a low balance beam. Bright friendly colors, soft rounded shapes, no text or letters anywhere in the image, square 1:1 aspect ratio, cheerful and inviting for young readers."},{"text":"Jamal had his first tennis lesson on Saturday. The coach showed him how to hold the racket, like shaking someone's hand. At first, Jamal swung too early, and the ball flew over the fence! The coach told him to watch the ball until it touched his strings. Jamal tried again and again. By the end of the hour, he hit the ball over the net five times in a row.","questions":[{"question":"How should Jamal hold the racket?","choices":["like holding a spoon of soup","with both feet","like shaking someone's hand","with only two fingers"],"answer":"like shaking someone's hand"},{"question":"What happened when Jamal swung too early?","choices":["The racket broke in half.","The ball flew over the fence.","The coach fell down.","The net fell over."],"answer":"The ball flew over the fence."},{"question":"How many times in a row did Jamal hit the ball over the net?","choices":["five","ten","two","twenty"],"answer":"five"}],"image_prompt":"A warm, simple flat-style children's-book illustration for a kids' reading app. Scene: Jamal had his first tennis lesson on Saturday. Focus on: a boy hitting a tennis ball over a net. Bright friendly colors, soft rounded shapes, no text or letters anywhere in the image, square 1:1 aspect ratio, cheerful and inviting for young readers."},{"text":"A traffic light helps cars and people take turns at a busy corner. Red means stop. Yellow means the light is about to change, so drivers slow down. Green means go. Many corners also have a walk signal for people. When it shows a little walking person, it is safe to cross. Even then, Ivan's mom reminds him to look both ways first.","questions":[{"question":"What does a yellow light mean?","choices":["Cars should go faster.","The light is about to change.","Cars must park.","People can cross now."],"answer":"The light is about to change."},{"question":"What does Ivan's mom remind him to do?","choices":["hold his breath","wave at the cars","run very fast","look both ways"],"answer":"look both ways"}],"image_prompt":"A warm, simple flat-style children's-book illustration for a kids' reading app. Scene: A traffic light helps cars and people take turns at a busy corner. Focus on: a traffic light and a walk signal at a corner. Bright friendly colors, soft rounded shapes, no text or letters anywhere in the image, square 1:1 aspect ratio, cheerful and inviting for young readers."},{"text":"Ella's grandparents live far away, across the ocean. Every Sunday, Ella talks to them on a video call. She holds up the tablet so they can see her drawings. Grandpa shows her the lemon tree in his yard. Because of the time difference, it is morning for Ella but evening for her grandparents. Even though they are far apart, the calls make them feel close.","questions":[{"question":"When does Ella talk to her grandparents?","choices":["every Sunday","every night","once a year","on her birthday"],"answer":"every Sunday"},{"question":"What does Grandpa show Ella?","choices":["his garden hose","his pet parrot","his lemon tree","his new car"],"answer":"his lemon tree"},{"question":"How do the calls make them feel?","choices":["close","worried","tired","lonely"],"answer":"close"}],"image_prompt":"A warm, simple flat-style children's-book illustration for a kids' reading app. Scene: Ella's grandparents live far away, across the ocean. Focus on: a girl on a video call with her grandparents. Bright friendly colors, soft rounded shapes, no text or letters anywhere in the image, square 1:1 aspect ratio, cheerful and inviting for young readers."},{"text":"When baby Nora came home, Jack felt left out. Everyone wanted to hold her, and Mom was always busy. One night, Nora would not stop crying. Jack sang the silly song Dad used to sing to him. Nora stopped crying and stared at him. Mom smiled. \"You are a great big brother,\" she said. After that, Jack sang to Nora every night.","questions":[{"question":"How did Jack feel when Nora first came home?","choices":["left out","very proud","sleepy","excited"],"answer":"left out"},{"question":"What made Nora stop crying?","choices":["a warm bottle","her favorite blanket","Jack's silly song","a ride in the car"],"answer":"Jack's silly song"}],"image_prompt":"A warm, simple flat-style children's-book illustration for a kids' reading app. Scene: When baby Nora came home, Jack felt left out. Focus on: a boy singing to his baby sister. Bright friendly colors, soft rounded shapes, no text or letters anywhere in the image, square 1:1 aspect ratio, cheerful and inviting for young readers."},{"text":"Ava's family moved to a new town. Her old house had a big tree she loved to climb. The new house had no tree at all, and Ava felt sad. The next day, a girl next door knocked with a plate of muffins. Her name was Jade. Jade showed Ava a park with the best climbing tree on the street. Ava began to feel at home.","questions":[{"question":"What did Ava miss about her old house?","choices":["the blue door","her old bedroom","the big tree","the pool"],"answer":"the big tree"},{"question":"What did Jade bring?","choices":["a box of toys","a plate of muffins","a new puppy","a basket of flowers"],"answer":"a plate of muffins"}],"image_prompt":"A warm, simple flat-style children's-book illustration for a kids' reading app. Scene: Ava's family moved to a new town. Focus on: two girls by a big climbing tree in a park. Bright friendly colors, soft rounded shapes, no text or letters anywhere in the image, square 1:1 aspect ratio, cheerful and inviting for young readers."},{"text":"Wes is great at the matching game, where you flip cards to find pairs. He almost always wins. Today his little cousin Ben found more pairs than he did. Wes's face felt hot, and he wanted to toss the cards. Instead, he took a deep breath. \"Good game,\" he said, and shook Ben's hand. Ben beamed. Wes realized that losing was not so terrible. Then he asked to play again.","questions":[{"question":"What did Wes do instead of tossing the cards?","choices":["took a deep breath","yelled at Ben","ran to his room","hid under the table"],"answer":"took a deep breath"},{"question":"What did Wes ask at the end?","choices":["for a snack","to watch a movie","to play again","to go outside"],"answer":"to play again"}],"image_prompt":"A warm, simple flat-style children's-book illustration for a kids' reading app. Scene: Wes is great at the matching game, where you flip cards to find pairs. Focus on: two boys playing a card matching game. Bright friendly colors, soft rounded shapes, no text or letters anywhere in the image, square 1:1 aspect ratio, cheerful and inviting for young readers."},{"text":"Pepper the cat went to the vet for her yearly checkup. The vet listened to her heart and looked in her ears. She checked Pepper's teeth too. Then Pepper stepped on a scale. She weighed ten pounds. \"She is healthy,\" said the vet. Pepper got a quick shot to keep her from getting sick. Then she curled up in her carrier, ready to go home.","questions":[{"question":"How much did Pepper weigh?","choices":["two pounds","one pound","ten pounds","fifty pounds"],"answer":"ten pounds"},{"question":"Why did Pepper get a shot?","choices":["to make her fur shiny","to make her grow taller","to help her sleep at night","to keep her from getting sick"],"answer":"to keep her from getting sick"}],"photo":"images/flashcards/cat.jpg"},{"text":"Drip, drip, drip! The kitchen faucet at Mia's house would not stop leaking. Mom called a plumber. A plumber is someone who fixes pipes and sinks. Mr. Ruiz arrived with a heavy toolbox. He turned off the water under the sink. Then he found a worn-out rubber ring inside the faucet and replaced it. The dripping stopped. Mia thanked him for saving water.","questions":[{"question":"What does a plumber do?","choices":["paints houses","fixes pipes and sinks","bakes bread and rolls","delivers the mail"],"answer":"fixes pipes and sinks"},{"question":"What was causing the leak?","choices":["a worn-out rubber ring","a cracked plate","a hole in the roof","a broken window latch"],"answer":"a worn-out rubber ring"}],"image_prompt":"A warm, simple flat-style children's-book illustration for a kids' reading app. Scene: Drip, drip, drip! The kitchen faucet at Mia's house would not stop leaking. Focus on: a plumber fixing a kitchen faucet. Bright friendly colors, soft rounded shapes, no text or letters anywhere in the image, square 1:1 aspect ratio, cheerful and inviting for young readers."},{"text":"A lighthouse stands on a rocky shore to help ships stay safe at night. At the top is a very bright lamp and a large glass lens. The lens turns slowly, so the beam sweeps across the dark water like a giant flashlight. Sailors can see the light from many miles away. When they spot it, they know there are rocks nearby and steer their ships to safety.","questions":[{"question":"Why is a lighthouse built on a rocky shore?","choices":["To help ships stay safe at night","To give sailors a place to sleep","To store fish for the winter","To make the waves smaller"],"answer":"To help ships stay safe at night"},{"question":"What does the glass lens do?","choices":["It sends messages to other ships by radio","It keeps the lamp from getting rained on at night","It makes the tower stand up straight","It turns so the beam sweeps across the water"],"answer":"It turns so the beam sweeps across the water"},{"question":"What do sailors do when they see the light?","choices":["They sail straight toward the shore","They drop anchor and go to sleep right there","They steer away from the rocks","They turn off their own lights"],"answer":"They steer away from the rocks"}]},{"text":"On a chilly morning, Leah breathed out and saw a puff of white cloud! Her breath is always warm and a little wet, even if she cannot see it. When warm, wet air hits very cold air, the water in it turns into tiny drops. Those drops make the little cloud. On a warm day, the water stays invisible, so Leah cannot see her breath at all.","questions":[{"question":"What did Leah see when she breathed out?","choices":["a puff of white cloud","a swirl of gray smoke","a tiny rainbow","sparkly snowflakes"],"answer":"a puff of white cloud"},{"question":"When can Leah NOT see her breath?","choices":["when it snows","on a warm day","in the morning","on a cold day"],"answer":"on a warm day"}],"image_prompt":"A warm, simple flat-style children's-book illustration for a kids' reading app. Scene: On a chilly morning, Leah breathed out and saw a puff of white cloud! Her breath is always warm and a little wet, even if she cannot see it. Focus on: a girl seeing her breath as a white puff on a cold morning. Bright friendly colors, soft rounded shapes, no text or letters anywhere in the image, square 1:1 aspect ratio, cheerful and inviting for young readers."},{"text":"Why do trees drop their leaves in the fall? Leaves need lots of water and sunlight. In winter, days are short and the ground may freeze, so there is less water and sunlight. To save energy, trees like maples and oaks let their leaves fall. A small bud stays on each branch. When spring comes, new green leaves grow from those buds.","questions":[{"question":"Why do trees let their leaves fall?","choices":["because the wind is mad","to make room for birds","so squirrels can play","to save energy"],"answer":"to save energy"},{"question":"Where do new leaves grow from in spring?","choices":["the roots","old leaves","small buds","acorns"],"answer":"small buds"}],"image_prompt":"A warm, simple flat-style children's-book illustration for a kids' reading app. Scene: Why do trees drop their leaves in the fall? Leaves need lots of water and sunlight. Focus on: trees dropping fall leaves with buds on branches. Bright friendly colors, soft rounded shapes, no text or letters anywhere in the image, square 1:1 aspect ratio, cheerful and inviting for young readers."},{"text":"The arctic fox lives in the far north, where winters are long and snowy. In winter, its thick fur turns white, so it blends in with the snow. In summer, when the snow melts, its fur turns brown or gray to match the rocks. Its small ears and furry paws help it stay warm. It can even hear animals moving under the snow.","questions":[{"question":"What color is the fox's fur in winter?","choices":["white","black","brown","red"],"answer":"white"},{"question":"Why does its fur change color?","choices":["to match the snow or rocks","to look nice for friends","because it gets hot","because it eats berries"],"answer":"to match the snow or rocks"}],"image_prompt":"A warm, simple flat-style children's-book illustration for a kids' reading app. Scene: The arctic fox lives in the far north, where winters are long and snowy. Focus on: a white arctic fox in the snow. Bright friendly colors, soft rounded shapes, no text or letters anywhere in the image, square 1:1 aspect ratio, cheerful and inviting for young readers."},{"text":"Flying squirrels cannot really fly, but they can glide a long way. They have a stretchy flap of skin between their front and back legs. When a flying squirrel leaps from a tree, it spreads its legs wide. The flap opens like a kite. It uses its flat tail to steer. Flying squirrels come out at night, so few people ever see them.","questions":[{"question":"What helps a flying squirrel glide?","choices":["tiny wings","long feathers","a flap of skin","a puffy tail"],"answer":"a flap of skin"},{"question":"Why do few people see flying squirrels?","choices":["They are invisible.","They come out at night.","They live underground.","They hide under the water."],"answer":"They come out at night."}],"image_prompt":"A warm, simple flat-style children's-book illustration for a kids' reading app. Scene: Flying squirrels cannot really fly, but they can glide a long way. Focus on: a flying squirrel gliding between trees. Bright friendly colors, soft rounded shapes, no text or letters anywhere in the image, square 1:1 aspect ratio, cheerful and inviting for young readers."},{"text":"Wild turkeys are big birds that live in forests and fields across North America. Male turkeys are called toms. When a tom wants attention, he puffs up his feathers and spreads his tail like a fan. He also makes a gobble sound that can be heard a mile away! Even though they are heavy, wild turkeys can fly short distances and run fast.","questions":[{"question":"What is a male turkey called?","choices":["a tom","a drake","a hen","a chick"],"answer":"a tom"},{"question":"How far away can a gobble be heard?","choices":["one foot","ten miles","a block","a mile"],"answer":"a mile"}],"image_prompt":"A warm, simple flat-style children's-book illustration for a kids' reading app. Scene: Wild turkeys are big birds that live in forests and fields across North America. Focus on: a wild turkey tom with its tail fanned out. Bright friendly colors, soft rounded shapes, no text or letters anywhere in the image, square 1:1 aspect ratio, cheerful and inviting for young readers."},{"text":"On Thanksgiving morning, Carmen watched a big city parade on TV with her family. Giant balloons shaped like animals and cartoon characters floated above the street. Each balloon was filled with helium, a gas lighter than air. Dozens of helpers held ropes to keep each balloon from floating away. Marching bands played, and dancers waved. Carmen's favorite was a huge balloon turkey.","questions":[{"question":"What gas filled the balloons?","choices":["oxygen","smoke","helium","steam"],"answer":"helium"},{"question":"Why did helpers hold ropes?","choices":["to pull the balloons faster","to keep the balloons from floating away","to tie the balloons to the trees","to make the balloons dance to music"],"answer":"to keep the balloons from floating away"},{"question":"What was Carmen's favorite balloon?","choices":["a rocket ship","a dragon","a turkey","a bear"],"answer":"a turkey"}],"image_prompt":"A warm, simple flat-style children's-book illustration for a kids' reading app. Scene: On Thanksgiving morning, Carmen watched a big city parade on TV with her family. Focus on: giant parade balloons held by ropes. Bright friendly colors, soft rounded shapes, no text or letters anywhere in the image, square 1:1 aspect ratio, cheerful and inviting for young readers."},{"text":"Hana's family keeps a jar on the kitchen table. Every night at dinner, each person writes one good thing from the day on a slip of paper and drops it in. Hana wrote, \"My friend shared her markers.\" Her brother wrote, \"I scored a goal.\" At the end of the month, they read every slip out loud. It reminds them of all the good things that happened.","questions":[{"question":"When do they write on the slips?","choices":["every morning","before school each day","every night at dinner","on Saturdays only"],"answer":"every night at dinner"},{"question":"What did Hana write about?","choices":["her friend sharing markers","a goal in soccer","her new puppy learning tricks","a trip to the zoo"],"answer":"her friend sharing markers"}],"photo":"images/flashcards/jar.jpg"},{"text":"Owen wanted to buy a birthday gift for his sister. The gift cost eight dollars, but he had only three. He put a piggy bank on his dresser. Each week, he saved the coins he earned for feeding the cat and folding towels. Some weeks he wanted to spend them on candy, but he waited. After two months, he finally had enough money.","questions":[{"question":"How much did the gift cost?","choices":["two dollars","three dollars","eight dollars","twenty dollars"],"answer":"eight dollars"},{"question":"How did Owen earn coins?","choices":["feeding the cat and folding towels","selling lemonade on the corner","washing cars for neighbors","walking dogs"],"answer":"feeding the cat and folding towels"}],"image_prompt":"A warm, simple flat-style children's-book illustration for a kids' reading app. Scene: Owen wanted to buy a birthday gift for his sister. Focus on: a boy dropping coins into a piggy bank. Bright friendly colors, soft rounded shapes, no text or letters anywhere in the image, square 1:1 aspect ratio, cheerful and inviting for young readers."},{"text":"Zane collects rocks. He keeps them in an egg carton, one rock in each cup. His favorite is a piece of quartz that sparkles in the light. He also has a smooth gray rock from a river and a black rock that came from a volcano. Zane uses a magnifying glass to look at tiny lines and colors. He labels each one.","questions":[{"question":"Where does Zane keep his rocks?","choices":["an egg carton","a shoebox","a glass jar","a tin lunch pail"],"answer":"an egg carton"},{"question":"What does Zane use to look closely?","choices":["binoculars","a flashlight","a magnifying glass","his dad's glasses"],"answer":"a magnifying glass"},{"question":"Which rock sparkles in the light?","choices":["a brick","the black volcano rock","the gray river rock","quartz"],"answer":"quartz"}],"image_prompt":"A warm, simple flat-style children's-book illustration for a kids' reading app. Scene: Zane collects rocks. Focus on: a boy with a rock collection in an egg carton. Bright friendly colors, soft rounded shapes, no text or letters anywhere in the image, square 1:1 aspect ratio, cheerful and inviting for young readers."},{"text":"Lucy wanted to tie her own shoes before first grade ended. Her big brother taught her the bunny ears trick. First, she made two loops, like rabbit ears. Then she crossed them and pulled one through the hole. The first ten times, the bow fell apart. But Lucy kept trying. On Friday, she tied both shoes all by herself and did a happy dance.","questions":[{"question":"What trick did her brother teach her?","choices":["the bow tie trick","the snake trick","the loop trick","the bunny ears trick"],"answer":"the bunny ears trick"},{"question":"What did Lucy do on Friday?","choices":["tied both shoes by herself","lost her shoes","bought new shoes","taught her brother to read"],"answer":"tied both shoes by herself"}],"image_prompt":"A warm, simple flat-style children's-book illustration for a kids' reading app. Scene: Lucy wanted to tie her own shoes before first grade ended. Focus on: a girl tying her shoe with bunny ears loops. Bright friendly colors, soft rounded shapes, no text or letters anywhere in the image, square 1:1 aspect ratio, cheerful and inviting for young readers."}],"2":[{"text":"Veterans Day began as a way to remember the end of a terrible war. World War I ended with an agreement called an armistice, which took effect on November 11, 1918, at eleven o'clock in the morning. People called it the eleventh hour of the eleventh day of the eleventh month. The next year, the United States began observing Armistice Day to honor the soldiers who had served. Later, Americans served in other wars too, so in 1954 Congress changed the name to Veterans Day. The holiday now honors everyone who has served in the military, in war or in peace. Many towns hold parades, and schools invite veterans to share their stories. Unlike Memorial Day, which remembers those who died in service, Veterans Day thanks all living veterans.","questions":[{"question":"In the passage, the word \"armistice\" means?","choices":["a parade with marching bands","a medal for brave soldiers","a soldier's home","an agreement to stop fighting"],"answer":"an agreement to stop fighting"},{"question":"Why did Congress change the name in 1954?","choices":["because the first name was too long","because the war started again","to honor veterans of other wars too","to move the holiday to summer"],"answer":"to honor veterans of other wars too"},{"question":"How is Veterans Day different from Memorial Day?","choices":["It honors only generals.","It happens in the spring.","It has no parades at all.","It thanks living veterans."],"answer":"It thanks living veterans."}],"image_prompt":"A warm, simple flat-style children's-book illustration for a kids' reading app. Scene: Veterans Day began as a way to remember the end of a terrible war. Focus on: a Veterans Day parade with veterans and flags. Bright friendly colors, soft rounded shapes, no text or letters anywhere in the image, square 1:1 aspect ratio, cheerful and inviting for young readers."},{"text":"Twice a year, most people in the United States change their clocks. In spring, clocks move forward one hour, and in early November, they move back. This system is called daylight saving time. The idea is to shift an hour of daylight from the early morning, when many people are asleep, to the evening, when they are awake. Daylight saving time became common during World War I, when countries hoped to save fuel used for lighting. Today, people still debate whether it is worth the trouble. Some say the extra evening light encourages outdoor play. Others point out that losing an hour of sleep in spring leaves people groggy for days. Hawaii and most of Arizona do not change their clocks at all.","questions":[{"question":"In the passage, the word \"groggy\" means?","choices":["cheerful and loud","sleepy and slow","hungry and thirsty","angry and upset"],"answer":"sleepy and slow"},{"question":"What is the passage mostly about?","choices":["why Hawaii has warmer weather than other states","the history and debate over changing clocks","how World War I began and which countries fought","how clocks are built and repaired in factories"],"answer":"the history and debate over changing clocks"},{"question":"Why did daylight saving time become common during World War I?","choices":["to help farmers plant more corn","to give soldiers more sleep","to save fuel used for lighting","to make school days shorter"],"answer":"to save fuel used for lighting"}],"image_prompt":"A warm, simple flat-style children's-book illustration for a kids' reading app. Scene: Twice a year, most people in the United States change their clocks. Focus on: clocks being changed between daylight and evening. Bright friendly colors, soft rounded shapes, no text or letters anywhere in the image, square 1:1 aspect ratio, cheerful and inviting for young readers."},{"text":"Have you ever seen a cat's eyes glow in a dark room? That eerie shine comes from a layer at the back of the eye called the tapetum lucidum, which is Latin for \"bright tapestry.\" Light that enters the eye passes through the retina, where cells detect it. Any light that is not absorbed bounces off the tapetum and passes through the retina a second time. This gives the cells another chance to catch it, which helps cats see in dim light. Many animals that are active at dawn, dusk, or night, such as deer, dogs, and raccoons, share this feature. Humans do not have a tapetum, which is one reason we struggle to see at night. In photographs, the reflected light can make an animal's eyes look green, yellow, or even blue.","questions":[{"question":"In the passage, the word \"absorbed\" means?","choices":["made louder","taken in","painted over","thrown away"],"answer":"taken in"},{"question":"How does the tapetum help cats see in dim light?","choices":["It helps the cat hear sounds in the dark.","It reflects light back through the retina.","It blocks bright sunlight.","It makes the eye glow with its own light."],"answer":"It reflects light back through the retina."},{"question":"Why do humans struggle to see at night compared with cats?","choices":["Our retinas reflect too much light.","We blink more often than cats.","We do not have a tapetum.","Our eyes are much too small."],"answer":"We do not have a tapetum."}],"photo":"images/flashcards/cat.jpg"},{"text":"Thanksgiving was not always a national holiday. For many years, each state chose its own day to give thanks, and some states did not celebrate at all. A magazine editor named Sarah Josepha Hale wanted to change that. Starting in the 1840s, she wrote letter after letter to governors and presidents, asking for one national day of thanks. For about seventeen years, her requests were mostly ignored. Hale did not give up. In 1863, during the Civil War, she wrote to President Abraham Lincoln. Lincoln agreed that a shared holiday might help unite a divided country, and he declared that Thanksgiving would be celebrated on the last Thursday of November. Hale, who also wrote the poem \"Mary Had a Little Lamb,\" is sometimes called the Mother of Thanksgiving.","questions":[{"question":"In the passage, the word \"unite\" means?","choices":["split apart","make louder","bring together","keep hidden from view"],"answer":"bring together"},{"question":"Which word best describes Sarah Josepha Hale?","choices":["forgetful","shy and quiet","determined","impatient"],"answer":"determined"},{"question":"Why did Lincoln agree to a national Thanksgiving?","choices":["to celebrate the end of a long winter","to move the holiday to summertime","to sell more copies of her magazine","to help unite a divided country"],"answer":"to help unite a divided country"}],"image_prompt":"A warm, simple flat-style children's-book illustration for a kids' reading app. Scene: Thanksgiving was not always a national holiday. Focus on: Sarah Josepha Hale writing letters for a national Thanksgiving. Bright friendly colors, soft rounded shapes, no text or letters anywhere in the image, square 1:1 aspect ratio, cheerful and inviting for young readers."},{"text":"Every fall, billions of birds migrate south, some flying thousands of miles to places they have never seen. How do they find their way? Scientists have discovered that birds use several tools at once. Many birds can sense Earth's magnetic field, almost like having a built-in compass. Others use the position of the sun during the day and patterns of stars at night. Young birds may also follow experienced adults and learn landmarks such as coastlines, rivers, and mountain ranges. Some seabirds even seem to use smell to locate their nesting islands. Because birds combine these clues, a cloudy night does not usually leave them lost. However, bright city lights can confuse birds that migrate at night, so some cities encourage buildings to turn off extra lights during migration season.","questions":[{"question":"In the passage, the word \"experienced\" means?","choices":["lost and confused","from far away","very young and small","having done something many times"],"answer":"having done something many times"},{"question":"What is the main idea of the passage?","choices":["Birds only migrate on clear nights.","Seabirds fly faster than songbirds.","Birds use many clues to find their way.","City lights help birds travel faster."],"answer":"Birds use many clues to find their way."},{"question":"Why do some cities turn off extra lights?","choices":["Birds need dark places to build nests.","It saves money for city parks and schools.","Bright lights can confuse migrating birds.","Lights attract too many insects at night."],"answer":"Bright lights can confuse migrating birds."}],"image_prompt":"A warm, simple flat-style children's-book illustration for a kids' reading app. Scene: Every fall, billions of birds migrate south, some flying thousands of miles to places they have never seen. Focus on: migrating birds flying under stars at night. Bright friendly colors, soft rounded shapes, no text or letters anywhere in the image, square 1:1 aspect ratio, cheerful and inviting for young readers."},{"text":"In autumn, many trees put on a brilliant show of red, orange, and yellow. These colors are not new paint. During spring and summer, leaves are packed with chlorophyll, a green pigment that captures sunlight to make food. Chlorophyll is so plentiful that it hides other pigments already in the leaf. As days grow shorter and cooler, trees stop making chlorophyll, and the green slowly fades. The yellow and orange pigments, called carotenoids, are finally revealed. Red and purple colors come from a different pigment that some trees produce in the fall, especially after sunny days and cool nights. That is why a dry, sunny autumn often brings brighter colors than a rainy one.","questions":[{"question":"In the passage, the word \"revealed\" means?","choices":["hidden","erased","shown","painted"],"answer":"shown"},{"question":"Why do yellow and orange colors appear in fall?","choices":["Birds carry colored pollen to leaves.","Trees soak up colors from the soil.","Cold rain paints the leaves.","The green chlorophyll fades away."],"answer":"The green chlorophyll fades away."},{"question":"Which autumn would likely bring the brightest red leaves?","choices":["one with sunny days and cool nights","one with rain almost every day","one with steady drizzle","one with warm, cloudy nights"],"answer":"one with sunny days and cool nights"}],"photo":"images/flashcards/leaf.jpg"},{"text":"Walk along certain beaches and you may find smooth, frosted pebbles of green, brown, or white glass. This is sea glass, and each piece began as something broken, such as an old bottle or jar. When glass ends up in the ocean, waves tumble it against sand and rocks for years. Slowly, the sharp edges wear away until they become rounded and safe to hold. The water also reacts with chemicals in the glass, leaving its surface cloudy instead of shiny. A single piece may take twenty to thirty years or more to form. Because glass bottles are recycled more often today, new sea glass is becoming scarce, and collectors treasure rare colors like red and turquoise. Some collectors even try to guess the age of a piece from its color and shape.","questions":[{"question":"In the passage, the word \"scarce\" means?","choices":["easy to break","hard to find","very heavy","bright and shiny"],"answer":"hard to find"},{"question":"Why is sea glass smooth instead of sharp?","choices":["Sunlight melts the edges.","Fish nibble the sharp edges away.","Waves tumble it against sand and rocks.","People sand it before tossing it in."],"answer":"Waves tumble it against sand and rocks."},{"question":"Why is new sea glass becoming harder to find?","choices":["Glass sinks to the bottom of the sea.","More bottles are recycled today.","Beaches are closed to collectors.","Waves have stopped reaching the shore."],"answer":"More bottles are recycled today."}],"image_prompt":"A warm, simple flat-style children's-book illustration for a kids' reading app. Scene: Walk along certain beaches and you may find smooth, frosted pebbles of green, brown, or white glass. Focus on: colorful sea glass on a sandy beach. Bright friendly colors, soft rounded shapes, no text or letters anywhere in the image, square 1:1 aspect ratio, cheerful and inviting for young readers."},{"text":"In 1974, farmers digging a well in northwestern China struck something unexpected: pieces of a life-sized clay soldier. Archaeologists soon realized they had found part of an enormous burial site built for Qin Shi Huang, the first emperor to unite China, who died more than 2,200 years ago. Buried in pits near his tomb were thousands of terracotta warriors, along with horses and chariots. Experts estimate there are about 8,000 soldiers, and remarkably, no two faces appear exactly alike. Many figures were once painted in bright colors, but most of the paint flaked away soon after being exposed to air. The emperor apparently believed the army would protect him in the afterlife. Today, the site is one of the most visited places in China.","questions":[{"question":"In the passage, the word \"remarkably\" means?","choices":["sadly","slowly and carefully","surprisingly","quietly"],"answer":"surprisingly"},{"question":"Who discovered the first pieces of the army?","choices":["farmers digging a well","children playing in a field","a museum guide on a tour","soldiers guarding a tomb"],"answer":"farmers digging a well"},{"question":"What happened to most of the paint?","choices":["Visitors scraped it off.","Workers washed it off on purpose.","It flaked off after touching the air.","Rain soaked it deep into the clay."],"answer":"It flaked off after touching the air."}],"image_prompt":"A warm, simple flat-style children's-book illustration for a kids' reading app. Scene: In 1974, farmers digging a well in northwestern China struck something unexpected: pieces of a life-sized clay soldier. Focus on: rows of terracotta warrior statues. Bright friendly colors, soft rounded shapes, no text or letters anywhere in the image, square 1:1 aspect ratio, cheerful and inviting for young readers."},{"text":"Benjamin Banneker was born in Maryland in 1731 to a free Black family. As a young man, he borrowed a pocket watch, took it apart, and studied how its gears worked. Using what he learned, he carved a wooden clock by hand that kept accurate time for decades. Banneker taught himself astronomy by studying borrowed books and watching the night sky. In 1791, he helped survey the land that would become Washington, D.C. Soon after, he began publishing almanacs, yearly books that predicted sunrises, eclipses, and tides. Banneker sent his work to Thomas Jefferson, along with a letter arguing that Black Americans deserved the same rights and opportunities as everyone else. His life showed what curiosity and careful study could accomplish.","questions":[{"question":"In the passage, the word \"survey\" means?","choices":["build a tall fence","measure and map land","plant crops in rows","guard a city at night"],"answer":"measure and map land"},{"question":"What did Banneker study to build his clock?","choices":["a church bell tower","drawings of clocks","a borrowed pocket watch","a book about ships"],"answer":"a borrowed pocket watch"},{"question":"What does his letter to Jefferson show about Banneker?","choices":["He was planning to move to France.","He spoke up for fairness.","He disliked writing almanacs.","He wanted a job in Jefferson's house."],"answer":"He spoke up for fairness."}],"image_prompt":"A warm, simple flat-style children's-book illustration for a kids' reading app. Scene: Benjamin Banneker was born in Maryland in 1731 to a free Black family. Focus on: Benjamin Banneker studying the stars with a wooden clock. Bright friendly colors, soft rounded shapes, no text or letters anywhere in the image, square 1:1 aspect ratio, cheerful and inviting for young readers."},{"text":"When the Eiffel Tower was built for the 1889 World's Fair in Paris, many people hated it. A group of artists called it a useless and monstrous tower. The iron structure, designed by Gustave Eiffel's engineering company, stood about 1,000 feet tall, making it the tallest structure in the world at the time. It was supposed to stand for only twenty years before being taken down. Eiffel, however, found a clever way to save it. He encouraged scientists to use the tower for experiments, and it became an important antenna for radio signals. Because the tower was useful, the city let it remain. Today, millions of visitors climb it every year, and it has become a beloved symbol of France.","questions":[{"question":"In the passage, the word \"monstrous\" means?","choices":["old and broken","ugly and huge","small and cute","quiet and calm"],"answer":"ugly and huge"},{"question":"Why was the tower allowed to remain?","choices":["It was too heavy to take apart.","Visitors paid to have it painted gold.","It became useful for radio signals.","Artists asked the city to keep it."],"answer":"It became useful for radio signals."},{"question":"What is the main idea of the passage?","choices":["Eiffel designed all of Paris.","The tower was built only from wood.","Radio was invented inside the tower.","The tower went from disliked to beloved."],"answer":"The tower went from disliked to beloved."}],"image_prompt":"A warm, simple flat-style children's-book illustration for a kids' reading app. Scene: When the Eiffel Tower was built for the 1889 World's Fair in Paris, many people hated it. Focus on: the Eiffel Tower at the 1889 World's Fair. Bright friendly colors, soft rounded shapes, no text or letters anywhere in the image, square 1:1 aspect ratio, cheerful and inviting for young readers."},{"text":"In 1895, a physical education teacher named William G. Morgan was working at a gym in Holyoke, Massachusetts. Basketball had been invented just a few years earlier, but Morgan noticed that it was too rough for some of the older businessmen in his classes. He wanted a game with less physical contact. Morgan borrowed ideas from tennis and handball, raised a net about six and a half feet off the floor, and had players hit a ball back and forth over it. He first called the game \"mintonette.\" After watching a demonstration, someone pointed out that players seemed to volley the ball, and the name volleyball stuck. Today, volleyball is played by millions of people around the world, including on sandy beaches.","questions":[{"question":"In the passage, the word \"volley\" means?","choices":["roll across the floor","kick along the ground","hit back and forth in the air","throw into a basket"],"answer":"hit back and forth in the air"},{"question":"Why did Morgan invent a new game?","choices":["Basketball was too rough for some players.","His students were bored with tennis.","The gym was too small for running.","He wanted an Olympic sport."],"answer":"Basketball was too rough for some players."},{"question":"What was the first name of the game?","choices":["handball","netball","volleytennis","mintonette"],"answer":"mintonette"}],"image_prompt":"A warm, simple flat-style children's-book illustration for a kids' reading app. Scene: In 1895, a physical education teacher named William G. Focus on: people playing early indoor volleyball over a net. Bright friendly colors, soft rounded shapes, no text or letters anywhere in the image, square 1:1 aspect ratio, cheerful and inviting for young readers."},{"text":"When you tap a phone or tablet screen, how does it know where your finger is? Most modern touchscreens are capacitive, meaning they sense tiny electrical changes. Beneath the glass lies a nearly invisible grid of conductive lines carrying a small electric charge. Because the human body also conducts electricity, your fingertip draws away a tiny bit of charge at the spot you touch. The device measures where the charge changed and calculates your finger's exact position in a fraction of a second. This explains a puzzle many people notice in winter: ordinary gloves block the connection, so the screen ignores you. Special gloves with conductive threads in the fingertips solve the problem. A pencil eraser will not work either, since rubber does not conduct electricity.","questions":[{"question":"In the passage, the word \"conducts\" means?","choices":["lets electricity flow through","makes a loud buzzing sound","shines a bright light","keeps things very cold"],"answer":"lets electricity flow through"},{"question":"Why doesn't an ordinary glove work on a touchscreen?","choices":["It makes the screen too cold to work.","It hides your fingerprint.","Its fingers are too thick to fit.","Its cloth blocks the electrical connection."],"answer":"Its cloth blocks the electrical connection."},{"question":"What is the passage mostly about?","choices":["the history of winter gloves","how to clean a tablet screen safely","how touchscreens sense a finger","why phones break when dropped"],"answer":"how touchscreens sense a finger"}],"image_prompt":"A warm, simple flat-style children's-book illustration for a kids' reading app. Scene: When you tap a phone or tablet screen, how does it know where your finger is? Most modern touchscreens are capacitive, meaning they sense tiny electrical changes. Focus on: a finger tapping a tablet touchscreen. Bright friendly colors, soft rounded shapes, no text or letters anywhere in the image, square 1:1 aspect ratio, cheerful and inviting for young readers."},{"text":"Weather forecasters rely on radar to track storms as they happen. A radar station sends out short pulses of radio waves that travel through the air. When the waves strike raindrops, snowflakes, or hail, some of the energy bounces back to the station. By timing how long the echo takes to return, computers calculate how far away the precipitation is. The strength of the echo reveals how heavy the rain or snow is, which is why radar maps often show light rain in green and intense storms in red. Modern Doppler radar also measures whether the drops are moving toward or away from the station. This helps forecasters spot rotating winds inside thunderstorms and issue tornado warnings, often before a tornado touches down.","questions":[{"question":"In the passage, the word \"precipitation\" means?","choices":["warm, sunny weather","bright flashes of light","rain, snow, or hail","strong gusts of wind"],"answer":"rain, snow, or hail"},{"question":"What color often shows intense storms on a radar map?","choices":["red","blue","white","green"],"answer":"red"},{"question":"Why is Doppler radar helpful for tornado warnings?","choices":["It counts every raindrop that falls.","It detects rotating winds in storms.","It warms the air so storms fade.","It makes storms move away from towns."],"answer":"It detects rotating winds in storms."}],"image_prompt":"A warm, simple flat-style children's-book illustration for a kids' reading app. Scene: Weather forecasters rely on radar to track storms as they happen. Focus on: a weather radar map with green and red storm colors. Bright friendly colors, soft rounded shapes, no text or letters anywhere in the image, square 1:1 aspect ratio, cheerful and inviting for young readers."},{"text":"On a clear day, the sky looks blue, but sunlight is actually a mix of all colors. When sunlight enters Earth's atmosphere, it collides with tiny molecules of gas. These molecules scatter shorter wavelengths of light, like blue and violet, much more than longer wavelengths, like red and orange. The scattered blue light bounces around the sky and reaches our eyes from every direction. Violet is scattered even more, but our eyes are less sensitive to violet, so the sky appears blue. At sunrise and sunset, sunlight travels through much more air to reach us. By then, most of the blue has been scattered away, leaving the reds and oranges that paint the evening sky.","questions":[{"question":"In the passage, the word \"scatter\" means?","choices":["shrink into one small bright point","spread out in many directions","stick together in a clump","freeze into ice"],"answer":"spread out in many directions"},{"question":"Why are sunsets red and orange?","choices":["The ocean reflects red light upward.","Sunlight passes through more air.","Clouds paint the sky with dust.","The sun cools down in the evening."],"answer":"Sunlight passes through more air."},{"question":"According to the passage, what is sunlight made of?","choices":["only yellow light","only blue light","a mix of all colors","tiny drops of water"],"answer":"a mix of all colors"}],"image_prompt":"A warm, simple flat-style children's-book illustration for a kids' reading app. Scene: On a clear day, the sky looks blue, but sunlight is actually a mix of all colors. Focus on: a blue sky above and an orange sunset below. Bright friendly colors, soft rounded shapes, no text or letters anywhere in the image, square 1:1 aspect ratio, cheerful and inviting for young readers."},{"text":"Pangolins are shy mammals that look a bit like walking pinecones. Their bodies are covered in overlapping scales made of keratin, the same material as human fingernails. When threatened, a pangolin curls into a tight ball, and its sharp-edged scales protect it from predators such as lions. Pangolins have no teeth. Instead, they use a long, sticky tongue to slurp up ants and termites. A single pangolin may eat about seventy million insects in a year, helping control insect populations. Sadly, pangolins are among the most endangered mammals, because people hunt them for their scales. Many countries now protect them by law, and rescue centers care for injured pangolins before releasing them into the wild.","questions":[{"question":"In the passage, the word \"threatened\" means?","choices":["very hungry","sound asleep","warm and dry","in danger"],"answer":"in danger"},{"question":"What are pangolin scales made of?","choices":["keratin","bone","wood","chalk and sand"],"answer":"keratin"},{"question":"How do pangolins help their environment?","choices":["They clean rivers of trash.","They dig ponds for other animals.","They keep insect numbers down.","They plant seeds in the forest."],"answer":"They keep insect numbers down."}],"image_prompt":"A warm, simple flat-style children's-book illustration for a kids' reading app. Scene: Pangolins are shy mammals that look a bit like walking pinecones. Focus on: a pangolin curled into a scaly ball. Bright friendly colors, soft rounded shapes, no text or letters anywhere in the image, square 1:1 aspect ratio, cheerful and inviting for young readers."},{"text":"While helping her grandfather clean out a kitchen drawer, Leah found a faded index card covered in looping handwriting. \"That is my mother's corn bread recipe,\" Grandpa said softly. \"I have not tasted it in thirty years.\" Some of the words had smudged, and one measurement was impossible to read. Leah decided to solve the mystery. She baked three test batches, each with a different amount of sugar, while Grandpa tasted every one. The first was too plain, and the second was far too sweet. When he bit into the third, Grandpa closed his eyes and was quiet for a long moment. \"That is it,\" he whispered. Leah copied the recipe onto a fresh card, adding the missing number in bright blue ink.","questions":[{"question":"In the passage, the word \"faded\" means?","choices":["brand new","torn into pieces","lighter from age","soaking wet"],"answer":"lighter from age"},{"question":"Why was Grandpa quiet after tasting the third batch?","choices":["He did not like the taste at all.","It tasted like his mother's corn bread.","He had lost his glasses.","He was too full to speak."],"answer":"It tasted like his mother's corn bread."},{"question":"How did Leah figure out the missing amount?","choices":["by asking a chef on the radio","by baking several test batches","by finding the number in a cookbook","by guessing and skipping the tasting"],"answer":"by baking several test batches"}],"image_prompt":"A warm, simple flat-style children's-book illustration for a kids' reading app. Scene: While helping her grandfather clean out a kitchen drawer, Leah found a faded index card covered in looping handwriting. Focus on: a girl and her grandfather tasting corn bread. Bright friendly colors, soft rounded shapes, no text or letters anywhere in the image, square 1:1 aspect ratio, cheerful and inviting for young readers."},{"text":"Mr. Ito paired Darnell with Sophie for the bridge-building project, and Darnell groaned. Sophie was so quiet that he assumed she would not help. On the first day, Darnell grabbed the craft sticks and started gluing without asking her opinion. His bridge sagged in the middle. Sophie pointed to a drawing in her notebook. \"Triangles are stronger,\" she said. \"Engineers use them because they do not bend out of shape easily.\" Darnell hesitated, then agreed to try her design. Together they built a bridge braced with triangles. On test day, it held more weight than any other bridge in the class. Darnell admitted he had misjudged his partner, and he asked Sophie to work with him again.","questions":[{"question":"In the passage, the word \"misjudged\" means?","choices":["chose to work far away from","formed a wrong opinion about","copied the work of","argued loudly with"],"answer":"formed a wrong opinion about"},{"question":"Why did Sophie suggest using triangles?","choices":["They keep their shape under weight.","They use less glue than other shapes.","They look prettier than squares.","They are the easiest shape to cut."],"answer":"They keep their shape under weight."},{"question":"What lesson does Darnell learn?","choices":["Bridges should be made of metal.","Quiet people can have great ideas.","It is best to work alone.","Teachers pick partners well."],"answer":"Quiet people can have great ideas."}],"image_prompt":"A warm, simple flat-style children's-book illustration for a kids' reading app. Scene: Mr. Focus on: two kids testing a craft-stick bridge braced with triangles. Bright friendly colors, soft rounded shapes, no text or letters anywhere in the image, square 1:1 aspect ratio, cheerful and inviting for young readers."},{"text":"When Amir joined Ms. Kaplan's fourth-grade class, he had just moved from Jordan and knew only a few words of English. At recess, he stood by the fence, watching a soccer game. Grace noticed him and remembered how lonely she had felt when her own family moved. She jogged over, pointed to the ball, and raised her eyebrows. Amir grinned and nodded. On the field, words did not matter much; a good pass needed no translation. Afterward, Amir taught Grace how to say \"thank you\" in Arabic, and she taught him \"great shot.\" By the end of the month, the two had a notebook full of words in both languages, and half the class wanted to learn them too.","questions":[{"question":"In the passage, the word \"translation\" means?","choices":["a rule about where players may stand","a loud cheer from the crowd","changing words into another language","a long soccer field"],"answer":"changing words into another language"},{"question":"Why did Grace go over to Amir?","choices":["She wanted to borrow his soccer ball.","She remembered being new herself.","She needed one more player to win.","Her teacher told her to invite him."],"answer":"She remembered being new herself."},{"question":"What did the two friends fill with words?","choices":["a notebook","a lunch box","a backpack","a scrapbook of photos"],"answer":"a notebook"}],"image_prompt":"A warm, simple flat-style children's-book illustration for a kids' reading app. Scene: When Amir joined Ms. Focus on: two boys passing a soccer ball at recess. Bright friendly colors, soft rounded shapes, no text or letters anywhere in the image, square 1:1 aspect ratio, cheerful and inviting for young readers."},{"text":"Some towns near the Great Lakes can receive several feet of snow in a single storm while places just a short drive away see only flurries. This happens because of lake-effect snow. In late fall and early winter, the lakes are still relatively warm from summer, but the air above them turns bitterly cold. When frigid wind blows across the open water, it picks up heat and moisture from the lake. The warm, moist air rises, cools, and forms clouds that drop heavy snow once they reach land. The snow often falls in narrow bands, which explains why one neighborhood can be buried while another stays nearly clear. Once a lake freezes over, the supply of moisture is cut off, and lake-effect snow usually stops.","questions":[{"question":"In the passage, the word \"frigid\" means?","choices":["warm and sticky","full of dust","extremely cold","very gentle"],"answer":"extremely cold"},{"question":"Why does lake-effect snow usually stop once a lake freezes?","choices":["The wind stops blowing over the lake.","The air above the lake turns warm.","The ice cuts off the moisture supply.","Snow cannot fall on frozen ground."],"answer":"The ice cuts off the moisture supply."},{"question":"When is lake-effect snow most likely?","choices":["early summer","late fall, when lakes are still warm","spring, after the lakes freeze","midsummer, when the air is hot"],"answer":"late fall, when lakes are still warm"}],"image_prompt":"A warm, simple flat-style children's-book illustration for a kids' reading app. Scene: Some towns near the Great Lakes can receive several feet of snow in a single storm while places just a short drive away see only flurries. Focus on: heavy lake-effect snow falling near a big lake. Bright friendly colors, soft rounded shapes, no text or letters anywhere in the image, square 1:1 aspect ratio, cheerful and inviting for young readers."},{"text":"Chopping an onion can make even the toughest cook cry, and the reason is chemistry. An onion stores sulfur compounds inside its cells. When a knife slices through those cells, it releases enzymes that mix with the compounds and create a new, irritating gas. That gas drifts upward and reaches your eyes. Your eyes sense the irritation and quickly produce tears to wash it away. Cooks have invented many tricks to reduce the sting. A very sharp knife damages fewer cells than a dull one, so less gas escapes. Chilling the onion before cutting slows the chemical reaction. Some people even chop near a running fan, which blows the gas away before it reaches their eyes.","questions":[{"question":"In the passage, the word \"irritating\" means?","choices":["invisible and harmless","bothersome and stinging","sweet-smelling","calming and pleasant"],"answer":"bothersome and stinging"},{"question":"Why does a sharp knife help?","choices":["It makes the onion colder.","It soaks up the gas.","It damages fewer cells.","It cuts the onion faster."],"answer":"It damages fewer cells."},{"question":"Why do your eyes make tears when you chop onions?","choices":["to cool down the hot kitchen air","because the onion smell is sweet","to help you see the knife better","to wash away the irritating gas"],"answer":"to wash away the irritating gas"}],"photo":"images/flashcards/onion.jpg"},{"text":"On rocks, tree bark, and old fences, you may notice crusty patches of gray, green, or orange. These patches are lichens, and each one is actually a partnership between two very different living things. A fungus forms the lichen's body, gathering water and minerals and sheltering its partner. The partner, usually algae, uses sunlight to make food and shares it with the fungus. Neither could survive as well alone in such harsh places. Lichens grow incredibly slowly, sometimes less than a millimeter each year, yet some patches are thousands of years old. Because many lichens are sensitive to air pollution, scientists use them as natural indicators. A forest with plenty of lichens usually has clean air.","questions":[{"question":"In the passage, the word \"indicators\" means?","choices":["kinds of mushrooms","tools for cutting wood","signs that show something","insects that eat bark"],"answer":"signs that show something"},{"question":"What does the algae give the fungus?","choices":["protection from bugs","water and minerals","food made from sunlight","shelter from the wind"],"answer":"food made from sunlight"},{"question":"A town notices lichens disappearing from its trees. What might that suggest?","choices":["Birds are eating them.","The air may be getting polluted.","It has rained too much lately.","The trees are growing too fast."],"answer":"The air may be getting polluted."}],"image_prompt":"A warm, simple flat-style children's-book illustration for a kids' reading app. Scene: On rocks, tree bark, and old fences, you may notice crusty patches of gray, green, or orange. Focus on: lichens growing on a rock and tree bark. Bright friendly colors, soft rounded shapes, no text or letters anywhere in the image, square 1:1 aspect ratio, cheerful and inviting for young readers."},{"text":"The Dead Sea, located between Jordan and Israel, is not really a sea but a salty lake. Its shore lies more than 1,400 feet below sea level, making it the lowest place on land on Earth. Rivers flow into the Dead Sea, but no rivers flow out. In the hot, dry climate, the water evaporates quickly and leaves its salt behind. As a result, the water is nearly ten times saltier than the ocean. That much salt makes the water very dense, so swimmers float easily, almost as if they were lying on a raft. The extreme saltiness also explains the name: fish and plants cannot live in it, although tiny microbes can survive. In recent decades, the lake has been shrinking as people use water from the river that feeds it.","questions":[{"question":"In the passage, the word \"evaporates\" means?","choices":["turns into vapor and rises","freezes into ice","becomes thick mud","sinks into the sand"],"answer":"turns into vapor and rises"},{"question":"Why is the Dead Sea so salty?","choices":["People pour salt into it every year.","Salt mines are melting.","Rivers carry salt out to the ocean.","Water evaporates and leaves salt behind."],"answer":"Water evaporates and leaves salt behind."},{"question":"Why do swimmers float easily in the Dead Sea?","choices":["The water is extremely warm.","Waves push swimmers up.","The lake is only a few feet deep.","The salty water is very dense."],"answer":"The salty water is very dense."}],"image_prompt":"A warm, simple flat-style children's-book illustration for a kids' reading app. Scene: The Dead Sea, located between Jordan and Israel, is not really a sea but a salty lake. Focus on: a person floating on the salty Dead Sea. Bright friendly colors, soft rounded shapes, no text or letters anywhere in the image, square 1:1 aspect ratio, cheerful and inviting for young readers."},{"text":"In 1889, a newspaper reporter named Nellie Bly set out to do something many people thought was impossible. A popular novel described a character traveling around the world in eighty days, and Bly wanted to do it faster. At the time, there were no airplanes, so she traveled by steamship and train, carrying only a single small bag. Readers followed her progress in the newspaper, and some entered a contest to guess how long her journey would take. Bly crossed Europe, sailed through the Suez Canal, and crossed the Pacific before speeding across the United States by train. She arrived back in New York after just seventy-two days, setting a world record. Her trip proved that a determined traveler could circle the globe faster than fiction had imagined.","questions":[{"question":"In the passage, the word \"progress\" means?","choices":["a kind of ticket","forward movement toward a goal","a heavy suitcase","a made-up story"],"answer":"forward movement toward a goal"},{"question":"How long did Nellie Bly's trip take?","choices":["one full year","seventy-two days","ninety-five days","eighty days"],"answer":"seventy-two days"},{"question":"Why did readers follow her trip so closely?","choices":["They wondered if she could beat eighty days.","They were planning the same trip.","They were paid to read.","They had to read it for school."],"answer":"They wondered if she could beat eighty days."}],"image_prompt":"A warm, simple flat-style children's-book illustration for a kids' reading app. Scene: In 1889, a newspaper reporter named Nellie Bly set out to do something many people thought was impossible. Focus on: Nellie Bly traveling by steamship with one small bag. Bright friendly colors, soft rounded shapes, no text or letters anywhere in the image, square 1:1 aspect ratio, cheerful and inviting for young readers."},{"text":"Most frogs hide from winter in mud or water, but the wood frog of North America does something astonishing: it freezes. As temperatures drop below freezing, ice forms in the spaces of the frog's body, and its heart stops beating. It stops breathing too. The frog survives because its liver floods its cells with sugar, which works like antifreeze and keeps ice from forming inside the cells, where it would cause damage. The frog stays frozen under a blanket of leaves for weeks or even months. When spring warms the forest floor, the frog slowly thaws. Within hours its heart starts beating again, and before long it hops off to find a pond. Scientists study wood frogs to learn how to better preserve organs for medicine.","questions":[{"question":"In the passage, the word \"astonishing\" means?","choices":["slightly scary","very surprising","easy to explain","very boring"],"answer":"very surprising"},{"question":"How does sugar help the wood frog survive?","choices":["It keeps ice out of its cells.","It gives the frog energy to keep hopping.","It makes the frog's skin turn white.","It warms the leaves around the frog."],"answer":"It keeps ice out of its cells."},{"question":"Why might doctors be interested in wood frogs?","choices":["to teach frogs to live in ponds","to learn how to keep organs safe","to find new kinds of sugar","to train frogs for hospitals"],"answer":"to learn how to keep organs safe"}],"image_prompt":"A warm, simple flat-style children's-book illustration for a kids' reading app. Scene: Most frogs hide from winter in mud or water, but the wood frog of North America does something astonishing: it freezes. Focus on: a frozen wood frog under autumn leaves. Bright friendly colors, soft rounded shapes, no text or letters anywhere in the image, square 1:1 aspect ratio, cheerful and inviting for young readers."}]},"synonyms":{"easy":{"cap":"hat","jog":"run","sack":"bag","yummy":"tasty","hop":"jump","gaze":"stare","dash":"sprint","bunny":"rabbit","pebble":"stone","sofa":"couch","chef":"cook","glow":"shine","noisy":"loud","kitty":"cat","smash":"break","fetch":"get","shove":"push","boulder":"rock","shop":"store","path":"trail"},"hard":{"gloomy":"dreary","bashful":"shy","cunning":"sly","dainty":"delicate","eerie":"spooky","gallant":"brave","keen":"eager","novice":"beginner","quarrel":"argue","ravenous":"hungry","sincere":"honest","thrifty":"frugal","wholesome":"healthy","astonish":"amaze","conceal":"hide"}},"antonyms":{"easy":{"many":"few","tight":"loose","above":"below","enter":"exit","more":"less","forget":"remember","whisper":"shout","start":"stop","giant":"tiny","plus":"minus","under":"over","morning":"evening","summer":"winter","city":"country","add":"subtract","black":"white","save":"spend","rise":"fall","floor":"ceiling","sit":"stand"},"hard":{"brief":"lengthy","sturdy":"flimsy","optimist":"pessimist","visible":"invisible","minor":"major","attract":"repel","feast":"famine","prompt":"tardy","serious":"playful","expensive":"cheap","liquid":"solid","advance":"retreat","courage":"fear","drowsy":"alert","graceful":"clumsy"}},"rhymes":{"easy":[["back","pack","sack","snack","track"],["mice","rice","dice","nice","twice"],["tub","cub","rub","club","scrub"],["hot","pot","dot","spot","knot"],["hole","mole","pole","stole"],["line","fine","mine","nine","pine"],["ear","near","hear","year","dear"],["junk","bunk","skunk","trunk","chunk"],["brush","rush","mush","hush","crush"],["tent","bent","dent","sent","went"]],"hard":[["table","cable","stable","fable"],["zipper","slipper","skipper","flipper","clipper"],["yellow","fellow","mellow","bellow"],["chatter","scatter","platter","matter","flatter"],["jingle","tingle","mingle","single"],["noodle","doodle","poodle","caboodle"],["arrow","narrow","sparrow","barrow"],["buckle","chuckle","knuckle","honeysuckle"]]},"analogies":[["chef","kitchen","teacher","classroom"],["petal","flower","page","book"],["doctor","hospital","librarian","library"],["lion","pride","wolf","pack"],["inch","foot","minute","hour"],["cub","bear","joey","kangaroo"],["hungry","eat","thirsty","drink"],["nickel","five","dime","ten"],["turkey","gobble","duck","quack"],["bird","nest","bee","hive"],["tired","sleep","bored","play"],["november","autumn","july","summer"],["snow","winter","flower","spring"],["hammer","carpenter","stethoscope","doctor"],["day","week","month","year"],["sand","beach","snow","mountain"],["leaf","tree","feather","bird"],["lamb","sheep","piglet","pig"],["triangle","three","hexagon","six"],["kitten","cat","chick","hen"]],"word_problem_templates":{"add":["{name}'s soccer team brought {a} {item} to the picnic, and the other team brought {b} {item}. How many {item} were at the picnic altogether?","On the bus ride to Grandma's house, {name} counted {a} {item} in one bag and {b} {item} in another bag. How many {item} did {name} count on the trip?","{name}'s class has {a} {item} in the classroom closet. The principal drops off {b} more {item}. How many {item} does the class have now?","During a board game, {name} won {a} {item} in the first round and {b} {item} in the second round. How many {item} did {name} win?","For helping at the pet shop, {name} got {a} {item} in the morning and {b} {item} in the afternoon. How many {item} did {name} get that day?","{name} packs {a} {item} for a camping trip, and a friend packs {b} {item}. How many {item} did they pack together?"],"sub":["{name} brought {a} {item} to soccer practice and handed out {b} of them to teammates. How many {item} does {name} have left?","The class had {a} {item} for the craft fair and sold {b} of them. How many {item} are left to sell?","A game started with {a} {item} in the pile. After {name} took {b} {item} on the first turn, how many {item} were left in the pile?","{name} packed {a} {item} for a long car trip and shared {b} of them with a cousin in the back seat. How many {item} does {name} still have?","A pet store had {a} {item} in a bin by the door. Customers took {b} {item} home. How many {item} are still in the bin?","{name} had {a} {item} in a backpack. After leaving {b} {item} in the classroom, how many {item} are still in the backpack?"],"mul":["{name}'s soccer team has {a} players, and each player brings {b} {item} to the game. How many {item} are there in all?","On a road trip, {name} stops in {a} towns and picks up {b} {item} in each town. How many {item} does {name} collect?","A baker fills {a} trays with {b} {item} on each tray. How many {item} are on the trays in all?","In the art room there are {a} cubbies, and each cubby holds {b} {item}. How many {item} are in the cubbies?","{name} plays {a} rounds of a game and earns {b} {item} every round. How many {item} does {name} earn?","{name} makes {a} gift bags for a party and puts {b} {item} into every bag. How many {item} does {name} use?"],"div":["The coach has {a} {item} to split evenly among {b} teams at practice. How many {item} does each team get?","{name}'s family is taking {b} cars on a trip, and they pack {a} {item} equally into the cars. How many {item} go in each car?","A camp counselor divides {a} {item} equally among {b} cabins. How many {item} does each cabin get?","{name} sets {a} {item} out on {b} picnic blankets, with the same number on each blanket. How many {item} are on each blanket?","At the pet shop, {a} {item} are placed in {b} baskets with the same number in every basket. How many {item} are in each basket?","During a card game, {name} deals {a} {item} equally to {b} players. How many {item} does each player get?"]}},{"id":"2026-11-03-central-ideas","released":"2026-11-03","kind":"manual","central_idea_stories":{"1":[{"text":"Mateo wanted to swim all the way across the pool without stopping. On the first day he got tired halfway and grabbed the wall. On the second day he made it a little farther. He kept trying a few minutes after school every day. After two weeks, Mateo touched the far wall and cheered so loudly that the lifeguard laughed.","idea":"Trying a little more each day helps you reach a hard goal.","distractors":["Mateo got tired halfway across the pool on the very first day.","Lifeguards enjoy watching children cheer loudly at the pool.","Swimming is only fun when you are the fastest one there."]},{"text":"Amina studied her spelling list every night, but she still missed six words on the first quiz. She felt like giving up. Her dad showed her how to write each hard word three times and say the letters out loud. The next week she missed only one word. Amina taped the quiz to the refrigerator and drew a star on it.","stem":"What lesson does this story teach?","idea":"Finding a better way to practice can turn a failure into success.","distractors":["Amina missed six words on her first spelling quiz.","Refrigerators are the best place to keep school papers.","Spelling lists are too hard for most children to learn."]},{"text":"Jin was tired of asking his mom to tie his sneakers. He sat on the porch step with the laces in his hands and tried the bunny-ears trick. The first loop slipped. The second bow came out crooked. He tried again until the bow stayed. When Mom came outside, Jin stood up and showed her his shoes without saying a word.","idea":"Keep trying until you can do something on your own.","distractors":["Jin sat on the porch step and tied his sneakers.","Bunny ears are the only right way to make a bow.","Moms are always too busy to help with small jobs."]},{"text":"Priya and her little brother built a tower from blocks on the living room rug. When it was almost as tall as the couch, it wobbled and crashed. Priya sighed. Then she made the bottom wider and started again. This time the tower stood so steady that it was still there when their dad came home from work.","idea":"When something falls apart, you can learn from it and build it better.","distractors":["The tower was almost as tall as the couch before it fell.","Little brothers always knock down the towers their sisters build.","Dads should be the ones who build with blocks."]},{"text":"A glove fell out of Mrs. Park's bag on the bus. A girl named Luz saw it slide under a seat. Even though it was not her stop yet, she picked it up and ran after Mrs. Park. Mrs. Park smiled and said it was her lucky glove from her late husband. Luz walked back to the bus stop feeling warm all over.","idea":"Small acts of kindness can mean a lot to someone else.","distractors":["Mrs. Park's glove fell under a seat on the bus.","Gloves should always be kept inside a zipped bag.","It is best to let other people take care of their own things."]},{"text":"On the first day at his new school, Ezra stood alone near the cafeteria door holding his tray. He did not know where anyone sat. A boy named Hugo waved from a table and slid over to make room. They talked about trading cards through the whole lunch. The next day, Ezra looked for new kids who needed a place to sit.","stem":"What is the main message of this story?","idea":"Welcoming a new person can make them feel like they belong.","distractors":["Ezra was holding his tray near the cafeteria door.","Trading cards are the best way to make a new friend.","It is easier to eat lunch alone than with a group."]},{"text":"After the blizzard, Mr. Okafor's front steps were buried under a pile of heavy snow. He was too old to lift a shovel. Twelve-year-old Dev grabbed his family's shovel and cleared the steps before breakfast. When Mr. Okafor tried to pay him, Dev shook his head. 'My mom says neighbors help neighbors,' he said, and he went home to eat.","idea":"Neighbors who help each other make a community stronger.","distractors":["A blizzard covered the front steps with heavy snow.","Old people should never try to lift anything heavy.","Children should be paid every time they do a favor."]},{"text":"It started to pour while Rosa waited at the crosswalk with her small umbrella. Next to her stood a boy with no umbrella at all, shivering in his thin jacket. Rosa stepped closer and held hers over both of them. They walked together the whole way to school, and both of them stayed mostly dry.","idea":"Sharing what you have can help someone else feel better.","distractors":["Rosa waited at the crosswalk when it started to pour.","Umbrellas are too small to keep more than one person dry.","It is smart to stay home on every rainy day."]},{"text":"Caleb dribbled his ball too hard in the kitchen and knocked over Grandma's blue vase. It broke into three pieces. He thought about hiding the pieces in the trash. Instead he picked them up, carried them to Grandma, and told her exactly what happened. She hugged him and said the vase could be glued, but a lie could not be fixed as easily.","stem":"What lesson does this story teach?","idea":"Telling the truth is better than hiding a mistake.","distractors":["Caleb dribbled a ball in the kitchen and broke a vase.","Children should never play with a ball indoors.","Grandmas always get angry when something breaks at home."]},{"text":"Mei found a crumpled five-dollar bill on the sidewalk outside the bakery. She wanted to buy a cookie with it. But she looked around and saw a woman checking her pockets and looking at the ground. Mei walked over and asked if she had lost something. The woman gasped, thanked her, and bought Mei a cookie anyway.","idea":"Doing the honest thing often leads to something good.","distractors":["Mei found a five-dollar bill outside the bakery.","Bakeries sell the best cookies in the whole city.","Money on the ground belongs to whoever finds it first."]},{"text":"The cashier at the corner store gave Omar too much change. He counted it twice on the sidewalk. He had been given two extra dollars. Omar thought about the candy he could buy, but he marched back inside and set the bills on the counter. The cashier nodded and said honest customers were the reason he loved his job.","idea":"Being honest matters even when no one would know.","distractors":["Omar counted his change on the sidewalk outside the store.","Cashiers make mistakes because they work too fast.","Candy tastes better when you pay for it with your own money."]},{"text":"A giant cardboard box had to go from the garage to the backyard for the class play. It was too heavy for Noor alone. She tried pushing, then pulling, and it hardly moved. Then three classmates grabbed the other sides. Together they lifted, counted to three, and carried it across the grass. Everyone cheered when it landed on the stage.","idea":"Working together makes hard jobs easier.","distractors":["Noor pushed and pulled a cardboard box across the garage.","Cardboard boxes are the best things for class plays.","It is always faster to do a big job all by yourself."]},{"text":"The second graders planned a garden behind the school. Some dug holes, some carried water, and some pressed seeds into the soil. When one boy got tired, a girl took his shovel while he rested. By the end of the afternoon the whole plot was planted. The teacher said no one child could have finished it so fast.","stem":"What is the main message of this story?","idea":"A group can do more when each person does a part.","distractors":["The second graders carried water to a garden at school.","Shovels are the most important tool for any garden.","Tired children should always stop working and go inside."]},{"text":"In the relay race, Tomas ran the second leg. His heart pounded as the first runner came toward him. He kept his eyes forward and his hand ready. The baton slapped into his palm, and he sprinted. After his turn he handed it off to Aisha, and she dashed to the finish. Their team won by a step, and nobody could say whose run counted most.","idea":"Every teammate's effort matters to the whole team's success.","distractors":["Tomas ran the second leg of the relay race.","The fastest runner on a team should always go last.","Races are only fun for the children who finish first."]},{"text":"Lucia had to read aloud in front of the class for the first time. Her hands shook and her voice sounded tiny. She took a deep breath and read the first sentence anyway. By the third sentence her voice grew steadier. When she finished, her classmates clapped. Lucia smiled all the way home because she had done something that scared her.","idea":"Facing a fear can help you feel proud and strong.","distractors":["Lucia read aloud in front of her class for the first time.","Shaking hands means a person is not ready to try anything.","Classmates clap for everyone who finishes reading a story."]},{"text":"A loud thunderstorm rattled the windows, and Yara's little sister crept into her room. Yara was scared too, but she pulled the blanket over both of them. She told jokes about silly clouds and counted the seconds between flashes. Soon the thunder sounded farther away. Her sister fell asleep, and Yara realized she was no longer afraid.","idea":"Helping someone else can make you feel braver too.","distractors":["A loud thunderstorm shook the windows during the night.","Thunder only happens when the sky is very dark outside.","Sisters should always sleep in separate rooms."]},{"text":"Felix wanted to jump off the diving board, but the water looked very far down. He climbed the ladder, walked to the end, and then climbed back down. Twice. The third time, he closed his eyes, bent his knees, and jumped. Splash! He came up laughing and shouted that he wanted to do it again.","stem":"What is the main message of this story?","idea":"Trying something scary often turns out better than you expected.","distractors":["Felix climbed the ladder and walked to the end of the board.","Diving boards are only for children who are not afraid.","It is safer to watch from the side of the pool forever."]},{"text":"Imani promised to feed the class goldfish every Friday. One Friday she nearly forgot because of a school assembly. At the last minute she remembered and ran back to the classroom. The fish were swimming in circles near the top of the tank. She sprinkled in their food and checked the water. She had kept her promise, and the fish seemed to thank her.","idea":"Keeping a promise shows others that you can be trusted.","distractors":["Imani nearly forgot the goldfish during a school assembly.","Goldfish swim in circles whenever the water is dirty.","Assemblies should never be held on Fridays."]},{"text":"Hugo promised his neighbor that he would walk her dog every afternoon while she was at the hospital. On the second day it rained. Hugo wanted to stay home and play video games. Instead he put on his rain boots, grabbed a leash, and took the dog around the block. When the neighbor came home, she said she could not have asked for a better friend.","idea":"A person who keeps a promise can be counted on.","distractors":["Hugo wore rain boots to walk the dog around the block.","Dogs enjoy playing in the rain more than anything else.","Video games are the best way to spend a rainy afternoon."]},{"text":"Zainab liked to play before cleaning her room, but this time she picked up her toys first. She put the blocks in one bin and the books on the shelf. When she finished, she had plenty of time left, and her mom had even more time for a bedtime story. Zainab thought that chores went by faster when she did them first.","idea":"Finishing your jobs first leaves more time for fun.","distractors":["Zainab put her blocks in one bin and her books on a shelf.","Mothers can only read bedtime stories after the chores are done.","Playing is a waste of time for children who have chores."]},{"text":"Every morning, Nia thanked the school bus driver, Mr. Greene, as she stepped off. One cold day he looked tired. When Nia said thank you, he grinned and told her it was the best part of his route. The next morning, three more children said thanks too. By the end of the week, the whole bus was waving at him.","stem":"What lesson does this story teach?","idea":"Saying thank you can brighten someone's day.","distractors":["Nia said thank you to the bus driver every morning.","Bus drivers are always tired in the cold winter months.","Children should only say thanks when a teacher is watching."]},{"text":"Grandma Lena made soup for Jamal when he had a cold. It had too many carrots, and it was a little salty. Jamal ate every bite anyway. When Grandma asked how it tasted, he said it was the best soup he ever had, because it was made just for him. She dabbed her eyes with her apron and smiled.","idea":"We should be thankful for the care that others show us.","distractors":["Grandma Lena made carrot soup because Jamal had a cold.","Salty soup is the best way to get over a cold.","Aprons are good for wiping away tears when you cook."]},{"text":"Sami planted bean seeds in a paper cup and checked them every morning. For days there was only brown soil. He wanted to dig them up to see what was wrong, but his teacher said roots grow in the dark first. Finally, on the ninth day, a small green curl pushed through the dirt. Sami grinned and decided waiting had been worth it.","idea":"Some good things take time, so it pays to be patient.","distractors":["Sami planted bean seeds in a paper cup and watered them.","Soil is brown because seeds need darkness to turn green.","Digging up a seed is the best way to check on it."]},{"text":"The slide at the park had a long line, and Ravi was near the back. A younger boy tried to cut in front of him. Ravi felt a little angry, but he took a breath and let the boy go ahead. When it was finally his turn, nobody was waiting behind him, and he could slide three times in a row. He thought waiting nicely was not so bad.","idea":"Being patient and polite can turn out better than being pushy.","distractors":["The slide at the park had a long line that day.","Younger children should always be allowed to go first.","Playground slides are only fun when nobody else is there."]},{"text":"Dani had six crayons left in the box and wanted to draw a rainbow. Her friend Sol had only a pencil. Dani looked at the crayons, then at Sol's blank page. She handed him the red, the yellow, and the blue. Sol drew a giant sunrise, and Dani drew the rainbow with her other three. Their pictures hung side by side on the classroom wall.","idea":"Sharing makes it possible for friends to create more together.","distractors":["Dani had six crayons left in her box that morning.","A rainbow is the best picture a child can draw.","Pencils work better than crayons for most pictures."]},{"text":"At the bodega, Mr. Haddad gave every child who came in on Friday a free piece of fruit. Tasha noticed that one boy was very quiet and looked hungry. She split her orange in half and gave him the bigger part. He smiled and said it was the juiciest orange he ever tasted. Tasha walked home feeling full of something besides fruit.","stem":"What is the main message of this story?","idea":"Giving to others can bring happiness to both people.","distractors":["Mr. Haddad gave a free fruit to children on Fridays.","Oranges are tastier than any other kind of fruit.","A person should always keep the biggest piece for herself."]},{"text":"The librarian asked for volunteers to bring in books for a book swap. Elijah had read his favorite adventure story ten times. It was hard to give away, but he knew someone else might love it. At the swap he found a book about volcanoes that he had never seen before. He left with a new favorite and the feeling that the old one was in good hands.","idea":"Sharing something you love can lead to something new.","distractors":["Elijah had read his favorite adventure story ten times.","Volcano books are always better than adventure stories.","Libraries should never ask children to give away their books."]},{"text":"Marcus and Leo built a tall sand castle together at the beach. When Marcus went to get water, a wave rolled in and washed one tower away. Leo felt terrible and said he was sorry. Marcus was upset for a minute, then shrugged. 'It was the ocean, not you,' he said. They grabbed their buckets and started the tower again.","idea":"Forgiving a friend helps you enjoy each other's company again.","distractors":["A wave washed away one tower of the sand castle.","The ocean should never be allowed near the beach.","Sand castles last longer when they are built far from water."]},{"text":"Ines said something mean to her cousin Paco during a board game, and he stormed out of the room. All afternoon she felt a knot in her stomach. At dinner she looked at him across the table and said she was sorry for what she said. Paco nodded and passed her the rice. After dinner, they finished the game and laughed again.","stem":"What lesson does this story teach?","idea":"A sincere apology can mend hurt feelings.","distractors":["Ines and Paco were playing a board game when the argument began.","Board games are only fun when nobody ever loses.","Cousins should eat dinner at separate tables when they are mad."]},{"text":"Two girls had a fight over a jump rope at recess. Each pulled one end, and the rope snapped in the middle. They stared at the pieces. Then Hana laughed a little and said they could tie the ends back together. Soon the rope was shorter, but it still worked. After that, they took turns every day and never argued about it again.","idea":"Working things out peacefully is better than fighting.","distractors":["A jump rope snapped in the middle during recess.","Short jump ropes are better than long ones for games.","Recess is the best time to settle every disagreement."]},{"text":"Rain kept the class indoors, and Wen had no toys. He found an empty paper towel tube, two buttons, and some tape. He taped the buttons to the tube and called it a spaceship telescope. Soon four classmates lined up to look through it at the ceiling stars. Wen smiled, because he had made something wonderful from things that were headed for the trash.","idea":"You can make something wonderful out of ordinary things.","distractors":["Wen found a paper towel tube and two buttons in the classroom.","Telescopes can only be made from cardboard and tape.","Rainy days are always boring for children indoors."]},{"text":"Yusuf's costume for the school play was missing a hat, and the show was that afternoon. He looked in the closet and found nothing. Then he noticed his mom's yellow mixing bowl. He turned it upside down on his head, tied a ribbon around it, and strutted onto the stage as a royal baker. The audience laughed and clapped at his clever idea.","stem":"What is the main message of this story?","idea":"Using your imagination can solve a problem.","distractors":["Yusuf's costume for the school play was missing a hat.","Mixing bowls are the best hats for any costume party.","Plays should never start before the costumes are ready."]},{"text":"Mira had no wrapping paper for her friend's birthday present, and the stores were closed. She looked around the apartment. On the shelf was a stack of old maps. She wrapped the box in a map of the city, tied it with yarn, and drew a little star on the top. Her friend loved it and traced the streets to find her own building.","idea":"A little creativity can turn plain things into special gifts.","distractors":["The stores were closed when Mira needed wrapping paper.","Maps are the best thing to wrap any present in.","Birthday gifts are only good when they come from a store."]},{"text":"A new girl named Anya wore thick glasses and read at lunch instead of talking. Some kids whispered that she was strange. Then Jordan sat next to her and asked what she was reading. It was a book about dragons, and Jordan loved dragons too. By the end of the week, Anya had a whole table of friends who wanted to talk about books.","idea":"Getting to know someone shows how much you have in common.","distractors":["Anya wore thick glasses and read at lunch every day.","Dragon books are the only good books for lunchtime.","Quiet children usually do not want to make friends."]},{"text":"The kids chose teams for kickball, and Beto was picked last again. He kicked at the grass and looked down. Then Carlos, the team captain, said he wanted Beto at first base. Beto caught the first ball that came his way and tagged a runner out. After that, the kids wanted him on their team, and Beto stood up a little taller.","stem":"What lesson does this story teach?","idea":"Everyone has something to offer when given a chance.","distractors":["Beto was chosen last for kickball again that day.","The team captain always picks the best players first.","Kickball is more fun when you play it at first base."]},{"text":"At the playground, a boy with a cast on his leg watched the others play tag. He couldn't run, so he sat on a bench. A girl named Kali noticed and changed the rules. Everyone had to freeze when she called, and the boy was the one who yelled 'freeze' from the bench. Soon the whole group laughed together as he led the game.","idea":"We can change a game so that everyone can join in.","distractors":["A boy with a cast on his leg sat on a bench.","Tag is a game that can only be played by fast runners.","Playgrounds should have more benches for children who are tired."]},{"text":"Teo's grandfather told him not to touch the stove while the pot was hot. Teo thought the pot did not look very hot, so he reached for the handle. 'Ow!' he yelled, pulling back his hand. Grandpa ran cold water over it and wrapped it gently. Teo said he understood now that grown-ups give rules for good reasons.","idea":"Listening to advice can keep you safe.","distractors":["Teo reached for the handle of a hot pot on the stove.","Cold water is the cure for every kind of injury.","Grandfathers know more about cooking than everybody else."]},{"text":"Before the field trip, Ms. Rivera told the class to stay with their partner at the museum. Two boys wandered off to see the dinosaur bones and lost the group. They spent ten frightened minutes looking for everyone until a guard found them. After that, they held their partners' hands and listened closely to every direction the teacher gave.","stem":"What is the main message of this story?","idea":"Following the rules helps keep you from getting lost.","distractors":["Two boys wandered off to see the dinosaur bones.","Museums are scary places for most young children.","Teachers give directions because they like being in charge."]},{"text":"Lila had never tasted mango, and the orange slices on her plate looked strange. Her friend Ruby said it was juicy and sweet, but Lila crossed her arms and shook her head. At last she took a tiny bite, then a bigger one. Her eyes widened. By the end of lunch, the plate was empty, and she asked her mom to buy some at the store.","idea":"Trying new things can lead to a new favorite.","distractors":["Lila had never tasted mango before lunch that day.","Orange slices are always sweeter than mango slices.","A person should say no to every food she does not know."]},{"text":"Ben was nervous about joining the swim team. He had never swum without floaties. On the first day he clung to the wall while others splashed across the pool. His coach told him to kick for one minute at a time. By the end of the month, Ben swam the whole length and later won a ribbon for the most improved swimmer.","idea":"Starting something new takes courage, but it can be rewarding.","distractors":["Ben clung to the wall on the first day of swim team.","Ribbons are given to swimmers who are fast from the start.","Floaties should be worn by all children until they are older."]},{"text":"A new art class started at the community center, and Soraya was afraid her pictures would not be good enough. She almost stayed home. But she went, sat at the end of the table, and painted a blue horse. The teacher said it was full of feeling. Soraya went back every week and soon painted a whole wall of horses in the hallway.","stem":"What lesson does this story teach?","idea":"It is worth trying something new even if you feel unsure.","distractors":["Soraya painted a blue horse in her first art class.","Horses are the best animals for a child to paint.","A person who is afraid should wait until she is much older."]},{"text":"The park on Maple Street was covered in candy wrappers and soda cans. Nina and her brother grabbed trash bags and began picking up litter. Soon a neighbor joined them, then three more. By the afternoon the park was clean and neat. The next day, children came to play on the grass, and nobody dropped anything on the ground.","idea":"When people help to care for a place, it becomes better for everyone.","distractors":["Nina and her brother picked up candy wrappers and soda cans.","Parks are too messy for children to play in.","Only grown-ups can keep a neighborhood tidy and safe."]},{"text":"Every Saturday, Mr. Kim's class collected cans of food for the neighborhood pantry. Rosa brought three cans from her cupboard, and Jaden brought two. At the end of the month, the shelves in the pantry were full. A woman from the pantry came to thank them and said many families would have dinner because of them.","stem":"What is the main message of this story?","idea":"Even small gifts from many people can help a lot.","distractors":["Mr. Kim's class collected cans of food on Saturdays.","Pantries are places where families go to do their shopping.","Only people with large amounts of money can help others."]},{"text":"A strong wind blew over the fence at the animal shelter, and the dogs ran loose in the yard. The staff could not catch them all alone. Neighbors heard the barking and rushed over. They held out treats, called gently, and soon every dog was safe inside. The shelter workers said they were lucky to live next to such helpful people.","idea":"A caring community comes together when someone needs help.","distractors":["A strong wind blew down the fence at the animal shelter.","Dogs will always run away if they are given treats.","Animal shelters need very tall fences to stay safe."]},{"text":"Omar answered every question on the math test quickly and handed it in first. When the teacher gave it back, it had a red mark on half the page. He had skipped the word problems because he rushed. Omar sighed, then decided that next time he would read each problem slowly. On the next quiz, he checked his work and earned a big smile from the teacher.","idea":"Mistakes can teach you how to do better next time.","distractors":["Omar handed in his math test before the rest of the class.","Word problems are harder than any other part of a test.","A student should always finish first to earn a good grade."]},{"text":"Priya made her first loaf of bread, and it came out flat and hard as a rock. She almost threw it in the trash. Instead she asked Grandma what happened. Grandma said she had forgotten to let the dough rise. The next Sunday Priya waited patiently, and her bread came out tall, soft, and golden. She shared it with the whole building.","stem":"What lesson does this story teach?","idea":"You can learn from a mistake and try again.","distractors":["Priya's first loaf of bread came out flat and hard.","Grandmothers know the only right way to bake bread.","Bread is only good when it is eaten on a Sunday."]},{"text":"During a loud fire drill, a few children started to cry. Mr. Dunn, the teacher, took a slow breath and spoke softly. He told them to hold the rope and walk in a line to the yard. The children copied his calm voice, and they walked quietly outside. Later the principal praised the class for the safest drill of the year.","idea":"Staying calm can help others feel calm too.","distractors":["Mr. Dunn asked the children to hold the rope and walk outside.","Fire drills are held in the yard so children can play.","A teacher must speak loudly to be heard in an emergency."]},{"text":"The power went out in Gabi's apartment during dinner, and everything went dark. Her little brother started to shout. Gabi found a flashlight, lit two candles, and told a funny story with shadow puppets on the wall. Soon he was giggling instead of yelling. When the lights came back on, he asked if they could do shadow puppets again tomorrow.","stem":"What is the main message of this story?","idea":"A calm and cheerful attitude can make a hard moment easier.","distractors":["Gabi made shadow puppets on the wall of her apartment.","Flashlights are the most important tool during dinner.","Little brothers always shout when they are in the dark."]},{"text":"Owls hunt at night when most animals are asleep. Their big eyes can see well in the dark, and their ears can hear a mouse moving in the grass. Soft feathers help an owl fly without making a sound. Because of these special features, an owl can sneak up on its food and catch it before the animal knows it is there.","stem":"What is a central idea of this passage?","idea":"Owls have special body parts that help them hunt at night.","distractors":["Owls have big eyes and very soft feathers on their wings.","Mice are the only food that an owl will ever eat.","Most animals sleep through the whole day and the night."]},{"text":"In the freezing cold of Antarctica, emperor penguins stand together in a huge, tight group. The penguins on the outside get cold, so they slowly move toward the middle where it is warm. Then other penguins take a turn on the outside. By taking turns, every penguin stays warm enough to survive the icy wind.","idea":"Penguins work together to stay warm in the cold.","distractors":["Emperor penguins live in the freezing cold of Antarctica.","Penguins are the only birds that are able to live on ice.","Warm places are the best homes for any kind of bird."]},{"text":"A camel's body is built for the desert. Its wide feet do not sink into the hot sand. Its long eyelashes keep out blowing dust. The hump on its back stores fat, which the camel can use for energy when food is hard to find. A camel can walk for many days across the desert without eating much.","stem":"What is the main idea of this passage?","idea":"A camel's body has features that help it live in the desert.","distractors":["A camel's hump stores fat that it can use for energy.","Deserts are the only places on Earth where camels can survive.","Long eyelashes are the most important part of any animal."]},{"text":"A giraffe's long neck helps it reach leaves at the tops of tall trees. Other animals cannot reach those leaves, so the giraffe does not have to share. Its tall body also lets it see far across the grassland, so it can spot a lion from a long way off. A giraffe's height helps it find food and stay safe.","idea":"A giraffe's height helps it get food and stay safe.","distractors":["Giraffes eat leaves from the tops of very tall trees.","Lions are afraid of every animal that is taller than they are.","Grasslands have more tall trees than anywhere else on Earth."]},{"text":"Many people are afraid of bats, but bats are helpful. A single bat can eat hundreds of insects in one night, including mosquitoes that bite people. Some bats also carry pollen from flower to flower while they drink nectar, which helps plants make fruit. Without bats, gardens and farms would have more pests and less fruit.","stem":"What is a central idea of this passage?","idea":"Bats are helpful animals, even though some people fear them.","distractors":["A single bat can eat hundreds of insects in one night.","Mosquitoes are the only food that bats like to eat.","Gardens are the safest places for bats to sleep in the day."]},{"text":"Ants live together in big groups called colonies. Every ant has a job. Some ants search for food. Others take care of the eggs, and a few guard the nest. When one ant finds a crumb that is too big to carry, it calls other ants to help. Working together, the colony gets more done than a single ant could.","idea":"Ants get big jobs done by working together in a colony.","distractors":["Some ants search for food while other ants guard the nest.","Crumbs are the most important food that any insect will eat.","One ant can carry more than a whole group of ants can."]},{"text":"A caterpillar eats leaves day after day and grows bigger. Then it hangs from a twig and makes a hard shell called a chrysalis. Inside, the caterpillar slowly changes. After about two weeks, the shell opens and a butterfly crawls out. It waits for its wings to dry, and then it flies away to look for flowers.","idea":"A caterpillar goes through big changes to become a butterfly.","distractors":["A chrysalis is a hard shell that hangs from a twig.","Butterflies are the only insects in the world that can fly.","Caterpillars eat more food than any other animal does."]},{"text":"A baby kangaroo is no bigger than a jellybean when it is born. It crawls up its mother's fur and into a pouch on her belly. Inside, it drinks milk and grows for many months. When it is bigger, it peeks out of the pouch and hops in and out. The pouch keeps a baby kangaroo safe while it grows.","stem":"What is the main idea of this passage?","idea":"A kangaroo's pouch keeps its baby safe while it grows.","distractors":["A newborn kangaroo is no bigger than a jellybean.","Kangaroos are the only animals that can hop on two legs.","Mother kangaroos carry their babies in the pouch for only a day."]},{"text":"A seed needs a few things to grow into a plant. It needs water to wake it up, warmth from the soil, and later some sunshine. First, a tiny root grows down into the dirt. Then a green shoot pushes up toward the light. With enough water and sun, the shoot grows leaves and becomes a plant.","idea":"A seed needs water, warmth, and light to grow into a plant.","distractors":["A tiny root grows down into the dirt before anything else.","Plants grow best when they are never given any water at all.","Gardens are the only places where seeds are able to grow."]},{"text":"In the fall, the days get shorter and cooler, and the leaves on many trees change color. The green fades away and shows bright red, orange, and yellow. Soon the leaves dry up and fall to the ground. The tree rests through the cold winter and grows new leaves when spring comes back.","stem":"What is a central idea of this passage?","idea":"Many trees lose their leaves in fall and rest in winter.","distractors":["The leaves turn red, orange, and yellow before they fall.","Trees die every autumn and have to be planted again in spring.","Cool days are the best time to play outside in the park."]},{"text":"Rain comes from clouds, but where do clouds come from? The sun warms water in oceans and lakes, and some of it rises into the air as an invisible gas. High up, the air is cold, and the gas turns into tiny drops that form clouds. When the drops get heavy, they fall as rain, and the water begins its trip again.","idea":"Water travels from the ground to the sky and back as rain.","distractors":["The sun warms the water in oceans and lakes.","Clouds are made of smoke that rises from big cities.","Rain falls only when the whole sky is full of dark clouds."]},{"text":"The Earth spins like a top, and it takes one whole day to turn all the way around. When your part of the Earth faces the sun, it is daytime. As the Earth keeps turning, your part moves away from the sun, and it becomes night. The sun does not move across the sky. We do.","stem":"What is the main idea of this passage?","idea":"Day and night happen because the Earth spins.","distractors":["The Earth takes one whole day to turn around one time.","The sun travels around the Earth and then hides at night.","Nighttime is the best part of the day to look at stars."]},{"text":"Stars look like tiny dots of light, but each star is a giant ball of burning gas. Our sun is a star too. It looks bigger and brighter than the others only because it is much closer to us. The other stars are so far away that their light takes many years to reach our eyes.","idea":"Stars are huge, faraway balls of gas, just like our sun.","distractors":["The sun is a star that is much closer to Earth than the others.","Stars are small lights that people hang in the night sky.","Light always travels from faraway places in a single second."]},{"text":"Squirrels stay busy in the fall. They gather nuts and acorns and bury them in many different places. When snow covers the ground in winter, they sniff the nuts out and dig them up to eat. Some buried nuts are forgotten, and those sprout into new trees. So squirrels help forests grow.","stem":"What is a central idea of this passage?","idea":"Squirrels store food for winter and help new trees grow.","distractors":["Squirrels bury nuts and acorns in many different places.","Snow covers every nut so that squirrels can never find any.","Forests are the only places where squirrels are able to live."]},{"text":"Bright flowers are not just pretty. Their colors and sweet smells attract bees, butterflies, and birds. When an insect lands to drink nectar, a powder called pollen sticks to its body. The insect carries it to the next flower, and that helps the plant make seeds. The flowers need the insects, and the insects need the flowers.","idea":"Flowers and insects help each other.","distractors":["Pollen sticks to the body of an insect while it drinks nectar.","Bees are the only animals that ever visit a flower.","Flowers that have no smell at all are the prettiest ones."]},{"text":"Dolphins talk to each other using clicks, squeaks, and whistles. Each dolphin has its own special whistle that works like a name. When one dolphin calls another dolphin's whistle, that friend swims over. Dolphins also use clicks to find fish, because the sounds bounce off objects and come back as echoes. Sound helps dolphins talk and find food.","stem":"What is the main idea of this passage?","idea":"Dolphins use sounds to talk to each other and find food.","distractors":["Each dolphin has its own special whistle that works like a name.","Dolphins are the only animals that live in ocean water.","Fish are afraid of any sound that they hear under the water."]},{"text":"An elephant's trunk is like a nose, a hand, and a hose all in one. It can smell food from far away. It can pick up a tiny peanut or lift a heavy log. On a hot day, an elephant sucks up water and sprays it over its back to cool off. Its trunk is one of the most useful tools in the animal world.","idea":"An elephant's trunk helps it do many different jobs.","distractors":["An elephant can spray water over its back to cool off.","Peanuts are the only food that an elephant ever picks up.","Hot days are the best time for elephants to take a nap."]},{"text":"Fish live under water, but they still need oxygen to breathe. They do not have lungs like people do. Instead, they take in water through their mouths and pass it over small, feathery parts called gills. The gills pull oxygen out of the water. Then the fish pushes the water back out. Gills let fish breathe under water.","stem":"What is a central idea of this passage?","idea":"Fish use gills to get the oxygen they need from water.","distractors":["Fish take water in through their mouths and push it out again.","Fish stay under the water because they do not need air at all.","Feathers are the main part that helps a fish swim fast."]},{"text":"A snake grows all through its life, but its skin does not stretch. When the skin gets too tight, the snake rubs its nose on a rock to loosen it. Then it slides out of the old skin, leaving it behind like an empty sock. Underneath is a fresh, shiny new skin. Shedding lets a snake keep growing.","idea":"A snake sheds its old skin so that it can keep growing.","distractors":["A snake rubs its nose on a rock to loosen its tight skin.","Snakes shed their skin because it gets dirty and gives them a rash.","A snake can only grow when it spends the day in the warm sun."]},{"text":"A cactus can live in a place with almost no rain. Its thick green stem stores water like a sponge, and its spines keep thirsty animals away. A waxy coat on the outside stops the water from drying up in the hot sun. When rain finally comes, shallow roots soak up water quickly. A cactus is built to save water.","stem":"What is the main idea of this passage?","idea":"A cactus has special parts that help it save water.","distractors":["A cactus stores water inside its thick green stem.","Rain falls in the desert much more often than in forests.","Sharp spines are the most useful part of every plant."]},{"text":"The desert gets very hot in the daytime, so many animals hide in shady holes and sleep. When the sun goes down, the sand cools off and the animals wake up. Mice, snakes, and foxes come out to look for food in the dark. Staying out of the heat helps them save water. Many desert animals live their lives at night.","idea":"Many desert animals sleep by day to stay out of the heat.","distractors":["Mice, snakes, and foxes look for their food in the dark.","Deserts are quiet places where no animals are able to live.","The sand stays very hot all night long in a desert."]},{"text":"A seashell is the empty home of a sea animal. Snails, clams, and oysters grow hard shells to protect their soft bodies. The shell gets bigger as the animal grows. When the animal dies, the shell may wash up on the beach. If you pick up a shell, you are holding a home that once kept a sea animal safe.","idea":"Seashells are homes that protect soft sea animals.","distractors":["A shell gets bigger as the animal inside it grows.","Beaches are the only places where a shell can ever be found.","Sea animals make shells because they like bright colors."]},{"text":"Birds build many kinds of nests. A robin makes a cup of grass and mud in a tree. An eagle piles sticks into a giant nest on a cliff. A woodpecker pecks a hole in a tree trunk. Some birds just scrape a little hollow in the sand. Each nest keeps eggs warm and safe in the way that suits the bird.","stem":"What is a central idea of this passage?","idea":"Different birds build different nests to protect their eggs.","distractors":["A robin builds a cup of grass and mud in a tree.","Every bird in the world makes a nest from sticks and big leaves.","Eggs only stay warm when they sit in the bright sun."]},{"text":"Koalas live in Australia and eat almost nothing but eucalyptus leaves. The leaves are tough and do not give much energy, so koalas sleep for most of the day to save their strength. They spend hours curled up in the branches of a tree. A koala's quiet life matches its food.","idea":"A koala sleeps a lot because its food gives it little energy.","distractors":["Koalas live in Australia and eat eucalyptus leaves.","Eucalyptus trees are the tallest trees in the whole world.","Animals that sleep a lot are lazy and do not like to move."]},{"text":"Your heart is a muscle about the size of your fist. It beats all day and all night to push blood through your body. The blood carries oxygen from the air you breathe to every part of you. When you run, your heart beats faster to send more oxygen to your tired muscles. Your heart works hard to keep you going.","idea":"The heart pumps blood to carry oxygen to the whole body.","distractors":["Your heart is a muscle that is about the size of your fist.","The heart beats only when a person is running or playing.","Oxygen is something that people get only from the food they eat."]},{"text":"Your skeleton has more than two hundred bones. Bones give your body its shape, like the frame of a house. They protect soft parts, too. Your skull guards your brain, and your ribs protect your heart and lungs. Bones are hard, but they are alive, and they get stronger when you exercise and drink milk.","stem":"What is a central idea of this passage?","idea":"Bones support the body and protect its soft parts.","distractors":["Your skull guards your brain and your ribs protect your lungs.","Strong bones are only important for children who play sports.","Bones are hard objects that never grow or change in any way."]},{"text":"When you sleep, your body is busy even though you are still. Your brain sorts what you learned during the day. Your muscles rest and repair themselves. Children grow the most while they sleep. If you do not get enough sleep, you may feel grumpy and have trouble paying attention at school.","idea":"Sleep gives your body and brain the rest they need.","distractors":["Children grow the most while they are fast asleep.","Grumpy people always need more food and less exercise.","The brain stops working completely when a person goes to sleep."]},{"text":"People have different kinds of teeth for different jobs. Sharp front teeth cut food. Pointed teeth tear it. Flat back teeth grind food into small pieces so you can swallow it. Teeth need care. Brushing twice a day and eating fewer sweets keep them strong and free of holes.","stem":"What is the main idea of this passage?","idea":"Different teeth do different jobs, and they need good care.","distractors":["Flat back teeth grind food into small pieces.","People who eat sweets will lose all of their teeth by age ten.","Brushing is the only thing that keeps the whole body healthy."]},{"text":"A seesaw is a simple machine called a lever. A lever is a stiff board that rocks on a point in the middle. When you push down on one end, the other end goes up. A lever can help you lift something heavy with less effort. Crowbars, scissors, and wheelbarrows are levers too.","idea":"Levers are simple machines that make lifting easier.","distractors":["A seesaw is a board that rocks on a point in the middle.","Heavy objects cannot be lifted by any kind of machine at all.","Playgrounds are the only places where levers can be found."]},{"text":"The wheel is one of the oldest and most useful inventions. A wheel on a bike or a wagon helps things roll instead of drag, so it takes less strength to move them. Long ago, people used rolling logs to move heavy stones. Later they fixed wheels to a rod, called an axle, and built carts. Wheels make moving things much easier.","stem":"What is a central idea of this passage?","idea":"Wheels are a useful invention that makes moving things easier.","distractors":["People once used rolling logs to move very heavy stones.","Wagons were the first thing that anyone ever built.","A bike is faster than a cart because it has an axle."]},{"text":"Before electric lights, people used candles and oil lamps after dark. Those lights were dim and could start fires. In 1879, Thomas Edison made a bulb that glowed for many hours by running electricity through a thin thread. Soon cities had bright lights at night. The electric bulb made evenings safer and let people work and read after sunset.","idea":"The electric light bulb made nights brighter and safer.","distractors":["Edison made a bulb that glowed for many hours in 1879.","Candles and oil lamps are still the best lights we have today.","People did not need any light at night until the year 1879."]},{"text":"Millions of New Yorkers ride the subway every day. The trains run under the streets, so they do not get stuck in traffic. They stop at stations all over the city, and a ride costs the same no matter how far you go. The subway helps people get to school, work, and parks quickly.","stem":"What is the main idea of this passage?","idea":"The subway helps people travel around New York City easily.","distractors":["Subway trains run under the streets of the city.","Riding the subway is the only way to get anywhere in New York.","Traffic gets worse every year because more people ride trains."]},{"text":"Bridges help people cross rivers and valleys. A beam bridge is a simple board laid over a gap. An arch bridge curves upward and spreads weight to the ground on both sides. A suspension bridge hangs from strong cables, like the Brooklyn Bridge. Builders choose a shape that fits the place, the length, and the weight.","idea":"Builders pick different bridge shapes for different places.","distractors":["The Brooklyn Bridge hangs from strong steel cables.","Beam bridges are the strongest kind of bridge ever built.","Rivers are too wide for any person to cross without a bridge."]},{"text":"Community helpers do jobs that keep our neighborhood safe and running. Firefighters put out fires and rescue people. Sanitation workers take away trash so the streets stay clean. Mail carriers bring letters and packages to each door. Without these helpers, our neighborhoods would be harder and less safe places to live.","stem":"What is a central idea of this passage?","idea":"Community helpers keep a neighborhood safe, clean, and running.","distractors":["Firefighters put out fires and rescue people in trouble.","Mail carriers deliver packages only on days when it is sunny.","Neighborhoods are safest when children do all of the jobs."]},{"text":"Rice is a main food for more than half the people in the world. It grows in fields flooded with shallow water called paddies. Farmers plant small seedlings by hand and wait for months. When the plants turn golden, they cut them and dry the grains. Rice grows best where there is plenty of water and warm weather.","idea":"Rice needs warm weather and lots of water to grow well.","distractors":["Farmers plant the rice seedlings by hand in the paddies.","Rice is the only food that can grow in a flooded field.","Golden plants are always ready to eat on the very same day."]},{"text":"Tiny germs are too small to see, but they can make you sick. They hide on your hands after you touch doorknobs, pets, or your face. Washing with soap and warm water for twenty seconds lifts germs off your skin and sends them down the drain. Clean hands help keep you and the people around you healthy.","stem":"What is the main idea of this passage?","idea":"Washing your hands helps stop germs from making people sick.","distractors":["Germs can hide on doorknobs, pets, and even your face.","Warm water alone is enough to kill every germ on your hands.","Soap is only needed when your hands look very dirty."]},{"text":"Paper starts as a tree. Workers cut down trees and chop them into tiny chips. The chips are mixed with water to make a soft pulp. The pulp is pressed flat and dried into thin sheets. That is how your notebook was made. Because paper comes from trees, recycling it saves forests.","idea":"Paper is made from trees, so recycling it helps save forests.","distractors":["Tiny chips of wood are mixed with water to make soft pulp.","Notebooks are the only things that are ever made of paper.","Trees grow back so quickly that nobody needs to save them."]},{"text":"An aluminum can can be recycled again and again. The can is melted, rolled into thin sheets, and made into a new can, all within about two months. Making a can from recycled metal uses much less energy than making one from scratch. Putting cans in the recycling bin saves energy and keeps trash out of landfills.","stem":"What is a central idea of this passage?","idea":"Recycling cans saves energy and cuts down on trash.","distractors":["Used cans are melted and rolled into thin sheets to make new cans.","Landfills are the best place for any kind of old metal.","New cans taste better when they are made from recycled cans."]},{"text":"Fresh water is precious. Only a tiny part of the Earth's water is clean and safe to drink. When we leave a faucet running, gallons go down the drain. Turning off the tap while brushing teeth, taking shorter showers, and fixing leaks can save a lot of water. Small habits help make sure there is enough for everyone.","idea":"Small habits can help us save fresh water.","distractors":["Turning off the tap while brushing teeth saves water.","The oceans hold enough clean water to last us forever.","Leaks only happen in old houses that are about to fall down."]},{"text":"Trees do more than give shade. Their leaves take in carbon dioxide and give off oxygen, the air we need to breathe. Roots hold soil in place so rain does not wash it away. Birds and squirrels build homes in the branches. A tree cleans the air, protects the ground, and houses animals.","stem":"What is the main idea of this passage?","idea":"Trees help people and animals in many different ways.","distractors":["Tree roots hold the soil in place when it rains.","Shade is the only thing that trees give to the world.","Birds and squirrels can live only in trees along city streets."]},{"text":"Wind is air that is moving. The sun heats the ground in some places more than in others. Warm air rises, and cooler air rushes in to take its place. That moving air is the wind. A light breeze can sway leaves, while a strong wind can bend trees. People use wind to fly kites, sail boats, and turn big wind turbines that make electricity.","idea":"Wind is moving air that people can use in helpful ways.","distractors":["Warm air rises and cooler air rushes in to take its place.","Kites can only be flown when the wind is blowing very strongly.","Wind happens because the trees move their leaves back and forth."]},{"text":"Lightning is a giant spark of electricity in the sky. It heats the air around it so fast that the air booms. That boom is thunder. Light travels faster than sound, so you see lightning first and hear thunder later. If you count the seconds between them, you can tell how far away the storm is.","stem":"What is a central idea of this passage?","idea":"Lightning and thunder come from one event, but light is faster than sound.","distractors":["You can count the seconds between lightning and thunder.","Thunder happens first and lightning is the sound that follows it.","Storms are always farthest away when the sky is very bright."]},{"text":"Every snowflake begins as a tiny ice crystal high in a cold cloud. As the crystal falls, more water freezes onto it, and it grows little arms. Most snowflakes have six sides, but each one has its own pattern. When many flakes fall together, they pile up into soft white snow.","idea":"Snowflakes form from ice crystals, and each has its own pattern.","distractors":["Most snowflakes have six sides, like a small star.","Snow is made of frozen raindrops that fall up from the ground.","Winter is the only time that ice can form in the sky."]},{"text":"A rainforest has layers, like a tall building. The top layer is the canopy, where leaves block the sunlight and monkeys swing. The middle layer is shady. At the bottom, the forest floor is dark and damp, with ferns and mushrooms. Different animals live at each level because each one has the food and shelter they need.","stem":"What is the main idea of this passage?","idea":"Different rainforest animals live in different layers of the forest.","distractors":["The top layer of the rainforest is called the canopy.","Monkeys are the only animals that live in a rainforest.","The forest floor is bright and dry in the middle of the day."]},{"text":"Each part of a plant has a job. Roots hold the plant in place and drink water. The stem carries water up to the leaves. Leaves use sunlight to make food. Flowers make seeds so new plants can grow. When every part does its job, the plant stays healthy.","idea":"Every part of a plant has a job that helps it live.","distractors":["Roots hold the plant in place and drink water from the soil.","Flowers grow only on plants that have very long stems.","Leaves are the only part of a plant that is alive."]},{"text":"A magnet is a piece of metal that pulls on some other metals, like iron. Every magnet has two ends called poles. Opposite poles pull toward each other, but the same poles push apart. Magnets stick to refrigerator doors, and they are inside compasses that show which way is north. Magnets can push and pull without touching.","stem":"What is a central idea of this passage?","idea":"Magnets can push and pull some objects without touching them.","distractors":["Every magnet has two ends that are called poles.","A magnet will stick to any object that is made of plastic.","Refrigerators are the only things that magnets are able to hold."]},{"text":"An echo happens when sound bounces off something hard and comes back to your ears. If you shout in a canyon or in a big empty room, you may hear your own voice again a moment later. Soft things like pillows and curtains soak up sound, so there is no echo. Sound travels in waves that can bounce.","idea":"Echoes happen when sound waves bounce back.","distractors":["You can hear an echo in a canyon or a big empty room.","Pillows make echoes louder when you talk into them.","Sound waves move only when a person shouts very loudly."]},{"text":"A map is a drawing of a place as seen from above. A map key explains what the little pictures and colors mean. Blue often shows water, and green shows parks. A compass rose shows north, south, east, and west. When you know how to read these parts, a map can guide you to a new place without getting lost.","stem":"What is the main idea of this passage?","idea":"Maps use special parts that help people find their way.","distractors":["A map is a drawing of a place as seen from above.","Green on every map means that you should stop and wait.","People who read maps never need to ask anyone for directions."]}],"2":[{"text":"Three weeks before the school concert, Anselm could not play the hard part of his violin piece. Every time he reached the fast notes, his fingers tangled and the sound squeaked. He wanted to quit. His teacher suggested practicing only four measures at a time, very slowly, and then speeding up a little each day. It felt boring, but by the night of the concert the squeaks were gone, and Anselm played the whole piece without a single mistake.","stem":"What is a central idea of this story?","idea":"Breaking a hard task into small steps makes it easier to master.","distractors":["Anselm played his violin without a mistake at the school concert.","Teachers know the secret to making every task feel fun and exciting.","A person should quit if a skill is still hard after three weeks."]},{"text":"Dalia was sure she would win the geography bee, so she barely studied. In the final round, the judge asked her to name the longest river in South America, and her mind went blank. She finished in third place. On the bus home she felt embarrassed, but then she took out her atlas. The next year, she studied a little every night, and when the same kind of question came up, she answered it right away.","idea":"Preparation matters more than being confident.","distractors":["Dalia finished the geography bee in third place that year.","Atlases are the best tool for winning any kind of contest.","A person who feels embarrassed should never enter a contest again."]},{"text":"When Mr. Alvarez's chess club lost its first three matches, the students were ready to give up. 'We are just not good enough,' sighed Wen. But their coach asked them to study each lost game and find the move that went wrong. Slowly the team noticed patterns in their mistakes. They won their last four matches of the season and tied for first place in the city.","stem":"What lesson does this story teach?","idea":"Studying your mistakes can help you improve and eventually succeed.","distractors":["The chess club lost its first three matches of the season.","Coaches are the only people who can help a team win games.","A team that loses its first matches early in the season has no chance of winning."]},{"text":"The stream behind the Garcia family's apartment was clogged with plastic bags and bottles. Lucía wrote a letter to the mayor, but she never heard back. So she asked her neighbors to join her on a Saturday morning with gloves and buckets. Forty people showed up. Within three hours they filled sixty bags with trash, and by spring, ducks were nesting along the clean banks.","idea":"People who act together can solve a problem in their community.","distractors":["Forty neighbors collected sixty bags of trash from the stream.","Letters to the mayor are the only way to get things done.","Ducks will only nest in streams that are free from every bit of litter."]},{"text":"Backstage at the school play, nothing was going right. The curtain rope was stuck, a prop sword was missing, and the lead actor had lost his voice. Instead of panicking, the stage crew split up. One student fixed the rope, one found the sword in a locker, and another handed the actor a cup of warm tea. When the curtain rose on time, no one in the audience knew about the chaos.","stem":"What is the main message of this story?","idea":"A team can solve many problems when each person does a job.","distractors":["The lead actor in the school play had lost his voice.","Plays go best when the audience knows nothing about the stage crew.","Warm tea is the most useful thing to keep backstage at a show."]},{"text":"Jamal found a wallet on the subway platform. Inside were forty dollars and a library card with a name and a phone number. He thought about all the games he could buy with that money. Instead, he gave the wallet to the station agent, who called the owner. A week later, an elderly man stopped Jamal on the street and shook his hand. 'That wallet held my wife's photo,' he said quietly.","idea":"Doing the right thing matters more than keeping something that isn't yours.","distractors":["Jamal found a wallet with forty dollars on a subway platform.","Station agents are the only people who should ever be trusted to hold lost items.","Money is worth less to a person than a photograph in a wallet."]},{"text":"During the science test, Priya noticed that the girl next to her had written the answers on her palm. When the teacher asked why Priya looked so worried, she paused. Telling would get her classmate in trouble, but staying quiet felt wrong. After class she quietly told the teacher. The teacher thanked her for her honesty, and the classmate later admitted she had been afraid of failing and apologized.","stem":"What lesson does this story teach?","idea":"It can be hard, but honesty is the right choice.","distractors":["Priya noticed answers written on a classmate's palm during a test.","Science tests are harder than any other kind of test in school.","A student should never tell a teacher about what another child does."]},{"text":"A boy named Teo was being teased on the playground about his accent. Most kids looked away. Maya walked over, stood beside him, and said, 'He's my friend, and I like how he talks.' The teasing stopped. At first her hands were shaking, but afterward, she felt taller. Teo's smile that afternoon was the biggest she had ever seen.","idea":"Standing up for others takes courage and can change things.","distractors":["Maya walked over and stood beside Teo on the playground.","Playgrounds are the places where children make most of their friends.","Accents are something that people should try to change as soon as they can."]},{"text":"The town council was voting to close the neighborhood library. Ten-year-old Rafi had borrowed his first chapter book there, and he could not bear to lose it. Though his knees trembled, he walked to the microphone at the meeting and explained what the library meant to him. When he finished, the room was silent. A moment later, people stood and clapped, and the council voted to keep the library open.","stem":"What is a central idea of this story?","idea":"Speaking up, even when you are nervous, can make a difference.","distractors":["Rafi borrowed his first chapter book from the neighborhood library.","Town councils should always keep libraries open for children to visit.","People clap loudest for the speakers who have the shortest speeches."]},{"text":"When Mom was called to work late, she asked Noor to watch her little brother for an hour. Noor wanted to finish her video game, but she turned it off and set out crackers and a puzzle for Sami. She helped him with the corners and washed his sticky hands afterward. When Mom came home, the apartment was quiet and tidy, and she gave Noor a long hug.","idea":"Taking responsibility earns trust from the people who count on you.","distractors":["Noor watched her little brother Sami for one hour while her mother worked late.","Video games should be turned off whenever a child is at home.","Puzzles are the best way to keep a younger child busy."]},{"text":"At the winter toy drive, Elias held his favorite stuffed dinosaur for a long time. His mother did not ask him to give it away. But when he saw a small boy at the shelter table staring at the toy, he slowly placed it in the boy's arms. The boy hugged it tight. Elias felt a pang of missing it, but also a warm glow that stayed with him all night.","stem":"What lesson does this story teach?","idea":"Giving something you love to someone in need can bring joy.","distractors":["Elias held his favorite stuffed dinosaur for a long time.","A person should keep the things that matter the most to them.","Shelters need stuffed animals more than any other kind of toy."]},{"text":"Mr. Greene, the crossing guard, was retiring after thirty years. The students in Room 12 wanted to thank him, so each one wrote a note about a time he had helped them. They taped the notes together into a long paper scroll and presented it at his last morning shift. Mr. Greene read every line, took off his glasses, and wiped his eyes. He said it was the best gift he had ever received.","idea":"Showing gratitude lets people know their work is valued.","distractors":["Mr. Greene was a crossing guard for thirty years.","Paper scrolls are the best way to say goodbye to a worker.","Crossing guards need many notes to keep their jobs interesting."]},{"text":"Ines wanted to bake bread with a sourdough starter, but the recipe said it needed seven days to bubble. She stirred the mixture every morning, but for days it looked like plain paste. On day six she almost threw it out. On day seven it smelled sour, rose to the top of the jar, and popped with bubbles. Her bread came out crusty and delicious, and she was glad she had waited.","stem":"What is a central idea of this story?","idea":"Some things only work out when you give them enough time.","distractors":["Ines stirred her sourdough starter every morning for a week.","Bread tastes best when it is baked with a sour kind of dough.","A recipe should be followed only on the days when it looks right."]},{"text":"Mateo's best friend promised to save him a seat on the field trip bus, but when Mateo got on, the seat was taken by someone else. Mateo felt hurt and did not speak to him the whole ride. That night his friend called and explained that the seat had been taken by a teacher. Mateo took a breath and said he was sorry for being angry before asking. They both laughed.","idea":"Listening to someone's side of the story can repair a friendship.","distractors":["Mateo's friend had promised him a seat on the field trip bus.","Teachers are the only people who should be able to choose where students sit.","A person should stay angry until the other person says sorry first."]},{"text":"Mrs. Abrams lived alone on the third floor, and no one ever seemed to visit her. Imani noticed that her mailbox was often full. One afternoon, she knocked on the door with a plate of cookies. Mrs. Abrams invited her in for tea and told stories about growing up in Poland. After that, Imani visited every Friday, and the apartment hallway felt warmer to both of them.","stem":"What is the main message of this story?","idea":"A small gesture of kindness can end someone's loneliness.","distractors":["Imani brought a plate of cookies to Mrs. Abrams one afternoon.","Cookies are the best way to start a conversation with a neighbor.","Older people should visit children more often than children visit them."]},{"text":"Diego crossed the finish line first at the school race, but he noticed that Colin had fallen near the last turn. Diego waited, then walked back and held out his hand. Colin limped over the line a minute later. The crowd cheered louder for Colin than they had for the winner. Diego did not mind. He said that winning meant more when you could help somebody else.","idea":"Being kind matters more than being first.","distractors":["Diego crossed the finish line first at the school race.","The fastest runner should never stop to help anyone during a race.","A crowd always cheers the loudest for the person who falls down."]},{"text":"Anaya lost the class president election by only three votes. She felt crushed and wanted to skip school. Instead she congratulated the winner, Ravi, and offered to help him with his plans. Together they organized a recycling program that the whole school joined. Later, Ravi told the class that Anaya's ideas had made the program work, and Anaya realized she could lead without a title.","stem":"What lesson does this story teach?","idea":"You can still make a difference after a disappointment.","distractors":["Anaya lost the class president election by three votes.","Only students with titles are able to lead a school program.","Winners of an election should always thank the voters in public."]},{"text":"Whenever Ken saw something he did not understand, he asked why. Why did the moon follow the car? Why did ice float? His older cousins sometimes rolled their eyes. But one day, his question about how shadows work led his teacher to build a sundial in the courtyard. The whole class learned to tell time from the sun, and the teacher thanked Ken for asking.","idea":"Asking questions can help everyone learn something new.","distractors":["Ken asked why the moon seemed to follow the car.","Older cousins know the answers to most questions that children ask.","A sundial is the best tool for teaching a class about shadows."]},{"text":"Farah was sure her way of solving the math puzzle was the best. When the group got stuck, a quiet boy named Tomás suggested drawing a picture first. Farah wanted to argue, but she tried it. The picture made the pattern clear, and the puzzle was solved in minutes. Farah told the class that she had learned that the best idea can come from anyone, even someone who rarely speaks.","stem":"What is a central idea of this story?","idea":"Being open to other people's ideas can lead to better solutions.","distractors":["Tomás suggested drawing a picture to solve the math puzzle.","Quiet students in a class usually have the best answers to give.","A person's own way of solving a problem is always the fastest."]},{"text":"Two soccer teams from different schools had to share one muddy field. At first, they argued about who got to use the goals. Then the coaches suggested that each team play half a game against the other. The kids were nervous, but they cheered for good plays on both sides. By the end, players from both teams were trading jerseys and planning a rematch together.","idea":"Working with others can turn rivals into friends.","distractors":["Two soccer teams shared a muddy field for their practice.","Coaches should decide which team is allowed to use the goals.","A rematch is only fun when it is played on a clean field."]},{"text":"When Sasha moved from Russia, she barely spoke English. At school she kept quiet and ate lunch alone. One day, she drew a picture of her old house and showed it to the girl beside her. The girl pointed to her own drawing, and soon they were sketching together without words. Within a few months, Sasha was speaking in full sentences and had five friends.","stem":"What is the main message of this story?","idea":"Friendship can grow even when people do not share a language.","distractors":["Sasha moved to a new country from Russia and spoke little English.","Drawing pictures is the fastest way to learn a new language.","A person who moves to a new place should eat lunch alone."]},{"text":"Leo's paper airplane crashed in the school contest again and again. His design looked sleek, but it nose-dived every time. After the contest he spent an afternoon testing wing shapes and found that folding the tips upward kept it level. At the next contest, his plane glided longer than anyone else's. He said the earlier failures had shown him exactly what to fix.","idea":"Failure can show you what to change so you can succeed.","distractors":["Leo's plane glided longer than anyone else's at the next contest.","Sleek designs are usually better than plain ones in a contest.","A person should only enter contests that are easy to win."]},{"text":"On the group science project, Mina's idea for the volcano's color mix was the one that earned the praise. The teacher announced that Mina deserved the credit, but Mina shook her head. She explained that three of her teammates had built the model and written the report. The teacher smiled and gave credit to the whole group, and Mina felt prouder than she would have felt alone.","stem":"What lesson does this story teach?","idea":"Sharing credit with others shows honesty and fairness.","distractors":["Mina's color idea earned praise on the science project.","Volcano models are the most common projects at science fairs.","A teacher should always give credit to the best idea."]},{"text":"A thirsty crow flew over a dry field until it spotted a tall pitcher. Inside was water, but only a little at the bottom, and the crow's beak could not reach it. The crow tried to tip the pitcher over, but it was far too heavy. Then the crow noticed a pile of small pebbles on the ground. One by one it dropped them into the pitcher, and the water crept higher with each stone. At last the crow could drink all it wanted.","stem":"What is the central message of this fable?","idea":"Clever thinking can solve a problem that strength cannot.","distractors":["A thirsty crow dropped pebbles into a tall pitcher of water.","Birds should always carry a few small stones with them to use.","A heavy pitcher is much harder to tip over than a light one."]},{"text":"All summer long, a grasshopper sang and played in the sunshine while a line of ants carried grains of wheat to their nest. The grasshopper laughed at them for working so hard. When winter arrived, the fields were bare and white with snow, and the grasshopper had nothing to eat. Shivering, it knocked at the ants' door. The ants shared a little food, but they reminded the grasshopper that summer was the time to prepare.","idea":"Preparing ahead protects you when hard times come.","distractors":["The ants carried grains of wheat to their nest all summer.","Music is something that should be played only during the winter.","Ants are always happy to share food with every hungry visitor."]},{"text":"A sleeping lion woke up when a tiny mouse ran across his paw. He caught the mouse and was about to eat it. The mouse begged for mercy and promised to repay the favor someday. The lion laughed at the idea that a mouse could help him, but he let it go. Later, hunters trapped the lion in a strong net. The mouse heard his roars, hurried over, and gnawed through the ropes until the lion was free.","stem":"What lesson does this fable teach?","idea":"Even a small helper can repay kindness in a big way.","distractors":["A mouse gnawed through the ropes of a net to free a lion.","Lions are the only animals that can ever be caught by hunters.","The strongest animal in the forest should never spare a smaller one."]},{"text":"A shepherd boy was bored watching the sheep on the hillside, so he shouted, 'Wolf! Wolf!' The villagers dropped their work and ran up the hill, but there was no wolf. The boy giggled. He did the same thing the next day. On the third day, a real wolf crept out of the woods. The boy screamed for help, but no one came, because the villagers thought it was another joke.","idea":"If you lie often, people will not believe you when you tell the truth.","distractors":["The shepherd boy shouted that there was a wolf on the hillside.","Villagers should never leave their work to help a shepherd.","Sheep are in the greatest danger on a quiet and sunny day, when the wolves are hungry."]},{"text":"A dog trotted home from the butcher shop with a juicy bone in its mouth. To get there, it crossed a wooden bridge over a creek. Looking down, the dog saw another dog in the water, and that dog had a bone, too. The dog thought the other bone looked bigger. It snapped at the reflection, and its own bone dropped into the creek and floated away. The dog went home with nothing.","stem":"What is the central message of this fable?","idea":"Being greedy can cause you to lose what you already have.","distractors":["A dog crossed a wooden bridge with a bone in its mouth.","A reflection in the water shows exactly how things look underneath.","Dogs should always cross a bridge in the very early morning."]},{"text":"A hare mocked a tortoise for being slow, so the tortoise challenged him to a race. The hare sprinted ahead and was so far in front that he lay down under a tree for a nap. The tortoise kept moving, one steady step after another. When the hare woke up, he raced toward the finish line, only to find the tortoise already crossing it. The hare had been too sure of his speed to keep going.","idea":"Steady effort can beat talent that is not used well.","distractors":["The hare took a nap under a tree during the race.","Tortoises are able to move faster than hares if they train.","A race should always be run in the cool part of the day."]},{"text":"A fox spotted a bunch of ripe grapes hanging from a high vine. His mouth watered. He jumped, but the grapes were just out of reach. He tried again and again, panting after each jump. At last, the fox turned away with his nose in the air. 'Those grapes are probably sour anyway,' he said. And off he went, pretending he had never wanted them at all.","stem":"What is the central message of this fable?","idea":"People sometimes say they do not want what they cannot get.","distractors":["A fox jumped again and again to reach some grapes.","Grapes taste sour when they grow on a vine that is very high.","Foxes should always stay away from the vines in a garden."]},{"text":"Every Sunday, Mateo's grandmother pulled out a worn, grease-spotted recipe card for arroz con pollo. The handwriting was her mother's, and the corners were soft as cloth. Mateo helped chop the peppers and stir the rice while she told stories of the kitchen in Puerto Rico where she learned the dish. When Mateo grew up and moved away, he made the same meal each Sunday in a tiny apartment, and the smell made it feel like home.","idea":"Family traditions connect people across generations and places.","distractors":["Mateo's grandmother kept a worn recipe card for arroz con pollo.","Cooking in a tiny apartment is much harder than cooking at home.","Handwriting becomes easier to read when it is passed down from a parent."]},{"text":"Before Lunar New Year, Mei's family gathered around the kitchen table to fold dumplings. Her grandfather showed her how to pinch the edges in a neat row, but Mei's first ones looked like lumpy clouds. Everybody laughed, including Mei. Her grandfather said that the shape did not matter, because the dumplings tasted the same and the best part was folding together. That night, she ate the lumpiest one first.","stem":"What is a central idea of this story?","idea":"Spending time together matters more than doing things perfectly.","distractors":["Mei's first dumplings looked like lumpy clouds on the table.","Lunar New Year is the only time that dumplings are ever eaten.","A good cook can fold dumplings perfectly the first time he tries."]},{"text":"On the night of Diwali, Priya's family lit small clay lamps and set them along the apartment windowsills. Then her mother handed Priya a plate of sweets and asked her to knock on every door in the building. Some neighbors had never celebrated Diwali, but they accepted a sweet and wished her a happy holiday. By the end of the evening, the whole hallway glowed with candles and laughter.","idea":"Sharing a celebration can bring neighbors closer together.","distractors":["Priya's family lit small clay lamps along the windowsills.","Sweets are the most important part of any holiday meal.","Only people who know a holiday well should celebrate it with others."]},{"text":"When Lucia's grandmother came to live with them, Lucia felt shy because they spoke different languages. Each evening, Abuela pointed at objects and said the Spanish words, and Lucia repeated them. Lucia taught her English words in return: window, spoon, shoe. After a few months, they could tell each other jokes. Lucia realized that learning a language with someone could build a bridge between them.","stem":"What lesson does this story teach?","idea":"Learning from each other can help two people grow closer.","distractors":["Lucia taught her grandmother English words like window and spoon.","Grandmothers should learn a new language before they move in.","A person learns a language quickly when she works alone."]},{"text":"On hot summer nights, Mr. Okoye's family sat on the front stoop because the apartment was too warm. Neighbors drifted by, and soon a crowd gathered. Mr. Okoye told stories of his village, of a clever spider and a brave drummer. The children listened with wide eyes. When the street lamps flickered on, nobody wanted to go inside. Many years later, those same children told the stories to their own families.","idea":"Stories carry a community's history from one generation to the next.","distractors":["Mr. Okoye told stories about a clever spider and a drummer.","Front stoops are the best places in the city to cool down.","Children listen more closely at night than they do during the day, when it is loud."]},{"text":"When Jonah's best friend moved to another state, they promised to stay close. They wrote letters every week and drew small comics in the margins. At first, the letters felt like a poor substitute for hanging out. But as the months passed, Jonah noticed that he shared things in writing that he had never said out loud. When they finally visited, they hugged and then picked up right where their last letter had ended.","stem":"What is a central idea of this story?","idea":"Friendships can stay strong across a long distance.","distractors":["Jonah and his friend wrote letters to each other every week.","Writing letters is a better way to talk than speaking aloud.","A person should find a new best friend after the first move."]},{"text":"The first snow of the year fell overnight, and the whole street seemed to wake up outside. Nia had only just moved in and knew no one. She stood by her door holding her sled until a boy from next door shouted, 'The best hill is behind the church!' Soon six kids were racing down the slope, tumbling into drifts. By sunset, Nia's cheeks were red and she felt like she belonged on the block.","idea":"An invitation to join in can help a newcomer feel at home.","distractors":["The best sledding hill on the street was behind the church.","Snow is the best weather for meeting new people in the city.","A new neighbor should always wait for others to ask her to play."]},{"text":"Marcus carried five heavy grocery bags up four flights of stairs rather than ask for help. By the third floor his arms were shaking, and one bag split open. Oranges rolled down the steps. Mrs. Chen, his neighbor, came out and helped gather them. She smiled and said that everybody needs help sometimes. Marcus nodded, and the next time he was stuck with heavy bags, he asked right away.","stem":"What lesson does this story teach?","idea":"Asking for help when you need it is a smart choice.","distractors":["Marcus carried five heavy grocery bags up four flights of stairs.","Oranges are the most difficult kind of fruit to carry upstairs.","A person should do every task alone to prove he is strong."]},{"text":"Two cousins argued about whose turn it was to pick the next game. Each one remembered it differently. Their grandfather said he had a solution. He wrote their names on slips of paper and put them in a hat, then drew one. The cousin who was not picked felt a flash of disappointment, but she knew it was fair, and the next time, her name was drawn. After that, whenever they disagreed, they asked for the hat.","idea":"A fair way to decide can end an argument.","distractors":["The cousins wrote their names on slips of paper in a hat.","Grandfathers always remember exactly whose turn it is to choose.","A person who is not chosen should walk away from the game."]},{"text":"After the ice storm, the power went out across the neighborhood for three days. The Santos family had a gas stove and a pile of blankets. Instead of keeping the warmth to themselves, they invited neighbors to come over for hot soup. By the second night, eleven people were packed in the living room, playing cards by candlelight. When the lights finally returned, the neighbors said they almost missed it.","stem":"What is a central idea of this story?","idea":"Hard times can bring neighbors together to help one another.","distractors":["The power was out across the neighborhood for three days.","Gas stoves are the safest way to cook during a storm.","A family should keep its supplies and blankets to itself during bad weather."]},{"text":"When Hudson's dog ran away, he made fifty flyers and walked the neighborhood taping them to lampposts. A week passed with no calls. His mother said maybe it was time to stop. But Hudson kept walking his route each afternoon, calling his dog's name. On the tenth day, a woman called to say a dog with a blue collar was sleeping on her porch. Hudson hugged his dog until it squirmed.","idea":"Not giving up hope can lead to a happy ending.","distractors":["Hudson taped fifty flyers to lampposts around the neighborhood.","Blue collars are the easiest kind for people to notice on a dog.","A person should stop searching after about a week of looking."]},{"text":"Ravi did not make the basketball team, and he was crushed. For two days he stayed in his room. Then his older sister dribbled a ball into his doorway and challenged him to a game. They played every afternoon for weeks. By the next tryouts, his shots swished through the net, and his name was on the list. Ravi said the cut had hurt, but it had made him work harder than he ever had.","stem":"What lesson does this story teach?","idea":"A setback can push you to work harder and improve.","distractors":["Ravi's older sister played basketball with him for weeks.","Tryouts are held every year at the same time for every team.","A person who is cut from a team should find a different sport."]},{"text":"Dana wanted a new bike, but it cost one hundred and twenty dollars. She started a jar and put in her allowance every week. She walked the neighbor's dog and washed cars on weekends. It took four months, and at times the jar looked hopelessly empty. When she finally carried the money to the bike shop, she felt prouder than she would have if her parents had simply bought it for her.","idea":"Working toward a goal over time makes success feel more rewarding.","distractors":["Dana saved her allowance in a jar for four months.","Bikes at the shop cost more than the ones sold online.","Parents should always buy their children whatever they ask for, as soon as they ask."]},{"text":"Omar and Beth both made it to the final round of the spelling bee, and the winner would go to the city contest. Beth spelled 'rhythm' correctly and won. Omar felt a lump in his throat, but he walked over and shook her hand. 'You deserved it,' he said. Beth said he was the reason she studied so hard. They practiced together for the city contest, and both of them were proud.","stem":"What is a central idea of this story?","idea":"Being a good sport means celebrating a friend's success.","distractors":["Beth spelled the word rhythm correctly to win the spelling bee.","Spelling bees are the most difficult kind of school contest.","The person who comes in second should practice alone from then on."]},{"text":"While playing catch, Kofi's throw sailed through the neighbor's front window with a loud crash. His friends ran away. Kofi stood there, heart pounding, looking at the broken glass. He walked up to the door and told the neighbor what had happened. She told him it was an accident. Kofi offered to earn the money for a new pane, and he mowed her lawn all summer until the debt was paid.","idea":"Taking responsibility for a mistake is the right thing to do.","distractors":["Kofi's throw sailed through the neighbor's front window.","Friends should stay and help whenever someone breaks a window.","Lawns are mowed best on a warm summer day by older boys."]},{"text":"On Arbor Day, Mr. Costa brought his granddaughter Elsa to the park to plant a small oak tree. He told her that oaks grow slowly and that it would not be tall for many years. Elsa wondered whether he would see it as a big tree. He smiled and said that planting it was for people who came after them. Years later, Elsa brought her own children to rest in its shade.","stem":"What is the main message of this story?","idea":"Some of the best things we do are for people in the future.","distractors":["Mr. Costa planted a small oak tree with Elsa at the park.","Oak trees grow faster than any other tree in the park.","A grandparent should wait to plant a tree until the child is older."]},{"text":"The Arctic tern makes the longest migration of any animal on Earth. Each year it flies from the Arctic, where it nests in the northern summer, all the way to Antarctica, where it spends the southern summer, and then back again. The round trip can add up to about forty-four thousand miles, which is nearly twice the distance around the world. Because the tern follows summer to both ends of the planet, it sees more daylight than any other creature. Yet a bird that weighs about as much as a small apple completes this journey every year for decades.","stem":"What is a central idea of this passage?","idea":"The Arctic tern's yearly trip is the longest migration of any animal.","distractors":["The Arctic tern nests in the Arctic and spends the southern summer in Antarctica.","Small birds are able to fly much farther than large birds can.","Daylight lasts longer in the Arctic than in any other place on Earth."]},{"text":"Sunlight looks white, but it is really a mixture of every color. When light enters the atmosphere, it bumps into tiny molecules of gas in the air. Blue light travels in short waves, so it is scattered in all directions far more than red light is. That scattered blue light reaches your eyes from every part of the sky, which is why the sky looks blue during the day. At sunset, the light must travel through much more air to reach you, so most of the blue is scattered away and the reds and oranges are left behind.","idea":"The sky looks blue because air scatters blue light more than other colors.","distractors":["Sunlight is really a mixture that contains every color of light.","The sky is blue because it reflects the color of the oceans.","Sunsets turn orange because the sun gets hotter in the evening."]},{"text":"About sixty-six million years ago, a huge asteroid, roughly six miles wide, struck Earth near what is now Mexico. The crash threw so much dust and ash into the sky that sunlight was blocked for months. Plants could not grow, so the animals that ate them starved, and then the animals that ate those animals starved too. Most living things, including the dinosaurs, died out. Scientists found a thin layer of rock around the world that is rich in a metal called iridium, which is common in asteroids.","stem":"What is the main idea of this passage?","idea":"A giant asteroid strike may have caused the dinosaurs and many other animals to die out.","distractors":["The asteroid that struck Earth was roughly six miles wide.","Dinosaurs disappeared because Earth became too hot for plants to grow.","Scientists have found iridium only in the rocks of one country."]},{"text":"In 1992, a ship crossing the Pacific Ocean lost a shipping container in a storm. Inside were nearly thirty thousand plastic bath toys, including yellow ducks. Over the next years, the toys washed up on beaches in Alaska, Hawaii, and even along the Atlantic coast. Scientists mapped where each duck landed and learned how ocean currents carry things around the world. The accident, though unplanned, gave researchers valuable information about how the ocean moves.","idea":"A lost shipment of bath toys helped scientists learn how ocean currents move.","distractors":["A storm swept a shipping container into the Pacific Ocean in 1992.","Plastic toys float better than any other object in the sea.","Beaches in Alaska get more visitors than beaches along the Atlantic."]},{"text":"A fossil is the remains or the print of a living thing that died long ago. When an animal dies and is quickly buried by mud or sand, its hard parts, such as bones and shells, may be protected from decay. Over millions of years, minerals in the water seep into the bone and slowly turn it to stone. Scientists dig up fossils and study them to learn what ancient animals looked like, what they ate, and how Earth has changed over time.","stem":"What is a central idea of this passage?","idea":"Fossils give scientists clues about life on Earth long ago.","distractors":["Minerals can slowly turn buried bones into stone over millions of years.","Fossils are only made from the bones of very large animals.","Mud and sand are the most important things that scientists study."]},{"text":"Before water reaches your faucet, it goes through a careful cleaning process. First, screens catch leaves, sticks, and other large objects. Next, special chemicals are added that make tiny bits of dirt clump together and sink to the bottom. The water then flows through layers of sand and gravel that filter out smaller particles. Finally, a small amount of chlorine is added to kill germs. Each step removes something different, and together they make the water safe to drink.","idea":"Water goes through several steps that make it safe to drink.","distractors":["Special chemicals make tiny bits of dirt clump together and sink.","Chlorine is the only thing that is needed to clean dirty water.","Screens, sand, and gravel are used to build most water pipes."]},{"text":"For a light bulb to glow, electricity needs a complete path to follow, called a circuit. The path starts at a battery, travels through a wire to the bulb, and returns through another wire back to the battery. If there is a break anywhere along the path, the electricity stops moving and the bulb goes dark. A switch works by opening and closing a gap in the circuit, which is how you turn a lamp on and off.","stem":"What is the main idea of this passage?","idea":"Electricity can flow only when it has a complete, unbroken path to follow.","distractors":["A switch opens and closes a gap in the circuit.","Batteries are the most important part of every light bulb.","A bulb glows brighter when the wire is cut in half."]},{"text":"During a total solar eclipse, the moon moves directly between the sun and Earth and blocks the sun's light. The moon is about four hundred times smaller than the sun, but it is also about four hundred times closer, so it just covers the sun's disk in the sky. For a few minutes, inside a narrow path on Earth, daytime turns to twilight, the air cools, and birds grow quiet. Looking directly at the sun without special glasses can permanently damage the eyes.","idea":"A solar eclipse happens when the moon blocks the sun's light from reaching Earth.","distractors":["The moon is about four hundred times closer to Earth than the sun.","An eclipse happens when Earth moves between the sun and the moon.","People can safely watch an eclipse without any special glasses."]},{"text":"Sometimes people carry plants or animals to a new place without meaning to. If the newcomers have no natural enemies there, they can spread quickly and crowd out the species that already live in the area. Lionfish, for example, were released into the Atlantic Ocean from home aquariums. With no predators nearby, they eat huge numbers of young reef fish. Scientists call these newcomers invasive species, and they work hard to keep them from harming the places they enter.","stem":"What is a central idea of this passage?","idea":"Species moved to new places can harm the plants and animals living there.","distractors":["Lionfish were released into the Atlantic Ocean from home aquariums by their owners.","Aquariums are the best place to keep any kind of wild fish.","Native animals grow faster whenever a new species arrives."]},{"text":"When winter comes and food is scarce, a groundhog crawls into its burrow and goes into a deep sleep called hibernation. Its body temperature drops, and its heartbeat slows from about eighty beats a minute to just a few. Because its body uses so little energy, the groundhog can live for months on the fat it stored during the fall. In spring, it wakes up thin and hungry, ready to find food again.","idea":"Hibernation lets animals survive the winter by using very little energy.","distractors":["A groundhog's heartbeat slows down to just a few beats a minute.","Groundhogs sleep all winter because they are afraid of the cold.","Animals store up their fat in the spring so that they can wake up in the winter."]},{"text":"Every sound begins with a vibration, a back-and-forth movement that is often too fast to see. When you pluck a guitar string, it vibrates and pushes on the air around it. The moving air carries the vibration to your ear as a sound wave. A short, tight string vibrates quickly and makes a high sound, while a long, loose string vibrates slowly and makes a low sound. That is why a musician can change the note by pressing a finger on the string.","stem":"What is the main idea of this passage?","idea":"Sounds are made by vibrations, and faster vibrations make higher notes.","distractors":["A short, tight string vibrates quickly and makes a high sound.","Guitar strings are the only objects that are able to make sound.","Sound waves are carried to your ear by the strings of an instrument, not by the air."]},{"text":"An igloo is a dome-shaped house built from blocks of packed snow. It may seem strange that a house made of snow could keep people warm, but snow is full of tiny pockets of trapped air. Air does not carry heat well, so the snow blocks the cold wind and holds in the warmth from a small lamp and from the people inside. Even when it is far below freezing outside, the inside of an igloo can feel comfortable.","idea":"Snow keeps people warm because the air trapped inside it holds in heat.","distractors":["An igloo is a dome-shaped house that people build from blocks of packed snow.","Igloos stay warm because the sun heats the ice inside the walls.","Lamps are used in igloos mainly to melt the walls of snow."]},{"text":"In 1609, an Italian scientist named Galileo Galilei heard about a new invention, the telescope, and built a better one. When he pointed it at the sky, he saw mountains on the moon and four small moons circling Jupiter. These discoveries showed that not everything in the sky circled the Earth, which went against what most people believed at the time. Galileo faced harsh criticism, but his observations changed the way people understood the universe.","stem":"What is a central idea of this passage?","idea":"Galileo's telescope revealed new things that changed how people saw the universe.","distractors":["Galileo saw four small moons circling the planet Jupiter.","Galileo was the first person who ever invented the telescope.","Most people welcomed Galileo's discoveries as soon as he shared them."]},{"text":"In 1876, Alexander Graham Bell and his assistant, Thomas Watson, were working on a machine that could send voices over a wire. One day, Bell spilled acid and called out, 'Mr. Watson, come here, I want to see you.' In another room, Watson heard the words through the device. It was the first successful telephone call. Within a few years, phones began appearing in homes and businesses, changing how quickly people could share news.","idea":"The telephone changed communication by letting people hear each other from far away.","distractors":["Bell's assistant heard his voice through a wire in another room.","Bell was working on a machine to carry music across the country.","Phones were in every home in the country on the day they were invented."]},{"text":"Wilbur and Orville Wright ran a bicycle shop in Ohio, but they dreamed of flying. They studied how birds twist their wings and built a wooden glider to test their ideas. They even built a small wind tunnel to measure how different wing shapes behaved. On December 17, 1903, near Kitty Hawk, North Carolina, Orville flew their engine-powered airplane for twelve seconds. The short flight proved that people could fly, and it opened the door to modern aviation.","stem":"What is the main idea of this passage?","idea":"Careful testing helped the Wright brothers achieve the first airplane flight.","distractors":["Orville flew the first airplane for twelve seconds near Kitty Hawk.","The Wright brothers learned to fly by working in a bicycle shop.","Flying became possible only after the wind tunnel was invented."]},{"text":"The Statue of Liberty was a gift from the people of France to the United States, and it was dedicated in 1886. The statue is made of thin sheets of copper over an iron frame. When it was new, it was shiny and brownish-red like a penny. Over about thirty years, the copper reacted with air and water and slowly turned green. That green layer actually protects the copper underneath, so the statue has stayed strong for well over a century.","idea":"The statue turned green because copper changes when it is exposed to air and water.","distractors":["The Statue of Liberty was a gift from France in 1886.","The statue was painted green so it would be easy to see from boats.","A new penny turns green after only a few weeks in the rain."]},{"text":"Before Central Park, New York City had very few places where people could rest outdoors. In the 1850s, the city held a contest for a design, and Frederick Law Olmsted and Calvert Vaux won with a plan called the Greensward. They turned rocky, swampy land into meadows, lakes, and winding paths, and they even built roads that dip below the walking paths so that visitors never meet traffic. Today the park covers more than eight hundred acres where millions of people escape the busy city.","stem":"What is a central idea of this passage?","idea":"Central Park was designed to give city people a peaceful place to enjoy nature.","distractors":["Olmsted and Vaux won a contest with a design called the Greensward.","The land for the park was already full of meadows and lakes.","Roads inside the park were built to carry visitors quickly from one end of the park to the other."]},{"text":"From 1892 to 1954, Ellis Island in New York Harbor was the main gateway for people arriving in the United States. After weeks at sea, newcomers climbed into the Great Hall, where doctors checked their health and officials asked questions about their plans. Most people were approved within a few hours and then boarded ferries to the city. More than twelve million people entered the country through Ellis Island, and many Americans today have ancestors who walked through its halls.","idea":"Ellis Island was the place where millions of immigrants first entered the United States.","distractors":["Doctors checked the health of newcomers in the Great Hall.","Most people who arrived at Ellis Island were turned away.","The trip across the sea took only a few hours for each ship."]},{"text":"When the Brooklyn Bridge opened in 1883, it was the longest suspension bridge in the world. Its designer, John Roebling, died before the work began, so his son Washington took over. Washington became so sick from working in the underwater chambers that he could not leave his bed. His wife, Emily, learned about engineering and carried his instructions to the workers for years. Without her determination, the bridge might never have been finished.","stem":"What is the main idea of this passage?","idea":"The Brooklyn Bridge was completed thanks to the determination of the Roebling family.","distractors":["John Roebling died before the construction of the bridge began.","The bridge was the shortest suspension bridge in the world.","Washington Roebling spent each day directing workers at the site."]},{"text":"On October 27, 1904, thousands of New Yorkers lined up to ride the city's first subway line. A ticket cost a nickel. The trains ran from City Hall to Harlem in about twenty-six minutes, a trip that could take hours by horse and wagon. Before the subway, people lived close to where they worked because traveling was slow. After it opened, neighborhoods grew, and the city spread out along the train lines.","idea":"The subway made travel faster and helped New York City grow.","distractors":["The first subway line ran from City Hall to Harlem in 1904.","A ticket for the first subway ride cost more than a dollar.","People lived far from their work before the subway because rides were long."]},{"text":"By the 1960s, the Hudson River was so polluted that people were told not to swim or fish in it. Singer Pete Seeger and a group of friends built a wooden sailboat called the Clearwater and sailed up and down the river to teach people about the pollution. Their effort helped inspire new laws, including the Clean Water Act of 1972. Over the following decades, the river became cleaner, and fish and swimmers returned.","stem":"What is a central idea of this passage?","idea":"Ordinary people who spoke up helped clean a polluted river.","distractors":["Pete Seeger and his friends built a sailboat called the Clearwater.","The Hudson River was always too polluted for fish to live in.","Laws about water pollution were passed before the river became dirty."]},{"text":"Every year, millions of tons of plastic end up in the ocean. Sunlight and waves break it into tiny pieces, but the plastic never fully disappears. Sea turtles mistake floating bags for jellyfish, and seabirds feed small plastic fragments to their chicks. Scientists say the best way to protect ocean animals is to use less single-use plastic, recycle what we do use, and keep trash from reaching the water.","idea":"Plastic in the ocean harms animals, so people need to use less of it.","distractors":["Sea turtles sometimes mistake floating bags for jellyfish.","Waves and sunlight make all of the plastic in the ocean disappear completely over time.","Recycling is the only way to keep animals safe in the ocean."]},{"text":"Because the Earth spins, the sun rises at different times in different places. Long ago, each town set its clocks by the sun, which caused confusion once railroads began to connect towns. In 1883, railroad companies divided the United States into time zones, so that everyone within a zone used the same time. Today the world has twenty-four main time zones. When it is noon in New York, it is already evening in London.","stem":"What is a central idea of this passage?","idea":"Time zones were created so people across a large area could share the same clock time.","distractors":["Railroad companies divided the United States into time zones in 1883.","The sun rises at exactly the same time in every town on Earth.","Each time zone is exactly one mile wide from east to west."]},{"text":"Many of the medicines we use today began as plants. Aspirin, a common pain reliever, was first made from the bark of the willow tree. Quinine, used to treat a disease called malaria, comes from the bark of the cinchona tree in South America. Scientists search rain forests for other plants that might hold cures, which is one reason that protecting forests matters. Some plants may disappear before we learn what they can do.","idea":"Plants are a valuable source of medicines, so protecting them is important.","distractors":["Aspirin was first made from the bark of the willow tree.","Quinine is a medicine that is used to treat headaches.","Scientists have already studied every plant in the rain forest."]},{"text":"Every law in the United States starts as an idea. A member of Congress writes the idea as a bill and introduces it. Small groups called committees study the bill, change it, and vote on it. If it passes, the whole House of Representatives and the Senate must each vote. When both agree on the same version, the bill goes to the president. The president can sign it into law or veto it, and Congress can still pass it with a strong two-thirds vote.","stem":"What is a central idea of this passage?","idea":"A bill must pass through many steps before it becomes a law.","distractors":["A member of Congress writes an idea down as a bill.","The president is the only person in the country who can write a new law.","A veto means that a bill automatically becomes a law."]},{"text":"In 1731, Benjamin Franklin and his friends in Philadelphia pooled their money to buy books that all of them could borrow. It was one of the first lending libraries in America. Later, a wealthy businessman named Andrew Carnegie paid for more than sixteen hundred public libraries across the country because he believed that everyone should have a chance to learn. Today, libraries lend books, computers, and even tools. They are places where anyone, rich or poor, can find knowledge for free.","idea":"Libraries give everyone free access to books and knowledge.","distractors":["Benjamin Franklin and his friends bought books for a lending library in 1731.","Andrew Carnegie wrote many of the books that fill public libraries.","Only people who pay a yearly fee are allowed to borrow library books."]},{"text":"When you put money into a savings account, the bank does not just keep it in a vault. The bank lends some of the money to other people and businesses, and in return it pays you a small amount called interest. If you leave your savings alone, the interest is added to your balance, and next time you earn interest on the new, larger amount too. Over many years, even a small deposit can grow a great deal.","stem":"What is the main idea of this passage?","idea":"Money saved in a bank can grow through interest.","distractors":["The bank lends some of the money in savings accounts to other people.","Banks keep every dollar in a vault and never use any of it.","A larger deposit always earns less interest than a smaller one does."]},{"text":"Imagine that only ten tickets remain for a popular concert, but a thousand people want them. Because the supply is small and the demand is large, sellers can charge a high price, and many buyers will pay it. If a store has a thousand umbrellas but few people want them on a sunny day, the store may lower the price to attract buyers. Prices tend to rise when something is scarce and many people want it, and fall when it is plentiful and few want it.","idea":"Prices go up when many people want something that is scarce.","distractors":["A store may lower the price of umbrellas on a sunny day.","Prices stay exactly the same no matter how many people want an item.","Concert tickets are always cheap because so many people want to attend."]},{"text":"On December 1, 1955, Rosa Parks boarded a city bus in Montgomery, Alabama. When the driver ordered her to give up her seat to a white passenger, as the law required at the time, she quietly refused and was arrested. Her stand sparked a boycott in which thousands of Black residents refused to ride the buses for more than a year. In 1956, the Supreme Court ruled that segregated buses were against the law. One person's quiet courage helped change the country.","stem":"What is a central idea of this passage?","idea":"One person's brave choice can inspire change for many people.","distractors":["Rosa Parks was arrested after refusing to give up her bus seat in Montgomery.","The boycott ended after only a few days because the buses stopped running.","Rosa Parks was the only person in Montgomery who ever broke the law."]},{"text":"In 1947, Jackie Robinson walked onto Ebbets Field in Brooklyn as the first Black player in modern major league baseball. Many fans and even some teammates treated him cruelly, shouting insults and refusing to play beside him. Robinson had promised the Dodgers' manager not to fight back, and he kept that promise while letting his playing speak for him. He won Rookie of the Year, and his courage opened the door for many other players.","idea":"Jackie Robinson's courage and skill helped break baseball's color barrier.","distractors":["Robinson played for the Brooklyn Dodgers at Ebbets Field in 1947.","Baseball fans have always welcomed every new player with cheers.","Robinson won Rookie of the Year because he fought anyone who insulted him."]},{"text":"Amelia Earhart loved airplanes from the first time she saw one. She took flying lessons in 1921, working odd jobs to pay for them. In 1932, she became the first woman to fly alone across the Atlantic Ocean, a trip of nearly fifteen hours through storms and ice. She set many more records and encouraged girls to follow their dreams. In 1937, she vanished over the Pacific while attempting to fly around the world, leaving a mystery that remains unsolved.","stem":"What is the main idea of this passage?","idea":"Amelia Earhart's bold flights inspired others to chase their dreams.","distractors":["Earhart worked odd jobs to pay for her first flying lessons.","Earhart became the first person in history to fly across the Atlantic.","Her flight around the world was completed safely in 1937."]},{"text":"Helen Keller lost both her sight and her hearing from an illness when she was nineteen months old. As a child, she was angry and lonely because she could not communicate. Then a teacher named Anne Sullivan came to live with her. At a water pump, Sullivan spelled the word water into one of Helen's hands while cool water ran over the other. Suddenly Helen understood that everything had a name. She went on to graduate from college and write books.","idea":"With a patient teacher's help, Helen Keller learned to communicate and succeed.","distractors":["Anne Sullivan spelled the word water into Helen's hand at a pump.","Helen Keller was born unable to see or hear anything at all.","Water pumps are the best tools for teaching words to children."]},{"text":"Marie Curie grew up in Poland at a time when girls were not allowed to attend university there, so she saved money and moved to Paris to study science. Working with her husband Pierre in a leaky shed, she discovered two new elements, polonium and radium. She won the Nobel Prize in Physics in 1903 and a second Nobel Prize, in Chemistry, in 1911. She is still the only person to win Nobel Prizes in two different sciences, and she trained a generation of scientists.","stem":"What is a central idea of this passage?","idea":"Marie Curie's determination and discoveries made her a pioneer in science.","distractors":["Curie discovered the elements polonium and radium with her husband Pierre.","Women in Poland were welcomed to study at every university.","Her Nobel Prize in Physics came after her prize in Chemistry."]},{"text":"On July 20, 1969, Neil Armstrong climbed down a ladder from the Apollo 11 lunar module and became the first person to walk on the moon. As he stepped onto the dusty gray surface, he said, 'That's one small step for man, one giant leap for mankind.' Hundreds of millions of people watched on television. The mission took the effort of about four hundred thousand workers, and it showed what people can achieve when they work toward a shared goal.","idea":"Reaching the moon took the work of thousands of people toward a shared goal.","distractors":["Neil Armstrong was the first person to walk on the moon.","The Apollo 11 mission was completed by one astronaut working alone.","The moon's surface was covered in thick green plants in 1969."]},{"text":"For hundreds of years, the Haudenosaunee people of what is now New York State planted corn, beans, and squash together in a method called the Three Sisters. The tall corn stalks gave the bean vines a pole to climb. The beans added nutrients to the soil that the corn needs to grow. The wide squash leaves covered the ground, keeping weeds down and the soil moist. Each plant helped the others, so the garden produced more food than it would have if the crops were grown separately.","stem":"What is the main idea of this passage?","idea":"Growing certain plants together helps each plant grow better.","distractors":["Corn stalks gave the bean vines a pole to climb.","Squash is the most important crop for any garden.","Haudenosaunee farmers planted each crop in a different field."]},{"text":"Long before skyscrapers, the land that is now New York City was home to the Lenape people. They fished in the rivers, hunted in the forests, and planted gardens near the shore. They traveled along trails, and one of the main paths ran the length of Manhattan Island. Today that path is called Broadway. The name Manhattan itself comes from a Lenape word. Many places in the city still carry reminders of the people who lived here first.","idea":"The Lenape people lived in New York long ago, and their influence remains.","distractors":["The Lenape fished in the rivers and planted gardens near the shore.","Broadway was first built by the Dutch as a path for wagons.","The name Manhattan comes from the first skyscraper built in the city."]},{"text":"In 1625, Dutch settlers built a small trading post at the southern tip of Manhattan and named it New Amsterdam. Ships brought furs and goods across the Atlantic, and people from many countries came to live and work there. Even then, many different languages could be heard in its streets. In 1664, English ships sailed into the harbor, and the Dutch leader surrendered without a battle. The English renamed the town New York, but its mix of cultures remained.","stem":"What is a central idea of this passage?","idea":"New York began as a trading town where many cultures mixed together.","distractors":["English ships sailed into the harbor and took over New Amsterdam in 1664, renaming it.","The Dutch fought a long battle to keep control of the town.","New Amsterdam was built as a farm with only a few people."]},{"text":"On April 22, 1970, about twenty million Americans took part in the first Earth Day. They planted trees, cleaned parks, and held rallies to ask leaders to protect the air and water. Rivers were catching fire from pollution, and smog blanketed cities. The huge turnout showed that people cared deeply about the environment. Within a few years, the United States created the Environmental Protection Agency and passed new laws to clean the air and water.","idea":"Many people working together helped lead to laws that protect the environment.","distractors":["About twenty million people took part in the first Earth Day.","Earth Day was created because trees had stopped growing in cities.","The Environmental Protection Agency was created long before the first Earth Day took place."]},{"text":"The heart is a muscle, and like other muscles, it grows stronger with exercise. When you run, jump, or swim, your heart beats faster to deliver oxygen to your muscles. Over time, regular exercise makes the heart bigger and stronger, so it can pump more blood with each beat. A strong heart does not need to beat as often when you are resting. Doctors recommend that children get about an hour of active play every day.","stem":"What is the main idea of this passage?","idea":"Regular exercise makes the heart stronger and healthier.","distractors":["The heart beats faster when you run, jump, or swim.","A strong heart has to beat more often when a person is resting.","Doctors say that children should spend an hour each day sitting still."]},{"text":"A single twelve-ounce can of soda contains about ten teaspoons of sugar. That is more than most doctors recommend for a child in an entire day. When you drink sugar, your body gets a quick burst of energy, but it soon wears off and leaves you tired. Sugar also feeds bacteria in your mouth, which make acid that can wear holes in teeth. Water and milk are better choices for healthy bodies.","idea":"Sugary drinks can harm your body, so water and milk are healthier choices.","distractors":["A twelve-ounce can of soda has about ten teaspoons of sugar.","Sugar gives the body energy that lasts for the entire day.","Bacteria in your mouth cannot live in the presence of sugar."]},{"text":"When people feel nervous or angry, their hearts beat fast and their breathing becomes quick and shallow. Taking slow, deep breaths sends a message to the brain that everything is okay. The heartbeat slows, the muscles relax, and thoughts become clearer. Athletes use this trick before a big game, and students use it before a test. One simple method is to breathe in for four counts and out for four counts. Even a minute of slow breathing can help.","stem":"What is a central idea of this passage?","idea":"Slow, deep breathing can help a person calm down.","distractors":["Athletes and students both use slow breathing before big events.","Quick, shallow breaths help a person feel relaxed and calm.","Breathing slowly is useful only for people who are very nervous."]},{"text":"Every living thing needs energy, and a food chain shows how that energy moves. It begins with the sun. Plants use sunlight to make their own food, so they are called producers. A grasshopper eats the plant, and then a frog eats the grasshopper. A hawk might eat the frog. Each animal in the chain is a consumer. When living things die, decomposers like fungi and bacteria break them down and return nutrients to the soil, and the cycle starts again.","idea":"A food chain shows how energy passes from the sun through living things.","distractors":["Plants are called producers because they make their own food.","Hawks are the only animals that take part in a food chain.","Decomposers are animals that eat plants before the frogs do."]},{"text":"Many people call spiders insects, but scientists place them in different groups. An insect has six legs, three body parts, and usually two antennae. A spider has eight legs, only two body parts, and no antennae. Spiders also lack wings, while many insects can fly. Because of these differences, spiders belong to a group called arachnids, along with scorpions and ticks. Looking closely at an animal's features helps scientists put it in the right group.","stem":"What is the main idea of this passage?","idea":"Scientists sort animals into groups by looking at their body features.","distractors":["Spiders have eight legs and only two body parts.","Insects and spiders belong together because both have wings.","Scorpions and ticks belong to the same group as butterflies."]},{"text":"In the 1700s, sailors on long voyages often developed a painful disease called scurvy. Their gums bled and their wounds would not heal. A Scottish doctor named James Lind tried an experiment on sick sailors, giving different groups different foods. Only the sailors who ate oranges and lemons recovered. Today we know that citrus fruits contain vitamin C, which the body needs to stay healthy. Soon ships carried limes, and scurvy nearly disappeared.","idea":"An experiment helped doctors discover that vitamin C prevents a serious disease.","distractors":["Sailors with scurvy had bleeding gums and wounds that would not heal.","James Lind gave every sailor the same food in his experiment.","Scurvy affected only people who ate too many oranges and lemons."]},{"text":"About one out of every three bites of food we eat depends on pollinators such as bees, butterflies, and bats. These animals carry pollen from flower to flower, allowing plants to make fruit and seeds. But in many places, pollinators are disappearing because of lost habitat, pesticides, and disease. Without them, apples, almonds, and blueberries would be harder to grow. People can help by planting flowers, avoiding harmful chemicals, and leaving small patches of wild grass in yards.","stem":"What is a central idea of this passage?","idea":"Pollinators are important to our food, and people can help protect them.","distractors":["Pollinators carry pollen from one flower to another.","Bees are the only animals that move pollen between flowers.","Planting flowers makes it harder for pollinators to find food."]},{"text":"Volunteers give their time to help without being paid. They might read to children, deliver meals to older neighbors, or pick up trash in a park. Communities benefit because more work gets done than money alone could pay for. Volunteers benefit too. Studies show that people who help others often feel happier, make new friends, and learn new skills. When volunteering becomes a habit, it can make a whole neighborhood a better place to live.","idea":"Volunteering helps both the community and the person who volunteers.","distractors":["Volunteers might read to children, deliver meals to neighbors, or pick up litter.","People who volunteer are always paid for their time and effort.","Neighborhoods are happiest when no one is willing to help."]},{"text":"In 1928, a scientist named Alexander Fleming came back from vacation and noticed something odd in a dish where he had been growing bacteria. A patch of blue-green mold had grown there, and around the mold, the bacteria had died. Instead of throwing the dish away, Fleming studied it. He discovered that the mold made a substance that killed germs. That substance, penicillin, became the first antibiotic and has saved millions of lives.","stem":"What is the main idea of this passage?","idea":"Noticing an unexpected result led to a discovery that saved many lives.","distractors":["Fleming found a patch of mold in a dish of bacteria.","Fleming planned to make penicillin before he left on vacation.","Mold is useful because it helps bacteria grow more quickly."]},{"text":"Harriet Tubman escaped from slavery in Maryland in 1849 and made her way north to freedom. She could have stayed safe, but she chose to return. Over the next ten years, she made about thirteen trips back to the South and guided roughly seventy people along secret routes known as the Underground Railroad. She traveled at night, using the North Star for direction, and was never caught. Her bravery has made her a lasting symbol of courage.","idea":"Harriet Tubman risked her life again and again to help others reach freedom.","distractors":["Tubman made about thirteen trips back to the South.","The Underground Railroad was a real railroad with trains that ran beneath the ground.","Tubman stayed safe in the North after she escaped in 1849."]}]}},{"id":"2026-11-04-nys-style-passages","released":"2026-11-04","kind":"manual","reading_passages":{"1":[{"long":true,"text":"Sea Otters\n\nThick Fur\n\nSea otters have the thickest fur of any animal on Earth. A single square inch can hold about one million hairs. Most sea mammals stay warm with a layer of fat, but sea otters have only a little. Instead, their fur traps a layer of air next to the skin, and that air acts like a warm blanket in the cold ocean. Otters spend hours each day grooming their fur to keep it clean and fluffy.\n\nUsing Tools\n\nSea otters are clever. They dive to the ocean floor to find clams and snails, and they carry a favorite rock back up with them. Then they float on their backs, place the rock on their chests, and crack the shell against it. Using tools is rare in the animal world, but sea otters do it every day.\n\nFloating Families\n\nA mother sea otter floats on her back with her pup resting on her belly. A pup's fur holds so much air that it bobs like a cork and cannot dive. While the mother searches for food, she wraps her pup in seaweed so the waves will not carry it away.\n\nHelping the Ocean\n\nSea otters eat sea urchins, which munch on kelp. Kelp forests shelter fish and many other animals. By keeping the urchins in check, otters protect the kelp and all of the animals that live there.","questions":[{"question":"How do sea otters stay warm in cold water?","choices":["They wrap themselves in thick strips of kelp each night.","They grow a very thick layer of fat under the skin.","Their thick fur traps a layer of air against their skin.","They spend the whole day swimming close to shore."],"answer":"Their thick fur traps a layer of air against their skin.","level":0},{"question":"In which section would a reader find information about how sea otters crack open clams?","choices":["Thick Fur","Floating Families","Helping the Ocean","Using Tools"],"answer":"Using Tools","level":1},{"question":"What does the word \"grooming\" mean in the section \"Thick Fur\"?","choices":["cleaning and brushing","hunting for food","swimming quickly","hiding from enemies"],"answer":"cleaning and brushing","level":2},{"question":"Why does a mother sea otter wrap her pup in seaweed?","choices":["So the pup will stay warm during the cold nights.","So the pup can learn to dive.","So the pup will not need any food.","So the waves will not carry it away."],"answer":"So the waves will not carry it away.","level":1},{"question":"Which detail best supports the idea that sea otters help the ocean?","choices":["Otters eat sea urchins, which protects the kelp forests.","Otters have about one million hairs in each square inch.","Otters float on their backs with their pups.","Otters use rocks to crack open their shells."],"answer":"Otters eat sea urchins, which protects the kelp forests.","level":2}]},{"long":true,"text":"Giant Pandas\n\nA Bamboo Diet\n\nA giant panda eats almost nothing but bamboo. It sits upright and holds the stalks in its front paws, peeling off the tough outer layer to reach the soft inside. Bamboo is not very nutritious, so a panda must eat for about twelve hours a day. An adult can eat more than twenty pounds of bamboo in one day.\n\nA Special Thumb\n\nA panda has a small bump on each front paw that works almost like a thumb. This extra thumb is really a wrist bone that has grown longer. It helps the panda grip bamboo stalks tightly while it eats.\n\nQuiet Mornings\n\nPandas live high in the mountains of China, where mist covers the forests. They spend most of their time alone and rarely make loud sounds. A mother panda is gentle with her cub, which is born as tiny as a stick of butter and is pink and blind.\n\nProtecting Pandas\n\nLong ago, pandas lived across much of China. As people cut down forests, the pandas lost their homes and their bamboo. Today, workers protect forests and plant bamboo corridors. Thanks to this help, there are more wild pandas than there were forty years ago.","questions":[{"question":"About how many hours a day does a panda spend eating?","choices":["about eight hours","about twelve hours","about four hours","about twenty hours"],"answer":"about twelve hours","level":0},{"question":"What does the word \"upright\" mean in the section \"A Bamboo Diet\"?","choices":["lying down flat","moving very fast","sitting straight up","hanging upside down"],"answer":"sitting straight up","level":2},{"question":"How does a panda's special thumb help it?","choices":["It helps the panda catch fish in a stream.","It helps the panda climb tall trees quickly.","It helps the panda hold bamboo tightly.","It helps the panda dig a den in the snow."],"answer":"It helps the panda hold bamboo tightly.","level":1},{"question":"Why did pandas lose their homes long ago?","choices":["The mist covered the forests all year long and killed the bamboo.","Other animals ate all of their bamboo.","The mountains became too warm for them.","People cut down the forests where they lived."],"answer":"People cut down the forests where they lived.","level":1},{"question":"What is a central idea of this passage?","choices":["Pandas are the loudest animals in the mountains of China.","Bamboo is the only plant that animals are able to eat.","Pandas have special features and need help to stay safe.","A baby panda is born as large as a full-grown adult."],"answer":"Pandas have special features and need help to stay safe.","level":2}]},{"long":true,"text":"Flamingos\n\nWhy They Are Pink\n\nFlamingos are not born pink. A baby flamingo has soft gray feathers. Its pink color comes from the food it eats. Tiny shrimp and algae contain colorful substances, and as the flamingo eats them, its feathers slowly turn pink or orange. The more of these foods a flamingo eats, the brighter its feathers become.\n\nStanding on One Leg\n\nFlamingos often stand on one leg, with the other tucked up under their bodies. Scientists believe this helps them save energy and stay warm, because the bird loses less heat through its legs. A flamingo can balance this way for hours, even while it sleeps.\n\nUpside-Down Eating\n\nTo eat, a flamingo puts its head upside down in shallow water. Its bent beak works like a strainer. The flamingo sucks in water and then pushes it out, and the beak catches tiny shrimp and algae. Flamingos feed in large groups called flocks, sometimes with thousands of birds.\n\nNesting Together\n\nWhen it is time to nest, flamingos build small mounds of mud. Each mother lays just one egg. Both parents take turns sitting on the egg and, later, feeding the chick with a special milk that is made in their throats.","questions":[{"question":"What color are baby flamingos?","choices":["gray","pink","orange","white"],"answer":"gray","level":0},{"question":"What makes a flamingo's feathers turn pink?","choices":["the sunshine that it feels each day","the mud that it uses to build nests","the muddy water in which it swims","the shrimp and algae it eats"],"answer":"the shrimp and algae it eats","level":1},{"question":"According to the passage, why do flamingos stand on one leg?","choices":["It lets them run faster across the mud.","It helps them look taller than other birds.","It may help them save energy and stay warm.","It keeps their feathers dry during storms."],"answer":"It may help them save energy and stay warm.","level":1},{"question":"What does the word \"strainer\" tell the reader about a flamingo's beak?","choices":["It makes loud sounds to call others.","It cuts food into small pieces.","It lets water out and catches food.","It keeps the bird's head warm."],"answer":"It lets water out and catches food.","level":2},{"question":"In which section would a reader learn about how flamingo parents care for their chick?","choices":["Upside-Down Eating","Standing on One Leg","Nesting Together","Why They Are Pink"],"answer":"Nesting Together","level":1}]},{"long":true,"text":"Polar Bears\n\nBuilt for the Cold\n\nA polar bear's fur looks white, but each hair is really clear with a hollow center. The hairs scatter light, which makes the fur look white. Underneath, the bear's skin is black, and it soaks up warmth from the sun. A thick layer of fat called blubber keeps the bear warm even in icy water. Big, wide paws spread out like snowshoes so the bear does not sink in the snow.\n\nHunting on Ice\n\nPolar bears hunt seals. A bear waits patiently beside a hole in the ice, perfectly still, until a seal comes up to breathe. Then it strikes. Polar bears are also strong swimmers and can paddle for many miles between pieces of ice.\n\nGrowing Cubs\n\nIn winter, a mother digs a den in the snow and has her cubs there. The cubs are tiny and drink her rich milk. When spring comes, the cubs follow their mother out onto the ice, and she teaches them how to hunt.\n\nMelting Ice\n\nPolar bears need sea ice to hunt. But warmer weather is making the ice melt earlier each year, so bears have less time to hunt. Scientists study the bears and the ice to find ways to protect them.","questions":[{"question":"Why does a polar bear's fur look white?","choices":["The fur grows white only in the winter.","Each clear hair scatters light, so the fur looks white.","The skin under the fur is white.","The hairs are painted white by the falling snow each winter morning."],"answer":"Each clear hair scatters light, so the fur looks white.","level":0},{"question":"What does a polar bear's black skin do?","choices":["It makes the bear's fur look white.","It keeps the bear from sinking in the snow.","It soaks up warmth from the sun.","It helps the bear hide from seals."],"answer":"It soaks up warmth from the sun.","level":1},{"question":"What does the word \"blubber\" mean in the section \"Built for the Cold\"?","choices":["a thick layer of fat","a wide kind of paw","a type of thick fur","a hole in the ice"],"answer":"a thick layer of fat","level":2},{"question":"Why do polar bears have less time to hunt each year?","choices":["The ice grows too thick for the bears to break through it.","The sea ice melts earlier than it used to.","The bears sleep longer in the winter.","The seals have learned how to hide."],"answer":"The sea ice melts earlier than it used to.","level":1},{"question":"Which detail best shows that polar bears are patient?","choices":["Cubs drink their mother's rich milk while they stay in the den.","Polar bears are strong swimmers who paddle for many miles.","A mother digs a den in the snow for her cubs.","A bear waits beside a hole in the ice until a seal comes up."],"answer":"A bear waits beside a hole in the ice until a seal comes up.","level":2}]},{"long":true,"text":"Seahorses\n\nStrange Swimmers\n\nA seahorse swims upright, not sideways like most fish. It is not a fast swimmer. A tiny fin on its back flutters dozens of times each second to push it slowly through the water. Instead of racing, a seahorse often rests, curling its tail around a piece of seagrass so the current will not carry it away.\n\nHungry Hunters\n\nSeahorses have no teeth and no stomach. A seahorse sucks tiny shrimp through its long snout and swallows them whole. Because food passes through so quickly, a seahorse must eat almost all day. It can gobble up to three thousand tiny shrimp in a single day.\n\nDad Has the Babies\n\nSeahorses are one of the few animals where the father carries the babies. The mother places her eggs into a pouch on the father's belly. He keeps them safe for about two weeks, and then he gives birth to hundreds of tiny seahorses.\n\nHidden in the Grass\n\nSeahorses can change color to match the seagrass or coral where they live. Staying hidden keeps them safe from crabs and fish that might eat them.","questions":[{"question":"How does a seahorse swim?","choices":["It races across the water using its big, powerful fins.","It swims upright, using a tiny fin on its back.","It swims sideways using its long tail.","It floats on its back near the surface."],"answer":"It swims upright, using a tiny fin on its back.","level":0},{"question":"Why does a seahorse curl its tail around seagrass?","choices":["So it can look for shrimp in the sand.","So the current will not carry it away.","So it can hide from other seahorses.","So it can keep its eggs warm."],"answer":"So the current will not carry it away.","level":1},{"question":"In which section would a reader learn who carries the baby seahorses?","choices":["Dad Has the Babies","Hidden in the Grass","Hungry Hunters","Strange Swimmers"],"answer":"Dad Has the Babies","level":1},{"question":"What does the word \"current\" mean in the section \"Strange Swimmers\"?","choices":["moving water in the sea","a loud noise under water","a tiny kind of fish","a bright color of grass"],"answer":"moving water in the sea","level":2},{"question":"What is a central idea of this passage?","choices":["Seahorses are the fastest swimmers in the ocean.","Seahorses eat only a few shrimp each day.","Fathers carry the babies of all kinds of animals.","Seahorses are unusual animals with surprising habits."],"answer":"Seahorses are unusual animals with surprising habits.","level":2}]},{"long":true,"text":"Walruses\n\nTusks and Whiskers\n\nBoth male and female walruses have long ivory tusks, which can grow longer than three feet. Walruses use their tusks to haul their heavy bodies out of the water onto the ice, almost like climbing with ice picks. Their stiff whiskers are very sensitive. A walrus sweeps its whiskers across the dark seafloor to feel for clams.\n\nBlubber for Warmth\n\nA walrus can weigh as much as a small car. Under its wrinkled skin is a layer of blubber up to six inches thick. This blubber keeps a walrus warm in freezing water and gives it energy when food is hard to find. When a walrus is warm, its skin looks pink. When it is cold, blood moves away from the skin, and the walrus looks pale.\n\nCrowded Beaches\n\nWalruses are social animals. Hundreds, even thousands, crowd together on beaches and ice, piled on top of one another for warmth. They bark, grunt, and bellow to talk to each other. A mother walrus keeps her calf close and protects it fiercely.\n\nStaying Safe\n\nWalruses have few enemies, but they must stay alert for polar bears and orcas. If one walrus senses danger, it makes noise, and the whole herd rushes into the sea.","questions":[{"question":"What do walruses use their whiskers to do?","choices":["feel for clams on the dark seafloor","dig holes in the sand","catch fish swimming near the ice edge","scare away polar bears"],"answer":"feel for clams on the dark seafloor","level":0},{"question":"What does the word \"sensitive\" mean in the section \"Tusks and Whiskers\"?","choices":["quick to feel even small touches","too stiff to bend at all","very long and sharp","colored a bright pink"],"answer":"quick to feel even small touches","level":2},{"question":"Why does a walrus look pale in the cold?","choices":["It stops eating food for many days.","Its blubber melts and turns white.","Its tusks reflect the bright snow.","Blood moves away from its skin."],"answer":"Blood moves away from its skin.","level":1},{"question":"What does a herd of walruses do when one walrus senses danger?","choices":["The herd stays quiet and hides.","The herd climbs farther up the ice.","The mothers carry the calves away.","The whole herd rushes into the sea."],"answer":"The whole herd rushes into the sea.","level":1},{"question":"Which detail best supports the idea that walruses are social animals?","choices":["Its blubber can be up to six inches thick.","Hundreds crowd together and talk with barks and grunts.","Tusks help a walrus climb out of the water.","A walrus can weigh as much as a small car."],"answer":"Hundreds crowd together and talk with barks and grunts.","level":2}]}],"2":[{"long":true,"text":"Wolves Return to Yellowstone\n\nA Missing Predator\n\nBy the 1920s, wolves had disappeared from Yellowstone National Park. Ranchers and hunters had killed them to protect livestock, and for decades the park had no wolves at all. Without this top predator, the number of elk, a large deer-like animal, grew quickly. The hungry elk ate so many young trees and shrubs along the rivers that few new plants could grow.\n\nA Bold Plan\n\nIn 1995, scientists decided to bring wolves back. They captured wolves in Canada and released them in the park over two years. Some people worried the wolves would harm nearby ranches, while others were excited to see the park's natural balance restored. Scientists promised to watch the wolves closely.\n\nChanges in the Park\n\nOver the following years, the wolves changed how the elk behaved. Elk began avoiding open valleys where wolves could hunt them easily, and they moved from place to place more often. In some areas, young willows and aspens began to grow again along the streams. Beavers returned to use the trees for building dams, and songbirds nested in the thicker plants. Scientists are still studying exactly how large these changes were, but most agree that wolves play an important role.\n\nWhy It Matters\n\nThe story of Yellowstone's wolves shows how every living thing is connected. When one animal is removed from a community, the effects can ripple through plants, rivers, and other animals. Bringing a missing species back can help an ecosystem heal, though it takes careful planning and many years.","questions":[{"question":"Why did the number of elk grow quickly after the wolves disappeared?","choices":["No top predator remained to hunt them.","Elk moved into the park from nearby Canada.","Ranchers fed the elk during the cold winters.","Many new plants were growing along the rivers."],"answer":"No top predator remained to hunt them.","level":0},{"question":"What does the word \"restored\" mean in the section \"A Bold Plan\"?","choices":["made smaller than before","moved far away from home","kept hidden from the public for many years","brought back to a former condition"],"answer":"brought back to a former condition","level":2},{"question":"How did the wolves change the way elk behaved?","choices":["Elk began living only in the high, cold mountains far from wolves.","Elk stopped eating the plants along the rivers.","Elk learned to hunt young wolves in the valleys.","Elk avoided open valleys where wolves could easily hunt them."],"answer":"Elk avoided open valleys where wolves could easily hunt them.","level":1},{"question":"In which section does the author explain how the park's plants and animals changed?","choices":["A Bold Plan","A Missing Predator","Why It Matters","Changes in the Park"],"answer":"Changes in the Park","level":1},{"question":"Which sentence best expresses a central idea of the passage?","choices":["Elk are among the most common large animals in North America.","Scientists should never move animals from one country to another.","Ranchers should be allowed to hunt any animal that threatens their farms or their livestock.","When a species is removed or returned, the effects can spread through an ecosystem."],"answer":"When a species is removed or returned, the effects can spread through an ecosystem.","level":2}]},{"long":true,"text":"The Giant Redwoods\n\nThe Tallest Trees\n\nCoast redwoods are the tallest trees on Earth. The tallest known redwood, named Hyperion, stands more than three hundred and eighty feet high, which is taller than the Statue of Liberty from the ground to the tip of its torch. These giants grow along the foggy coast of northern California, and they can live for more than two thousand years.\n\nDrinking From the Fog\n\nRedwoods grow in a region that gets little rain during the summer, yet the trees thrive. Their secret is the fog that rolls in from the Pacific Ocean. The needles of a redwood collect tiny droplets of fog, which drip to the ground and feed the roots. Scientists have found that fog can supply about a third of the water the trees use each year. Some water is even absorbed directly through the needles.\n\nShallow Roots, Strong Bonds\n\nIt would seem that such tall trees need deep roots, but redwood roots reach down only about six feet. Instead, they spread out wide, sometimes a hundred feet from the trunk, and they weave together with the roots of neighboring trees. Because the roots are linked, the trees help hold each other up in strong winds and floods.\n\nA Home in the Sky\n\nThe high branches of an old redwood hold small gardens of soil, ferns, and even other trees. Salamanders and many insects live their whole lives high above the ground without ever touching the forest floor.\n\nSaving the Giants\n\nOver a century ago, loggers cut down most of the old redwoods. Today, only a small part of the original forest remains, and it is protected in parks. Visitors can walk among the ancient trees and see for themselves why they are worth saving.","questions":[{"question":"How do redwoods get water during the dry summer?","choices":["Their needles collect droplets from the fog.","Rain falls on them almost every afternoon.","Their deep roots reach water far underground.","Their trunks store water from the winter."],"answer":"Their needles collect droplets from the fog.","level":0},{"question":"What does the word \"thrive\" mean in the section \"Drinking From the Fog\"?","choices":["dry out slowly","grow only in winter","grow strong and healthy","lose all of their needles in the winter"],"answer":"grow strong and healthy","level":2},{"question":"Why do the linked roots of redwoods matter?","choices":["They help the trees stand up in strong winds and floods.","They help the trees grow much taller and faster each year.","They keep other plants from growing nearby.","They let the trees move to new places."],"answer":"They help the trees stand up in strong winds and floods.","level":1},{"question":"In which section would a reader find information about animals that live in the treetops?","choices":["Saving the Giants","Shallow Roots, Strong Bonds","A Home in the Sky","The Tallest Trees"],"answer":"A Home in the Sky","level":1},{"question":"What is a central idea of this passage?","choices":["California is the best place to see a very tall statue.","Redwoods are the only trees that can survive in dry weather.","Loggers cut down the redwoods because their wood was too heavy.","Redwoods have remarkable features that help them live, and they deserve protection."],"answer":"Redwoods have remarkable features that help them live, and they deserve protection.","level":2}]},{"long":true,"text":"Sharks: Misunderstood Hunters\n\nAncient Fish\n\nSharks have been swimming in Earth's oceans for more than four hundred million years, long before the first dinosaurs. There are more than five hundred kinds of sharks, ranging from the dwarf lantern shark, which could rest in your hand, to the whale shark, which can grow as long as a school bus.\n\nBuilt to Hunt\n\nA shark's body is designed for hunting. Its skeleton is made of cartilage, the same flexible material found in your ears, instead of bone, which makes it light and quick. Rows of teeth replace themselves throughout life, so a shark may use thousands of teeth. Sharks can also detect tiny electrical signals from other animals' muscles and hear sounds from far away.\n\nHealthy Oceans\n\nSharks help keep ocean communities in balance. By hunting sick and weak fish, they keep populations healthy. In places where sharks have disappeared, their prey has become so numerous that it has overgrazed important habitats like seagrass beds.\n\nRarely a Danger\n\nMany people fear sharks, but attacks on humans are very rare. Most sharks avoid people, and many eat only small fish or tiny sea creatures. Far more people are injured each year by dogs than by sharks.\n\nSharks in Trouble\n\nEach year, humans catch tens of millions of sharks, sometimes only for their fins. Because sharks grow slowly and have few young, their numbers recover slowly. Protecting sharks with laws and marine reserves helps keep the ocean healthy.","questions":[{"question":"What is a shark's skeleton made of?","choices":["shell, like the covering of a turtle","cartilage, the same material found in your ears","bone, the same material found in your ribs","metal that it picks up from the seafloor"],"answer":"cartilage, the same material found in your ears","level":0},{"question":"What does the word \"numerous\" mean in the section \"Healthy Oceans\"?","choices":["very small","able to swim very quickly","hard to find","present in large numbers"],"answer":"present in large numbers","level":2},{"question":"Why are sharks important to ocean communities?","choices":["They keep fish populations healthy and balanced.","They clean the water by eating the plants that grow in it.","They protect smaller fish from storms.","They build the reefs where fish live."],"answer":"They keep fish populations healthy and balanced.","level":1},{"question":"What can the reader conclude from the section \"Rarely a Danger\"?","choices":["People should swim near sharks often.","Fear of sharks is greater than the real danger they pose.","Dogs are the most dangerous animals in the world.","Sharks never attack any animals."],"answer":"Fear of sharks is greater than the real danger they pose.","level":1},{"question":"Which detail best supports the idea that sharks are in trouble?","choices":["Whale sharks can grow as long as a school bus.","Sharks have been swimming in the oceans for hundreds of millions of years.","A shark's teeth replace themselves throughout its life.","Humans catch tens of millions of sharks each year."],"answer":"Humans catch tens of millions of sharks each year.","level":2}]},{"long":true,"text":"Komodo Dragons\n\nThe Largest Lizard\n\nThe Komodo dragon is the largest lizard in the world. An adult can grow longer than nine feet and weigh as much as a grown man. It lives on only a few islands in Indonesia, where the land is hot and dry and covered in grass. Despite its heavy body, a Komodo dragon can run in short bursts at about twelve miles per hour.\n\nA Powerful Hunter\n\nKomodo dragons are patient predators. A dragon hides beside a trail and waits for a deer or a wild pig. When it strikes, its serrated teeth and strong jaws cause deep wounds. Scientists discovered that the dragon's bite also delivers venom from glands in its lower jaw, which makes the prey's blood pressure drop and keeps wounds from clotting. After a bite, the dragon often follows its victim for many hours until the animal grows weak.\n\nA Keen Sense of Smell\n\nA Komodo dragon tastes the air with its long, yellow, forked tongue, flicking it in and out. The tongue carries tiny particles to a special organ in the roof of the mouth. With this sense, a dragon can detect a dead animal from miles away.\n\nGrowing Up Safely\n\nYoung dragons are small and make easy meals for adults, so hatchlings climb trees and spend their first years high in the branches, eating insects and small lizards. Only when they are bigger and stronger do they come down.\n\nAn Uncertain Future\n\nOnly a few thousand Komodo dragons remain in the wild. Their habitat is shrinking, and the warming climate is making their island homes smaller. Indonesia protects the dragons in a national park, and visitors travel from all over the world to see them.","questions":[{"question":"Where do Komodo dragons live in the wild?","choices":["along the coasts of Australia","in the thick rainforests of northern South America","in the deserts of northern Africa","on a few hot, dry islands in Indonesia"],"answer":"on a few hot, dry islands in Indonesia","level":0},{"question":"What does the word \"venom\" mean in the section \"A Powerful Hunter\"?","choices":["a poison that an animal injects","a strong smell that warns enemies","a way of hiding in tall grass","a kind of heavy armor"],"answer":"a poison that an animal injects","level":2},{"question":"Why do young Komodo dragons spend their first years in trees?","choices":["The young dragons are not yet able to run on the ground.","Their favorite food grows only in the trees.","The trees are cooler than the hot ground.","Adults might eat them if they stayed on the ground."],"answer":"Adults might eat them if they stayed on the ground.","level":1},{"question":"How does a Komodo dragon use its tongue?","choices":["It uses the tongue to dig up buried eggs.","It uses the tongue to clean its scaly skin.","It tastes the air to find food from far away.","It uses the tongue to call other dragons."],"answer":"It tastes the air to find food from far away.","level":1},{"question":"What is a central idea of this passage?","choices":["Lizards are the most dangerous animals living on islands.","Young animals are safest when they live in the trees.","Indonesia has more national parks than any other country.","The Komodo dragon has remarkable abilities, but its future is uncertain."],"answer":"The Komodo dragon has remarkable abilities, but its future is uncertain.","level":2}]},{"long":true,"text":"Pangolins: The Armored Mammals\n\nScales Like a Pinecone\n\nThe pangolin is the only mammal in the world covered in scales. Its scales are made of keratin, the same material found in your fingernails and hair. They overlap like the scales of a pinecone and make up about a fifth of the animal's weight. When a pangolin senses danger, it curls into a tight ball, and its sharp scales protect its soft belly. Even a lion has trouble opening one.\n\nA Tongue Built for Ants\n\nPangolins have no teeth. Instead, they eat ants and termites. A pangolin tears open a nest with its strong claws, then slides out a sticky tongue that can be longer than its body. The tongue is anchored deep inside the animal, which lets it reach far into narrow tunnels. A single pangolin can eat millions of insects in a year, helping to keep insect numbers in check.\n\nNight Wanderers\n\nMost pangolins are active at night. They find food using their sense of smell, because their eyesight is poor. During the day, some sleep in burrows, while others sleep curled up in tree hollows. Pangolins are shy and spend most of their lives alone.\n\nPangolins in Danger\n\nSadly, pangolins are among the most trafficked mammals in the world. They are hunted for their scales and meat, even though the scales have no proven medical value. All eight kinds of pangolin are now protected by international law, and conservation groups work to rescue and release them.","questions":[{"question":"What are a pangolin's scales made of?","choices":["metal that it picks up from rocks in its burrow","keratin, the same material as fingernails","shell, like the shell of a turtle","bone, the same material as ribs"],"answer":"keratin, the same material as fingernails","level":0},{"question":"What does the word \"trafficked\" most nearly mean in the section \"Pangolins in Danger\"?","choices":["moved to new homes","illegally bought and sold","protected by large crowds","carefully studied by scientists"],"answer":"illegally bought and sold","level":2},{"question":"Why does a pangolin curl into a ball?","choices":["It is trying to stay warm at night.","It wants to roll quickly down a hill.","Its scales protect its soft belly from danger.","It is searching for ants inside a nest."],"answer":"Its scales protect its soft belly from danger.","level":1},{"question":"How do pangolins find food?","choices":["They watch for movement with their eyes.","They listen for the sound of ants.","They follow other animals at night.","They use their sense of smell."],"answer":"They use their sense of smell.","level":1},{"question":"Which detail best supports the idea that pangolins are helpful?","choices":["Pangolins sleep in burrows and tree hollows.","Pangolins have no teeth in their mouths.","A pangolin can eat millions of insects in a year.","Pangolins are shy and live mostly alone."],"answer":"A pangolin can eat millions of insects in a year.","level":2}]},{"long":true,"text":"City Falcons\n\nFastest Animal on Earth\n\nThe peregrine falcon is the fastest animal on Earth. When it dives to catch a bird in midair, it can reach speeds of more than two hundred miles per hour. Its body is shaped like a teardrop, and special bumps inside its nostrils help it breathe while rushing through the air.\n\nA Near Disappearance\n\nBy the 1960s, peregrine falcons were disappearing. A powerful chemical called DDT, which farmers sprayed to kill insects, built up in the birds' bodies and made their eggshells so thin that they cracked. In 1972, the United States banned DDT, and scientists began raising young falcons to release into the wild. Slowly, the number of falcons grew.\n\nCliffs of Glass and Steel\n\nPeregrines usually nest on high cliffs, but many have discovered that tall buildings and bridges are just as good. A ledge on a skyscraper looks like a cliff, and city streets are full of pigeons, an easy meal. New York City now has about twenty pairs of nesting peregrines, including some on famous bridges and towers.\n\nWatching the Nests\n\nEach spring, bird watchers keep an eye on city nests with cameras. They count the eggs and follow the chicks as they grow. Seeing a falcon family so close to home reminds city dwellers that wildlife can thrive almost anywhere, if people help to protect it.","questions":[{"question":"What caused peregrine falcon eggshells to become so thin?","choices":["a chemical called DDT","a lack of food in cities","cracks made by city noise","cold weather in the spring"],"answer":"a chemical called DDT","level":0},{"question":"What does the word \"thrive\" mean in the section \"Watching the Nests\"?","choices":["hide from view","become very quiet","move away quickly","grow and do very well"],"answer":"grow and do very well","level":2},{"question":"Why do peregrines nest on skyscrapers?","choices":["There are no other places left to nest.","The buildings are warmer and safer than natural cliffs are.","People feed the falcons on the ledges.","The ledges are like cliffs, and pigeons are nearby to eat."],"answer":"The ledges are like cliffs, and pigeons are nearby to eat.","level":1},{"question":"In which section would a reader learn how the falcon population was helped to recover?","choices":["A Near Disappearance","Fastest Animal on Earth","Cliffs of Glass and Steel","Watching the Nests"],"answer":"A Near Disappearance","level":1},{"question":"What is a central idea of this passage?","choices":["Peregrine falcons recovered with human help and now live in cities.","Pigeons are the most important animals in the city.","Peregrine falcons are faster than any machine ever built.","Bird watchers should not use cameras near any nest."],"answer":"Peregrine falcons recovered with human help and now live in cities.","level":2}]}]}},{"id":"2026-11-04-math-banks","released":"2026-11-04","kind":"monthly","math_banks":{"graph_sets":[{"theme":"favorite snacks","categories":["Popcorn","Pretzels","Crackers","Cheese","Raisins"],"noun":"students"},{"theme":"favorite subjects","categories":["Reading","Math","Science","Art","Music"],"noun":"students"},{"theme":"playground games","categories":["Tag","Hopscotch","Swings","Kickball","Jumprope"],"noun":"students"},{"theme":"bake sale items sold","categories":["Cookies","Muffins","Brownies","Cupcakes","Scones"],"noun":"items"},{"theme":"favorite zoo animals","categories":["Lions","Zebras","Monkeys","Giraffes","Penguins"],"noun":"students"}],"mass_items":[{"item":"a banana","value":120,"unit":"grams"},{"item":"an egg","value":60,"unit":"grams"},{"item":"a baseball","value":150,"unit":"grams"},{"item":"a loaf of bread","value":500,"unit":"grams"},{"item":"a laptop","value":2,"unit":"kilograms"},{"item":"a bag of flour","value":2,"unit":"kilograms"},{"item":"a pumpkin","value":4,"unit":"kilograms"},{"item":"a bag of rice","value":5,"unit":"kilograms"}],"liquid_items":[{"item":"a rain barrel","liters":200},{"item":"a washing machine","liters":60},{"item":"a water cooler jug","liters":19},{"item":"a pitcher of juice","liters":2},{"item":"a can of paint","liters":4},{"item":"a camping water tank","liters":25}],"time_activities":["Choir practice","The bake sale","Swim lessons","The book fair","Chess club","Story time","The science fair","Gym class","The parade","Math club"],"two_step_stories":[{"form":"mul_sub","text":"{name} sets out {g} trays with {p} {item} on each tray. Then {name} puts {s} {item} away. How many {item} are still out?"},{"form":"mul_sub","text":"{name} has {g} boxes with {p} {item} in each box. {name} donates {s} {item} to the school. How many {item} are left?"},{"form":"mul_sub","text":"{name} collects {g} bags of {item} with {p} in each bag. On the bus, {s} {item} are lost. How many {item} does {name} still have?"},{"form":"mul_add","text":"{name} plants {g} rows of flowers with {p} flowers in each row. Then {name} plants {e} more flowers. How many flowers are there now?"},{"form":"mul_add","text":"{name} arranges {g} shelves with {p} {item} on each shelf. {name} also has {e} {item} in a box. How many {item} does {name} have in all?"},{"form":"mul_add","text":"At the fair, {name} wins {g} prizes that each have {p} {item}. {name} then picks up {e} extra {item}. How many {item} does {name} have in total?"},{"form":"div_add","text":"{name} divides {t} {item} equally into {b} bowls. Then {name} adds {e} more {item} to each bowl. How many {item} are in each bowl now?"},{"form":"div_add","text":"{name} shares {t} {item} equally among {b} teammates. Each teammate then finds {e} more {item}. How many {item} does each teammate have?"},{"form":"div_add","text":"{t} {item} are packed equally into {b} boxes. {name} puts {e} more {item} in each box. How many {item} are in each box now?"},{"form":"add_div","text":"{name} has {a} {item} and gets {b} more. {name} then packs them equally into {c} bags. How many {item} are in each bag?"},{"form":"add_div","text":"{name} picks {a} apples in the morning and {b} apples in the afternoon. {name} puts them equally into {c} baskets. How many apples are in each basket?"},{"form":"add_div","text":"{name} collects {a} {item} and a friend collects {b} {item}. They share all the {item} equally among {c} children. How many {item} does each child get?"}]}}];
